x": {"title": "Don Diamont", "text": "on \"The Young and the Restless\" (head written by Lynn Marie Latham & Scott Hamner) has paralleled his personal life in some ways. The show has revealed that the persona of \"Brad Carlton\" is actually \"George Kaplan.\" (For dramatic purposes, the show has explained this by disclosing that George/Brad had to hide his past, and his Jewishness, for his own safety.) Don Diamont Don Diamont (born Donald Feinberg; December 31, 1962) is an American actor. His best known roles include Brad Carlton on \"The Young and the Restless\" and Bill Spencer Jr. on \"The Bold and the Beautiful\". In 1990,"}, "final_score": 1.9939188540811634, "relevance_score": 0.9997917498500998, "ground_score": 0.9941271042310637, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2878198556908501, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0002082481963906254, "[Relevant]": 0.9997823712123793}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9912155925343252, "[Partially supported]": 0.005283331603314393, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003229635828251265}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000793295215883836, "[Utility:2]": 0.000683863221895915, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018441904335871656, "[Utility:4]": 0.0007138884228699823, "[Utility:5]": 0.5955788095435887}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Don Diamont played the role of Brad Carlton on Young and the Restless from 1985 to 1996 and from 1998 to 2009.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Don Diamont played the role of Brad Carlton on Young and the Restless from 1985 to 1996 and from 1998 to 2009.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9951815786445275, "ctx": {"title": "Don Diamont", "text": "with the show for a year. Diamont is best known for his portrayal of opportunistic businessman Brad Carlton on \"The Young and the Restless\". He initially joined the show in 1985 and left the cast in 1996, but returned in 1998, remaining on the show until his character was killed off in early 2009. On March 5, 2009 it was announced that Diamont would be joining the cast of the CBS daytime drama \"The Bold and the Beautiful\" in the contract role of Bill Spencer Jr. His first air date was May 12, 2009. \"Don is a versatile actor with"}, "final_score": 1.9951815786445275, "relevance_score": 0.9995522992019596, "ground_score": 0.9956292794425677, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1958002306987114, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00044769211710314654, "[Relevant]": 0.9995329178411758}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9935333814210778, "[Partially supported]": 0.0038143391766682164, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0024627224687120124}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00027106240997008325, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004985600728974972, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001193471638806892, "[Utility:4]": 0.0008513985407564489, "[Utility:5]": 0.894402862643458}], "n_tokens": 46}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Brad Carlton was first introduced as the Abbotts' gardener, who caught the eye of the youngest Abbott daughter, Traci.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The two were married twice, with the second marriage producing a daughter, Colleen Carlton.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Brad Carlton was first introduced as the Abbotts' gardener, who caught the eye of the youngest Abbott daughter, Traci.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The two were married twice, with the second marriage producing a daughter, Colleen Carlton.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.879018364284019, "ctx": {"title": "Brad Carlton", "text": "Brad Carlton was first introduced as the Abbotts' gardener, who caught the eye of the youngest Abbott daughter, Traci. The two were married twice, with the second marriage producing a daughter, Colleen Carlton. During his second marriage to Traci, Brad was held captive in a cage by his unstable ex-wife, Lisa Mansfield. After he divorced Traci, Brad was tricked into marrying the wealthy widow, Cassandra Rawlins, after a ski trip to Aspen. Brad eventually returned to Genoa City with plans to divorce Cassandra, but en route to finalize her divorce plans, Cassandra was hit by a truck and killed. Brad"}, "final_score": 1.879018364284019, "relevance_score": 0.9923637827572406, "ground_score": 0.8866545815267785, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.06628270332442554, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.007635268974298109, "[Relevant]": 0.9922405506322941}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8649205116685044, "[Partially supported]": 0.03101628903662579, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.09704138328029471}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00034829042580644107, "[Utility:2]": 0.0009504570162367537, "[Utility:3]": 8.46880231642809e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010520600134124453, "[Utility:5]": 0.5428558042736749}], "n_tokens": 56}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 4], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "\"Despicable Me 3\" premiered on June 14, 2017, at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival, and had a theatrical release in the United States on June 30, 2017, by Universal Pictures in 3D, RealD 3D, Dolby Cinema and IMAX. The film received mixed reviews from critics and grossed over $1 billion worldwide, making it the fourth highest-grossing film of 2017, the eighth highest-grossing animated film of all time and the 38th highest-grossing film of all time. It is Illumination's second film to gross over $1 billion, after \"Minions\" in 2015, becoming Illumination's first ever animated franchise to do so.", "question": "When does the dispicable me 3 come out at Annecy?", "short_answers": ["June 14, 2017"], "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3"}, {"context": "The film premiered on June 14, 2017, at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival, and was released in the United States on June 30, 2017. It was the first film by Illumination to be released in Dolby Vision and to be in the 2.39:1 anamorphic format.", "question": "When does the dispicable me 3 come out in the United States?", "short_answers": ["June 30, 2017"], "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3"}, {"context": "\"Despicable Me 3\" premiered on June 14, 2017, at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival, and had a theatrical release in the United States on June 30, 2017, by Universal Pictures in 3D, RealD 3D, Dolby Cinema and IMAX. The film received mixed reviews from critics and grossed over $1 billion worldwide, making it the fourth highest-grossing film of 2017, the eighth highest-grossing animated film of all time and the 38th highest-grossing film of all time. It is Illumination's second film to gross over $1 billion, after \"Minions\" in 2015, becoming Illumination's first ever animated franchise to do so.", "question": "When does the despicable me 3 come out in Annecy?", "short_answers": ["June 14, 2017"], "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3"}, {"context": "The film premiered on June 14, 2017, at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival, and was released in the United States on June 30, 2017. It was the first film by Illumination to be released in Dolby Vision and to be in the 2.39:1 anamorphic format.", "question": "When does the despicable me 3 come out in United States?", "short_answers": ["June 30, 2017"], "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Despicable Me 3", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Despicable%20Me%203"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Despicable Me 3 is a 2017 American computer-animated comedy film produced by Illumination and distributed by Universal Pictures. ", "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3"}], "long_answer": "An early premier of Universal's Despicable Me 3 will show on June 14, 2017 at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival. It will then be available across the United States on June 30, 2017."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Despicable Me 3 is a 2017 American computer-animated comedy film produced by Illumination and distributed by Universal Pictures.", "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3"}, {"content": "Despicable Me 3 was released on DVD, Blu-ray, Blu-ray 3D, and Ultra HD Blu-ray in the United Kingdom on November 6.[23] In the US, Despicable Me 3 was released on digital download on November 21, and was released on DVD and Blu-ray on December 5.", "wikipage": "Despicable Me 3 Home media"}], "long_answer": "The 2017 comedy film Despicable Me 3 came out at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival on June 14, 2017, and came out in the United States on June 30, 2017. The film was released on DVD, Blu-ray, Blu-ray 3D, and Ultra HD Blu-ray in the United Kingdom on November 6 and in the US was released on digital download on November 21 and on DVD and Blu-ray on December 5."}], "sample_id": "6774732418951895235", "question": "When does the dispicable me 3 come out?", "docs": [{"id": "16840688", "title": "Despicable Me (franchise)", "text": "who recruits Gru to take down a new villain. New cast members include Benjamin Bratt as Eduardo; Bratt replaced the previously cast Al Pacino. Steve Coogan voices Silas Ramsbottom, lead director of the Anti-Villain League (AVL). The sequel was met with generally positive reviews and grossed more than its predecessor with over $970 million worldwide. A third film, \"Despicable Me 3\", was released on June 30, 2017. The film was directed by Pierre Coffin and Kyle Balda, and co-directed by Eric Guillon. Cinco Paul and Ken Daurio, the writers of the first two films, returned to write the screenplay for", "score": 0.77490234375, "summary": "\"Despicable Me 3\" was released on June 30, 2017.", "extraction": "\"Despicable Me 3\" was released on June 30, 2017."}], "answer": "The 2017 comedy film Despicable Me 3 came out at the Annecy International Animated Film Festival on June 14, 2017, and came out in the United States on June 30, 2017. The film was released on DVD, Blu-ray, Blu-ray 3D, and Ultra HD Blu-ray in the United Kingdom on November 6 and in the US was released on digital download on November 21 and on DVD and Blu-ray on December 5.", "output": "The third film in the Despicable Me franchise, Despicable Me 3, was released on June 30, 2017 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The third film in the Despicable Me franchise, Despicable Me 3, was released on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 306, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The film was released in the United States on June 14, 2017, and in the United Kingdom on July 14, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The film was released in the United States on June 14, 2017, and in the United Kingdom on July 14, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9011623181639017, "ctx": {"title": "Despicable Me 3", "text": "was released on April 21, 2017. A third trailer was released on May 24, 2017. \"Despicable Me 3\" was released on DVD, Blu-ray, Blu-ray 3D and Ultra HD Blu-ray in the United Kingdom on November 6, 2017. In the US, \"Despicable Me 3\" was released on digital download on November 21, 2017, and was released on DVD, Blu-ray and Ultra HD Blu-ray on December 5, 2017. The releases also include a short film, titled \"The Secret Life of Kyle\". \"Despicable Me 3\" grossed $264.6 million in the United States and Canada and $770.2 million in other territories for a worldwide"}, "final_score": 1.9011623181639017, "relevance_score": 0.9976032688487401, "ground_score": 0.9035590493151615, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21829235758391072, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0023966318349754444, "[Relevant]": 0.9975619299401226}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8381342138385347, "[Partially supported]": 0.12753179266690295, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.032497988261221175}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00027643341683144645, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011545987994606712, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002818855774414768, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010890524203572465, "[Utility:5]": 0.8370128374598275}], "n_tokens": 41}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The film was released in the United States on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The film was released in the United States on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8499209548945883, "ctx": {"title": "Despicable Me 2", "text": "Balda, and produced by Chris Meledandri and Janet Healy. The film, set in the 1960s, focuses on the Minions before they met Gru, where they compete for the right to become henchmen of an ambitious villain, Scarlet Overkill, voiced by Sandra Bullock. A sequel, titled \"Despicable Me 3\", was released on June 30, 2017. The film was directed by Pierre Coffin and Kyle Balda, with Eric Guillon serving as co-director, and was produced by Chris Meledandri and Janet Healy. Cinco Paul and Ken Daurio returned to write the screenplay for the film. Steve Carell, Kristen Wiig, Miranda Cosgrove, Dana Gaier,"}, "final_score": 1.8499209548945883, "relevance_score": 0.9935117301001682, "ground_score": 0.8564092247944202, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.31798879606806923, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006487623701439191, "[Relevant]": 0.993412781429281}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7985222443439981, "[Partially supported]": 0.10474331784645409, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.09029438395735005}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005438551672039964, "[Utility:2]": 0.000267132682895807, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002788612160788784, "[Utility:4]": 0.0013619213303488523, "[Utility:5]": 0.7748321658204749}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The film was released in the United States on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The film was released in the United States on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7997098218355199, "ctx": {"title": "Despicable Me 2", "text": "became the second-highest-grossing animated film of 2013 and the third highest-grossing film of 2013. It is also the most profitable film in the 101-year history of Universal Studios. A spinoff/prequel film, \"Minions\", focusing on the little yellow henchmen before they met Gru, was released on July 10, 2015. A sequel, \"Despicable Me 3\", was released on June 30, 2017. A mysterious vehicle using a giant magnet steals a highly potent mutagen known as PX-41 from a secret laboratory in the Arctic Circle. The Anti-Villain League (AVL), led by Silas Ramsbottom, tries to recruit former super-villain Gru to track down the"}, "final_score": 1.7997098218355199, "relevance_score": 0.9932024687388591, "ground_score": 0.8065073530966608, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3023492756134753, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006796851324751383, "[Relevant]": 0.9931031217149523}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7490827214847194, "[Partially supported]": 0.10541573569560737, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.1396531599797311}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004553039823494437, "[Utility:2]": 0.00022146457419702068, "[Utility:3]": 0.00027723680031931927, "[Utility:4]": 0.001185590852431786, "[Utility:5]": 0.7613438756431848}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The third film in the Despicable Me franchise, Despicable Me 3, was released on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The third film in the Despicable Me franchise, Despicable Me 3, was released on June 30, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9144709266261168, "ctx": {"title": "Despicable Me (franchise)", "text": "who recruits Gru to take down a new villain. New cast members include Benjamin Bratt as Eduardo; Bratt replaced the previously cast Al Pacino. Steve Coogan voices Silas Ramsbottom, lead director of the Anti-Villain League (AVL). The sequel was met with generally positive reviews and grossed more than its predecessor with over $970 million worldwide. A third film, \"Despicable Me 3\", was released on June 30, 2017. The film was directed by Pierre Coffin and Kyle Balda, and co-directed by Eric Guillon. Cinco Paul and Ken Daurio, the writers of the first two films, returned to write the screenplay for"}, "final_score": 1.9144709266261168, "relevance_score": 0.9976768379484804, "ground_score": 0.9167940886776366, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1856546656447274, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002323044761847567, "[Relevant]": 0.9976264681565418}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8896489904069272, "[Partially supported]": 0.047519466112151076, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05913893108688156}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003146787854865281, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011667180292695842, "[Utility:3]": 0.00024127259405178547, "[Utility:4]": 0.0005501250758985924, "[Utility:5]": 0.7108440472585639}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The first film, Despicable Me, was released on June 14, 2010.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The second film, Despicable Me 2, was released on June 14, 2013.[Continue to Use Evidence]The third film, Despicable Me 3, was released on June 14, 2017.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first film, Despicable Me, was released on June 14, 2010.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The second film, Despicable Me 2, was released on June 14, 2013.[Continue to Use Evidence]The third film, Despicable Me 3, was released on June 14, 2017.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1202906995232884, "ctx": {"title": "Despicable Me (franchise)", "text": "with a scheduled release date of July 3, 2020. It will be directed by Kyle Balda, with co-direction by \"Legends of Chamberlain Heights\" co-creator, Brad Ableson, and written by Brian Lynch. Three short films based on the \"Despicable Me\" film, \"Home Makeover\", \"Orientation Day\" and \"Banana\", were released on December 14, 2010 on the film's DVD and Blu-ray. The \"Despicable Me 2\" DVD and Blu-ray, released on December 10, 2013, included another three short films: \"Puppy\", \"Panic in the Mailroom\", and \"Training Wheels\". A series of Minions short films screened in 2015 with \"Minions\". Another short film, titled \"Mower Minions\","}, "final_score": 1.1202906995232884, "relevance_score": 0.8438951045175643, "ground_score": 0.27639559500572414, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.13397784547270952, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.15605800804148948, "[Relevant]": 0.8436416334028015}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.13973234344099156, "[Partially supported]": 0.2693411415026492, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5837169905580881}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00013297654596211114, "[Utility:2]": 0.00010747756644111232, "[Utility:3]": 0.00019347973272045436, "[Utility:4]": 0.002548596879446073, "[Utility:5]": 0.9472012007732656}], "n_tokens": 79}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 4], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did McCrory's originally file for bankruptcy?", "short_answers": ["January, 1933"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "In 1989, 1,300 stores were operated by the McCrory company. However, as the decade turned, its fortunes decreased, and by 1992 it filed for bankruptcy. The changing retail landscape including the migrating of shoppers from the inner cities to the influx of superstores such as Target and Wal-Mart sealed the fate of the once mighty Five And Ten. Several rounds of store closures followed, with one of the biggest coming in 1997 when McCrory's shuttered 300 of its last 460 stores. The company also converted some stores to their Dollar Zone format of dollar store, but these closed in early 2002. In December 2001, McCrory Stores announced the remaining McCrory's, TG&Y, G. C. Murphy and J.J. Newberry stores it was operating would begin liquidating and in February 2002 the company ceased operation.", "question": "When did McCrory's file for bankruptcy the second time?", "short_answers": ["1992"], "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}, {"context": "In 1989, 1,300 stores were operated by the McCrory company. However, as the decade turned, its fortunes decreased, and by 1992 it filed for bankruptcy. The changing retail landscape including the migrating of shoppers from the inner cities to the influx of superstores such as Target and Wal-Mart sealed the fate of the once mighty Five And Ten. Several rounds of store closures followed, with one of the biggest coming in 1997 when McCrory's shuttered 300 of its last 460 stores. The company also converted some stores to their Dollar Zone format of dollar store, but these closed in early 2002. In December 2001, McCrory Stores announced the remaining McCrory's, TG&Y, G. C. Murphy and J.J. Newberry stores it was operating would begin liquidating and in February 2002 the company ceased operation.", "question": "When did McCrory's completely cease operation?", "short_answers": ["February 2002"], "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "McCrory Stores", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McCrory%20Stores"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "McCrory Stores or J.G. McCrory's was a chain of five and dime stores in the United States based in York, Pennsylvania.", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}, {"content": "With 244 retail units in its chain, the company entered bankruptcy in January 1933.[3] The company was dissolved, but it was eventually re-established as McCrory Stores and resumed operations.", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores Decline"}, {"content": "As the economic expansion of the 1980s progressed, so did the successes of McCrory. They continued to expand and remodel stores as volume and profits grew exponentially.", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}, {"content": "John Graham McCrorey (1860-1923) opened his first store in Scottdale, Pennsylvania, in 1882", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}, {"content": "The changing retail landscape including the migrating of shoppers from the inner cities to the influx of superstores such as Target and Wal-Mart sealed the fate of the once mighty Five And Ten.", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}], "long_answer": "The U.S. based five and dime store established in 1882, McCrory's, was once a successful business with hundreds of chain locations. Despite\u00a0its early success, the company entered bankruptcy in January 1933. Fortunately, they were able to re-establish themselves and continue to grow once more. However, they had to file for bankruptcy again in 1992 due to the changing retail landscape. Superstores such as Target and Walmart took the shoppers who once frequented the five and dime stores. The company finally had to close its doors in February 2002."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "McCrory Stores or J.G. McCrory's was a chain of five and dime stores in the United States based in York, Pennsylvania. The stores typically sold shoes, clothing, housewares, fabrics, penny candy, toys, cosmetics, and often included a lunch counter or snack bar.", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores"}, {"content": "With 244 retail units in its chain, the company entered bankruptcy in January 1933.[3] The company was dissolved, but it was eventually re-established as McCrory Stores and resumed operations.", "wikipage": "McCrory Stores Founding"}], "long_answer": "McCrory Stores or J.G. McCrory's was a chain of five and dime stores in the United States based in York, Pennsylvania. The stores typically sold shoes, clothing, housewares, fabrics, penny candy, toys, cosmetics, and often included a lunch counter or snack bar. With 244 retail units in its chain, the company entered bankruptcy in January, 1933. The company was dissolved, but it was eventually re-established as McCrory Stores and resumed operations. It filed for bankruptcy the second time in 1992 due to the changing retail landscape and in February 2002 the company ceased operation."}], "sample_id": "-8270287755298735414", "question": "When did mccrory's go out of business?", "docs": [{"id": "4532442", "title": "G. C. Murphy", "text": "sold the division to the McCrory's chain led by Meshulam Riklis. McCrory's rebranded some and closed others. The McCrory's chain filed for bankruptcy in 1992, closed many of their stores (including former G.C. Murphy's outlets) in 1997, and ceased its remaining operations in 2002. Although the stores ceased business operations some time ago, the chain's foundation, the G.C. Murphy Foundation, is still in existence. Retirees and former employees of the stores have a volunteer organization that allows them to keep in touch. In 2005, the \"Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\" reported that the Murphy Foundation had commissioned a writer to compile the G.C.", "score": 0.80810546875, "summary": "McCrory's went out of business in 2002.", "extraction": "The McCrory's chain filed for bankruptcy in 1992, closed many of their stores (including former G.C. Murphy's outlets) in 1997."}], "answer": "McCrory Stores or J.G. McCrory's was a chain of five and dime stores in the United States based in York, Pennsylvania. The stores typically sold shoes, clothing, housewares, fabrics, penny candy, toys, cosmetics, and often included a lunch counter or snack bar. With 244 retail units in its chain, the company entered bankruptcy in January, 1933. The company was dissolved, but it was eventually re-established as McCrory Stores and resumed operations. It filed for bankruptcy the second time in 1992 due to the changing retail landscape and in February 2002 the company ceased operation.", "output": "McCrory's, a chain of retail stores, filed for bankruptcy in 1992 and closed many of their stores in 1997 [1]. The chain ceased its remaining operations in 2002 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of retail stores, filed for bankruptcy in 1992 and closed many of their stores in 1997.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The chain ceased its remaining operations in 2002.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 307, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of variety stores, filed for bankruptcy in 1992 and by 1997, 300 of its 460 stores were closed.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The company also converted some stores to their Dollar Zone format of dollar store, but these closed in early 2002.[Continue to Use Evidence]In December 2001, McCrory Stores announced the remaining McCrory's, TG&Y, G.C. Murphy and J.J. Newberry stores it was operating would begin liquidating and in February 2002 the company ceased operation.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of variety stores, filed for bankruptcy in 1992 and by 1997, 300 of its 460 stores were closed.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The company also converted some stores to their Dollar Zone format of dollar store, but these closed in early 2002.[Continue to Use Evidence]In December 2001, McCrory Stores announced the remaining McCrory's, TG&Y, G.C. Murphy and J.J. Newberry stores it was operating would begin liquidating and in February 2002 the company ceased operation.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9590839404601974, "ctx": {"title": "McCrory Stores", "text": "were operated by the McCrory company. However, as the decade turned, its fortunes decreased, and by 1992 it filed for bankruptcy. Several rounds of store closures followed, with one of the biggest coming in 1997 when McCrory's shuttered 300 of its last 460 stores. The company also converted some stores to their Dollar Zone format of dollar store, but these closed in early 2002. In December 2001, McCrory Stores announced the remaining McCrory's, TG&Y, G. C. Murphy and J.J. Newberry stores it was operating would begin liquidating and in February 2002 the company ceased operation. McCrory Stores McCrory Stores or"}, "final_score": 1.9590839404601974, "relevance_score": 0.998909809890934, "ground_score": 0.9601741305692633, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14939673823000896, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0010901689694573866, "[Relevant]": 0.9988904402761993}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9317343082373091, "[Partially supported]": 0.05134719036004783, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.014037537352621901}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.325727315659317e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00018152214854299885, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010588093168889348, "[Utility:4]": 0.0015258149302451434, "[Utility:5]": 0.9313091615240293}], "n_tokens": 141}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of retail stores, filed for bankruptcy in 1992 and closed many of their stores in 1997.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The chain ceased its remaining operations in 2002.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of retail stores, filed for bankruptcy in 1992 and closed many of their stores in 1997.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The chain ceased its remaining operations in 2002.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9668172561042323, "ctx": {"title": "G. C. Murphy", "text": "sold the division to the McCrory's chain led by Meshulam Riklis. McCrory's rebranded some and closed others. The McCrory's chain filed for bankruptcy in 1992, closed many of their stores (including former G.C. Murphy's outlets) in 1997, and ceased its remaining operations in 2002. Although the stores ceased business operations some time ago, the chain's foundation, the G.C. Murphy Foundation, is still in existence. Retirees and former employees of the stores have a volunteer organization that allows them to keep in touch. In 2005, the \"Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\" reported that the Murphy Foundation had commissioned a writer to compile the G.C."}, "final_score": 1.9668172561042323, "relevance_score": 0.9980882304705039, "ground_score": 0.9687290256337284, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.27416185462134635, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0019116655390073471, "[Relevant]": 0.9980339395733615}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9428842185506794, "[Partially supported]": 0.047311628663323654, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.007544498441527283}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001288941379065775, "[Utility:2]": 0.00042844519458311894, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018644421071433996, "[Utility:4]": 0.0028712609832175773, "[Utility:5]": 0.8951030776566063}], "n_tokens": 56}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of discount stores, went out of business in 1987.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of discount stores, went out of business in 1987.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8068115235552396, "ctx": {"title": "McCrory Stores", "text": "his last name so he would not have to pay for extra letters on his store signs. McCrory died in 1923 having built McCrory Stores Corporation into a success. However, in 1933 the company declared bankruptcy and was dissolved, but it was eventually re-established as McCrory Stores and resumed operations. One of the early investors was Sebastian Spering Kresge, who later founded the S.S. Kresge chain, which became Kmart. In 1899, Kresge traded his interest in the McCrory's Memphis, Tennessee store for McCrory's interest in the Detroit, Michigan, store, giving him control there. In 1987, the Kmart Corporation sold its"}, "final_score": 1.8068115235552396, "relevance_score": 0.9923043535328052, "ground_score": 0.8145071700224343, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4551086355904975, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.007695197163206293, "[Relevant]": 0.9922464186593996}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7518875515290936, "[Partially supported]": 0.11804009674088602, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.12565302891080124}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004306026899361478, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003514476727893179, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003487126853013777, "[Utility:4]": 0.003321442919696685, "[Utility:5]": 0.6404159416377729}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of variety stores, went out of business in 1989.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]McCrory's, a chain of variety stores, went out of business in 1989.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.347540779834073, "ctx": {"title": "McCrory Stores", "text": "stores were beyond the typical 10-15K Sq. Ft. footprint that the company operated in and the merchandise mix was very different. The TG&Y stores were not profitable and drained McCrory of valuable assets. Many of the TG&Y stores were converted to the Bargain Time banner that McCrory operated and were closed as the 1980's came to an end. In 1987, McCrory Stores purchased the 76 remaining Kresge and Jupiter stores from the K Mart Corporation which had long given up on the variety stores division, reuniting the companies. All stores were converted to the McCrory banner. In 1989, 1,300 stores"}, "final_score": 1.347540779834073, "relevance_score": 0.9768465879803441, "ground_score": 0.370694191853729, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.418377986490803, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0231515638157514, "[Relevant]": 0.9767686119275534}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.291297383604442, "[Partially supported]": 0.15470687263140429, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.548483459033264}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003077526858530528, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003446664940490587, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003380000405912131, "[Utility:4]": 0.006922811592408012, "[Utility:5]": 0.8191438383303509}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The McCrory's chain of 300 stores, mostly Newberry's, closed in 1997.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The McCrory's chain of 300 stores, mostly Newberry's, closed in 1997.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9405020669821953, "ctx": {"title": "J.J. Newberry", "text": "opened stores under the Newberry banner especially in the Northeast and California where the name had a strong presence. The company thrived throughout the 1980s but fell on hard times in the early 1990s. The demise of the company became evident following a Chapter 11 Bankruptcy filing in 1992. 300 McCrory stores, mostly Newberry's, closed in 1997, but some lingered on, with at least one Newberry's (in Portland, Oregon) closing as late as 2001. Early J.J. Newberry stores featured a recognizable logo composed of gold or white sans serif letters on a red background that usually occupied the entire width"}, "final_score": 1.9405020669821953, "relevance_score": 0.9924227602623785, "ground_score": 0.9480793067198168, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.33457893087256707, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.007576483044375183, "[Relevant]": 0.9923236529850362}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9148790127049243, "[Partially supported]": 0.06423458484363821, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.019744091269875787}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004575803642213492, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007398306279035314, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004833011840566942, "[Utility:4]": 0.007074370751151352, "[Utility:5]": 0.5798261867426068}], "n_tokens": 32}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. While Queenstown is referred to during the series, Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop. The scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake.", "question": "Where was season 1 of  'top of the lake' filmed almost entirely?", "short_answers": ["Glenorchy", "Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand", "Queenstown"], "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}, {"context": "Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. While Queenstown is referred to during the series, Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop. The scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake.", "question": "Where were the scenes in the women's commune in season 1 of 'top of the lake' filmed?", "short_answers": ["Moke Lake", "Closeburn, Queenstown, South Island"], "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}, {"context": "In early 2013, co-creator Jane Campion said that \"Top of the Lake\" comes to a distinct ending, and there would be no additional series. Despite this, it was announced in October 2014 that the series had been renewed for a second season. \"China Girl\" began shooting on location in Sydney, Australia in December 2015.", "question": "Where was season 2 of 'top of the lake' filmed?", "short_answers": ["Sydney, Australia"], "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}, {"context": "Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. While Queenstown is referred to during the series, Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop. The scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake.", "question": "Where was the original 'top of the lake' filmed except for the women's commune?", "short_answers": ["Queenstown and Glenorchy", "Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago"], "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}, {"context": "Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. While Queenstown is referred to during the series, Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop. The scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake.", "question": "Where was the original 'top of the lake' filmed for the women's commune?", "short_answers": ["Moke Lake"], "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}, {"context": "In early 2013, co-creator Jane Campion said that \"Top of the Lake\" comes to a distinct ending, and there would be no additional series. Despite this, it was announced in October 2014 that the series had been renewed for a second season. \"China Girl\" began shooting on location in Sydney, Australia in December 2015.", "question": "Where was 'top of the lake: China Girl' filmed?", "short_answers": ["Sydney", "Sydney, Australia"], "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Top of the Lake", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top%20of%20the%20Lake"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series created and written by Jane Campion and Gerard Lee, and directed by Campion and Garth Davis.", "wikipage": null}, {"content": "Moke Lake is a small lake near the suburb of Closeburn in Queenstown, in the South Island of New Zealand.", "wikipage": "Moke Lake"}], "long_answer": "The television drama, Top of the Lake, was filmed in multiple locations. The first season was filmed entirely in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. The only exception are the scenes at the women's commune, which were shot at Moke Lake, a small lake near the suburb of Closeburn in Queenstown. The second season of the show, China Girl, is set and filmed in Sydney, Australia. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series created and written by Jane Campion and Gerard Lee, and directed by Campion and Garth Davis.", "wikipage": "Top of the Lake"}, {"content": "Moke Lake is a small lake near the suburb of Closeburn in Queenstown, in the South Island of New Zealand.", "wikipage": "Moke Lake"}], "long_answer": "Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series. Except for the women's commune scenes, which were filmed at Moke Lake, a small lake near the suburb of Closeburn in Queenstown, in the South Island of New Zealand, season 1 was filmed in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. Season 2, Top of the Lake: China Girl, was filmed in Sydney, Australia."}], "sample_id": "3015237958274673918", "question": "Where was 'top of the lake' filmed?", "docs": [{"id": "17171842", "title": "Top of the Lake", "text": "filled the funding gap left by the ABC. Philippa Campbell was the New Zealand-based producer. Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. While Queenstown is referred to during the series, Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop. The scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake. In early 2013, co-creator Jane Campion said that \"Top of the Lake\" comes to a distinct ending, and there would be no additional series. Despite this, it was announced in October 2014 that the series", "score": 0.84228515625, "summary": "\"Top of the Lake\" was filmed entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop, and the scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake.", "extraction": "\"Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop.\""}], "answer": "Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series. Except for the women's commune scenes, which were filmed at Moke Lake, a small lake near the suburb of Closeburn in Queenstown, in the South Island of New Zealand, season 1 was filmed in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. Season 2, Top of the Lake: China Girl, was filmed in Sydney, Australia.", "output": "The first season of the mystery drama television series Top of the Lake was filmed entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand [1]. The second season was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The first season of the mystery drama television series Top of the Lake was filmed entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The second season was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 309, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The first season of the mystery drama television series Top of the Lake was filmed entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The second season was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first season of the mystery drama television series Top of the Lake was filmed entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The second season was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.991195029653513, "ctx": {"title": "Top of the Lake", "text": "filled the funding gap left by the ABC. Philippa Campbell was the New Zealand-based producer. Filming took 18 weeks and was shot entirely on location in Queenstown and Glenorchy, in Otago, on the South Island of New Zealand. While Queenstown is referred to during the series, Glenorchy doubles as the fictitious town of Laketop. The scenes in the women's commune were filmed at Moke Lake. In early 2013, co-creator Jane Campion said that \"Top of the Lake\" comes to a distinct ending, and there would be no additional series. Despite this, it was announced in October 2014 that the series"}, "final_score": 1.991195029653513, "relevance_score": 0.9971104188138458, "ground_score": 0.9940846108396673, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2454960716993369, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0028895392176539886, "[Relevant]": 0.9970959367051476}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9889611734482571, "[Partially supported]": 0.01008165551519574, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0008740698267391547}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004604155620081843, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004901098145572643, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023469841565826953, "[Utility:4]": 0.0038101000489268854, "[Utility:5]": 0.8163499531386187}], "n_tokens": 61}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series created and written by Jane Campion and Gerard Lee, and directed by Campion and Garth Davis.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It aired in 2013, and the sequel, entitled \"Top of the Lake: China Girl\", in 2017.[Continue to Use Evidence]It marks Campion's first work for television since \"An Angel at My Table\" in 1990.[Continue to Use Evidence]Season 1 follows Detective Robin Griffin and deals with her investigation of the disappearance of a pregnant 12-year-old girl in New Zealand.[Continue to Use Evidence]Season 2, \"China Girl\", is set in Sydney four years later, as Detective Griffin investigates the murder of a young Chinese girl whose body is found in the same lake where the first season's events took place.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series created and written by Jane Campion and Gerard Lee, and directed by Campion and Garth Davis.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It aired in 2013, and the sequel, entitled \"Top of the Lake: China Girl\", in 2017.[Continue to Use Evidence]It marks Campion's first work for television since \"An Angel at My Table\" in 1990.[Continue to Use Evidence]Season 1 follows Detective Robin Griffin and deals with her investigation of the disappearance of a pregnant 12-year-old girl in New Zealand.[Continue to Use Evidence]Season 2, \"China Girl\", is set in Sydney four years later, as Detective Griffin investigates the murder of a young Chinese girl whose body is found in the same lake where the first season's events took place.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5353743901287755, "ctx": {"title": "Top of the Lake", "text": "Top of the Lake Top of the Lake is a mystery drama television series created and written by Jane Campion and Gerard Lee, and directed by Campion and Garth Davis. It aired in 2013, and the sequel, entitled \"Top of the Lake: China Girl\", in 2017. It marks Campion's first work for television since \"An Angel at My Table\" in 1990. Season 1 follows Detective Robin Griffin (Elisabeth Moss) and deals with her investigation of the disappearance of a pregnant 12-year-old girl in New Zealand. Season 2, \"China Girl\", is set in Sydney four years later, as Detective Griffin investigates"}, "final_score": 1.5353743901287755, "relevance_score": 0.9746286886637742, "ground_score": 0.5607457014650015, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1345171505292741, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.025370018316913705, "[Relevant]": 0.9745790178485796}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5177751771622552, "[Partially supported]": 0.0794033998596559, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3969919991155817}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008350217799895384, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006266250988481073, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002497415438084158, "[Utility:4]": 0.0015298524293514816, "[Utility:5]": 0.8435940400307999}], "n_tokens": 183}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Top of the Lake is an Australian mystery drama television series that aired on the Sundance Channel.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The series was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Top of the Lake is an Australian mystery drama television series that aired on the Sundance Channel.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The series was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9728271664714763, "ctx": {"title": "Top of the Lake", "text": "letter through a mutual friend. \"Top of the Lake\" screened in its entirety at the January 2013 Sundance Film Festival, in a single seven-hour session with one intermission and a break for lunch. This was the first such screening in the history of the festival. \"Top of the Lake\" was additionally shown at the 63rd Berlin International Film Festival. The US premiere was on the Sundance Channel on 18 March 2013, in Australia on BBC UKTV on 24 March 2013, and in New Zealand on 25 March, also on BBC UKTV. \"China Girl\" was screened in its entirety at the"}, "final_score": 0.9728271664714763, "relevance_score": 0.8910549535215687, "ground_score": 0.08177221294990751, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.42694189972471225, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.10892446482107687, "[Relevant]": 0.8908866173894531}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.04401328160705781, "[Partially supported]": 0.0748691140559956, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8771508006618326}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0006553554773439757, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006735226003932591, "[Utility:3]": 0.00027106680459510206, "[Utility:4]": 0.004426365298655676, "[Utility:5]": 0.9595695651928503}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Top of the Lake is an Australian mystery drama television series.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The series was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Top of the Lake is an Australian mystery drama television series.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The series was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1293972486926829, "ctx": {"title": "Top of the Lake", "text": "the death of an unidentified Asian girl found at Bondi Beach. \"Top of the Lake\" was co-produced for BBC Two in the UK, BBC UKTV in Australia and New Zealand, and Sundance Channel in the United States. It has been generally very well received. Elisabeth Moss plays the central role of Robin Griffin, a Sydney detective specialising in sexual assault, in both series. Additional cast members are as follows: David Wenham returns as Al in one episode. Kip Chapman, Jacqueline Joe, Byron Coll and Cohen Holloway also reprise their \"Top of the Lake\" roles in a flashback sequence, with Mark"}, "final_score": 1.1293972486926829, "relevance_score": 0.9661559621014587, "ground_score": 0.1632412865912241, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4439285339119909, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.03384142028162408, "[Relevant]": 0.9660812362014747}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.045903760871914334, "[Partially supported]": 0.23130439027290328, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7124676801473088}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007902227982840644, "[Utility:2]": 0.0010468751191668032, "[Utility:3]": 0.00026781145371622455, "[Utility:4]": 0.004356159845444721, "[Utility:5]": 0.8699761094337882}], "n_tokens": 31}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The British television drama series, The Lakes, was filmed in and around Patterdale and The Ullswater Hotel, Glenridding.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Australian drama series, Top of the Lake, was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The British television drama series, The Lakes, was filmed in and around Patterdale and The Ullswater Hotel, Glenridding.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Australian drama series, Top of the Lake, was filmed in Sydney, New South Wales, Australia.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9611252386921096, "ctx": {"title": "The Lakes (TV series)", "text": "The Lakes (TV series) The Lakes is a British television drama series, created and principally written by Jimmy McGovern, first broadcast on BBC1 on 14 September 1997. The series, which was principally filmed in and around Patterdale and The Ullswater Hotel, Glenridding, stars John Simm as Danny Kavanagh, a hotel porter, compulsive gambler, and philanderer who escapes from the dole queues in Liverpool to live in the Lake District. After he meets and marries local girl Emma Quinlan (Emma Cunniffe), they move back to Liverpool. However, the consequences of Danny's gambling habit results in Emma moving back to the Lakes."}, "final_score": 1.9611252386921096, "relevance_score": 0.9956005871880788, "ground_score": 0.9655246515040308, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2591510844170952, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0043991752632354785, "[Relevant]": 0.9955468291932862}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9460078221931826, "[Partially supported]": 0.037845078951148324, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.01553158910731811}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003150538350537789, "[Utility:2]": 0.00013871632620529318, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012556476159944737, "[Utility:4]": 0.0021783811303368174, "[Utility:5]": 0.9465282995742741}], "n_tokens": 59}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "In March 2011, \"Baby\", which then had 1.17 million dislikes, was surpassed by the video for Rebecca Black's \"Friday\", yielding more than 1.2 million dislikes. \"Friday\" amassed more than three million dislikes before the video was taken down in June 2011. The video was reinstated three months later and has not been taken down since.", "question": "What was the video with the most dislikes on YouTube on June 16, 2011?", "short_answers": ["\"Baby\""], "wikipage": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos"}, {"context": "In March 2011, \"Baby\", which then had 1.17 million dislikes, was surpassed by the video for Rebecca Black's \"Friday\", yielding more than 1.2 million dislikes. \"Friday\" amassed more than three million dislikes before the video was taken down in June 2011. The video was reinstated three months later and has not been taken down since.", "question": "What was the video with the most dislikes on YouTube on March 29, 2011?", "short_answers": ["\"Friday\""], "wikipage": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What was the video with the most dislikes on YouTube on approximately August 21, 2010?", "short_answers": ["\"Baby\""], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20most-disliked%20YouTube%20videos"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "On December 13, 2018, YouTube Rewind 2018: Everyone Controls Rewind became the most disliked video on the video sharing platform, with 15 million dislikes rapidly surpassed the music video for Justin Bieber's song Baby,[1] which entered the Guinness World Records book as the most disliked video on YouTube and on the Internet.[5] As of July 9, 2021, YouTube Rewind 2018 has over 7.1 million more dislikes than Justin Bieber's Baby.", "wikipage": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos"}], "long_answer": "In March 2011, the video for Justin Bieber's music video, \"Baby\" had the most dislikes until it was surpassed by the music video for Rebecca Black's song \"Friday.\" Her song received more than 3 million dislikes and was removed from the YouTube platform in June 2011. Three months later, the video was re-uploaded and as not been removed since. As of December 13, 2018, the YouTube Rewind 2018: Everyone Controls Rewind video became the most disliked video on the platform, with 15 million dislikes rapidly surpassed the music video for Justin Bieber's song Baby."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "\"Baby\"*[22]\tJustin Bieber\t~200,000\tFebruary 19, 2010\t~August 21, 2010\t~220\t[78][79][80][81]", "wikipage": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos"}, {"content": "4\t\"Baby\"[22]\tJustin Bieber featuring Ludacris\t[4]\t12.29\t39.88%\tFeb 19, 2010\tMusic video", "wikipage": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos"}, {"content": "\"Friday\"\u2021[7]\ttrizzy66\t1,200,000\tFebruary 10, 2011\tMarch 29, 2011\t79", "wikipage": "List of most-disliked YouTube videos"}, {"content": "Friday\" is a song performed by American singer Rebecca Black, written and produced by Los Angeles record producers Clarence Jey and Patrice Wilson. It is Black's debut single. ", "wikipage": "Friday (Rebecca Black song) Music video"}], "long_answer": "On August 21, 2010 the video of Justin Bieber's song \"Baby\" had the most dislikes on YouTube. On March 29, 2011 the video of Rebecca Black's song \"Friday\" had the most dislikes on YouTube. On June 16, 2011 Justin Bieber's song \"Baby\" featuring Ludacris had the most dislikes on YouTube."}], "sample_id": "5071624972590720592", "question": "The video with the most dislikes on youtube?", "docs": [{"id": "18288066", "title": "YouTube Spotlight", "text": "video of all time with over 3.40 million likes. On December 14, 2016, shortly after the 2016 \"Rewind\" video was released, the \"Spotlight\" channel surpassed 1 billion total video views. On December 12, 2018, approximately 6 days and 10 hours after upload, YouTube Rewind 2018 became YouTube's most disliked video of all time, surpassing Justin Bieber's Baby. Shortly after, it also became the first YouTube video to reach 10 million dislikes, doing so in 6 days and 12 hours. The 2018 \"Rewind\" currently sits at over 13.3 million dislikes. In January 2014, \"YouTube Nation\" was launched on its own channel,", "score": 0.765625, "summary": "The most disliked video on YouTube is the 2018 \"Rewind\" video, with over 13.3 million dislikes, surpassing Justin Bieber's \"Baby\" in December 2018.", "extraction": "YouTube Rewind 2018 became YouTube's most disliked video of all time, surpassing Justin Bieber's Baby."}], "answer": "On August 21, 2010 the video of Justin Bieber's song \"Baby\" had the most dislikes on YouTube. On March 29, 2011 the video of Rebecca Black's song \"Friday\" had the most dislikes on YouTube. On June 16, 2011 Justin Bieber's song \"Baby\" featuring Ludacris had the most dislikes on YouTube.", "output": "The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is YouTube Rewind 2018, with 13. 3 million dislikes [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is YouTube Rewind 2018, with 13.3 million dislikes.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 310, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is YouTube Rewind 2018, with 13.3 million dislikes.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is YouTube Rewind 2018, with 13.3 million dislikes.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9337291589703938, "ctx": {"title": "YouTube Spotlight", "text": "video of all time with over 3.40 million likes. On December 14, 2016, shortly after the 2016 \"Rewind\" video was released, the \"Spotlight\" channel surpassed 1 billion total video views. On December 12, 2018, approximately 6 days and 10 hours after upload, YouTube Rewind 2018 became YouTube's most disliked video of all time, surpassing Justin Bieber's Baby. Shortly after, it also became the first YouTube video to reach 10 million dislikes, doing so in 6 days and 12 hours. The 2018 \"Rewind\" currently sits at over 13.3 million dislikes. In January 2014, \"YouTube Nation\" was launched on its own channel,"}, "final_score": 1.9337291589703938, "relevance_score": 0.9900635822010977, "ground_score": 0.9436655767692962, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.23589655800520207, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00993523141949031, "[Relevant]": 0.989945371486296}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9299591300368044, "[Partially supported]": 0.02556925387519815, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04349476600722133}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001968043638061392, "[Utility:2]": 7.440728905309973e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004928372058158264, "[Utility:4]": 0.001031181135976209, "[Utility:5]": 0.8018766473937355}], "n_tokens": 36}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The most disliked video on YouTube is \"How It Is (Wap Bap Re-Edited)\" by German DJ and record producer DJ BoBo.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The most disliked video on YouTube is \"How It Is (Wap Bap Re-Edited)\" by German DJ and record producer DJ BoBo.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1463568029241262, "ctx": {"title": "How It Is (Wap Bap \u2026)", "text": "video on any German YouTube channel, more than twice as much as the former record holder. Worldwide, it is the eighth most disliked YouTube video, with only about 44.5 million views. With over 1.7 million dislikes, the music video became one of the most disliked videos on YouTube only a few days after uploading. Later, the video reached more than 2 million dislikes. Currently it is one of the top ten most disliked videos on YouTube with over 2.8 million dislikes. The song and the video also attracted mockery by other content creators who satirized it. A few days after"}, "final_score": 1.1463568029241262, "relevance_score": 0.9136765228260896, "ground_score": 0.23268028009803665, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5332055788973309, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.08629586612804668, "[Relevant]": 0.9133842782918986}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.1593011432800134, "[Partially supported]": 0.14504534153613757, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.6919726443786669}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000746911049467547, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002131594181663251, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017568295048966322, "[Utility:4]": 0.0057838284328282814, "[Utility:5]": 0.8583964412898847}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is \"Friday\" by Rebecca Black.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is \"Friday\" by Rebecca Black.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8437054561950545, "ctx": {"title": "Friday (Rebecca Black song)", "text": "In\" reviewer concluded. The song has received almost universally negative reviews from music critics, for its songwriting, instrumentation, Black's singing voice, and the video choreography. Lyndsey Parker of \"Yahoo! Music\" asked if it could be \"the worst song ever.\" On March 29, 2011, it surpassed Justin Bieber's \"Baby\" as the most disliked YouTube video, with 1.17 million dislikes, and once had over 3 million \"dislikes\", accounting for 88% of the total ratings of the video. The video was later removed, although it has since been officially re-uploaded. The co-writer and producer of \"Friday\", Clarence Jey, said about the song that"}, "final_score": 1.8437054561950545, "relevance_score": 0.9899864284258814, "ground_score": 0.8537190277691733, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.23110469220647736, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.010012872705243151, "[Relevant]": 0.9899173351261774}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8114642231617664, "[Partially supported]": 0.07666653353768928, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.10727576733127905}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00032255003670524334, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011038703074157007, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002078476068448117, "[Utility:4]": 0.001390190096401902, "[Utility:5]": 0.7229547462616718}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is \"Everyone Controls Rewind\" by YouTube Rewind, with 13.3 million dislikes.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is \"Everyone Controls Rewind\" by YouTube Rewind, with 13.3 million dislikes.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8753768996510236, "ctx": {"title": "YouTube Rewind", "text": "on its side following a rather rocky 2018.\" \"Everyone Controls Rewind\" is currently the most disliked YouTube video of all time, with 13.3 million dislikes in comparison to its 2.2 million likes. It is also the first video to reach 10 million dislikes. \"Everyone Controls Rewind\" amassed 29.4 million views within 24 hours of release, making it the 13th most-viewed YouTube video in the first 24 hours. It currently has 139.2 million views. <onlyinclude></onlyinclude> <onlyinclude></onlyinclude> <onlyinclude></onlyinclude> <onlyinclude></onlyinclude> <onlyinclude></onlyinclude> Youtube Rewind 2017 had animation sequences, but they were mostly located at the end of the video. This included James Rallison (TheOdd1sOut),"}, "final_score": 1.8753768996510236, "relevance_score": 0.989181550111295, "ground_score": 0.8861953495397284, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2231937038039513, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.01081428410030179, "[Relevant]": 0.98880065256383}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8631156482975721, "[Partially supported]": 0.04230578060762139, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0924043203053543}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003753937453614277, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011697495332665099, "[Utility:3]": 0.000474542158682648, "[Utility:4]": 0.002657142862732027, "[Utility:5]": 0.8028667579496014}], "n_tokens": 41}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is YouTube Rewind 2018:[No support / Contradictory]Everyone Controls Rewind.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The video with the most dislikes on YouTube is YouTube Rewind 2018:[No support / Contradictory]Everyone Controls Rewind.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9954780076732967, "ctx": {"title": "YouTube Rewind 2018: Everyone Controls Rewind", "text": "particular year. [...] It became an honor to be included in Rewind. But now YouTube saw Rewind as a way to showcase all the best stuff that happens on YouTube for advertisers.\" He concluded that \"Instead of honoring creators, it is now a list of advertiser-friendly content. Rewind has turned into a giant ad for YouTube.\" On December 13, 2018, it became the most-disliked video in the history of the website, beating the previous record-holder: the music video for Justin Bieber\u2019s \"Baby\". \"Everyone Controls Rewind\" currently has 14.0 million dislikes in comparison to its 2.3 million likes. It is also"}, "final_score": 0.9954780076732967, "relevance_score": 0.9953544429282959, "ground_score": 0.00012356474500080407, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24666955149255942, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.004645112631456752, "[Relevant]": 0.9952592174110875}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 3.30501051765178e-05, "[Partially supported]": 5.470368084363526e-05, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.4887405572175948}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0019372225339832029, "[Utility:2]": 0.00023731803649720864, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005400527352743099, "[Utility:4]": 0.0014062727487425615, "[Utility:5]": 0.5520282663610827}], "n_tokens": 33}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n (born June 26, 1944) is President Emeritus of Miami Dade College (MDC). An economist by training, Padr\u00f3n earned his Ph.D. from the University of Florida. After serving as a faculty member at MDC, he became the school's president in 1995. \"Time\" named him one of the ten best college presidents in 2009, and he was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2016.", "question": "Who is the president of miami dade college from 1995 to 2019?", "short_answers": ["Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n", "Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n"], "wikipage": "Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the president of miami dade college until 1995?", "short_answers": ["Robert McCabe", "Robert H. McCabe"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the president of miami dade college starting in 1963?", "short_answers": ["Peter Masiko"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the interim president of miami dade college from after Padr\u00f3n retired?", "short_answers": ["Rolando Montoya", "Montoya"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Miami Dade College", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami%20Dade%20College"}, {"title": "Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eduardo%20J.%20Padr%C3%B3n"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "In 1963, the first new building was constructed, and Peter Masiko would become president for the next 18 years", "wikipage": "Miami Dade College"}, {"content": "Robert H. McCabe (December 23, 1928 \u2013 December 23, 2014) was an American educator and the President Emeritus of Miami-Dade Community College.[1] He won a 1992 MacArthur Fellowship.[2] He died on December 23, 2014 of cancer.[3]", "wikipage": "Robert McCabe"}, {"content": "On November 17, 2020, Pumariega was appointed the fifth president of Miami Dade College.", "wikipage": "Madeline Pumariega"}, {"content": "In office: 1995\u20132019", "wikipage": "Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n"}], "long_answer": "Miami Dade College has had a number of Presidents over the years. The first person to hold this role was Peter Masiko in 1963. He served as the school's president for 18 years. Following Masiko, was Robert H. McCabe. He took over as President until 1995. Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n the held the position from 1995 until her retired in 2019. Rolando Montoya stepped in as interim President until Madeline Pumariega become president on November 17, 2020. She is the school's current president."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Miami Dade College (Miami Dade, MDC or Dade)[6] is a public college in Miami, Florida.", "wikipage": "Miami Dade College"}, {"content": "In 1963, the first new building was constructed, and Peter Masiko would become president for the next 18 years.[11]", "wikipage": "Miami Dade College History"}, {"content": "Robert H. McCabe (December 23, 1928 \u2013 December 23, 2014) was an American educator and the President Emeritus of Miami-Dade Community College.[1]", "wikipage": "Robert McCabe"}], "long_answer": "Miami Dade College is a public college in Miami, Florida. In 1963, the first new building was constructed, and Peter Masiko became president for the next 18 years. Robert McCabe, an American educator, was president of Miami Dade College until 1995. Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n was president of the college from 1995 to 2019, when Ronaldo Montoya became the interim president."}], "sample_id": "2530647767888382788", "question": "Who is the president of miami dade college?", "docs": [{"id": "10969824", "title": "Eduardo J. Padro\u0301n", "text": "has a son and two grandchildren. Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n (born June 26, 1944) is the president of Miami Dade College (MDC). An economist by training, Padr\u00f3n earned his Ph.D. from the University of Florida. After serving as a faculty member at MDC, he became the school's president in 1995. \"Time\" named him one of the ten best college presidents in 2009, and he was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2016. Born in Cuba, Padr\u00f3n was a teenager when he came to the United States as part of Operation Pedro Pan. The initiative allowed Cuban parents", "score": 0.7548828125, "summary": "Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n is the president of Miami Dade College.", "extraction": "Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n is the president of Miami Dade College (MDC)."}], "answer": "Miami Dade College is a public college in Miami, Florida. In 1963, the first new building was constructed, and Peter Masiko became president for the next 18 years. Robert McCabe, an American educator, was president of Miami Dade College until 1995. Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n was president of the college from 1995 to 2019, when Ronaldo Montoya became the interim president.", "output": "Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n is the president of Miami Dade College [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n is the president of Miami Dade College.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 311, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n is the president of Miami Dade College.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n is the president of Miami Dade College.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.993825569929053, "ctx": {"title": "Eduardo J. Padro\u0301n", "text": "Eduardo J. Padr\u00f3n Eduardo Jos\u00e9 Padr\u00f3n (born June 26, 1944) is the president of Miami Dade College (MDC). An economist by training, Padr\u00f3n earned his Ph.D. from the University of Florida. After serving as a faculty member at MDC, he became the school's president in 1995. \"Time\" named him one of the ten best college presidents in 2009, and he was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2016. Born in Cuba, Padr\u00f3n was a teenager when he came to the United States as part of Operation Pedro Pan. The initiative allowed Cuban parents to send their children to the"}, "final_score": 1.993825569929053, "relevance_score": 0.9995826843341875, "ground_score": 0.9942428855948655, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1608848200205557, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0004172957340892452, "[Relevant]": 0.9995349425236921}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9928081069248017, "[Partially supported]": 0.001564259258857691, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.004971205643616517}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00021698480054959196, "[Utility:2]": 0.00039754019860092617, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004281676427401471, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009135401325068146, "[Utility:5]": 0.5057174728738653}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]David C. Hodge was the 21st president of Miami University in Oxford, Ohio.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]David C. Hodge was the 21st president of Miami University in Oxford, Ohio.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3630379274865592, "ctx": {"title": "David C. Hodge", "text": "from the Miami presidency on June 30, 2016. David C. Hodge David Charles Hodge (born September 27, 1948 in Stewartville, Minnesota) was the 21st president of Miami University in Oxford, Ohio. He began his tenure on July 1, 2006. Previously he was the Dean of the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of Washington, where he was also chair of the Department of Geography. Dr. Hodge was born and raised in Minnesota and earned a bachelor's degree in 1970 from Macalester College and earned a doctorate in 1975 from Pennsylvania State University; both were in geography. On May"}, "final_score": 1.3630379274865592, "relevance_score": 0.9190632885537288, "ground_score": 0.4439746389328304, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.28837680311941466, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.08087171480675798, "[Relevant]": 0.9183252300857154}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.42213470449009954, "[Partially supported]": 0.03926464391624099, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5336282673828929}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.002093414814213265, "[Utility:2]": 0.0013675425432143912, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005070365765186663, "[Utility:4]": 0.010548504697490357, "[Utility:5]": 0.5043002343850592}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Eduardo J. 