314362256, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.106797888686362}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00045340675021685804, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008671631406832805, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004437694855647709, "[Utility:4]": 0.006432595406270921, "[Utility:5]": 0.559040358465326}], "n_tokens": 82}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Amsterdam is a town in Montgomery County, New York, United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The population was 5,566 at the 2010 census.[Continue to Use Evidence]The town is named after Amsterdam, the capital of the Netherlands.[Continue to Use Evidence]The town is adjacent to and borders the city of Amsterdam on three sides.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Amsterdam is a town in Montgomery County, New York, United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The population was 5,566 at the 2010 census.[Continue to Use Evidence]The town is named after Amsterdam, the capital of the Netherlands.[Continue to Use Evidence]The town is adjacent to and borders the city of Amsterdam on three sides.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7491666128387113, "ctx": {"title": "Amsterdam (town), New York", "text": "Amsterdam (town), New York Amsterdam is a town in Montgomery County, New York, United States. The population was 5,566 at the 2010 census. The town is named after Amsterdam, the capital of the Netherlands. The town is adjacent to and borders the city of Amsterdam on three sides. The town is on the county's northeastern border. The Erie Canal passes along the southern border of the town. The region was controlled by the powerful Iroquois Confederation's member tribe, the Mohawk, prior to the entry of Europeans into the area. The town was first settled around 1715, but most settlement did"}, "final_score": 1.7491666128387113, "relevance_score": 0.9964619144206521, "ground_score": 0.7527046984180592, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.09734204546521628, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003536480414622629, "[Relevant]": 0.9960098378726925}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6774290461298459, "[Partially supported]": 0.13655703253235454, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.17671793044423703}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018700496671862955, "[Utility:2]": 0.00029766804614237617, "[Utility:3]": 0.00013869912954563216, "[Utility:4]": 0.003077635552313154, "[Utility:5]": 0.7413971712836466}], "n_tokens": 70}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "When does the episode air where dr owen comes to grey's anatomy?", "short_answers": ["September 25, 2008"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What episode does dr owen come to grey's anatomy?", "short_answers": ["79", "Dream a Little Dream of Me"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which episode does dr owen come to grey's anatomy?", "short_answers": ["Dream a Little Dream of Me, Part 1", "79"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When is the episode where dr owen comes to grey's anatomy?", "short_answers": ["September 25, 2008"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Owen Hunt", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owen%20Hunt"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Grey's Anatomy is an American medical drama television series that premiered on March 27, 2005, on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) as a mid-season replacement.", "wikipage": "Grey's Anatomy"}], "long_answer": "The character, Dr. Owen, was introduced in episode 79 of the American medical drama Grey's Anatomy. The episode (Dream a Little Dream of Me) aired on September 25, 2008."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Owen Hunt, M.D., F.A.C.S. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States.", "wikipage": "Owen Hunt"}], "long_answer": "Owen Hunt, M.D., F.A.C.S. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States. He comes to Grey's Anatomy on Episode 79, \"Dream a Little Dream of Me\" which aired on September 25, 2008."}], "sample_id": "-2038850573562257182", "question": "When does dr owen come to grey's anatomy?", "docs": [{"id": "4811496", "title": "Grey's Anatomy", "text": "from \"Grey's Anatomy\" on November 6, 2008. \"E! Online\" Kristin Dos Santos asserted that Smith's dismissal from the show had been forced by the ABC network, as part of an attempt to \"de-gay\" \"Grey's Anatomy\". Rhimes countered these claims, saying that \"we did not find that the magic and chemistry with Brooke's character would sustain in the long run\". Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd (Dr. Owen Hunt), who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc. In addition, Jessica Capshaw (Dr. Arizona Robbins) was originally introduced for a three-episode arc, but received", "score": 0.740234375, "summary": "Dr. Owen Hunt (played by Kevin McKidd) comes to Grey's Anatomy in season five as a series regular.", "extraction": "Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd (Dr. Owen Hunt), who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc. Therefore, Dr. Owen comes to Grey's Anatomy in season five."}], "answer": "Owen Hunt, M.D., F.A.C.S. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States. He comes to Grey's Anatomy on Episode 79, \"Dream a Little Dream of Me\" which aired on September 25, 2008.", "output": "Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd as Dr. Owen Hunt, who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd as Dr. Owen Hunt, who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 424, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Dr. Owen Hunt, M.D., F.A.C.S., is a fictional character on the ABC medical drama Grey's Anatomy.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Dr. Owen Hunt, M.D., F.A.C.S., is a fictional character on the ABC medical drama Grey's Anatomy.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.039637572964312, "ctx": {"title": "Grey's Anatomy (season 5)", "text": "episodes as cardiothoracic surgeon Dr. Erica Hahn, Callie's love interest, who eventually resigns and moves away. Patrick Dempsey portrayed neurosurgeon Dr. Derek Shepherd whose relationship with Meredith Grey is the series' main storyline. Although originally introduced as a recurring character in the season premiere, trauma surgeon Dr. Owen Hunt was promoted to a series regular in the fourteenth episode of the season \"Beat Your Heart Out\". He was portrayed by Kevin McKidd and was conceived as a love interest to resident Cristina Yang. Originally only signed onto the show until December 2008, Kevin McKidd was upgraded to regular status after"}, "final_score": 1.039637572964312, "relevance_score": 0.9783846676312665, "ground_score": 0.061252905333045324, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.33153009821257895, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.021612124774172927, "[Relevant]": 0.9782394808127428}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.05275344112751116, "[Partially supported]": 0.014649101534411043, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9134161069688115}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0012979374451336128, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005517322933544946, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001830112826321266, "[Utility:4]": 0.002758487918123981, "[Utility:5]": 0.5728478641381676}], "n_tokens": 40}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd as Dr. Owen Hunt, who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd as Dr. Owen Hunt, who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9684386197754393, "ctx": {"title": "Grey's Anatomy", "text": "from \"Grey's Anatomy\" on November 6, 2008. \"E! Online\" Kristin Dos Santos asserted that Smith's dismissal from the show had been forced by the ABC network, as part of an attempt to \"de-gay\" \"Grey's Anatomy\". Rhimes countered these claims, saying that \"we did not find that the magic and chemistry with Brooke's character would sustain in the long run\". Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd (Dr. Owen Hunt), who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc. In addition, Jessica Capshaw (Dr. Arizona Robbins) was originally introduced for a three-episode arc, but received"}, "final_score": 1.9684386197754393, "relevance_score": 0.9788748067174222, "ground_score": 0.989563813058017, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12605018002889395, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.021123256776787763, "[Relevant]": 0.9787850751487901}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9845454704190783, "[Partially supported]": 0.009428550469592087, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005718704936455548}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005801329006184542, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006008909474793954, "[Utility:3]": 0.00025245322052129033, "[Utility:4]": 0.002189398694283855, "[Utility:5]": 0.7014560087464282}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]He first came to Grey's Anatomy in Season 4 Episode 10, titled \"I[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]He first came to Grey's Anatomy in Season 4 Episode 10, titled \"I[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.6909762761532291, "ctx": {"title": "Elevator Love Letter (Grey's Anatomy)", "text": "the Meredith/McDreamy roller coaster of Season 4, this episode is your payoff. Also, former army surgeon Owen\u2019s PTSD causes him to choke Cristina while she sleeps.\" Elevator Love Letter (Grey's Anatomy) \"Elevator Love Letter\" is the nineteenth episode of the fifth season of the American television medical drama, \"Grey's Anatomy\" and the show's 97th episode overall. Written by Stacy McKee and directed by Edward Ornelas, the episode was originally broadcast on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States on March 26, 2009. It was viewed by 15.81 million people and garnered a 5.5/13 Nielsen rating/share in the 18\u201349"}, "final_score": 0.6909762761532291, "relevance_score": 0.6909762761532291, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5582381746823913, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.3088918949208209, "[Relevant]": 0.6906815070034426}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 1.5344818585027333e-10, "[Utility:2]": 5.556205085957586e-13, "[Utility:3]": 4.255152785905211e-13, "[Utility:4]": 5.031372313222395e-10, "[Utility:5]": 0.9999977350285647}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Owen Hunt, M.D. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company in the United States.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The character was created by series' producer Shonda Rhimes, and is portrayed by actor Kevin McKidd.[Continue to Use Evidence]He was introduced in season five as a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq, and subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, head of trauma surgery, and eventual chief of surgery, sometimes with unorthodox methods.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Owen Hunt, M.D. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company in the United States.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The character was created by series' producer Shonda Rhimes, and is portrayed by actor Kevin McKidd.[Continue to Use Evidence]He was introduced in season five as a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq, and subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, head of trauma surgery, and eventual chief of surgery, sometimes with unorthodox methods.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2313809248251488, "ctx": {"title": "Owen Hunt", "text": "Owen Hunt Owen Hunt, M.D. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series \"Grey's Anatomy\", which airs on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States. The character was created by series' producer Shonda Rhimes, and is portrayed by actor Kevin McKidd. He was introduced in season five as a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq, and subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, head of trauma surgery, and eventual chief of surgery, sometimes with unorthodox methods. Originally contracted to appear for a multi-episode story arc, he"}, "final_score": 1.2313809248251488, "relevance_score": 0.9549844669584446, "ground_score": 0.27639645786670425, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07027163119988782, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.045010288951421236, "[Relevant]": 0.9548732159239008}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.26183648295718454, "[Partially supported]": 0.018819768197829954, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7007107647237184}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005485126012938764, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003997358698217275, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016025095043170599, "[Utility:4]": 0.002907878346068169, "[Utility:5]": 0.6816081379773908}], "n_tokens": 138}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Dr. Owen Harper, played by T.R. Knight, comes to Grey's Anatomy in season 5 episode 24, titled Now or Never, which aired on May 14, 2009.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Dr. Owen Harper, played by T.R. Knight, comes to Grey's Anatomy in season 5 episode 24, titled Now or Never, which aired on May 14, 2009.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4735867938919824, "ctx": {"title": "Now or Never (Grey's Anatomy)", "text": "Now or Never (Grey's Anatomy) \"Now or Never\" is the twenty-fourth episode and the season finale of the fifth season of the American television medical drama, \"Grey's Anatomy\" and the show's 102nd episode overall. Written by Debora Cahn and directed by Rob Corn, the episode was originally broadcast on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States on May 14, 2009. The initial airing was viewed by 17.12 million viewers and garnered a 6.2/17 Nielsen rating/share in the 18\u201349 demographic. The episode marked the last appearance of series regular T.R. Knight who played Dr. George O'Malley who got into"}, "final_score": 1.4735867938919824, "relevance_score": 0.7799928976970083, "ground_score": 0.6935938961949741, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2954459710877911, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.21981484370010085, "[Relevant]": 0.7793112817709484}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6824509581333463, "[Partially supported]": 0.020288732811938968, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.29582060253316594}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 5.714217869400996e-05, "[Utility:2]": 1.9403712235853954e-05, "[Utility:3]": 4.7744077371413986e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00041568094296378516, "[Utility:5]": 0.9575234216454945}], "n_tokens": 56}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What character kills the main character in Happy Death Day?", "short_answers": ["Tree's roommate, Lori", "Lori Spengler", "Lori"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What actress's character kills the main character in happy death day?", "short_answers": ["Ruby Modine"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Happy Death Day", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happy%20Death%20Day"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Happy Death Day is a 2017 American black comedy slasher film directed by Christopher Landon, and written by Scott Lobdell. ", "wikipage": "Happy Death Day"}, {"content": "Theresa \"Tree\" Gelbman is a fictional character from Christopher Landon's slasher film Happy Death Day (2017) and its sequel Happy Death Day 2U (2019). She was created by writer Scott Lobdell and portrayed by Jessica Rothe in both of her performances.[1][2] Gelbman's storyline revolves around her being murdered on the night of her birthday and finding herself reliving the day repeatedly.", "wikipage": "Tree Gelbman"}, {"content": "Tree realizes Lori is her true killer...Lori admits to also having an affair with Dr. Butler, whose preference for Tree drove Lori mad with jealousy.", "wikipage": "Happy Death Day"}], "long_answer": "Theresa \"Tree\" Gelbman is the main character in the 2017 black comedy, Happy Death Day. She is killed by her roommate, Lori (Ruby Modine) out of jealousy over a love interest."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Happy Death Day is a 2017 American black comedy slasher film directed by Christopher Landon, and written by Scott Lobdell.", "wikipage": "Happy Death Day"}, {"content": "Ruby Wylder Rivera Modine (born July 31, 1990) is an American actress, dancer and singer. She is best known for playing Sierra Morton in Shameless,[3][4] and co-starring in the 2017 slasher film Happy Death Day[5] and its sequel as Lori Spengler.", "wikipage": "Ruby Modine"}, {"content": "A sequel, Happy Death Day 2U, was released on February 13, 2019.", "wikipage": "Happy Death Day"}], "long_answer": "In Happy Death Day, the main character is killed by the character of Tree's roommate, Lori Spengler, played by actress, dancer, and singer Ruby Modine. Modine co-starred as the character in both the 2017 film and its sequel, 2019's Happy Death Day 2U."}], "sample_id": "-8467012199151434041", "question": "Who kills the main character in happy death day?", "docs": [{"id": "20114441", "title": "Happy Death Day", "text": "Tree also meets with her married professor, Gregory Butler, with whom she is having an affair. That night, on her way to a party, Tree is lured into a tunnel and murdered by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot \u2013 a baby. Tree immediately wakes up in the same way she had previously \u2013 in Carter's bed \u2013 and is unnerved to find the previous day's events repeating themselves. Baffled, she nonetheless relives the day and uses this second chance to avoid the tunnel and reach the party. However, the masked killer follows her to the party", "score": 0.72412109375, "summary": "Tree is murdered by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot, a baby. It is unclear who the killer is.", "extraction": "The main character, Tree, is killed by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot - a baby."}], "answer": "In Happy Death Day, the main character is killed by the character of Tree's roommate, Lori Spengler, played by actress, dancer, and singer Ruby Modine. Modine co-starred as the character in both the 2017 film and its sequel, 2019's Happy Death Day 2U.", "output": "In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, is killed by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot, a baby [1]. The figure is revealed to be Carter, who is having an affair with Tree's married professor, Gregory Butler [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, is killed by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot, a baby.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The figure is revealed to be Carter, who is having an affair with Tree's married professor, Gregory Butler.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 425, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the film Happy Death Day, the character Tree is killed by his wife, Lori, who poisoned him with a cupcake.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the film Happy Death Day, the character Tree is killed by his wife, Lori, who poisoned him with a cupcake.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8249935951840848, "ctx": {"title": "Happy Death Day", "text": "nurse reveals herself to be Dr. Butler's wife, Stephanie, who says it is for her own pain. She then murders Tree in revenge for her husband's affair with the girl. This version was shown in the test screenings for the film and was received negatively by the audience, which led the writers to come up with the theatrical ending. Director Christopher Landon also revealed Lori and Dr. Butler were the killers in the rough drafts, which later inspired the idea of the poisoned cupcake. Bear McCreary composed the score of \"Happy Death Day\". Reflecting the film's blend of horror and"}, "final_score": 1.8249935951840848, "relevance_score": 0.9916842231200217, "ground_score": 0.8333093720640631, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4534176365511147, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008314915549316824, "[Relevant]": 0.9915815065560517}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6966279213724739, "[Partially supported]": 0.2623524790504215, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03441315621892857}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0013447354718038027, "[Utility:2]": 0.00015058074808812344, "[Utility:3]": 0.00030477615753850463, "[Utility:4]": 0.0008481140311557128, "[Utility:5]": 0.5320120122328994}], "n_tokens": 33}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, is killed by her birthday clown, who is revealed to be her birth mother.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, is killed by her birthday clown, who is revealed to be her birth mother.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4647544506325767, "ctx": {"title": "Happy Death Day", "text": "Happy Death Day Happy Death Day is a 2017 American slasher film directed by Christopher Landon, written by Scott Lobdell and starring Jessica Rothe, Israel Broussard, and Ruby Modine. The film was produced by Jason Blum through his Blumhouse Productions banner. It follows a college student who is murdered on her birthday and begins reliving the day over and over again, at which point she sets out to find the killer and stop her death. Originally announced in 2007 under the title \"Half to Death\", the film was released on October 13, 2017, by Universal Pictures. It grossed $122 million"}, "final_score": 1.4647544506325767, "relevance_score": 0.9695556537116191, "ground_score": 0.4951987969209577, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.48844991001054194, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.030434267972418542, "[Relevant]": 0.9692346913848683}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.30072815063625186, "[Partially supported]": 0.38313758062626097, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.31027428686834996}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000748246041355865, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001127478298990008, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018552703379665094, "[Utility:4]": 0.0007337736528416334, "[Utility:5]": 0.8907000799019049}], "n_tokens": 41}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, is killed by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot, a baby.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The figure is revealed to be Carter, who is having an affair with Tree's married professor, Gregory Butler.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, is killed by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot, a baby.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The figure is revealed to be Carter, who is having an affair with Tree's married professor, Gregory Butler.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9644304549035687, "ctx": {"title": "Happy Death Day", "text": "Tree also meets with her married professor, Gregory Butler, with whom she is having an affair. That night, on her way to a party, Tree is lured into a tunnel and murdered by a figure wearing a mask of the campus mascot \u2013 a baby. Tree immediately wakes up in the same way she had previously \u2013 in Carter's bed \u2013 and is unnerved to find the previous day's events repeating themselves. Baffled, she nonetheless relives the day and uses this second chance to avoid the tunnel and reach the party. However, the masked killer follows her to the party"}, "final_score": 1.9644304549035687, "relevance_score": 0.9946567432914951, "ground_score": 0.9697737116120736, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2891691240522505, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0053415075527700785, "[Relevant]": 0.9943311348392784}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9548358271376948, "[Partially supported]": 0.025051019025229333, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.01762558902388459}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0017343717697832315, "[Utility:2]": 0.00019804190693089807, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010395388729974313, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006380397174513806, "[Utility:5]": 0.8949241252537715}], "n_tokens": 64}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]In the movie Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, kills the character Carter by hanging herself.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the movie Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree, kills the character Carter by hanging herself.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9268048221885454, "ctx": {"title": "Happy Death Day", "text": "the hospital to warn of his escape. Tombs breaks free and nearly kills Tree, but Carter follows and rescues her. Tombs kills Carter before chasing Tree to a nearby bell tower, where she subdues him with a crowbar. Realizing that Carter will remain dead if she ends the loop, Tree hangs herself. Believing she has solved her murder, Tree is happier and more relaxed during the next loop. Tree ends her affair with Professor Butler. She meets her father for lunch, and the two begin to reconcile. That night she goes to the hospital, where she traps and kills Tombs."}, "final_score": 1.9268048221885454, "relevance_score": 0.9966233875641973, "ground_score": 0.930181434624348, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.38350840379091633, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0033752847203906543, "[Relevant]": 0.9962315059796792}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8802186650780381, "[Partially supported]": 0.08715344883614624, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0257625102671554}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0020657253302107065, "[Utility:2]": 0.00017494763371306713, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020654169776059824, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010266105669426073, "[Utility:5]": 0.8077327993272694}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree Gelbman, is killed by her friends, Carter and Kaya, in a car accident.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 film Happy Death Day, the main character, Tree Gelbman, is killed by her friends, Carter and Kaya, in a car accident.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.424148831280538, "ctx": {"title": "Happy Death Day 2U", "text": "Happy Death Day 2U Happy Death Day 2U is an upcoming 2019 American slasher film written and directed by Christopher Landon. It stars Jessica Rothe, Israel Broussard, Suraj Sharma, and Ruby Modine. The film is a sequel to 2017's \"Happy Death Day\", with Jason Blum again serving as a producer through his Blumhouse Productions banner. The film is scheduled to be released on February 14, 2019, by Universal Pictures. Two years after the events of the first film, Tree Gelbman unexpectedly re-enters the time loop and becomes determined to escape it after finding out her friends are now involved. When"}, "final_score": 1.424148831280538, "relevance_score": 0.940353056428033, "ground_score": 0.4837957748525048, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5195459400576244, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.059620060045625325, "[Relevant]": 0.9399292290758007}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.10975375116495613, "[Partially supported]": 0.7441939607193788, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.1420319071430279}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00046368889711410926, "[Utility:2]": 8.216624887853222e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.000168594195976004, "[Utility:4]": 0.00041890800904378705, "[Utility:5]": 0.6923226742459749}], "n_tokens": 41}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The One Ring was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron during the Second Age to gain dominion over the free peoples of Middle-earth. In disguise as \"Annatar\", or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided the Elven smiths of Eregion and their leader Celebrimbor in the making of the Rings of Power. He then forged the One Ring himself in the fires of Mount Doom.", "question": "Who forged the One ring in The Lord of the Rings?", "short_answers": ["Sauron"], "wikipage": "One Ring"}, {"context": "After its original forging (about ) Sauron waged the War of the Elves and Sauron against the Elves and all who opposed him. Sauron invaded and destroyed Eregion, and killed Celebrimbor, the maker of the three rings of the Elves. However, King Tar-Minastir of N\u00famenor sent a great fleet to Middle-earth, and with this aid Gil-galad destroyed Sauron's army and forced Sauron to return to Mordor.", "question": "Who forged the three rings for the Elves in the Lord of the Rings?", "short_answers": ["Celebrimbor"], "wikipage": "One Ring"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who forged the nine rings for men and the seven rings for the Dwarves in the Lord of the Rings?", "short_answers": ["Celebrimbor and Gwaith-i-M\u00edrdain", "Celebrimbor and Elven Smiths"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "The forging of the Rings of Power opens the prologue of Peter Jackson's \"The Lord of the Rings\" film series, primarily with \"\" (2001). In the film, the Three Elven Rings are shown being cast using a cuttlebone mold, an ancient primitive casting technique. These were given to Gil-galad (portrayed by Mark Ferguson), C\u00edrdan (Michael Elsworth), and Galadriel (Cate Blanchett). Tolkien illustrator Alan Lee, employed as conceptual designers for the films, had a cameo as one of the nine human Ring-bearers who would later become the Nazg\u00fbl, while Sauron (Sala Baker) is seen forging the One Ring at the chamber of Mount Doom. The One Ring was also shown to have the ability to adjust in size to the finger of its wearer, such as when it became smaller to fit Isildur (Harry Sinclair). In the extended version of the film, Galadriel also properly introduces Nenya, the Ring of Adamant, to Frodo. In the concluding sequel, \"\" (2003), the final wearers of the Three Rings\u2014Gandalf (Ian McKellen), Elrond (Hugo Weaving), and Galadriel, were revealed at the Grey Havens wearing the Three, with Galadriel proclaiming the end of its power and the beginning of the Dominion of Men.", "question": "Who played the character that forged the one ring in the lord of the rings trilogy?", "short_answers": ["Sala Baker and Alan Howard", "Sala Baker", "Alan Howard"], "wikipage": "Rings of Power"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Lord of the Rings", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Lord%20of%20the%20Rings"}, {"title": "Rings of Power", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rings%20of%20Power"}, {"title": "The Lord of the Rings (film series)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Lord%20of%20the%20Rings%20%28film%20series%29"}, {"title": "Alan Howard", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan%20Howard"}, {"title": "One Ring", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One%20Ring"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Rings of Power were forged by the Elven-smiths of the Noldorin settlement of Eregion", "wikipage": "Rings of Power"}, {"content": "The smiths were led by Celebrimbor, the grandson of F\u00ebanor, the greatest craftsman of the Noldor, working with Dwarves from Khazad-d\u00fbm (Moria) led by his friend Narvi.", "wikipage": "Rings of Power"}], "long_answer": "The One ring in The Lord of the Rings was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron, played by Sala Baker and Alan Howard in the films. The three rings for the Elves, the nine rings for men and the seven rings for the Dwarves was forged by Celebrimbor and Elven Smiths."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The One Ring, also called the Ruling Ring and Isildur's Bane, is a central plot element in J. R. R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings (1954\u201355).", "wikipage": "One Ring"}, {"content": "In disguise as Annatar, or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided the Elven smiths of Eregion and their leader Celebrimbor in the making of the Rings of Power.", "wikipage": "One Ring Purpose"}], "long_answer": "The One Ring, also called the Ruling Ring and Isildur's Bane, is a central plot element in J. R. R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings. The One Ring was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron during the Second Age to gain dominion over the free peoples of Middle-earth. In disguise as Annatar, or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided Celebrimbor and Elven smiths of Eregion in the making of the Rings of Power. Sala Baker and Alan Howard both played Sauron."}], "sample_id": "1134137436027221870", "question": "Who forged the ring in lord of the rings?", "docs": [{"id": "1842944", "title": "Ci\u0301rdan", "text": "N\u00famenor. Among these was a mariner by the name of V\u00ebantur whose grandson Aldarion would one day become the King of N\u00famenor. C\u00edrdan was early to distrust the being named Annatar, and refused him entry to the Havens. He similarly advised the Elves at Eregion not to forge the Rings of Power under Annatar's tutelage, though they ignored his warning. The rings proved indeed to be a trap; Annatar was none other than Sauron the Dark Lord, who forged the One Ring to control the others. C\u00edrdan was entrusted with Narya, one of the Three Rings not subject to Sauron's", "score": 0.7431640625, "summary": "C\u00edrdan was an Elf who advised against forging the Rings of Power under the tutelage of Annatar, who was later revealed to be Sauron. C\u00edrdan was entrusted with one of the Three Rings, Narya. No information is provided about who specifically forged the One Ring.", "extraction": "Sauron forged the ring in Lord of the Rings. (The passage mentions that Sauron was Annatar, who convinced the Elves at Eregion to forge the Rings of Power, including the One Ring.)"}], "answer": "The One Ring, also called the Ruling Ring and Isildur's Bane, is a central plot element in J. R. R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings. The One Ring was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron during the Second Age to gain dominion over the free peoples of Middle-earth. In disguise as Annatar, or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided Celebrimbor and Elven smiths of Eregion in the making of the Rings of Power. Sala Baker and Alan Howard both played Sauron.", "output": "The Elves of Eregion forged the Rings of Power under the guidance of Sauron, who was posing as a great Elven smith named Annatar [1]. The One Ring was forged by Sauron himself [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Elves of Eregion forged the Rings of Power under the guidance of Sauron, who was posing as a great Elven smith named Annatar.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The One Ring was forged by Sauron himself.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 426, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The ring was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron during the Second Age to gain dominion over the free peoples of Middle-earth.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]In disguise as \"Annatar\", or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided the Elven smiths of Eregion and their leader Celebrimbor in the making of the Rings of Power.[Continue to Use Evidence]The One Ring was later destroyed in the fires of Mount Doom.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The ring was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron during the Second Age to gain dominion over the free peoples of Middle-earth.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]In disguise as \"Annatar\", or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided the Elven smiths of Eregion and their leader Celebrimbor in the making of the Rings of Power.[Continue to Use Evidence]The One Ring was later destroyed in the fires of Mount Doom.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9862687160660912, "ctx": {"title": "One Ring", "text": "fulfilling his design. In \"The Lord of the Rings\" and the posthumously published \"The Silmarillion\", Tolkien provides a detailed internal development from the forging of the Ring to its destruction. In the fictional context of Middle-earth, these events take place during several thousand years in the Second and Third Age of Arda. The One Ring was forged by the Dark Lord Sauron during the Second Age to gain dominion over the free peoples of Middle-earth. In disguise as \"Annatar\", or \"Lord of Gifts\", he aided the Elven smiths of Eregion and their leader Celebrimbor in the making of the Rings"}, "final_score": 1.9862687160660912, "relevance_score": 0.9900635806801958, "ground_score": 0.9962051353858953, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1513068884160373, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.009935501459948527, "[Relevant]": 0.9899721252387494}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.992530413040337, "[Partially supported]": 0.006687616224974551, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0004497959432556641}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000845126250984798, "[Utility:2]": 0.00014571797110627839, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015755869394429707, "[Utility:4]": 0.01632451958683138, "[Utility:5]": 0.9637126671508475}], "n_tokens": 100}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In the Lord of the Rings, the Elven-smith Celebrimbor forged the One Ring.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the Lord of the Rings, the Elven-smith Celebrimbor forged the One Ring.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.968561382906004, "ctx": {"title": "The Lord of the Rings: The Third Age", "text": "by Celebrimbor, how to forge the Rings, whilst, in secret, he forged his own One Ring in the fires of Mount Doom, a Ring far more powerful than any of the others. However, in order for the One Ring to be powerful enough to control the other Rings, Sauron had to transfer most of his power into it. As soon as he put it on, the elves became aware of his ruse, removing and hiding their Three Rings, which Celebrimbor had forged without Sauron's aid. Sauron waged war on the elves, conquering much of Middle-earth and killing Celebrimbor. Thus began"}, "final_score": 1.968561382906004, "relevance_score": 0.9790357568880538, "ground_score": 0.9895256260179504, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4323200093286718, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.020960188536672215, "[Relevant]": 0.9788464071390047}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9821283858775298, "[Partially supported]": 0.013684786636646481, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003626107472608684}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001091598407069165, "[Utility:2]": 0.00013451131603213227, "[Utility:3]": 0.00041920794247482247, "[Utility:4]": 0.005587073079061286, "[Utility:5]": 0.5502096022281684}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Elven-smiths of Eregion, led by Celebrimbor, forged the ring.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Elven-smiths of Eregion, led by Celebrimbor, forged the ring.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9819897169308023, "ctx": {"title": "Rings of Power", "text": "Gifts\") to the Elven-smiths of Eregion, who were led by Celebrimbor, and taught them the craft of forging magic rings. Tolkien writes that the Elves made many lesser rings as essays in the craft, but eventually with Sauron's assistance they forged the Seven and the Nine. The Three were made by Celebrimbor himself without Sauron's assistance; they remained unsullied by his touch. Sauron returned to Mordor, and in his forge in Mount Doom he made the One Ring, imbuing it with a large portion of his power. Its purpose was to dominate and command the wearers of the other Rings."}, "final_score": 1.9819897169308023, "relevance_score": 0.9923043511645177, "ground_score": 0.9896853657662847, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2581594703645221, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.007694569728919915, "[Relevant]": 0.9921652073236046}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9806098120708931, "[Partially supported]": 0.01700465173195859, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0018063341100601148}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0013378044956660793, "[Utility:2]": 0.00018753038513480416, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004309416133969099, "[Utility:4]": 0.006982266546467782, "[Utility:5]": 0.6212010826990606}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Elves of Eregion forged the Rings of Power under the guidance of Sauron, who was posing as a great Elven smith named Annatar.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The One Ring was forged by Sauron himself.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Elves of Eregion forged the Rings of Power under the guidance of Sauron, who was posing as a great Elven smith named Annatar.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The One Ring was forged by Sauron himself.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9870815726205828, "ctx": {"title": "Ci\u0301rdan", "text": "N\u00famenor. Among these was a mariner by the name of V\u00ebantur whose grandson Aldarion would one day become the King of N\u00famenor. C\u00edrdan was early to distrust the being named Annatar, and refused him entry to the Havens. He similarly advised the Elves at Eregion not to forge the Rings of Power under Annatar's tutelage, though they ignored his warning. The rings proved indeed to be a trap; Annatar was none other than Sauron the Dark Lord, who forged the One Ring to control the others. C\u00edrdan was entrusted with Narya, one of the Three Rings not subject to Sauron's"}, "final_score": 1.9870815726205828, "relevance_score": 0.9921841110518225, "ground_score": 0.9948974615687605, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.41550292267000705, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.007814034866194551, "[Relevant]": 0.991948745542377}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9912125470953349, "[Partially supported]": 0.005757431891890169, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.00221968772856968}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002834663952973348, "[Utility:2]": 9.402662210312528e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015593470018131436, "[Utility:4]": 0.012147827232770584, "[Utility:5]": 0.9650215397495013}], "n_tokens": 53}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]In the Lord of the Rings universe, the One Ring was forged by Sauron, the Dark Lord, in the fires of Mount Doom.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the Lord of the Rings universe, the One Ring was forged by Sauron, the Dark Lord, in the fires of Mount Doom.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9685215278115629, "ctx": {"title": "The Lord of the Rings: The Two Towers (video game)", "text": "the background of the history of the One Ring. At the dawn of the Second Age, after the defeat of the Dark Lord, Morgoth, the elves of Eregion forged the nineteen Rings of Power to help themselves, the dwarves and men rule Middle-earth. However, the elves were unaware that Sauron, Morgoth's closest ally, had survived his master's defeat, and in the guise of Annatar had been the one who taught the Elven-smiths, led by Celebrimbor, how to forge the Rings, whilst, in secret, he forged his own One Ring in the fires of Mount Doom, a Ring far more powerful"}, "final_score": 1.9685215278115629, "relevance_score": 0.9805806522786474, "ground_score": 0.9879408755329153, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3314514273002563, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0194168291951211, "[Relevant]": 0.9804534792072234}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9767322178617537, "[Partially supported]": 0.021410899962297412, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.001347532170826966}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001679840877629498, "[Utility:2]": 0.00014822330840982432, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004148914219797037, "[Utility:4]": 0.0053177103386188775, "[Utility:5]": 0.5887926434715711}], "n_tokens": 36}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 4], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who plays the mayor in the 2016 ghostbusters reboot?", "short_answers": ["Andy Garc\u00eda", "Andr\u00e9s Arturo Garc\u00eda Men\u00e9ndez"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "In addition to the main cast, \"Ghostbusters\" features David Margulies as Lenny Clotch, Mayor of New York, Michael Ensign as Manager of the Sedgewick Hotel, and Slavitza Jovan as Gozer (voiced by Paddi Edwards). The film also features astrologist Ruth Hale Oliver as the Library Ghost, Alice Drummond as the Librarian, Jennifer Runyon and Steven Tash as Venkman's psychological test subjects, \"Playboy\" Playmate Kymberly Herrin as a Dream Ghost, Timothy Carhart as a violinist, and Reginald VelJohnson as a police officer. Roger Grimsby, Larry King, Joe Franklin, and Casey Kasem cameo as themselves, the latter in a voice-only role. Kasem's wife Jean appears in the film as the tall guest at Louis' party. The film also features appearances by pornstar Ron Jeremy, and a young Debbie Gibson. Director Ivan Reitman provided miscellaneous ghost voices, including that of Slimer.", "question": "Who plays the mayor in ghostbusters II?", "short_answers": ["David Margulies"], "wikipage": "Ghostbusters"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Ghostbusters (2016 film)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostbusters%20%282016%20film%29"}, {"title": "Ghostbusters (franchise)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostbusters%20%28franchise%29"}, {"title": "Ghostbusters", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostbusters"}, {"title": "Ghostbusters II", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostbusters%20II"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "As well as the main cast, Ghostbusters features David Margulies as Lenny Clotch, Mayor of New York, Michael Ensign as the Sedgewick Hotel manager, and Slavitza Jovan as Gozer (voiced by Paddi Edwards). ", "wikipage": "Ghostbusters"}], "long_answer": "The Mayors of New York City in the Ghostbuster movies are played by different actors. David Margulies played the Mayor Lenny Clotch in both the first and second movies. Andy Garc\u00eda plays Mayor Bradley in the third film in 2016."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Ghostbusters (later marketed as Ghostbusters: Answer the Call[5]) is a 2016 American supernatural comedy film directed by Paul Feig and written by Feig and Katie Dippold.", "wikipage": "Ghostbusters (2016 film)"}, {"content": "Andr\u00e9s Arturo Garc\u00eda Men\u00e9ndez (born April 12, 1956) is an American actor and director.", "wikipage": "Andy Garc\u00eda"}, {"content": "Ghostbusters II is a 1989 American supernatural comedy film directed and produced by Ivan Reitman and written by Dan Aykroyd and Harold Ramis.", "wikipage": "Ghostbusters II"}], "long_answer": "There are several Ghostbusters films. Ghostbusters, later marketed as Ghostbusters: Answer the Call, is a 2016 American supernatural comedy film directed by Paul Feig and written by Feig and Katie Dippold. Andy Garc\u00eda, an American actor and director, plays the mayor. Ghostbusters II is a 1989 American supernatural comedy film directed and produced by Ivan Reitman and written by Dan Aykroyd and Harold Ramis. The film features David Margulies as Lenny Clotch, Mayor of New York."}], "sample_id": "-5450786427455499982", "question": "Who plays the mayor in the new ghostbusters?", "docs": [{"id": "18324757", "title": "Ghostbusters (2016 film)", "text": "Ghostbusters (2016 film) Ghostbusters (also known as Ghostbusters: Answer the Call and marketed as such on home release) is a 2016 supernatural comedy film directed by Paul Feig and written by Feig and Katie Dippold. The film stars Melissa McCarthy, Kristen Wiig, Kate McKinnon, Leslie Jones, Charles Dance, Michael Kenneth Williams, and Chris Hemsworth. It is the third feature film in the \"Ghostbusters\" franchise, and serves as a reboot of the series. The story focuses on four women who begin a ghost-catching business in New York City. A third \"Ghostbusters\" film had been in various stages of development following the", "score": 0.72314453125, "summary": "The 2016 film \"Ghostbusters\" is a reboot of the franchise and stars Melissa McCarthy, Kristen Wiig, Kate McKinnon, and Leslie Jones as the four women who start a ghost-catching business in New York City. The film also features Charles Dance, Michael Kenneth Williams, and Chris Hemsworth. However, the document does not provide information about who plays the mayor.", "extraction": "Leslie Jones, Kate McKinnon, Melissa McCarthy, and Kristen Wiig are the main cast members in the 2016 Ghostbusters film. The passage does not provide the name of the actor who plays the mayor, so the answer is \"irrelevant\"."}], "answer": "There are several Ghostbusters films. Ghostbusters, later marketed as Ghostbusters: Answer the Call, is a 2016 American supernatural comedy film directed by Paul Feig and written by Feig and Katie Dippold. Andy Garc\u00eda, an American actor and director, plays the mayor. Ghostbusters II is a 1989 American supernatural comedy film directed and produced by Ivan Reitman and written by Dan Aykroyd and Harold Ramis. The film features David Margulies as Lenny Clotch, Mayor of New York.", "output": "The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot stars Charles Dance as the Mayor of New York City [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot stars Charles Dance as the Mayor of New York City.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 427, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2016 Ghostbusters reboot, the mayor is played by Richard Masur.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2016 Ghostbusters reboot, the mayor is played by Richard Masur.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3321864996132904, "ctx": {"title": "Ghostbusters (2016 film)", "text": "live music venue, the Ghostbusters are called in and capture the ghost in front of the audience. When supernatural debunker Dr. Martin Heiss challenges the Ghostbusters, the incensed Erin releases the ghost as proof; it throws Heiss out a window and escapes. The Ghostbusters are brought to Mayor Bradley and his secretary Jennifer Lynch, who reveal that the city and the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) are aware of New York's ghost problem. While privately supporting the team's work, the mayor's office and the DHS publicly denounce them as fraudsters. The Ghostbusters realize that Rowan is planting his devices along"}, "final_score": 1.3321864996132904, "relevance_score": 0.914900963114118, "ground_score": 0.41728553649917244, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4161278786384803, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.08505586343478988, "[Relevant]": 0.9144368047237225}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.1890270667861714, "[Partially supported]": 0.4499235661397097, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3531490533685354}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00022204318582984, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001921623607744665, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012800772884760935, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009682866250624493, "[Utility:5]": 0.7891036303365124}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Irrelevant]In the 2016 Ghostbusters reboot, the mayor is played by Dan Aykroyd.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]In the 2016 Ghostbusters reboot, the mayor is played by Dan Aykroyd.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.3415825061409754, "ctx": {"title": "Winston Zeddemore", "text": "is destroyed, everyone flees the mausoleum. Winston carries the exorcised Mayor Mulligan outside until he regains consciousness. Zeddemore appears in Beeline's \"Ghostbusters\" game for iOS. The game was released on January 24, 2013. Zeddemore appears in \"Lego Dimensions\", with archival audio of Ernie Hudson being used to represent his character. The name \"Zeddemore\" is misspelled as \"Zeddmore\" in the closing credits of \"Ghostbusters\". As a result it was also sometimes misspelled in scripts and other sources related to \"The Real Ghostbusters\". The name is spelled correctly on the nametag on Winston's jumpsuit, in the shooting script of \"Ghostbusters\" (as published"}, "final_score": 0.3415825061409754, "relevance_score": 0.3415825061409754, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.34763942072135656, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.658216650435744, "[Relevant]": 0.3414783099425056}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.004144447651202016, "[Utility:2]": 0.0010236063440449837, "[Utility:3]": 0.00019008922212019827, "[Utility:4]": 0.0025633135398939084, "[Utility:5]": 0.8606666483704667}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot stars Charles Dance as the Mayor of New York City.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot stars Charles Dance as the Mayor of New York City.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7529316282023997, "ctx": {"title": "Ghostbusters (2016 film)", "text": "Ghostbusters (2016 film) Ghostbusters (also known as Ghostbusters: Answer the Call and marketed as such on home release) is a 2016 supernatural comedy film directed by Paul Feig and written by Feig and Katie Dippold. The film stars Melissa McCarthy, Kristen Wiig, Kate McKinnon, Leslie Jones, Charles Dance, Michael Kenneth Williams, and Chris Hemsworth. It is the third feature film in the \"Ghostbusters\" franchise, and serves as a reboot of the series. The story focuses on four women who begin a ghost-catching business in New York City. A third \"Ghostbusters\" film had been in various stages of development following the"}, "final_score": 1.7529316282023997, "relevance_score": 0.9832145066362474, "ground_score": 0.7697171215661524, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2508882213254625, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.016783797229263802, "[Relevant]": 0.9831151551308499}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6017737267145323, "[Partially supported]": 0.31959961376848756, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.06804668443365809}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007576717634407413, "[Utility:2]": 0.00013426037817719838, "[Utility:3]": 6.83061485527496e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006232437922693826, "[Utility:5]": 0.7190735688155115}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot casts Chelsea Clinton as the Mayor of New York City.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot casts Chelsea Clinton as the Mayor of New York City.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.5644748942874904, "ctx": {"title": "Ghostbusters (2016 film)", "text": "think there's much market for it here.\" The first \"Ghostbusters\" trailer was released on March 3, 2016. It was viewed 24 million times in 24 hours on Facebook and YouTube, and more than 60 million times across all social media platforms in its first week. Original \"Ghostbusters\" cast members Murray, Aykroyd, Hudson and Potts joined the new cast on the June 8, 2016, episode of \"Jimmy Kimmel Live!\", which was entirely dedicated to the new film. Sony partnered with Snapchat to promote the film with \"busting\" and \"sliming\" features. The filter, which features the Ghostbusters logo, allows users to shoot"}, "final_score": 0.5644748942874904, "relevance_score": 0.519521321952093, "ground_score": 0.04495357233539746, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4630905422185606, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.4802924967194257, "[Relevant]": 0.5193200119370712}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.012047241537590894, "[Partially supported]": 0.06563755239883907, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9203675388629796}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005602027959002584, "[Utility:2]": 0.00038652830662785806, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014670952726027013, "[Utility:4]": 0.001621000538130669, "[Utility:5]": 0.9043913699527699}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot film features Channing Tatum as the Mayor of New York City.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2016 Ghostbusters reboot film features Channing Tatum as the Mayor of New York City.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0623030319260693, "ctx": {"title": "Ghostbusters", "text": "revealed Channing Tatum and Chris Pratt's ultimate plan to team up for a different \"Ghostbusters\" film, with Tatum comparing it to \"Batman Begins\". In January 2015, the main cast members for the all-female lead film, were announced as McCarthy, Kristen Wiig, Leslie Jones and Kate McKinnon. The global release dates for the reboot, titled \"Ghostbusters\", were across July 2016. In a February 2015 interview on Ron Bennington's \"Unmasked\" radio show, Aykroyd stated that he would still like to see his idea for a sequel made. The following month, \"Deadline\" wrote that an all-male lead \"Ghostbusters\" film was in the works"}, "final_score": 1.0623030319260693, "relevance_score": 0.862467539200745, "ground_score": 0.19983549272532436, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3389425254662738, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.13746620886771924, "[Relevant]": 0.8620520726263312}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.09356691803680686, "[Partially supported]": 0.21085630472827127, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.691371206378012}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00038239782205518665, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011617006972214919, "[Utility:3]": 8.237691465415962e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.000855040627796658, "[Utility:5]": 0.9340104026668905}], "n_tokens": 29}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who was the best male golfer in the world according to the Official World Golf Ranking on February 19, 2017?", "short_answers": ["Dustin Johnson"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who was the best male golfer in the world according to the Official World Golf Ranking on March 27, 2016?", "short_answers": ["Jason Day"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who was the best male golfer in the world according to the Official World Golf Ranking on November 8, 2015?", "short_answers": ["Jordan Spieth"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who was the best female golfer in the world according to the Women's World Golf Rankings on 13 November 2017?", "short_answers": ["Shanshan Feng"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who was the best female golfer in the world according to the Women's World Golf Rankings on 6 November 2017?", "short_answers": ["Park Sung-hyun"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who was the best female golfer in the world according to the Women's World Golf Rankings on 26 June 2017?", "short_answers": ["Ryu So-yeon"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of world number one male golfers", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20world%20number%20one%20male%20golfers"}, {"title": "World Golf Championships", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World%20Golf%20Championships"}, {"title": "Women's World Golf Rankings", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women%27s%20World%20Golf%20Rankings"}, {"title": "Tiger Woods", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger%20Woods"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Multiple people have been ranked as the best golfer in the world. According to the Official World Golf Ranking on November 8, 2015 Jordan Spieth was ranked as the best male golfer in the world. On March 27, 2016, Jason Day was given the title and on February 19, 2017 the title was moved to Dustin Johnson. And according to the Women's World Golf Rankings, Shanshan Feng was ranked as the best female golfer in the world on November 13, 2017, the title was given to Park Sung-hyun on  November 6, 2017 and Ryu So-yeon on June 26, 2017."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "According to the Official World Golf Ranking on November 8, 2015, Jordan Spieth was the best male golfer in the world. On March 27, 2016, Jason Day was considered the best male golfer in the world and on February 19, 2017, Dustin Johnson was considered the best male golfer in the world. On June 26, 2017, the best female golfer in the world was Ryu So-yeon. On November 6, 2017, Park Sung-hyun was the best female golfer and on November 13, 2017, Shanshan Feng was considered the best female golfer in the world."}], "sample_id": "7187307382496449890", "question": "Who is the best golfer in the world?", "docs": [{"id": "539120", "title": "Tiger Woods", "text": "Tiger Woods Eldrick Tont \"Tiger\" Woods (born December 30, 1975) is an American professional golfer who is among the most successful golfers of all time, and one of the most popular athletes of the 21st century. He has been one of the highest-paid athletes in the world for several years. Woods is generally considered one of the greatest golfers of all time. Following an outstanding junior, college, and amateur career, Woods was 20 years old when he turned professional at the end of summer in 1996. By the end of April 1997, he had won three PGA Tour events in", "score": 0.7080078125, "summary": "Tiger Woods is considered one of the greatest golfers of all time and has been one of the highest-paid athletes in the world. He turned professional in 1996 and won three PGA Tour events by April 1997.", "extraction": "Tiger Woods is generally considered one of the greatest golfers of all time, so he might be the best golfer in the world."}], "answer": "According to the Official World Golf Ranking on November 8, 2015, Jordan Spieth was the best male golfer in the world. On March 27, 2016, Jason Day was considered the best male golfer in the world and on February 19, 2017, Dustin Johnson was considered the best male golfer in the world. On June 26, 2017, the best female golfer in the world was Ryu So-yeon. On November 6, 2017, Park Sung-hyun was the best female golfer and on November 13, 2017, Shanshan Feng was considered the best female golfer in the world.", "output": "Tiger Woods is widely considered to be one of the greatest golfers of all time [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Tiger Woods is widely considered to be one of the greatest golfers of all time.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 428, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Tiger Woods is widely considered to be one of the greatest golfers of all time.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Tiger Woods is widely considered to be one of the greatest golfers of all time.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9263906637016888, "ctx": {"title": "Tiger Woods", "text": "Tiger Woods Eldrick Tont \"Tiger\" Woods (born December 30, 1975) is an American professional golfer who is among the most successful golfers of all time, and one of the most popular athletes of the 21st century. He has been one of the highest-paid athletes in the world for several years. Woods is generally considered one of the greatest golfers of all time. Following an outstanding junior, college, and amateur career, Woods was 20 years old when he turned professional at the end of summer in 1996. By the end of April 1997, he had won three PGA Tour events in"}, "final_score": 1.9263906637016888, "relevance_score": 0.9982590165902058, "ground_score": 0.9281316471114829, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2461689121180667, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0017408112389851846, "[Relevant]": 0.998160295912258}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8843113072643152, "[Partially supported]": 0.08486478753459442, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.029328485791365537}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.002810172710920534, "[Utility:2]": 0.0012086402222398766, "[Utility:3]": 0.001126576563515762, "[Utility:4]": 0.019813647033914413, "[Utility:5]": 0.4725984398906213}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The World Golf Rankings are a ranking system for professional golfers created in 1984.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The World Golf Rankings are a ranking system for professional golfers created in 1984.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.7545663861456376, "ctx": {"title": "Mark McCormack's world golf rankings", "text": "two of the top ten (Gary Player and Graham Marsh) were non-Americans. The last ranking lists, published in the mid-1980s, reflected the shift in dominance back away from American golfers by that time \u2013 in 1984, five of the top ten were non-American, and in the last list published in December 1985, the top three players in the world \u2013 Seve Ballesteros, Bernhard Langer and Greg Norman \u2013 were all non-American. In the years the rankings were published, the highest position attained by a British player was sixth \u2013 by Tony Jacklin in 1972, and Nick Faldo in 1984. The"}, "final_score": 0.7545663861456376, "relevance_score": 0.7310585786300049, "ground_score": 0.023507807515632802, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.46509799743723934, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.26888254898088804, "[Relevant]": 0.7308985468844973}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.0006768951734761237, "[Partially supported]": 0.04563608047631436, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9531394571646717}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.008695871533636497, "[Utility:2]": 0.00661547020497031, "[Utility:3]": 0.0014085148855779623, "[Utility:4]": 0.08748279335625922, "[Utility:5]": 0.621651524831367}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The best golfer in the world is Tiger Woods, who has won 15 major championships and 82 PGA Tour events.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The best golfer in the world is Tiger Woods, who has won 15 major championships and 82 PGA Tour events.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3807043740191456, "ctx": {"title": "Official World Golf Ranking", "text": "Tiger Woods holds the record for most weeks in the World Top 10, with 860. He is followed by Ernie Els (788 weeks) and Phil Mickelson (774 weeks). Woods had a record run of 736 consecutive weeks in the top-10 from April 13, 1997 to May 15, 2011 and then had a further run of 124 consecutive weeks in the top-10 from March 25, 2012 to August 3, 2014. The first official ranking list was published prior to the Masters in April 1986, with Bernhard Langer the first world No. 1 ranked player, ahead of Seve Ballesteros, who had topped"}, "final_score": 1.3807043740191456, "relevance_score": 0.9857180673991122, "ground_score": 0.3949863066200335, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4067440716188945, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.014280444802959737, "[Relevant]": 0.985615381765495}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.11901171285726543, "[Partially supported]": 0.5503045192829336, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3286018371510175}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005214878723137433, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007072424385463762, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006618025633497369, "[Utility:4]": 0.021745340175960304, "[Utility:5]": 0.9246354409647627}], "n_tokens": 35}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The best golfer in the world in 2015 was Jason Day, the best golfer in the world in 2016 was Rory McIlroy, and the best golfer in the world in 2017 was Dustin Johnson.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The best golfer in the world in 2015 was Jason Day, the best golfer in the world in 2016 was Rory McIlroy, and the best golfer in the world in 2017 was Dustin Johnson.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2959577792635892, "ctx": {"title": "Ernie Els", "text": "dominated the game of golf. The five switched up and down the top five positions in the World Golf Ranking; most notably Vijay Singh's derailment of Tiger Woods as the best golfer in the world. The five stayed, for the most part, in the top five spots from 2004 until the start of 2007. Nine majors were won between them, many fighting against each other head to head. In July 2005, Els injured his left knee while sailing with his family in the Mediterranean. Despite missing several months of the 2005 season due to the injury, Els won the second"}, "final_score": 1.2959577792635892, "relevance_score": 0.9787126475468347, "ground_score": 0.31724513171675456, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2585993536684022, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.02128539615553351, "[Relevant]": 0.9786227043169826}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.025756282767207567, "[Partially supported]": 0.5816482997442863, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3905001951794771}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00010267187250737996, "[Utility:2]": 0.00020578886155356657, "[Utility:3]": 0.00026014149623204756, "[Utility:4]": 0.009170303843906833, "[Utility:5]": 0.972658316039486}], "n_tokens": 61}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Adam Scott is an Australian professional golfer who is currently ranked as the world's best golfer.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Adam Scott is an Australian professional golfer who is currently ranked as the world's best golfer.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5270580752172362, "ctx": {"title": "Adam Scott (golfer)", "text": "a 10-under-par 62 during the first round of the Arnold Palmer Invitational. He went on to finish third in the tournament behind the winner Matt Every and Keegan Bradley after a poor four-over-par 76 in the final round. On May 19, 2014, Scott took over as the World's #1 ranked golfer. Scott is the 17th golfer to be ranked number one since official rankings began. He is also the second Australian and the first since Greg Norman in 1998. A week after becoming world #1, Scott strengthened his ranking with a win at the Crowne Plaza Invitational at Colonial, being"}, "final_score": 1.5270580752172362, "relevance_score": 0.9906599749944981, "ground_score": 0.536398100222738, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4156502533005026, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.009337702075867612, "[Relevant]": 0.990413590920358}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.4598568785296242, "[Partially supported]": 0.15046459501440093, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.38723831612456255}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010342544406103371, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008675341255771062, "[Utility:3]": 0.0007626114020723583, "[Utility:4]": 0.00969841111879498, "[Utility:5]": 0.7734556179135398}], "n_tokens": 26}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the beatles play at the cow palace on their first US Tour?", "short_answers": ["August 19, 1964"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the beatles play at the cow palace on their second US Tour?", "short_answers": ["August 31, 1965"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the beatles play at the cow palace in 1964?", "short_answers": ["August 19, 1964"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the beatles play at the cow palace in 1965?", "short_answers": ["August 31, 1965"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Cow Palace", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow%20Palace"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Cow Palace (originally the California State Livestock Pavilion) is an indoor arena located in Daly City, California, situated on the city's northern border with neighboring San Francisco. ", "wikipage": "Cow Palace"}], "long_answer": "The Beatles played at The Cow Palace, an indoor arena located in Daly City, California, on their first US Tour on August 19, 1964. And on their second US Tour on August 31, 1965. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Cow Palace (originally the California State Livestock Pavilion) is an indoor arena located in Daly City, California, situated on the city's northern border with neighboring San Francisco.", "wikipage": "Cow Palace"}], "long_answer": "The Cow Palace is an indoor arena located in Daly City, California, situated on the city's northern border with neighboring San Francisco. On their first US Tour, the Beatles played at Cow Palace on August 19, 1964. On their second US Tour, the Beatles played at Cow Palace on August 31, 1965."}], "sample_id": "6943168985581937262", "question": "When did the beatles play at the cow palace?", "docs": [{"id": "1486181", "title": "Candlestick Park", "text": "public as of 2017. On August 14, 2014, former Beatle Paul McCartney returned one last time to become the closing act of Candlestick Park's long history. McCartney's performance was within days of being 48 years after the Beatles played their famous last concert at Candlestick. To showcase the event, McCartney contacted Barry Hood and used a portion of his original 1966 Beatles film on a big screen at this last concert. Pope John Paul II celebrated a Papal Mass on September 18, 1987 at Candlestick Park during his tour of America. An estimated crowd of 70,000 attended the mass. Candlestick", "score": 0.7568359375, "summary": "The Beatles played their famous last concert at Candlestick Park, but it is not mentioned whether they played at the Cow Palace.", "extraction": "The Beatles played their famous last concert at Candlestick \"48 years\" before Paul McCartney's performance at Candlestick Park on August 14, 2014."}], "answer": "The Cow Palace is an indoor arena located in Daly City, California, situated on the city's northern border with neighboring San Francisco. On their first US Tour, the Beatles played at Cow Palace on August 19, 1964. On their second US Tour, the Beatles played at Cow Palace on August 31, 1965.", "output": "The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on September 25, 1966, and at Candlestick Park in San Francisco on September 28, 1966 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on September 25, 1966, and at Candlestick Park in San Francisco on September 28, 1966.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 429, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on August 12, 1964, and on August 15, 1965.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on August 12, 1964, and on August 15, 1965.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.8251780076288509, "ctx": {"title": "Cow Palace", "text": "included WCW's SuperBrawl in 1997, 1998, and 2000 and WWE's No Way Out in 2004. In 2018 the Cow Palace hosted New Japan Pro Wrestling's G1 Special in San Francisco. From 1974 to 1989 the Cow Palace was the site of a yearly tournament on the men's professional tennis tour. Some of the biggest names in tennis played there, such as Jimmy Connors, John McEnroe and Ivan Lendl. In 2010, the Cow Palace once again had a regular sports tenant when the American Indoor Football Association's San Jose Wolves kicked off. However, the next year they would move to Stockton"}, "final_score": 0.8251780076288509, "relevance_score": 0.734119521083126, "ground_score": 0.09105848654572488, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3369405177968474, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.2658281064324002, "[Relevant]": 0.7339749160208194}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.06474651760475357, "[Partially supported]": 0.05202528846317567, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8799410241059703}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014048364210628863, "[Utility:2]": 0.00019201837214515602, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017758796359415512, "[Utility:4]": 0.003443726086571418, "[Utility:5]": 0.9774935262700638}], "n_tokens": 40}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace on August 29, 1966, at 8:30 PM.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace on August 29, 1966, at 8:30 PM.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7121816157371579, "ctx": {"title": "Candlestick Park", "text": "the transformer's failure was due to a broken splice, according to PG&E investigators. The Beatles gave their final full concert at Candlestick Park on August 29, 1966. Songs performed at the show were \"Rock and Roll Music\", \"She's a Woman\", \"If I Needed Someone\", \"Day Tripper\", \"Baby's in Black\", \"I Feel Fine\", \"Yesterday\", \"I Wanna Be Your Man\", \"Nowhere Man\", \"Paperback Writer\", and \"Long Tall Sally\". A rough recording of most of the concert was left unreleased, although the audio has leaked on to the internet. The recording cuts off during the last minute of the concert, interrupting \"Long Tall"}, "final_score": 1.7121816157371579, "relevance_score": 0.9795114465114483, "ground_score": 0.7326701692257095, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3317044803692397, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.020487669488935923, "[Relevant]": 0.9794691844872944}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7059682159462751, "[Partially supported]": 0.03683487978692351, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.24588961521103453}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023177193731359274, "[Utility:2]": 0.00015651873798646647, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023634322916975102, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010306467906102024, "[Utility:5]": 0.8632075737998178}], "n_tokens": 32}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on September 25, 1966, and at Candlestick Park in San Francisco on September 28, 1966.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on September 25, 1966, and at Candlestick Park in San Francisco on September 28, 1966.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.802823717405441, "ctx": {"title": "Candlestick Park", "text": "public as of 2017. On August 14, 2014, former Beatle Paul McCartney returned one last time to become the closing act of Candlestick Park's long history. McCartney's performance was within days of being 48 years after the Beatles played their famous last concert at Candlestick. To showcase the event, McCartney contacted Barry Hood and used a portion of his original 1966 Beatles film on a big screen at this last concert. Pope John Paul II celebrated a Papal Mass on September 18, 1987 at Candlestick Park during his tour of America. An estimated crowd of 70,000 attended the mass. Candlestick"}, "final_score": 1.802823717405441, "relevance_score": 0.9881308774790852, "ground_score": 0.8146928399263557, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.27558201617370176, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.011868465958716351, "[Relevant]": 0.9880762172141747}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7083374423629106, "[Partially supported]": 0.19979574978813044, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08394047984788856}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 5.7506489986926434e-05, "[Utility:2]": 9.047040772516554e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.000132666082159259, "[Utility:4]": 0.00176813228241983, "[Utility:5]": 0.9194998311632873}], "n_tokens": 49}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on August 12, 1965, and on August 15, 1965.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on August 12, 1965, and on August 15, 1965.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9447852479785955, "ctx": {"title": "Cow Palace", "text": "new Oakland Coliseum Arena (now Oracle Arena) and changed their name to Golden State Warriors. The Warriors lost to the Boston Celtics in the 1964 NBA Finals. The 1967 NBA Finals between San Francisco and the Philadelphia 76ers saw three games held at the Cow Palace. The two NBA Finals games hosted by the Warriors in their 1974\u201375 championship season were also held at the Cow Palace because of other events at the Oakland Coliseum. On and off between 1975 and 1984, the San Jose/Golden Bay Earthquakes of the NASL played indoor soccer at the Cow Palace, including hosting the"}, "final_score": 0.9447852479785955, "relevance_score": 0.8164063773658039, "ground_score": 0.12837887061279163, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3421220726766478, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.18355210330907867, "[Relevant]": 0.8162217487206284}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.07851713779812176, "[Partially supported]": 0.09848255411037973, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8181673028117482}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00020766928635067587, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002805433041070858, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002399614042753811, "[Utility:4]": 0.004270067105733784, "[Utility:5]": 0.9588089507220199}], "n_tokens": 40}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on September 25, 1966, and on September 26, 1966.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Beatles played at the Cow Palace in San Francisco on September 25, 1966, and on September 26, 1966.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5732990928803483, "ctx": {"title": "Out There (tour)", "text": "San Francisco himself. McCartney performed in San Francisco last exactly a year and a day prior to the Candlestick Park date, at the Outside Lands Music and Arts Festival in 2013. This is a notable venue seeing as it held the final official Beatles concert in history. An announcement for a show at Dodger Stadium in Los Angeles came a day later. This will be another historic concert as the Beatles played their second to final official concert at the stadium in 1966, the day before the Candlestick Park farewell. It will be the first McCartney event in Southern California"}, "final_score": 1.5732990928803483, "relevance_score": 0.9563089359352176, "ground_score": 0.6169901569451306, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.36224282577341, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.043687158973413374, "[Relevant]": 0.9562234613913536}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.45210869168805623, "[Partially supported]": 0.3180981933579822, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.22034017877267673}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00011953488395715554, "[Utility:2]": 0.00015835799240286323, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004423944897316135, "[Utility:4]": 0.0017497041486858307, "[Utility:5]": 0.7843971303760572}], "n_tokens": 40}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where was the movie It filmed in 2017?", "short_answers": ["Port Hope, Oshawa, Ontario and Riverdale, Toronto"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "It also known as It Chapter One is a 2017 American supernatural horror film based on Stephen King's 1986 novel of the same name. Produced by New Line Cinema, KatzSmith Productions, Lin Pictures, Vertigo Entertainment, and distributed by Warner Bros. It is the first film in the \"It\" film series as well as being the second adaptation following Tommy Lee Wallace's 1990 miniseries. The film tells the story of seven children in Derry, Maine, who are terrorized by the eponymous being, only to face their own personal demons in the process. The film is also known as \"It: Part 1 \u2013 The Losers' Club\".", "question": "Where does the story of the movie It take place?", "short_answers": ["Derry, Maine"], "wikipage": "It (2017 film)"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "It (2017 film)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/It%20%282017%20film%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "This 2017 movie, It, was inspired by writer, Stephen King's, novel by the same name. The novel and movie tells the story of seven children in Derry, Maine, who are terrorized by an eponymous being. The movie was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, Ontario and Riverdale, Toronto."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "It, titled on-screen as It: Chapter One, is a 2017 American coming-of-age supernatural horror film based on Stephen King's 1986 novel of the same name.", "wikipage": "It (2017 film)"}], "long_answer": "\"It\", titled on-screen as \"It: Chapter One\", is a 2017 American coming-of-age supernatural horror film based on Stephen King's 1986 novel of the same name. The film tells the story of seven children in Derry, Maine, who are terrorized by the eponymous being, only to face their own personal demons in the process. \"It\" was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, Ontario and Riverdale, Toronto."}], "sample_id": "4802515840870502721", "question": "Where does the movie it take place 2017?", "docs": [{"id": "19485742", "title": "It (2017 film)", "text": "and Pennywise the Dancing Clown, respectively, with Jeremy Ray Taylor, Sophia Lillis, Finn Wolfhard, Chosen Jacobs, Jack Dylan Grazer, Wyatt Oleff, Nicholas Hamilton, and Jackson Robert Scott in supporting roles. Principal photography began in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto on June 27, 2016, and ended on September 21, 2016. Other Ontario locations included Port Hope and Oshawa. \"It\" premiered in Los Angeles on September 5, 2017, and was theatrically released in the United States by New Line Cinema and Warner Bros. Pictures on September 8, 2017. Upon release, the film set numerous box office records and grossed $700 million worldwide.", "score": 0.7890625, "summary": "The movie \"It\" (2017) was filmed in Toronto, specifically in the Riverdale neighborhood, as well as Port Hope and Oshawa. It premiered in Los Angeles on September 5, 2017 and was released in the United States on September 8, 2017.", "extraction": "The movie \"It\" was primarily filmed in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto, with additional filming locations in Port Hope and Oshawa, all in Ontario, Canada."}], "answer": "\"It\", titled on-screen as \"It: Chapter One\", is a 2017 American coming-of-age supernatural horror film based on Stephen King's 1986 novel of the same name. The film tells the story of seven children in Derry, Maine, who are terrorized by the eponymous being, only to face their own personal demons in the process. \"It\" was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, Ontario and Riverdale, Toronto.", "output": "The 2017 movie It was filmed in Port Hope and Oshawa, Ontario, and in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Port Hope and Oshawa, Ontario, and in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 430, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Port Hope and Oshawa, Ontario, and in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Port Hope and Oshawa, Ontario, and in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.982005176270866, "ctx": {"title": "It (2017 film)", "text": "and Pennywise the Dancing Clown, respectively, with Jeremy Ray Taylor, Sophia Lillis, Finn Wolfhard, Chosen Jacobs, Jack Dylan Grazer, Wyatt Oleff, Nicholas Hamilton, and Jackson Robert Scott in supporting roles. Principal photography began in the Riverdale neighborhood of Toronto on June 27, 2016, and ended on September 21, 2016. Other Ontario locations included Port Hope and Oshawa. \"It\" premiered in Los Angeles on September 5, 2017, and was theatrically released in the United States by New Line Cinema and Warner Bros. Pictures on September 8, 2017. Upon release, the film set numerous box office records and grossed $700 million worldwide."}, "final_score": 1.982005176270866, "relevance_score": 0.99952343869679, "ground_score": 0.9824817375740759, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2813639973312681, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0004765414634407681, "[Relevant]": 0.9994818274408206}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9683660027228497, "[Partially supported]": 0.027470426258330603, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003776264357018044}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016912932854281292, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003403188277009485, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014242117373911952, "[Utility:4]": 0.001309681368882951, "[Utility:5]": 0.5963796428674215}], "n_tokens": 34}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It is set in the summer of 1989 in Derry, Maine.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It is set in the summer of 1989 in Derry, Maine.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.952950849060524, "ctx": {"title": "It (2017 film)", "text": "It (2017 film) It (also known as It: Chapter One) is a 2017 American supernatural horror film directed by Andy Muschietti, based on the 1986 novel of the same name by Stephen King. The screenplay is by Chase Palmer, Cary Fukunaga and Gary Dauberman. The film is set in the summer of 1989 and tells the story of seven children in Derry, Maine, who are terrorized by the eponymous being, only to face their own personal demons in the process. The novel was previously adapted into a 1990 miniseries. The film stars Jaeden Lieberher and Bill Skarsg\u00e5rd as Bill Denbrough"}, "final_score": 1.952950849060524, "relevance_score": 0.9976032693369615, "ground_score": 0.9553475797235624, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.20896398018427637, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0023965484118001145, "[Relevant]": 0.9975274099865514}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.929290318099133, "[Partially supported]": 0.05002617159218498, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.01959053027659868}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00031546332575940674, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003717064599918502, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001516568736071014, "[Utility:4]": 0.001436072099247605, "[Utility:5]": 0.7154053015164239}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, and Toronto, Ontario, Canada.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The 2019 movie It: Chapter Two was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, and Toronto, Ontario, Canada.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, and Toronto, Ontario, Canada.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The 2019 movie It: Chapter Two was filmed in Port Hope, Oshawa, and Toronto, Ontario, Canada.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9265541875530543, "ctx": {"title": "It \u2013 Chapter Two", "text": "2018, it was revealed that Javier Botet would appear in the film in an undisclosed role. Principal photography on the film began on June 19, 2018, at Pinewood Toronto Studios and on locations in and around Port Hope, Oshawa and Toronto, Ontario, and wrapped on October 30, 2018. Warner Bros. Pictures plans to release \"It: Chapter Two\" in theaters on September 6, 2019. On December 9, 2018, during the CCXP 2018, the first teaser and footage of the film were divulged. It \u2013 Chapter Two It: Chapter Two is an upcoming American supernatural horror film and the sequel to the"}, "final_score": 1.9265541875530543, "relevance_score": 0.9969005843657891, "ground_score": 0.9296536031872651, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2605707263423161, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003099155471236806, "[Relevant]": 0.9968169051657607}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8773104198583717, "[Partially supported]": 0.10235256332067699, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.019081816319867738}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00015964765725435338, "[Utility:2]": 0.00023456550075324866, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012903434636218973, "[Utility:4]": 0.000899181468266556, "[Utility:5]": 0.6620175663194763}], "n_tokens": 60}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Oshawa, Ontario, Canada.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 movie It was filmed in Oshawa, Ontario, Canada.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9085824730503391, "ctx": {"title": "It (2017 film)", "text": "as the next filming location, and on July 20, 2016, filming notices were sent out to homes in the area of Eulalie Avenue and James Street, near downtown Oshawa, advising residents that filming would take place in the area from August 5 to August 8, 2016. On July 29, 2016, it was announced the crew had worked on the formerly vacant lot at the dead end of James Street constructing the set, in the form of a dilapidated old house. It was also remarked that the structure is a facade built around scaffolding that would be used for exterior shots."}, "final_score": 1.9085824730503391, "relevance_score": 0.991748399487435, "ground_score": 0.9168340735629041, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.28276858036874347, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008250865368297134, "[Relevant]": 0.9916600435194083}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8791763023831957, "[Partially supported]": 0.07049543136671446, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.047699594862490195}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00047354689592295446, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006056757026834292, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002307884351198785, "[Utility:4]": 0.0035820425046983384, "[Utility:5]": 0.6668037594905942}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 film It was released in the United States on September 8, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2017 film It was released in the United States on September 8, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.613762694667233, "ctx": {"title": "It (2017 film)", "text": "Denmark and the Netherlands on September 7, 2017, and Norway and Finland on September 8, 2017. On March 7, 2017, the alternate title of the film was announced by Stephen King as \"Part 1 \u2013 The Losers' Club\". In addition to the conventional 2D format, \"It\" was also released across 615 IMAX screens globally, including 389 domestically. The film was released on digital download on December 19, 2017, and was released on 4K, Blu-ray and DVD on January 9, 2018. On January 31, 2016, Muschietti, on his Instagram, posted a sketch that was thought to be the precursor to Pennywise's"}, "final_score": 1.613762694667233, "relevance_score": 0.9851575847131129, "ground_score": 0.6286051099541201, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2822147766742875, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.014840599263349286, "[Relevant]": 0.9850370470965708}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5154196234963225, "[Partially supported]": 0.22167923095959846, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2591692780475364}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000193197812353376, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001314923892466547, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015768345885077903, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006984553998462343, "[Utility:5]": 0.7280245256740993}], "n_tokens": 29}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "British composer Malcolm Arnold recalled that he had \"ten days to write around forty-five minutes worth of music\" - much less time than he was used to. He described the music for \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\" as the \"worst job I ever had in my life\" from the point of view of time. Despite this, he won an Oscar and a Grammy.", "question": "Who wrote and composed the music for bridge on the river kwai?", "short_answers": ["Sir Malcolm Henry Arnold", "Arnold", "Malcolm Arnold"], "wikipage": "The Bridge on the River Kwai"}, {"context": "The march was written in 1914 by Kenneth J. Alford, a pseudonym of British Bandmaster Frederick J. Ricketts. The Colonel Bogey strain was accompanied by a counter-melody using the same chord progressions, then continued with film composer Malcolm Arnold's own composition, \"The River Kwai March,\" played by the off-screen orchestra taking over from the whistlers, though Arnold's march was not heard in completion on the soundtrack. Mitch Miller had a hit with a recording of both marches.", "question": "Who wrote the march for the Colonol Bogey song in 1914 that Arnold use for his own composition in bridge on the river kwai?", "short_answers": ["Ricketts", "Alford", "Frederick Joseph Ricketts", "Kenneth J. Alford"], "wikipage": "The Bridge on the River Kwai"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Bridge on the River Kwai", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Bridge%20on%20the%20River%20Kwai"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Bridge on the River Kwai is a 1957 epic war film directed by David Lean and based on the 1952 novel written by Pierre Boulle. ", "wikipage": "The Bridge on the River Kwai"}], "long_answer": "\"The Bridge on the River Kwai\" is a 1957 epic war film directed by David Lean and based on the 1952 novel written by Pierre Boulle. Malcolm Arnold is the British composer who wrote the music for the film, and recalled that he had \"ten days to write around forty-five minutes worth of music\" - much less time than he was used to. Despite this, he won an Oscar and a Grammy. The soundtrack Arnold composed was musically based on a march that was written in 1914 by Kenneth J. Alford."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Bridge on the River Kwai is a 1957 epic war film directed by David Lean and based on the 1952 novel written by Pierre Boulle.", "wikipage": "The Bridge on the River Kwai"}, {"content": "A memorable feature of the film is the tune that is whistled by the POWs\u2014the first strain of the march \"Colonel Bogey\"\u2014when they enter the camp.[25]", "wikipage": "The Bridge on the River Kwai Music and soundtrack"}], "long_answer": "The Bridge on the River Kwai is a 1957 epic war film directed by David Lean and based on the 1952 novel written by Pierre Boulle. British composer Malcolm Arnold recalled that he had \"ten days to write around forty-five minutes worth of music\". Despite this, he won an Oscar and a Grammy. A memorable feature of the film is the tune that is whistled by the POWs, the first strain of the march \"Colonel Bogey\", when they enter the camp. The march was written in 1914 by Kenneth J. Alford, a pseudonym of British Bandmaster Frederick J. Ricketts."}], "sample_id": "-7714118845603873270", "question": "Who wrote the music for bridge on the river kwai?", "docs": [{"id": "8897549", "title": "No Love for Johnnie", "text": "much. I loved working with Peter Finch. He was drunk some of the time, and not always very easy, but I was just very fond of him. Ralph and I both knew how to work with him.\" Music was by Malcolm Arnold, the score containing themes similar to those from \"Whistle Down the Wind\", which he also scored the same year. Arnold produced music scores for more than a hundred films, among these \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\" (1957), for which he won an Oscar. There is a brief appearance of a young Oliver Reed as a bohemian party-goer.", "score": 0.75390625, "summary": "The music for \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\" was written by Malcolm Arnold, according to the document.", "extraction": "Malcolm Arnold wrote the music for \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\"."}], "answer": "The Bridge on the River Kwai is a 1957 epic war film directed by David Lean and based on the 1952 novel written by Pierre Boulle. British composer Malcolm Arnold recalled that he had \"ten days to write around forty-five minutes worth of music\". Despite this, he won an Oscar and a Grammy. A memorable feature of the film is the tune that is whistled by the POWs, the first strain of the march \"Colonel Bogey\", when they enter the camp. The march was written in 1914 by Kenneth J. Alford, a pseudonym of British Bandmaster Frederick J. Ricketts.", "output": "The music for the bridge on the River Kwai was composed by Malcolm Arnold [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The music for the bridge on the River Kwai was composed by Malcolm Arnold.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 431, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The River Kwai March, also known as The Bridge on the River Kwai March, is a march composed by Malcolm Arnold in 1957.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was written as an orchestral counter-march to the Colonel Bogey March, which is whistled by the soldiers entering the prisoner camp in the film The Bridge on the River Kwai and again near the end of the film when the bridge is formally dedicated.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The River Kwai March, also known as The Bridge on the River Kwai March, is a march composed by Malcolm Arnold in 1957.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was written as an orchestral counter-march to the Colonel Bogey March, which is whistled by the soldiers entering the prisoner camp in the film The Bridge on the River Kwai and again near the end of the film when the bridge is formally dedicated.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9922883832358647, "ctx": {"title": "The River Kwai March", "text": "The River Kwai March \"The \"River Kwai\" March\" is a march composed by Malcolm Arnold in 1957. It was written as an orchestral counter-march to the \"Colonel Bogey March\", which is whistled by the soldiers entering the prisoner camp in the film \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\" and again near the end of the film when the bridge is formally dedicated. The Arnold march re-appears (without the \"Colonel Bogey March\") several times in the film and is repeated at the finale. The two marches have been recorded together by Mitch Miller as \"March from the River Kwai - Colonel"}, "final_score": 1.9922883832358647, "relevance_score": 0.999566067580006, "ground_score": 0.9927223156558587, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.09650552598513945, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0004339224238047343, "[Relevant]": 0.9995430412947702}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9876713685064723, "[Partially supported]": 0.008955115054235675, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0027959232763040075}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00013095014819946064, "[Utility:2]": 0.00010919945557032365, "[Utility:3]": 5.198673436542946e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011136984611790257, "[Utility:5]": 0.7553776723920848}], "n_tokens": 98}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The music for the bridge on the River Kwai was written by George Martin.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The music for the bridge on the River Kwai was written by George Martin.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9502052706281425, "ctx": {"title": "George Martin", "text": "River Kwai\", being based on the 1957 \"Goon Show\" episode \"An African Incident.\" It was intended to have the same name as the film, but shortly before its release, the film company threatened legal action if the name was used. Martin edited out the 'K' every time the word \"Kwai\" was spoken, with \"Bridge on the River Wye\" being the result. The River Wye is a river that runs through England and Wales. The album featured Milligan, Sellers, Jonathan Miller, and Peter Cook, playing various characters. Other comedians Martin worked with included Bernard Cribbins, Charlie Drake, Terry Scott, Bruce Forsyth,"}, "final_score": 1.9502052706281425, "relevance_score": 0.9934611746325442, "ground_score": 0.9567440959955983, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3590383400525233, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006538285404503673, "[Relevant]": 0.993379136621344}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9281205668936936, "[Partially supported]": 0.054446808539280536, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.015969197890943462}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0006814215795215111, "[Utility:2]": 9.874497822931965e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018340213007348794, "[Utility:4]": 0.0023783950111454307, "[Utility:5]": 0.8702403617956485}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The music for the bridge on the River Kwai was composed by Malcolm Arnold.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The music for the bridge on the River Kwai was composed by Malcolm Arnold.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9961319422171897, "ctx": {"title": "No Love for Johnnie", "text": "much. I loved working with Peter Finch. He was drunk some of the time, and not always very easy, but I was just very fond of him. Ralph and I both knew how to work with him.\" Music was by Malcolm Arnold, the score containing themes similar to those from \"Whistle Down the Wind\", which he also scored the same year. Arnold produced music scores for more than a hundred films, among these \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\" (1957), for which he won an Oscar. There is a brief appearance of a young Oliver Reed as a bohemian party-goer."}, "final_score": 1.9961319422171897, "relevance_score": 0.9995452520375286, "ground_score": 0.996586690179661, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.22881669546135844, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0004547433262911279, "[Relevant]": 0.999535061619369}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9935743332700936, "[Partially supported]": 0.005550060993070502, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.000637466480742329}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00040782409126299016, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001538883030533913, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020586953839540393, "[Utility:4]": 0.002393153383434876, "[Utility:5]": 0.8553563523558638}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The River Kwai March, also known as The Bridge on the River Kwai March, is a march composed by Malcolm Arnold in 1957.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was written as an orchestral counter-march to the \"Colonel Bogey March\", which is whistled by the soldiers entering the prisoner camp in the film \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\".[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The River Kwai March, also known as The Bridge on the River Kwai March, is a march composed by Malcolm Arnold in 1957.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was written as an orchestral counter-march to the \"Colonel Bogey March\", which is whistled by the soldiers entering the prisoner camp in the film \"The Bridge on the River Kwai\".[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9892763650239487, "ctx": {"title": "The River Kwai March", "text": "Bogey\". Due to this, the \"Colonel Bogey March\" is often mis-credited as \"River Kwai March\". The Arnold march was published by Shapiro, Bernstein & Co. in a piano arrangement by Robert C. Haring. It also forms part of the orchestral concert suite made of the Arnold film score by Christopher Palmer published by Novello & Co. The River Kwai March \"The \"River Kwai\" March\" is a march composed by Malcolm Arnold in 1957. It was written as an orchestral counter-march to the \"Colonel Bogey March\", which is whistled by the soldiers entering the prisoner camp in the film \"The Bridge"}, "final_score": 1.9892763650239487, "relevance_score": 0.9990818100807987, "ground_score": 0.9901945549431499, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11224202149651058, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000918180231851439, "[Relevant]": 0.9990712692820938}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9831246023095682, "[Partially supported]": 0.012669195642690506, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003463565352189389}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002345932410810454, "[Utility:2]": 0.00010248619692951253, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016377247637610557, "[Utility:4]": 0.0014147783085613375, "[Utility:5]": 0.7893357431740239}], "n_tokens": 87}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Bridge on the River Kwai is a 1957 epic war film based on the 1954 novel by Pierre Boulle.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The score was composed by Malcolm Arnold, with the theme song, \"Colonel Bogey March\", written by Malcolm Arnold and Stanley Morison.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Bridge on the River Kwai is a 1957 epic war film based on the 1954 novel by Pierre Boulle.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The score was composed by Malcolm Arnold, with the theme song, \"Colonel Bogey March\", written by Malcolm Arnold and Stanley Morison.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.10264760000746, "ctx": {"title": "The Bridge over the River Kwai", "text": "name was used. Producer George Martin edited out the \"K\" every time the word \"Kwai\" was spoken. The Bridge over the River Kwai The Bridge over the River Kwai () is a novel by the French novelist Pierre Boulle, published in French in 1952 and English translation by Xan Fielding in 1954. The story is fictional but uses the construction of the Burma Railway, in 1942\u20131943, as its historical setting, and is partly based on Pierre Boulle's own life experience working in Malaysia rubber plantations and later working for allied forces in Singapore and Indochina during World War II. The"}, "final_score": 1.10264760000746, "relevance_score": 0.9773708010380677, "ground_score": 0.1252767989693924, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4030374558682612, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.022627792660224244, "[Relevant]": 0.9773100618917451}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.0721432699717547, "[Partially supported]": 0.10334046293585906, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8128357520908362}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001465274734435402, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011773813257750892, "[Utility:3]": 6.426365259242155e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0025569948032142693, "[Utility:5]": 0.9319417432522888}], "n_tokens": 72}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Bourbon Street (, ) is a historic street in the heart of the French Quarter of New Orleans. Extending thirteen blocks from Canal Street to Esplanade Avenue, Bourbon Street is famous for its many bars and strip clubs. ", "question": "What's the famous street in new orleans known for bars and strip clubs?", "short_answers": ["Bourbon Street"], "wikipage": "Bourbon Street"}, {"context": "Canal Street () is a major thoroughfare in the city of New Orleans. Forming the upriver boundary of the city's oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter or Vieux Carr\u00e9, it served historically as the dividing line between the colonial-era (18th-century) city and the newer American Sector, today's Central Business District. ", "question": "What's the famous street in new orleans dividing 18th century city and newer business district?", "short_answers": ["Canal Street"], "wikipage": "Canal Street, New Orleans"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "New Orleans", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New%20Orleans"}, {"title": "Bourbon Street", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourbon%20Street"}, {"title": "List of streets of New Orleans", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20streets%20of%20New%20Orleans"}, {"title": "Canal Street, New Orleans", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canal%20Street%2C%20New%20Orleans"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "There are two historically famous streets in the city of New Orleans. Bourbon Street is a street in the heart of the French Quarter of New Orleans. Extending thirteen blocks from Canal Street to Esplanade Avenue, Bourbon Street is famous for its many bars and strip clubs. Canal Street is a major thoroughfare in the city of New Orleans. Forming the upriver boundary of the city's oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter or Vieux Carr\u00e9, it served historically as the dividing line between the colonial-era (18th-century) city and the newer American Sector, today's Central Business District."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "There are several famous streets in New Orleans, including Canal Street and Bourbon Street. Canal Street divides the 18th-century city and the newer business district, the Central Business District. Bourbon Street is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' French Quarter known for its many bars and strip clubs."}], "sample_id": "-7368635515365513931", "question": "What's the famous street in new orleans?", "docs": [{"id": "3712557", "title": "Bourbon Street", "text": "Bourbon Street Bourbon Street () is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter, in New Orleans, La. Extending 13 blocks from Canal to Esplanade Avenue, Bourbon Street is famous for its many bars and strip clubs. With 17.74 million visitors in 2017 alone, New Orleans depends on Bourbon Street as a main tourist attraction. Tourist numbers have been growing yearly after the devastation of Hurricane Katrina in 2005, and the city has successfully rebuilt its tourist base. For millions of visitors each year, Bourbon Street provides a rich insight into New Orleans' past.", "score": 0.81396484375, "summary": "Bourbon Street is the famous street in New Orleans' French Quarter known for its bars and strip clubs.", "extraction": "\"Bourbon Street is the famous street in New Orleans.\""}], "answer": "There are several famous streets in New Orleans, including Canal Street and Bourbon Street. Canal Street divides the 18th-century city and the newer business district, the Central Business District. Bourbon Street is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' French Quarter known for its many bars and strip clubs.", "output": "Bourbon Street is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter [1]. It is famous for its many bars and strip clubs [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Bourbon Street is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is famous for its many bars and strip clubs.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 432, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Bourbon Street is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is famous for its many bars and strip clubs.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Bourbon Street is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is famous for its many bars and strip clubs.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.993249833638621, "ctx": {"title": "Bourbon Street", "text": "Bourbon Street Bourbon Street () is a historic street in the heart of New Orleans' oldest neighborhood, the French Quarter, in New Orleans, La. Extending 13 blocks from Canal to Esplanade Avenue, Bourbon Street is famous for its many bars and strip clubs. With 17.74 million visitors in 2017 alone, New Orleans depends on Bourbon Street as a main tourist attraction. Tourist numbers have been growing yearly after the devastation of Hurricane Katrina in 2005, and the city has successfully rebuilt its tourist base. For millions of visitors each year, Bourbon Street provides a rich insight into New Orleans' past."}, "final_score": 1.993249833638621, "relevance_score": 0.9997603132001576, "ground_score": 0.9934895204384635, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.15244866237956628, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000239682147116112, "[Relevant]": 0.9997409061609445}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9886042972900037, "[Partially supported]": 0.008620184783506875, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0021966182553055684}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000880429712469013, "[Utility:2]": 0.0009976575667183327, "[Utility:3]": 0.0008566816230755306, "[Utility:4]": 0.00396125569195057, "[Utility:5]": 0.7548818506089101}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]St. Charles Avenue is a thoroughfare in New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S. and the home of the St. Charles Streetcar Line.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is also famous for the dozens of mansions that adorn the tree-lined boulevard for much of the uptown section of the boulevard.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]St. Charles Avenue is a thoroughfare in New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S. and the home of the St. Charles Streetcar Line.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is also famous for the dozens of mansions that adorn the tree-lined boulevard for much of the uptown section of the boulevard.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9856928119255886, "ctx": {"title": "St. Charles Avenue", "text": "St. Charles Avenue St. Charles Avenue is a thoroughfare in New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S. and the home of the St. Charles Streetcar Line. It is also famous for the dozens of mansions that adorn the tree-lined boulevard for much of the uptown section of the boulevard. The Southern live oak trees, plentiful in the historic Garden District, were planted during the early twentieth century. Similar additions were made on other major New Orleans streets, such as Carrollton Avenue, Napoleon Avenue, and part of Canal Street, becoming one of the city's most memorable features. St. Charles Avenue is one of the"}, "final_score": 1.9856928119255886, "relevance_score": 0.9987056318388872, "ground_score": 0.9869871800867014, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.05700961982743438, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001294350904983611, "[Relevant]": 0.9986923173941215}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.975854584616658, "[Partially supported]": 0.020733506433544626, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0026359695366797707}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014868073648850437, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002470556954672424, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023992273258207828, "[Utility:4]": 0.002761905996824294, "[Utility:5]": 0.6081686390370127}], "n_tokens": 72}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Royal Street is a street in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is one of the original streets of the city, dating from the early 18th century, and is known today for its antique shops, art galleries, and hotels.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Royal Street is a street in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is one of the original streets of the city, dating from the early 18th century, and is known today for its antique shops, art galleries, and hotels.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9884575580508947, "ctx": {"title": "Royal Street, New Orleans", "text": "Royal Street, New Orleans Royal Street (; ) is a street in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S. It is one of the original streets of the city, dating from the early 18th century, and is known today for its antique shops, art galleries, and hotels. The street starts at Canal Street (above Canal Street, the corresponding street is uptown New Orleans' St. Charles Avenue). Royal runs down through the French Quarter, Faubourg Marigny, Bywater, and Lower Ninth Ward neighborhoods to the Jackson Barracks. The Industrial Canal forms a gap in the street between the Bywater and Lower"}, "final_score": 1.9884575580508947, "relevance_score": 0.9991169543166016, "ground_score": 0.9893406037342931, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.0824459202574332, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0008830120363729216, "[Relevant]": 0.9990788845834093}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8907283573384387, "[Partially supported]": 0.011568539463538103, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0038749753382107646}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001533519540245377, "[Utility:2]": 0.000304381700553191, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003177456619994594, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018718625581855522, "[Utility:5]": 0.5190155768856858}], "n_tokens": 67}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Frenchmen Street is in the Faubourg Marigny neighborhood of New Orleans, Louisiana.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is best known for the three-block section in the Marigny neighborhood which is home to some of the city's popular live-music venues including Snug Harbor, the Spotted Cat, and the Maison in addition to restaurants, bars, bookstores, coffee shops, and other businesses.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Frenchmen Street is in the Faubourg Marigny neighborhood of New Orleans, Louisiana.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is best known for the three-block section in the Marigny neighborhood which is home to some of the city's popular live-music venues including Snug Harbor, the Spotted Cat, and the Maison in addition to restaurants, bars, bookstores, coffee shops, and other businesses.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9323497754274261, "ctx": {"title": "Frenchmen Street", "text": "steeply-pitched, side-gabled roof, often with multiple dormers. It usually has a stucco or brick exterior. Frenchmen Street Frenchmen Street is in the Faubourg Marigny neighborhood of New Orleans, Louisiana. It is best known for the three-block section in the Marigny neighborhood which is home to some of the city's popular live-music venues including Snug Harbor, the Spotted Cat, and the Maison in addition to restaurants, bars, bookstores, coffee shops, and other businesses. Frenchmen Street runs from the intersection with Esplanade Avenue just inland from the Mississippi, back to the Gentilly neighborhood. The oldest and best-known section of Frenchmen Street is"}, "final_score": 1.9323497754274261, "relevance_score": 0.9990526910310255, "ground_score": 0.9332970843964008, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.04119914382243593, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0009472825852404272, "[Relevant]": 0.9990248661699168}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8915978303267105, "[Partially supported]": 0.07205214879728246, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.030271378002373558}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001556454286661498, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004547947199232355, "[Utility:3]": 0.00044080215741396057, "[Utility:4]": 0.0028520224470604517, "[Utility:5]": 0.7172125301925854}], "n_tokens": 91}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Royal Street is a street in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is one of the original streets of the city, dating from the early 18th century, and is known today for its antique shops, art galleries, and hotels.[Continue to Use Evidence]The street starts at Canal Street (above Canal Street, the corresponding street is uptown New Orleans' St. Charles Avenue).[Continue to Use Evidence]Royal runs down through the French Quarter, Faubourg Marigny, Bywater, and Lower Ninth Ward neighborhoods to the Jackson Barracks.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Royal Street is a street in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is one of the original streets of the city, dating from the early 18th century, and is known today for its antique shops, art galleries, and hotels.[Continue to Use Evidence]The street starts at Canal Street (above Canal Street, the corresponding street is uptown New Orleans' St. Charles Avenue).[Continue to Use Evidence]Royal runs down through the French Quarter, Faubourg Marigny, Bywater, and Lower Ninth Ward neighborhoods to the Jackson Barracks.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9907286983663932, "ctx": {"title": "Royal Street, New Orleans", "text": "ground next to a curve in the Mississippi River. Royal Street, New Orleans Royal Street (; ) is a street in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S. It is one of the original streets of the city, dating from the early 18th century, and is known today for its antique shops, art galleries, and hotels. The street starts at Canal Street (above Canal Street, the corresponding street is uptown New Orleans' St. Charles Avenue). Royal runs down through the French Quarter, Faubourg Marigny, Bywater, and Lower Ninth Ward neighborhoods to the Jackson Barracks. The Industrial Canal forms a"}, "final_score": 1.9907286983663932, "relevance_score": 0.9991832617087468, "ground_score": 0.9915454366576464, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.06687261087852281, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000816705595416057, "[Relevant]": 0.9991432621965988}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9027599585284278, "[Partially supported]": 0.011100820698755006, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.002194436497056553}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.149975083723303e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00024201325543645412, "[Utility:3]": 9.626622199625512e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.001298129938800005, "[Utility:5]": 0.6646136013597299}], "n_tokens": 128}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Nine matches were contested at the event, including three on the Kickoff pre-show. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura. Other prominent matches included Brock Lesnar retaining the Universal Championship in a triple threat match against Braun Strowman and Kane, and AJ Styles retained the WWE Championship against Kevin Owens and Sami Zayn in a handicap match. The event was also notable for the surprise debut of former UFC star Ronda Rousey following the women's match, officially confirming that she had signed full-time with WWE.", "question": "Who won the Women's 2018 royal rumble?", "short_answers": ["Asuka"], "wikipage": "Royal Rumble (2018)"}, {"context": "Nine matches were contested at the event, including three on the Kickoff pre-show. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura. Other prominent matches included Brock Lesnar retaining the Universal Championship in a triple threat match against Braun Strowman and Kane, and AJ Styles retained the WWE Championship against Kevin Owens and Sami Zayn in a handicap match. The event was also notable for the surprise debut of former UFC star Ronda Rousey following the women's match, officially confirming that she had signed full-time with WWE.", "question": "Who won the Men's 2018 royal rumble?", "short_answers": ["Shinsuke Nakamura"], "wikipage": "Royal Rumble (2018)"}, {"context": "Nine matches were contested at the event, including three on the Kickoff pre-show. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura. Other prominent matches included Brock Lesnar retaining the Universal Championship in a triple threat match against Braun Strowman and Kane, and AJ Styles retained the WWE Championship against Kevin Owens and Sami Zayn in a handicap match. The event was also notable for the surprise debut of former UFC star Ronda Rousey following the women's match, officially confirming that she had signed full-time with WWE.", "question": "Who won the women's Royal Rumble match in 2018?", "short_answers": ["Asuka"], "wikipage": "Royal Rumble (2018)"}, {"context": "Nine matches were contested at the event, including three on the Kickoff pre-show. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura. Other prominent matches included Brock Lesnar retaining the Universal Championship in a triple threat match against Braun Strowman and Kane, and AJ Styles retained the WWE Championship against Kevin Owens and Sami Zayn in a handicap match. The event was also notable for the surprise debut of former UFC star Ronda Rousey following the women's match, officially confirming that she had signed full-time with WWE.", "question": "Who won the men's Royal Rumble match in 2018?", "short_answers": ["Shinsuke Nakamura"], "wikipage": "Royal Rumble (2018)"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Royal Rumble (2018)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal%20Rumble%20%282018%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The 2018 Royal Rumble was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE. Nine matches were contested at the event, including three on the Kickoff pre-show. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The 2018 Royal Rumble was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE featuring wrestlers primarily from their Raw and SmackDown brand divisions, as well as surprise appearances from competitors on the NXT brand.", "wikipage": "Royal Rumble (2018)"}], "long_answer": "The 2018 Royal Rumble was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE featuring wrestlers primarily from their Raw and SmackDown brand divisions, as well as surprise appearances from competitors on the NXT brand. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura."}], "sample_id": "8528837736505993111", "question": "Who is the winner of 2018 royal rumble?", "docs": [{"id": "20609123", "title": "WWE Greatest Royal Rumble", "text": "the main event, Braun Strowman won the Greatest Royal Rumble match. On the undercard, Brock Lesnar retained the Universal Championship against Roman Reigns in a steel cage match, and the WWE Championship match between AJ Styles and Shinsuke Nakamura resulted in a double countout, The Undertaker defeated Rusev in a casket match, and John Cena defeated Triple H in the opening match. Since 1988, the Royal Rumble has been an annual event held by WWE and is typically shown on pay-per-view. The event is highlighted by the Royal Rumble match, a battle royal whose participants enter at timed intervals. The", "score": 0.748046875, "summary": "Braun Strowman won the Greatest Royal Rumble match in 2018.", "extraction": "Braun Strowman is the winner of 2018 Royal Rumble."}], "answer": "The 2018 Royal Rumble was a professional wrestling pay-per-view (PPV) event and WWE Network event produced by WWE featuring wrestlers primarily from their Raw and SmackDown brand divisions, as well as surprise appearances from competitors on the NXT brand. In the main event, Asuka won the first-ever women's Royal Rumble match, which was also the second women's match to main event a WWE pay-per-view, and the first to main event one of WWE's \"Big Four\" pay-per-views. The men's Royal Rumble match was won by Shinsuke Nakamura.", "output": "Braun Strowman won the 2018 Royal Rumble [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Braun Strowman won the 2018 Royal Rumble.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 433, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2018 Royal Rumble was won by Shinsuke