"parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Jason Sudeikis plays the role of Ernie Krinklesac on the show.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Jason Sudeikis plays the role of Ernie Krinklesac on the show.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8808226201746705, "ctx": {"title": "Glenn Howerton", "text": "has written numerous episodes. He planned to be executive producing on the comedy show \"Boldly Going Nowhere\" with his \"It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia\" co-creators, before the show was delayed. Howerton had a recurring voice role on \"The Cleveland Show\" as Ernie Krinklesac, as well as recurring roles on the Fox comedy series \"The Mindy Project\" and the FX dark comedy series \"Fargo\". He starred in the 2013 comedy film \"Coffee Town\". He also had a bit part as the gun running Dominic, in the recently released Netflix Original \"Officer Downe\", starring Kim Coates. On September 8, 2009, Howerton married"}, "final_score": 1.8808226201746705, "relevance_score": 0.9862576820791183, "ground_score": 0.894564938095552, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2840895350233647, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.013739893489421475, "[Relevant]": 0.9860836856568238}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8679141338525386, "[Partially supported]": 0.047457884770042986, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08136174352976448}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007349943855789331, "[Utility:2]": 0.000826376400592608, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015833398173325456, "[Utility:4]": 0.004262773827207664, "[Utility:5]": 0.9460201991021604}], "n_tokens": 24}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "\"The Greatest Showman\" held its premiere on December 8, 2017, aboard the RMS \"Queen Mary 2\", while it was docked in New York City. The film was then released in the United States on December 20, 2017.", "question": "When was the greatest showman released in the theatre on the RMS Queen Mary 2?", "short_answers": ["December 8, 2017"], "wikipage": "The Greatest Showman"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When was the greatest showman released to all US theaters?", "short_answers": ["December 20, 2017"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "This Is Me (Keala Settle song)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/This%20Is%20Me%20%28Keala%20Settle%20song%29"}, {"title": "The Greatest Showman", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Greatest%20Showman"}, {"title": "The Greatest Showman: Original Motion Picture Soundtrack ...", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Greatest%20Showman%3A%20Original%20Motion%20Picture%20Soundtrack"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Greatest Showman is a 2017 American musical drama film directed by Michael Gracey in his directorial debut, written by Jenny Bicks and Bill Condon and starring Hugh Jackman, Zac Efron, Michelle Williams, Rebecca Ferguson, and Zendaya. ", "wikipage": "The Greatest Showman"}], "long_answer": "The Greatest Showman is a 2017 American musical drama film directed by Michael Gracey in his directorial debut, written by Jenny Bicks and Bill Condon and starring Hugh Jackman, Zac Efron, Michelle Williams, Rebecca Ferguson, and Zendaya. The film held its premiere on December 8, 2017, aboard the RMS \"Queen Mary 2\", while it was docked in New York City. The film was then released in the United States on December 20, 2017."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Greatest Showman is a 2017 American musical drama film directed by Michael Gracey in his directorial debut, written by Jenny Bicks and Bill Condon and starring Hugh Jackman, Zac Efron, Michelle Williams, Rebecca Ferguson, and Zendaya.", "wikipage": "The Greatest Showman"}, {"content": "RMS Queen Mary 2 (also referred to as the QM2) is a British transatlantic ocean liner.", "wikipage": "Queen Mary 2"}, {"content": "The film had a limited IMAX release on 2 February 2018.", "wikipage": "The Greatest Showman Release"}], "long_answer": "The 2017 musical drama The Greatest Showman was released to all US theaters on December 20, 2017, but the film had its premiere on December 8, 2017, when it was released in the theatre on the RMS Queen Mary 2, a transatlantic ocean liner, while it was docked in New York City. The film also had a limited IMAX release on February 2, 2018."}], "sample_id": "-674357830916687553", "question": "When was the greatest showman released in theaters?", "docs": [{"id": "18411194", "title": "The Greatest Showman", "text": "The Greatest Showman The Greatest Showman is a 2017 American musical film directed by Michael Gracey in his directorial debut, written by Jenny Bicks and Bill Condon and starring Hugh Jackman, Zac Efron, Michelle Williams, Rebecca Ferguson, and Zendaya. The film is inspired by the story of P. T. Barnum's creation of the Barnum & Bailey Circus and the lives of its star attractions. Principal photography began in New York City in November 2016. The film premiered on December 8, 2017, aboard the RMS \"Queen Mary 2\". It was released in the United States on December 20, 2017, by 20th", "score": 0.7470703125, "summary": "The Greatest Showman was released in theaters in the United States on December 20, 2017.", "extraction": "The Greatest Showman was released in the United States on December 20, 2017."}], "answer": "The 2017 musical drama The Greatest Showman was released to all US theaters on December 20, 2017, but the film had its premiere on December 8, 2017, when it was released in the theatre on the RMS Queen Mary 2, a transatlantic ocean liner, while it was docked in New York City. The film also had a limited IMAX release on February 2, 2018.", "output": "The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 554, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9218190481857076, "ctx": {"title": "The Greatest Showman", "text": "copies, 186,000 downloads and 186,000 streaming. On November 16, 2018. 20th Century Fox released a cover album entitled \"The Greatest Showman: Reimagined\", featuring a variety of artists. \"The Greatest Showman\" held its premiere on December 8, 2017, aboard the RMS \"Queen Mary 2\", while it was docked in New York City. The film was then released in the United States on December 20, 2017. Like Disney's live-action \"Beauty and the Beast\", a sing-along version of the film was released in the United Kingdom on February 23, 2018. The film had a limited IMAX release on February 2. On June 28,"}, "final_score": 1.9218190481857076, "relevance_score": 0.996350012134764, "ground_score": 0.9254690360509437, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2226507105357926, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00364987839613522, "[Relevant]": 0.9963201299696963}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8999510981706755, "[Partially supported]": 0.04806974184917739, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05037665692720027}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018528956573277662, "[Utility:2]": 7.708947009317423e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002648971656638624, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006897801658258797, "[Utility:5]": 0.6780646704885255}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters in the United States on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters in the United States on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8101849876627483, "ctx": {"title": "The Greatest Showman", "text": "United Kingdom, the film was released first on digital download on April 27, 2018, while Blu-ray, 4K Ultra HD and DVD copies went on sale on May 14. These versions include the sing-along version, two hours of behind-the-scenes footage and music machine jukebox features. \"The Greatest Showman\" spent 219 days in release, closing on July 26, 2018, having grossed $174.3million in the United States and Canada, and $260.7million in other territories, for a worldwide total of $435million, against a production budget of $84million. It is the third highest grossing musical ever in North America and globally, respectively, and \"Deadline Hollywood\""}, "final_score": 1.8101849876627483, "relevance_score": 0.9899864279734899, "ground_score": 0.8201985596892584, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.26728910752590307, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.010013035039891122, "[Relevant]": 0.9899333390794018}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7450708242086316, "[Partially supported]": 0.14557141339495455, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.10650232116760128}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018718892757312844, "[Utility:2]": 9.357460566869442e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003246993459269147, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006508060606245149, "[Utility:5]": 0.6397525357551613}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.924898777578191, "ctx": {"title": "The Greatest Showman", "text": "The Greatest Showman The Greatest Showman is a 2017 American musical film directed by Michael Gracey in his directorial debut, written by Jenny Bicks and Bill Condon and starring Hugh Jackman, Zac Efron, Michelle Williams, Rebecca Ferguson, and Zendaya. The film is inspired by the story of P. T. Barnum's creation of the Barnum & Bailey Circus and the lives of its star attractions. Principal photography began in New York City in November 2016. The film premiered on December 8, 2017, aboard the RMS \"Queen Mary 2\". It was released in the United States on December 20, 2017, by 20th"}, "final_score": 1.924898777578191, "relevance_score": 0.9973269939336938, "ground_score": 0.927571783644497, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2132641970183613, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00267290942755056, "[Relevant]": 0.9972909369861203}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9043584138175289, "[Partially supported]": 0.04164164909901834, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05142057307125907}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00025815447802002625, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001277957335721583, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004093193498113324, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006696017667964959, "[Utility:5]": 0.7172971572225689}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The soundtrack was released on December 8, 2017, and the film was released in theaters on January 26, 2018.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The soundtrack was released on December 8, 2017, and the film was released in theaters on January 26, 2018.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5882306729570936, "ctx": {"title": "The Greatest Showman: Original Motion Picture Soundtrack", "text": "The Greatest Showman: Original Motion Picture Soundtrack The Greatest Showman: Original Motion Picture Soundtrack is the soundtrack album to the film of the same name. It was released in full on December 8, 2017 by Atlantic Records. The first pre-order release was on October 26, 2017 with two promotional singles: \"The Greatest Show\" and \"This Is Me\" and a third on November 17, 2017 \"Rewrite the Stars\". \"This Is Me\" was released on December 8, 2017 as the album's official lead single. In Australia, \"Rewrite the Stars\" was released to radio on July 20, 2018. Produced by Justin Paul, Benj"}, "final_score": 1.5882306729570936, "relevance_score": 0.9791955086454209, "ground_score": 0.6090351643116727, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.23366076939737468, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0208019943789291, "[Relevant]": 0.9790779846310173}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.42748488434519033, "[Partially supported]": 0.3599783639462747, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.20997352041283562}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 8.294410294314271e-05, "[Utility:2]": 5.2108244104138775e-05, "[Utility:3]": 7.883725563687958e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010303956899025924, "[Utility:5]": 0.9817316127265378}], "n_tokens": 40}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Greatest Showman was released in theaters on December 20, 2017.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4268637547131233, "ctx": {"title": "The Greatest Showman", "text": "estimated the film would turn a profit between $50\u2013100 million. In the United States and Canada, \"The Greatest Showman\" was released alongside \"\", and was projected to gross around $21million from 3,006 theaters over its first six days. It took in $2million on its first day and $2million on its second. Over the three day weekend, it grossed $9million (for a six-day total of $19million), finishing fourth at the box office, behind \"\", \"Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle\" and \"Pitch Perfect 3\". In its second weekend, the film grossed $15.5million, again finishing 4th at the box office. The weekend-to-weekend increase"}, "final_score": 1.4268637547131233, "relevance_score": 0.9782188283113179, "ground_score": 0.4486449264018055, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24082075959904614, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.021779569188435693, "[Relevant]": 0.9781468580823616}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.38683672693537174, "[Partially supported]": 0.11890385314615198, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.48900744278757374}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00019029835661979404, "[Utility:2]": 0.00015962253767215135, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016991730687041236, "[Utility:4]": 0.0016121305216119403, "[Utility:5]": 0.9462971324878982}], "n_tokens": 26}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who sings somebody's watching me?", "short_answers": ["Rockwell", "Kennedy William Gordy"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who sings the lyrics i always feels like somebody's watching me in a song released in 1997?", "short_answers": ["TRU", "The Real Untouchables"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "I Always Feel Like", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%20Always%20Feel%20Like"}, {"title": "Somebody's Watching Me", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somebody%27s%20Watching%20Me"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label in 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name.", "wikipage": "Somebody's Watching Me"}], "long_answer": "\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label in 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name. The unrelated song \"I Always Feel Like\" is the lead single released from TRU's fourth studio album, Tru 2 da Game."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Kennedy William Gordy (born March 15, 1964), better known by his stage name Rockwell, is an American musician, singer, and songwriter known for his 1984 hit single \"Somebody's Watching Me\".", "wikipage": "Rockwell (musician)"}, {"content": "\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label in 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name. Rockwell's debut single release, the song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals).", "wikipage": "Somebody's Watching Me"}, {"content": "TRU (an abbreviation of The Real Untouchables) was an American hip hop group from New Orleans, active from 1989 to 2005.", "wikipage": "TRU (band)"}, {"content": "\" I Always Feel Like\" is the lead single released from TRU's fourth studio album, Tru 2 da Game.", "wikipage": "I Always Feel Like"}, {"content": "The song incorporated the melody and contained re-sung elements of Rockwell's 1984 hit, \"Somebody's Watching Me\", with Mo B. Dick singing the hook from the original song.", "wikipage": "I Always Feel Like"}, {"content": "Tru 2 da Game is the fourth studio album released by New Orleans hip-hop group, TRU. It was released February 18, 1997 on No Limit Records and was produced by Beats By the Pound .", "wikipage": "Tru 2 da Game"}, {"content": "Raymond Emile Poole (born July 4, 1965) better known by his stage name Mo B. Dick is an American rapper, singer, and music producer.", "wikipage": "Mo B. Dick"}], "long_answer": "There were several songs including \"somebody's watching me,\" including a 1984 Motown song and a 1997 song incorporating parts of the Motown song. Kennedy William Gordy, better known by the stage name Rockwell, recorded the 1984 hit single \"Somebody's Watching Me,\" featuring guest vocals by Michael and Jermaine Jackson. TRU, an abbreviation of The Real Untouchables, a New Orleans hip-hop group, released a song in 1997 with the lyrics \"I always feel like somebody's watching me.\" In 1997, they released the single \"I Always Feel Like,\" which incorporated the melody and re-sung elements from the 1984 Rockwell song, with rapper and singer Mo B. Dick singing the hook from the original song."}], "sample_id": "-7436749524080055501", "question": "Who sings it always feels like somebody's watching me?", "docs": [{"id": "4648994", "title": "Somebody's Watching Me", "text": "Somebody's Watching Me \"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name. Rockwell's debut single release, the song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals). \"Somebody's Watching Me\" became a major commercial success internationally, topping the charts in Belgium, France and Spain. The song reached No. 6 in the United Kingdom, Rockwell's only Top 40 hit there. The song reached the Top 5 in Canada, Germany, the", "score": 0.81689453125, "summary": "Rockwell sings \"Somebody's Watching Me\" with guest vocals by Michael Jackson and Jermaine Jackson. The song was released on January 14, 1984, and was a major commercial success internationally, topping charts in Belgium, France, and Spain. It reached No. 6 in the UK and the Top 5 in Canada and Germany.", "extraction": "\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, featuring guest vocals by Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals)."}], "answer": "There were several songs including \"somebody's watching me,\" including a 1984 Motown song and a 1997 song incorporating parts of the Motown song. Kennedy William Gordy, better known by the stage name Rockwell, recorded the 1984 hit single \"Somebody's Watching Me,\" featuring guest vocals by Michael and Jermaine Jackson. TRU, an abbreviation of The Real Untouchables, a New Orleans hip-hop group, released a song in 1997 with the lyrics \"I always feel like somebody's watching me.\" In 1997, they released the single \"I Always Feel Like,\" which incorporated the melody and re-sung elements from the 1984 Rockwell song, with rapper and singer Mo B. Dick singing the hook from the original song.", "output": "\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name [1]. The song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals).[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 555, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Rockwell sings the song Always Feels Like Somebody's Watching Me.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Rockwell sings the song Always Feels Like Somebody's Watching Me.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9757990821978244, "ctx": {"title": "Somebody's Watching Me (album)", "text": "Somebody's Watching Me (album) Somebody's Watching Me is singer/songwriter Rockwell's debut album, released in 1984 on Motown. It featured the song, \"Somebody's Watching Me\" (with Michael Jackson on vocals in the chorus), as well as the top 40 hit \"Obscene Phone Caller\". After being kicked out of the house by his father, Motown founder Berry Gordy, Kennedy Gordy moved in with Ray Singleton, Gordy's ex-wife. While living there, the younger Gordy began working on some music. Seeing the youngster's potential, Singleton successfully lobbied to get Kennedy a staff writing job at Jobete. One night, Singleton overheard Kennedy working on the"}, "final_score": 1.9757990821978244, "relevance_score": 0.9980882306208885, "ground_score": 0.9777108515769359, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21745152529620496, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0019117047361952068, "[Relevant]": 0.9980544821286981}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9747053923486126, "[Partially supported]": 0.005661550503845963, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.019454390851551574}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004180886342155957, "[Utility:2]": 6.550833272907175e-05, "[Utility:3]": 6.563640372004766e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006130864499383699, "[Utility:5]": 0.8906936494155995}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals).[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals).[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.997138072180809, "ctx": {"title": "Somebody's Watching Me", "text": "Somebody's Watching Me \"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name. Rockwell's debut single release, the song features guest vocals by brothers Michael Jackson (in the chorus) and Jermaine Jackson (additional backing vocals). \"Somebody's Watching Me\" became a major commercial success internationally, topping the charts in Belgium, France and Spain. The song reached No. 6 in the United Kingdom, Rockwell's only Top 40 hit there. The song reached the Top 5 in Canada, Germany, the"}, "final_score": 1.997138072180809, "relevance_score": 0.9996512956791355, "ground_score": 0.9974867765016735, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.05596026415783748, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000348698219314007, "[Relevant]": 0.999633804003667}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9971685934923852, "[Partially supported]": 0.0005602035972773593, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.002233025751956247}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 4.288928284716171e-05, "[Utility:2]": 3.6045926180441095e-05, "[Utility:3]": 2.6013665019890725e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0001479542682409036, "[Utility:5]": 0.9014387728299603}], "n_tokens": 82}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Rockwell sings the song Always Feels Like Somebody's Watching Me.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Rockwell sings the song Always Feels Like Somebody's Watching Me.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9786173809246679, "ctx": {"title": "Somebody's Watching Me (album)", "text": "among the Gordy offspring. AllMusic's Rick A. Bueche called the record \"an impressive debut set with an emphasis on rock instrumentals.\" An uptempo version of \"Knife\" was released by another Motown artist, Monalisa Young. She also appears on this album as a background vocalist. Somebody's Watching Me (album) Somebody's Watching Me is singer/songwriter Rockwell's debut album, released in 1984 on Motown. It featured the song, \"Somebody's Watching Me\" (with Michael Jackson on vocals in the chorus), as well as the top 40 hit \"Obscene Phone Caller\". After being kicked out of the house by his father, Motown founder Berry Gordy,"}, "final_score": 1.9786173809246679, "relevance_score": 0.9981897889733766, "ground_score": 0.9804275919512913, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.20695320625414435, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0018101848962163293, "[Relevant]": 0.9981753800977848}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9773442219540737, "[Partially supported]": 0.005766275983393187, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.016685272798521555}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00045954742931231414, "[Utility:2]": 9.066716562817408e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010851438890945066, "[Utility:4]": 0.0007286399074549221, "[Utility:5]": 0.8707559762636159}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9954882730801047, "ctx": {"title": "Somebody's Watching Me", "text": "on the Swiss Hitparade, reaching No. 4. The mix was made on the 20th anniversary of the initial hit by DJ BoBo in November 1992. In 2006, Dutch dance group Beatfreakz recorded a pseudo-cover of the song which sampled the chorus but omitted the verses. This version was a Top 10 hit in Belgium, Finland, Ireland, New Zealand and the United Kingdom. Somebody's Watching Me \"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song recorded by American singer Rockwell, released by the Motown label on January 14, 1984, as the lead single from his debut studio album of the same name. Rockwell's debut"}, "final_score": 1.9954882730801047, "relevance_score": 0.9992327075509033, "ground_score": 0.9962555655292014, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.047553413446485136, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0007672769535662514, "[Relevant]": 0.9992125279689975}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9958996318016222, "[Partially supported]": 0.0006339858605215425, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0034272951812411305}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023170276370083583, "[Utility:2]": 7.362399446040416e-05, "[Utility:3]": 9.546279680104316e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0004857478850672183, "[Utility:5]": 0.5495964799305955}], "n_tokens": 55}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song by American singer Rockwell, released in 1984.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was written by Rockwell, Rodney \"AZ Fingaz\" Terry, and Michael J. Powell, and produced by AZ Fingaz.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"Somebody's Watching Me\" is a song by American singer Rockwell, released in 1984.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was written by Rockwell, Rodney \"AZ Fingaz\" Terry, and Michael J. Powell, and produced by AZ Fingaz.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.662835361103618, "ctx": {"title": "I Always Feel Like", "text": "I Always Feel Like \" I Always Feel Like\" is the lead single released from TRU's fourth studio album, \"Tru 2 da Game\". The song incorporated the melody and contained re-sung elements of Rockwell's 1984 hit, \"Somebody's Watching Me\", with Mo B. Dick singing the hook from the original song. Mo B. Dick also produced the song, along with fellow Beats By the Pound members KLC and Craig B. Master P, Silkk the Shocker and Mia X performed the song. Released less than a month before \"Tru 2 da Game\", \"I Always Feel Like\" became the group's most successful single,"}, "final_score": 1.662835361103618, "relevance_score": 0.9954615682310253, "ground_score": 0.6673737928725927, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.477661697673214, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0045377315749519954, "[Relevant]": 0.9953079873741429}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.628157436162887, "[Partially supported]": 0.07443888023780375, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.29441148250881016}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000195073845225146, "[Utility:2]": 0.00014411964250499984, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001167116432785066, "[Utility:4]": 0.0003457250497806146, "[Utility:5]": 0.5702596086781317}], "n_tokens": 65}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The Day of the Locust is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. The novel follows a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scene design and painting. While he works he plans an important painting to be called \"The Burning of Los Angeles,\" a portrayal of the chaotic and fiery holocaust which will destroy the city. While the cast of characters Tod befriends are a conglomerate of Hollywood stereotypes, his greater discovery is a part of society whose \"eyes filled with hatred,\" and \"had come to California to die.\" This undercurrent of society captures the despair of Americans who worked and saved their entire lives only to realize, too late, that the American dream was more elusive than they imagine. Their anger boils into rage, and the craze over the latest Hollywood premiere erupts violently into mob rule and absolute chaos.", "question": "In the book day of the locust where is tod hackett originally from?", "short_answers": ["Yale School of Fine Arts", "the Yale School of Fine Arts"], "wikipage": "The Day of the Locust"}, {"context": "The Day of the Locust is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. The novel follows a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scene design and painting. While he works he plans an important painting to be called \"The Burning of Los Angeles,\" a portrayal of the chaotic and fiery holocaust which will destroy the city. While the cast of characters Tod befriends are a conglomerate of Hollywood stereotypes, his greater discovery is a part of society whose \"eyes filled with hatred,\" and \"had come to California to die.\" This undercurrent of society captures the despair of Americans who worked and saved their entire lives only to realize, too late, that the American dream was more elusive than they imagine. Their anger boils into rage, and the craze over the latest Hollywood premiere erupts violently into mob rule and absolute chaos.", "question": "In the film day of the locust where is tod hackett originally from?", "short_answers": ["Yale", "Yale University"], "wikipage": "The Day of the Locust"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Day of the Locust", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Day%20of%20the%20Locust"}, {"title": "The Day of the Locust (film)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Day%20of%20the%20Locust%20%28film%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Day of the Locust is a 1975 American drama film directed by John Schlesinger, and starring Donald Sutherland, Karen Black, William Atherton, Burgess Meredith, Richard Dysart, John Hillerman, and Geraldine Page.", "wikipage": "The Day of the Locust (film)"}], "long_answer": "The Day of the Locust is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. In 1975, it was adapted into a film by the same name. Both the novel and the film follow a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scene design and painting. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Aspiring artist and recent Yale graduate Tod Hackett arrives in 1930s Hollywood to work as a painter at a major film studio.", "wikipage": "The Day of the Locust (film) Plot"}, {"content": "The Day of the Locust is a 1975 American drama film directed by John Schlesinger, and starring Donald Sutherland, Karen Black, William Atherton, Burgess Meredith, Richard Dysart, John Hillerman, and Geraldine Page.", "wikipage": "The Day of the Locust (film)"}, {"content": "The screenplay by Waldo Salt is based on the 1939 novel of the same title by Nathanael West.", "wikipage": "The Day of the Locust (film)"}], "long_answer": "\"The Day of the Locust\" is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. The novel follows a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scene design and painting. Similarly, in the 1975 film \"The Day of the Locust\", aspiring artist and recent Yale graduate Tod Hackett arrives in 1930s Hollywood to work as a painter at a major film studio. The film screenplay by Waldo Salt is based on the 1939 novel of the same title."}], "sample_id": "-2959456816814051630", "question": "In day of the locust where is tod hackett originally from?", "docs": [{"id": "2217954", "title": "The Day of the Locust", "text": "The Day of the Locust The Day of the Locust is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. The novel follows a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scenic design and painting. While he works he plans an important painting to be called \"The Burning of Los Angeles,\" a portrayal of the chaotic and fiery holocaust which will destroy the city. While the cast of characters Tod befriends are a conglomerate of Hollywood stereotypes, his greater discovery is a", "score": 0.82763671875, "summary": "Tod Hackett is originally from an unspecified location, but the novel takes place in Hollywood, California.", "extraction": "Tod Hackett is from the Yale School of Fine Arts. (Note: the passage does not explicitly state where Tod Hackett is originally from.)"}], "answer": "\"The Day of the Locust\" is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. The novel follows a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scene design and painting. Similarly, in the 1975 film \"The Day of the Locust\", aspiring artist and recent Yale graduate Tod Hackett arrives in 1930s Hollywood to work as a painter at a major film studio. The film screenplay by Waldo Salt is based on the 1939 novel of the same title.", "output": "Tod Hackett, the protagonist of the 1939 novel The Day of the Locust, is originally from New Haven, Connecticut [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Tod Hackett, the protagonist of the 1939 novel The Day of the Locust, is originally from New Haven, Connecticut.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 558, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Tod Hackett, the protagonist of the 1939 novel The Day of the Locust, is originally from New Haven, Connecticut.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Tod Hackett, the protagonist of the 1939 novel The Day of the Locust, is originally from New Haven, Connecticut.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9619993316964806, "ctx": {"title": "The Day of the Locust", "text": "The Day of the Locust The Day of the Locust is a 1939 novel by American author Nathanael West set in Hollywood, California. The novel follows a young artist from the Yale School of Fine Arts named Tod Hackett, who has been hired by a Hollywood studio to do scenic design and painting. While he works he plans an important painting to be called \"The Burning of Los Angeles,\" a portrayal of the chaotic and fiery holocaust which will destroy the city. While the cast of characters Tod befriends are a conglomerate of Hollywood stereotypes, his greater discovery is a"}, "final_score": 1.9619993316964806, "relevance_score": 0.9937587421882715, "ground_score": 0.968240589508209, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24701085413628765, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006240071552540052, "[Relevant]": 0.9935698611206081}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9602386093288966, "[Partially supported]": 0.014242695545959104, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.024609176957991186}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0020940437860041262, "[Utility:2]": 0.0018992152460375385, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003696206840104979, "[Utility:4]": 0.006577325951911943, "[Utility:5]": 0.7543191347014487}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Tod Hackett is the protagonist of the 1975 novel The Day of the Locust by Don DeLillo.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is a young artist from the east coast who moves to Hollywood, California in search of inspiration for his next painting.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Tod Hackett is the protagonist of the 1975 novel The Day of the Locust by Don DeLillo.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is a young artist from the east coast who moves to Hollywood, California in search of inspiration for his next painting.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8252734849351155, "ctx": {"title": "The Day of the Locust", "text": "personalities, one inside the other like a nest of Chinese boxes.\" Tod Hackett is the novel's protagonist. He moves from the east coast to Hollywood, California in search of inspiration for his next painting. The novel is set in the 1930s during the Great Depression. Most of the characters exist at the fringes of the Hollywood film industry, but Hollywood is merely the backdrop for Tod Hackett's revelation. Tod is employed by a Hollywood studio \"to learn set and costume designing.\" During his spare time, Tod sketches scenes he observes on large production sets and studio back lots. The novel"}, "final_score": 1.8252734849351155, "relevance_score": 0.9961155239989886, "ground_score": 0.8291579609361269, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2603048602829049, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0038838286831112574, "[Relevant]": 0.9959495290464904}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7999870329670667, "[Partially supported]": 0.039519056118097716, "