d Cup on two occasions, in 1978 and 1986.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Argentina has won the FIFA World Cup on two occasions, in 1978 and 1986.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7682615494314733, "ctx": {"title": "Argentina at the FIFA World Cup", "text": "at the 2004 and 2008 Olympic football tournament. Prior to that, Argentina won two silver medals in the 1928 and 1996 editions. On other levels of international competition, Argentina has won the FIFA U-20 World Cup a record six times. The FIFA U-17 World Cup is the only FIFA international competition yet to be won by Argentina. The inaugural FIFA World Cup tournament culminated with Argentina facing hosts and current Olympic champions Uruguay. The match was turned twice: Argentina went into half-time with a 2-1 lead in spite of an early goal for Uruguay, but the hosts ultimately won 4-2."}, "final_score": 1.7682615494314733, "relevance_score": 0.9906599740465916, "ground_score": 0.7776015753848817, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2412898375368665, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.009339193803721951, "[Relevant]": 0.9905717111883421}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6750093702783017, "[Partially supported]": 0.1964389199162477, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.12292833494758466}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00025381765957306445, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003765861754430882, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004402738442829397, "[Utility:4]": 0.007105659844686745, "[Utility:5]": 0.8181008366747892}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Argentina has won the FIFA World Cup in 1978 and 1986.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Argentina has won the FIFA World Cup in 1978 and 1986.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9477458969871084, "ctx": {"title": "Argentina national football team", "text": "4\u20132, to Uruguay. Argentina won in their next final in 1978, beating the Netherlands, 3\u20131. Argentina, led by Diego Maradona won again in 1986, a 3\u20132 victory over West Germany. Argentina last reached the World Cup final in 2014, where it lost 1-0 to Germany. Previous to this their last World Cup final was in 1990, which it also lost, 1\u20130, to West Germany by a much disputed penalty. Argentina's World Cup winning managers are C\u00e9sar Luis Menotti in 1978, and Carlos Bilardo in 1986. Argentina has been very successful in the Copa Am\u00e9rica, winning it 14 times and also"}, "final_score": 1.9477458969871084, "relevance_score": 0.9984632726321632, "ground_score": 0.9492826243549454, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.13668041144389392, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001536680332655013, "[Relevant]": 0.9984327122981487}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9232653459407929, "[Partially supported]": 0.04931504784781207, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.02598720404226196}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003541753996078129, "[Utility:2]": 0.00075566999742841, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005113118967890089, "[Utility:4]": 0.008284453951010533, "[Utility:5]": 0.8684625209297838}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Argentina has won the FIFA World Cup twice, in 1978 and 1986.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Argentina has won the FIFA World Cup twice, in 1978 and 1986.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.938084601603824, "ctx": {"title": "Football in Argentina", "text": "team to win the Intercontinental Cup. Argentine clubs have won the tournament a record nine times. In 1978, Argentina hosted the World Cup, where the team defeated the Netherlands 3\u20131 after extra time in the final to win their first World Cup. Argentina would win their second World Cup in the 1986 edition. In 1979, a young Diego Maradona was part of the Argentina under-20 team which won the FIFA Under-20 World Cup. Argentina have gone on to win a record six U-20 World Cups. In 1982, due to an uncertain political atmosphere and an extremely unstable government with multiple"}, "final_score": 1.938084601603824, "relevance_score": 0.9980732655231556, "ground_score": 0.9400113360806684, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14636091688186884, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0019267103656864357, "[Relevant]": 0.9980607756329088}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9059131395906216, "[Partially supported]": 0.064606718965199, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.027570763398877384}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00034413195041878107, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005392848503493951, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006633316959467566, "[Utility:4]": 0.008468851317032714, "[Utility:5]": 0.8504536740266174}], "n_tokens": 28}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the voice of the raccoon in the guardians of the galaxy episode of the Ultimate Spider-Man?", "short_answers": ["West", "Billy West"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the voice of the raccoon in the guardians of the galaxy animated tv series?", "short_answers": ["Trevor Devall"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the voice of the raccoon in the lego guardians of the galaxy tv series?", "short_answers": ["Trevor Devall"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "The character has appeared in several media adaptations as a member of that team, including animated television series, toys and video games. He appears in the Marvel Cinematic Universe films \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" (2014), \"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2\" (2017), \"\" (2018), and \"\" (2019). In these appearances, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper, with motion capture provided by Sean Gunn.", "question": "Who is the voice of the raccoon in the 2014 guardians of the galaxy film?", "short_answers": ["Bradley Cooper", "Cooper"], "wikipage": "Rocket Raccoon"}, {"context": "The character has appeared in several media adaptations as a member of that team, including animated television series, toys and video games. He appears in the Marvel Cinematic Universe films \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" (2014), \"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2\" (2017), \"\" (2018), and \"\" (2019). In these appearances, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper, with motion capture provided by Sean Gunn.", "question": "Who is the voice of the raccoon in the 2017 guardians of the galaxy vol 2 film?", "short_answers": ["Bradley Cooper", "Cooper"], "wikipage": "Rocket Raccoon"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Rocket Raccoon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocket%20Raccoon"}, {"title": "Bradley Cooper", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%20Cooper"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Rocket Raccoon is a fictional character appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics.", "wikipage": "Rocket Raccoon"}, {"content": "Rocket Raccoon appears in the animated special Lego Marvel Super Heroes - Guardians of the Galaxy: The Thanos Threat, voiced again by Trevor Devall", "wikipage": "Rocket Raccoon"}], "long_answer": "The Rocket Raccoon, in the Guardians of the Galaxy film is voiced by Bradley Cooper. The character is voiced by Trevor Devall in both the Guardians of the Galaxy animated tv series and the animated special Lego Marvel Super Heroes - Guardians of the Galaxy: The Thanos Threat. And he is voiced by Billy West in the Ultimate Spider-Man."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Bradley Charles Cooper (born January 5, 1975) is an American actor and filmmaker.", "wikipage": "Bradley Cooper"}, {"content": "Trevor Devall (born November 10, 1972[1]) is a Canadian\u2013American[citation needed] voice actor and podcaster.", "wikipage": "Trevor Devall"}, {"content": "William Richard Werstine (born April 16, 1952),[2][3] better known as Billy West, is an American voice actor, comedian, musician, singer, songwriter, radio personality and impressionist.", "wikipage": "Billy West"}, {"content": "Ultimate Spider-Man is an American superhero animated television series based on the Spider-Man comics published by Marvel Comics.", "wikipage": "Ultimate Spider-Man (TV series)"}], "long_answer": "Several actors have been the voice of the raccoon in Guardians of the Galaxy. Bradley Cooper was in the 2014 film Guardians of the Galaxy and the 2017 film Guardians of the Galaxy Vol 2. Trevor Devall was in the Guardians of the Galaxy animated TV series and the Lego Guardians of the Galaxy TV series. Billy West was in the Guardians of the Galaxy episode of Ultimate Spider-Man. "}], "sample_id": "446543549999220605", "question": "Who is the voice of raccoon in guardians of the galaxy?", "docs": [{"id": "6095151", "title": "Rocket Raccoon", "text": "including animated television series, toys, and video games. He appears in the live-action films \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" (2014), \"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2\" (2017), \"\" (2018), and the upcoming \"\" (2019), all set in the Marvel Cinematic Universe. In these appearances, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper, with motion capture provided by Sean Gunn. The character was created by Bill Mantlo and Keith Giffen, and inspired by the Beatles song \"Rocky Raccoon\". Other references to the song were featured in Rocket's appearance in \"The Incredible Hulk\" #271 (May 1982), which was titled \"Now Somewhere In the Black", "score": 0.7919921875, "summary": "Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper with motion capture by Sean Gunn in the live-action films \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" (2014), \"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2\" (2017), \"\" (2018), and the upcoming \"\" (2019), all set in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.", "extraction": "Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper."}], "answer": "Several actors have been the voice of the raccoon in Guardians of the Galaxy. Bradley Cooper was in the 2014 film Guardians of the Galaxy and the 2017 film Guardians of the Galaxy Vol 2. Trevor Devall was in the Guardians of the Galaxy animated TV series and the Lego Guardians of the Galaxy TV series. Billy West was in the Guardians of the Galaxy episode of Ultimate Spider-Man. ", "output": "In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper in the first film, and by Sean Gunn in the second and third films [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper in the first film, and by Sean Gunn in the second and third films.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 623, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper in the first film, and by Sean Gunn in the second and third films.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper in the first film, and by Sean Gunn in the second and third films.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9446819161399382, "ctx": {"title": "Rocket Raccoon", "text": "including animated television series, toys, and video games. He appears in the live-action films \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" (2014), \"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2\" (2017), \"\" (2018), and the upcoming \"\" (2019), all set in the Marvel Cinematic Universe. In these appearances, Rocket Raccoon is voiced by Bradley Cooper, with motion capture provided by Sean Gunn. The character was created by Bill Mantlo and Keith Giffen, and inspired by the Beatles song \"Rocky Raccoon\". Other references to the song were featured in Rocket's appearance in \"The Incredible Hulk\" #271 (May 1982), which was titled \"Now Somewhere In the Black"}, "final_score": 1.9446819161399382, "relevance_score": 0.9987355783477676, "ground_score": 0.9459463377921705, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.32662520579012944, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0012643950321442013, "[Relevant]": 0.9987145517944861}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8953178397588354, "[Partially supported]": 0.10045188812628061, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003804715236959697}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00025355782088784935, "[Utility:2]": 3.046112444687087e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010007344709136506, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011143879810873916, "[Utility:5]": 0.6722627875233917}], "n_tokens": 42}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Guardians of the Galaxy is a 2014 American superhero film based on the Marvel Comics superhero team of the same name.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The film stars an ensemble cast including Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Vin Diesel, Bradley Cooper, Michael Rooker, Karen Gillan, Pom Klementieff, Elizabeth Debicki, Chris Sullivan, Sean Gunn, and Sylvester Stallone.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Guardians of the Galaxy is a 2014 American superhero film based on the Marvel Comics superhero team of the same name.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The film stars an ensemble cast including Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Vin Diesel, Bradley Cooper, Michael Rooker, Karen Gillan, Pom Klementieff, Elizabeth Debicki, Chris Sullivan, Sean Gunn, and Sylvester Stallone.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.8828077207377567, "ctx": {"title": "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2", "text": "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 is a 2017 American superhero film based on the Marvel Comics superhero team Guardians of the Galaxy, produced by Marvel Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures. It is the sequel to 2014's \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" and the fifteenth film in the Marvel Cinematic Universe (MCU). Written and directed by James Gunn, the film stars an ensemble cast featuring Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Vin Diesel, Bradley Cooper, Michael Rooker, Karen Gillan, Pom Klementieff, Elizabeth Debicki, Chris Sullivan, Sean Gunn, Sylvester Stallone, and Kurt"}, "final_score": 0.8828077207377567, "relevance_score": 0.8344068822513288, "ground_score": 0.04840083848642793, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.13030065358658421, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.16554821632761915, "[Relevant]": 0.8341806285563801}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.012063751251829232, "[Partially supported]": 0.07106839278208006, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9002794895520706}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005709982633974016, "[Utility:2]": 0.00023942627563780793, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017585359342522398, "[Utility:4]": 0.0005058764241007369, "[Utility:5]": 0.852543959687588}], "n_tokens": 105}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Guardians of the Galaxy is a 2014 American superhero film based on the Marvel Comics superhero team of the same name, produced by Marvel Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The film features an ensemble cast including Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Vin Diesel, and Bradley Cooper as the titular Guardians, along with Lee Pace, Michael Rooker, and Karen Sheila Gillan as the voice of raccoon.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Guardians of the Galaxy is a 2014 American superhero film based on the Marvel Comics superhero team of the same name, produced by Marvel Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The film features an ensemble cast including Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Vin Diesel, and Bradley Cooper as the titular Guardians, along with Lee Pace, Michael Rooker, and Karen Sheila Gillan as the voice of raccoon.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2189294396954329, "ctx": {"title": "Guardians of the Galaxy (film)", "text": "Guardians of the Galaxy (film) Guardians of the Galaxy (retroactively referred to as Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 1) is a 2014 American superhero film based on the Marvel Comics superhero team of the same name, produced by Marvel Studios and distributed by Walt Disney Studios Motion Pictures. It is the tenth film in the Marvel Cinematic Universe (MCU). The film was directed by James Gunn, who wrote the screenplay with Nicole Perlman, and features an ensemble cast including Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Vin Diesel, and Bradley Cooper as the titular Guardians, along with Lee Pace, Michael Rooker,"}, "final_score": 1.2189294396954329, "relevance_score": 0.9012274198695515, "ground_score": 0.3177020198258815, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.10467701987179903, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.09871198643661219, "[Relevant]": 0.9006745468122275}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2031653259094116, "[Partially supported]": 0.22488351587691094, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5653571303281685}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00024132506152435676, "[Utility:2]": 9.303909932768274e-05, "[Utility:3]": 2.3249905736443485e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011694283334718113, "[Utility:5]": 0.9794426525461821}], "n_tokens": 114}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Guardians of the Galaxy features several characters voiced by members of the cast.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]In the first film, Bradley Cooper voices the character of Rocket Raccoon, while in the sequel, Vin Diesel voices the character.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Guardians of the Galaxy features several characters voiced by members of the cast.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]In the first film, Bradley Cooper voices the character of Rocket Raccoon, while in the sequel, Vin Diesel voices the character.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.8186235110798984, "ctx": {"title": "James Gunn", "text": "Kaufman. Bobcat Goldthwait, who worked as a voice actor on the 1997 Disney film \"Hercules\", responded to the incident by asking Disney to remove his voice from an upcoming park attraction based on the film. A number of media outlets openly criticized Disney's decision, including \"Collider\", \"Cartoon Brew\", \"The Daily Dot\", \"The Independent\", \"National Review\", MovieWeb, and \"Vulture\". An online petition urging Disney to re-hire Gunn received over 400,000 signatures. On July 30, 2018, \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" cast members Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Bradley Cooper, Vin Diesel, Sean Gunn, Karen Gillan, Pom Klementieff, and Michael Rooker released"}, "final_score": 0.8186235110798984, "relevance_score": 0.6926419831347361, "ground_score": 0.12598152794516226, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.46054541382491815, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.30732828312142935, "[Relevant]": 0.692574977108651}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.03865876840680091, "[Partially supported]": 0.17325657984149012, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7825721799715716}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002253083225198071, "[Utility:2]": 4.872634195790813e-05, "[Utility:3]": 7.576421143378396e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011486883859304076, "[Utility:5]": 0.9658378602229354}], "n_tokens": 54}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Bradley Cooper voices the character of Rocket Raccoon in the films Guardians of the Galaxy, Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2, Avengers: Endgame, and Avengers: Infinity War.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Bradley Cooper voices the character of Rocket Raccoon in the films Guardians of the Galaxy, Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2, Avengers: Endgame, and Avengers: Infinity War.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.866160831162243, "ctx": {"title": "Guardians of the Galaxy \u2013 Mission: Breakout!", "text": "in 2020 by a Marvel-themed area at Disney California Adventure. Chris Pratt, Zoe Saldana, Dave Bautista, Benicio del Toro, and Bradley Cooper reprise their roles as Star-Lord, Gamora, Drax, The Collector, and the voice of Rocket, respectively. Due to Marvel's 1994 contract with Universal Parks & Resorts (pre-dating Disney's 2009 purchase of Marvel Entertainment), the Marvel brand is not allowed to be used as part of the ride's title or advertising. James Gunn directed all of the scenes involving the cast. Tyler Bates, composer of the \"Guardians of the Galaxy\" films, wrote the incidental music for the attraction's queue. The"}, "final_score": 1.866160831162243, "relevance_score": 0.9884918520693035, "ground_score": 0.8776689790929396, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24711713632550528, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.011507124499670507, "[Relevant]": 0.98840394450707}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7769894434091498, "[Partially supported]": 0.19799450225681928, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.023099290046521455}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003615577122680736, "[Utility:2]": 2.4175592516654327e-05, "[Utility:3]": 8.72298575598817e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006781224833398195, "[Utility:5]": 0.8103854007634425}], "n_tokens": 60}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which world cup was the last time mexico made it to the quarter finals in the world cup?", "short_answers": ["1986 FIFA World Cup", "1986", "13th FIFA World Cup"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What date was the last time mexico made it to the quarter finals in the world cup?", "short_answers": ["21 June 1986"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Mexico at the FIFA World Cup", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%20at%20the%20FIFA%20World%20Cup"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The last time Mexico made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup was in the 1986 FIFA World Cup, on 21 June 1986."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The World Cup is the premier competitive international football tournament, first played in 1930, and then every four years since, except 1942 and 1946, due to World War II.", "wikipage": "Mexico at the FIFA World Cup"}, {"content": "The Mexico national team has competed in the tournament since the inaugural 1930 World Cup, and has entered ever since, for a total of 16 tournaments, although it has failed to qualify for the finals proper on 3 occasions: 1934 (Italy), 1974 (West Germany), and 1982 (Spain).", "wikipage": "Mexico at the FIFA World Cup"}], "long_answer": "The World Cup is the premier competitive international football tournament, first played in 1930, and then every four years since, except 1942 and 1946, due to World War II. The Mexico national team has competed in the tournament since the inaugural 1930 World Cup, and has entered ever since, for a total of 16 tournaments, although it has failed to qualify for the finals proper on 3 occasions: 1934 (Italy), 1974 (West Germany), and 1982 (Spain). The last time Mexico made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup was at the 13th FIFA World Cup in 1986, which was on 21 June 1986."}], "sample_id": "8520790328285265395", "question": "When was the last time mexico made it to the quarter finals in the world cup?", "docs": [{"id": "145448", "title": "FIFA World Cup", "text": "out of the first round were: USA, semi-finalists in 1930; Cuba, quarter-finalists in 1938; North Korea, quarter-finalists in 1966; and Mexico, quarter-finalists in 1970. The tournament was expanded to 24 teams in 1982, and then to 32 in 1998, also allowing more teams from Africa, Asia and North America to take part. Since then, teams from these regions have enjoyed more success, with several having reached the quarter-finals: Mexico, quarter-finalists in 1986; Cameroon, quarter-finalists in 1990; South Korea, finishing in fourth place in 2002; Senegal, along with USA, both quarter-finalists in 2002; Ghana, quarter-finalists in 2010; and Costa Rica, quarter-finalists", "score": 0.73974609375, "summary": "Mexico last made it to the quarter-finals in the World Cup in 1986.", "extraction": "Mexico, quarter-finalists in 1986."}], "answer": "The World Cup is the premier competitive international football tournament, first played in 1930, and then every four years since, except 1942 and 1946, due to World War II. The Mexico national team has competed in the tournament since the inaugural 1930 World Cup, and has entered ever since, for a total of 16 tournaments, although it has failed to qualify for the finals proper on 3 occasions: 1934 (Italy), 1974 (West Germany), and 1982 (Spain). The last time Mexico made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup was at the 13th FIFA World Cup in 1986, which was on 21 June 1986.", "output": "Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup in 1970, 1986, and 1998 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup in 1970, 1986, and 1998.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 624, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the 1970, 1986, and 2018 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the 1970, 1986, and 2018 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8692233213461877, "ctx": {"title": "Mexico national football team", "text": "However, goalkeeper Antonio Carbajal has the distinction of being the first player ever to appear in five consecutive World Cups. In 1965, Mexico won the 1965 CONCACAF Championship to become continental champions for the first time. In 1970, Mexico hosted the World Cup and kicked off their campaign with a scoreless draw against the Soviet Union. This was followed by a 4\u20130 win over El Salvador. Mexico advanced to the next round with a victory against Belgium. At the quarter-finals stage, Mexico was eliminated by Italy, losing 4\u20131. Mexico failed to qualify for the 1974 World Cup, but did make"}, "final_score": 1.8692233213461877, "relevance_score": 0.9973683254439385, "ground_score": 0.8718549959022491, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2093719159708062, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002631557644108565, "[Relevant]": 0.9973240174277909}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8155818914479965, "[Partially supported]": 0.10866577750795414, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0735269429813617}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002636840647558656, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002477082548874146, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002884712455250533, "[Utility:4]": 0.001971344130716513, "[Utility:5]": 0.9706191457919072}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup in 1970, 1986, and 1998.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the World Cup in 1970, 1986, and 1998.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9224728294622269, "ctx": {"title": "FIFA World Cup", "text": "out of the first round were: USA, semi-finalists in 1930; Cuba, quarter-finalists in 1938; North Korea, quarter-finalists in 1966; and Mexico, quarter-finalists in 1970. The tournament was expanded to 24 teams in 1982, and then to 32 in 1998, also allowing more teams from Africa, Asia and North America to take part. Since then, teams from these regions have enjoyed more success, with several having reached the quarter-finals: Mexico, quarter-finalists in 1986; Cameroon, quarter-finalists in 1990; South Korea, finishing in fourth place in 2002; Senegal, along with USA, both quarter-finalists in 2002; Ghana, quarter-finalists in 2010; and Costa Rica, quarter-finalists"}, "final_score": 1.9224728294622269, "relevance_score": 0.9978510510593702, "ground_score": 0.9246217784028568, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.17987745008968675, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002148841203712133, "[Relevant]": 0.9978010240929831}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8815430931589461, "[Partially supported]": 0.08263937916899725, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03391513287615385}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001275166999757841, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001556276174795993, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002907506672210697, "[Utility:4]": 0.0015293870852097035, "[Utility:5]": 0.9706754152872897}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the 1998, 2010, and 2014 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the 1998, 2010, and 2014 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8295364907771399, "ctx": {"title": "Mexico national football team", "text": "and Javier Hern\u00e1ndez, but would fall 3\u20130 to Sweden in the last group stage match. Despite the loss, Mexico qualified to the round of 16 for the seventh-consecutive tournament. In the round of 16, Mexico was defeated 0\u20132 by Brazil; the defeat meant that for the seventh tournament in a row, Mexico failed to reach the quarterfinals since they last hosted the World Cup in 1986. The Estadio Azteca, also known in Spanish as \"\"El Coloso de Santa \u00darsula\"\", was built in 1966. It is the official home stadium of the Mexican national team, as well as the Mexican club"}, "final_score": 1.8295364907771399, "relevance_score": 0.9952816311604635, "ground_score": 0.8342548596166763, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24882690268529983, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0047180420320778235, "[Relevant]": 0.9952126951633811}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7699258639775499, "[Partially supported]": 0.12373835047800974, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.1033872616350299}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00027799267264809474, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003288374336368956, "[Utility:3]": 0.00035834654791828133, "[Utility:4]": 0.0021951387363211535, "[Utility:5]": 0.965052848985063}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Mexico made it to the quarter finals in the 2010 and 2014 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mexico made it to the quarter finals in the 2010 and 2014 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7879903706910403, "ctx": {"title": "Mexico national football team", "text": "lost in the semi-finals 3\u20130 to Argentina. Mexico secured third place against Uruguay, winning 3\u20131. In July 2009, Mexico won their fifth Gold Cup, and eighth CONCACAF Championship overall, after beating the United States 5\u20130 in the final. Mexico qualified for the 2010 FIFA World Cup, where they were drawn into Group A alongside host South Africa, France and Uruguay. They drew 1\u20131 against South Africa, defeated France 2\u20130, and lost 1\u20130 to Uruguay, and advanced to the round of 16, where they were eliminated following a 1\u20133 defeat to Argentina. The 2011 CONCACAF Gold Cup saw Mexico win their"}, "final_score": 1.7879903706910403, "relevance_score": 0.9755769074162037, "ground_score": 0.8124134632748365, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2115795378991137, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.024420251762045604, "[Relevant]": 0.9754634312014929}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.735266144508293, "[Partially supported]": 0.15054996240943389, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.11187923215964486}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023422058688782265, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003831591252355392, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003102918636895174, "[Utility:4]": 0.0024121991565415435, "[Utility:5]": 0.9543283225589366}], "n_tokens": 31}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the 1962, 1966, 1970, 1986, and 2018 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mexico has made it to the quarter finals in the 1962, 1966, 1970, 1986, and 2018 World Cups.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6091302533888139, "ctx": {"title": "North, Central American and Caribbean nations at the FIFA World Cup", "text": "in what became the northern-most World Cup match in history. Mexico finished last overall among all sixteen teams. In the 1962 World Cup qualification, seven countries played for the sole CONCACAF spot, again won by Mexico, who then advanced to the CONMEBOL/CCCF/NAFC Intercontinental Play-off. Mexico beat Paraguay 1\u20130 in Mexico City and qualified for their fourth consecutive World Cup. In their group, Mexico lost to defending (and eventual) champions Brazil 0\u20132, as well as 0\u20131 to Spain. However, Mexico managed to get their first ever win at the World Cup by beating Czechoslovakia (who wound up being the tournament runners-up)"}, "final_score": 1.6091302533888139, "relevance_score": 0.9825575144018881, "ground_score": 0.6265727389869257, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.218741464627355, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.017440824029080253, "[Relevant]": 0.9824639160939718}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.49558968823432437, "[Partially supported]": 0.2571085432245092, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2434254761504798}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00017221084958777077, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001898768768912015, "[Utility:3]": 0.00021515868932242522, "[Utility:4]": 0.0016338902699438618, "[Utility:5]": 0.9818135382143458}], "n_tokens": 51}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Dr. Hudson Hornet, MD (also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet, Hud, Doc Hudson, or simply, Doc) is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film. He is modeled after a 1951 Hudson Hornet.", "question": "Who did Doc Hudson's voice in the first film, third film, archive recording, first video game, and Mater and the Ghostlight in the Cars franchise?", "short_answers": ["Paul Newman"], "wikipage": "Doc Hudson"}, {"context": "Doc Hudson is the town's medical doctor and a local judge, voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first film and its video game and Corey Burton in all other related media. He is based on the Hudson Hornet. He did not appear in the second film, as it is implied that Doc died, and Newman died in September 2008 from lung cancer.", "question": "Who did Doc Hudson's voice in every English language version of Cars where Paul Newman didn't voice the character?", "short_answers": ["Corey Burton"], "wikipage": "List of Cars characters"}, {"context": "Dr. Hudson Hornet, MD (also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet, Hud, Doc Hudson, or simply, Doc) is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film. He is modeled after a 1951 Hudson Hornet.", "question": "Who did Doc Hudson's voice in the  Rioplatense Spanish version of Cars?", "short_answers": ["Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso"], "wikipage": "Doc Hudson"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Doc Hudson", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doc%20Hudson"}, {"title": "List of Cars characters", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20Cars%20characters"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Dr. Hudson Hornet, MD (also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet, Hud, Doc Hudson, or simply, Doc) is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film Cars as a medical doctor and a local judge.", "wikipage": "Doc Hudson"}], "long_answer": "The voice of Doc Hudson, of the Pixar Cars films, in the first and third films and video game is done by Paul Newman, and Corey Burton in all other English media. And in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first movie is voiced by Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Dr. Hudson Hornet, MD, also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet, Hud, Doc Hudson, or simply, Doc, is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film."}], "sample_id": "-2258198823524440602", "question": "Who did doc hudson's voice in cars?", "docs": [{"id": "8048966", "title": "Doc Hudson", "text": "Doc Hudson Doc Hudson (also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet or simply Doc) is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film. He is modeled after a 1951 Hudson Hornet. Doc Hudson (Voiced by Paul Newman in his last non-documentary film role) was", "score": 0.8486328125, "summary": "Doc Hudson was voiced by Paul Newman in the first and third Cars films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media.", "extraction": "Doc Hudson is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media."}], "answer": "Dr. Hudson Hornet, MD, also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet, Hud, Doc Hudson, or simply, Doc, is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film.", "output": "In the 2006 Pixar film Cars, actor Paul Newman voiced the character of Doc Hudson, an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears as a medical doctor and a local judge [1]. Corey Burton voiced the character in all other media [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]In the 2006 Pixar film Cars, actor Paul Newman voiced the character of Doc Hudson, an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears as a medical doctor and a local judge.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Corey Burton voiced the character in all other media.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 625, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 Pixar film Cars, actor Paul Newman voiced the character of Doc Hudson, an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears as a medical doctor and a local judge.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Corey Burton voiced the character in all other media.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 Pixar film Cars, actor Paul Newman voiced the character of Doc Hudson, an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears as a medical doctor and a local judge.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Corey Burton voiced the character in all other media.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.996077175727713, "ctx": {"title": "Doc Hudson", "text": "Doc Hudson Doc Hudson (also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet or simply Doc) is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film. He is modeled after a 1951 Hudson Hornet. Doc Hudson (Voiced by Paul Newman in his last non-documentary film role) was"}, "final_score": 1.996077175727713, "relevance_score": 0.9993487317406651, "ground_score": 0.996728443987048, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21731667604178995, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0006511838400283511, "[Relevant]": 0.9992191932199355}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9939198503083809, "[Partially supported]": 0.005215612502650708, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0006630907182139861}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00042977826947983235, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001952355783134688, "[Utility:3]": 0.00019753695353015105, "[Utility:4]": 0.001753763665104964, "[Utility:5]": 0.9263902278587246}], "n_tokens": 64}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 Pixar film Cars, the character of Doc Hudson, an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car, is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 Pixar film Cars, the character of Doc Hudson, an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car, is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.995809244517712, "ctx": {"title": "Doc Hudson", "text": "to win two NASCAR premier series championships (1951 and 1953). Doc Hudson Doc Hudson (also known as The Fabulous Hudson Hornet or simply Doc) is an animated, anthropomorphic retired race car who appears in the 2006 Pixar film \"Cars\" as a medical doctor and a local judge. He is voiced by actor Paul Newman in the first and third films and video game, and Corey Burton in all other media. Six-time Turismo Carretera champion Juan Mar\u00eda Traverso voiced the character in the Rioplatense Spanish version of the first film. He is modeled after a 1951 Hudson Hornet. Doc Hudson (Voiced"}, "final_score": 1.995809244517712, "relevance_score": 0.9994766242812568, "ground_score": 0.9963326202364552, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.17890092585225426, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0005233304935065586, "[Relevant]": 0.9993902588554937}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.994189132113882, "[Partially supported]": 0.0041270067041689245, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0016035819972028509}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000416628682816374, "[Utility:2]": 0.00027971113205413253, "[Utility:3]": 0.00032447058970772925, "[Utility:4]": 0.0028582837212894, "[Utility:5]": 0.5300002814826227}], "n_tokens": 62}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Paul Newman, an American actor, race driver, and entrepreneur, did Doc Hudson's voice in the first Cars movie.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Newman was a race driver who won numerous national and international races, including the Daytona 500, the Indianapolis 500, and the 24 Hours of Le Mans.[Continue to Use Evidence]He also founded Newman's Own, a food company, which donates all profits and royalties to charity.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Paul Newman, an American actor, race driver, and entrepreneur, did Doc Hudson's voice in the first Cars movie.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Newman was a race driver who won numerous national and international races, including the Daytona 500, the Indianapolis 500, and the 24 Hours of Le Mans.[Continue to Use Evidence]He also founded Newman's Own, a food company, which donates all profits and royalties to charity.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9921606771693907, "ctx": {"title": "Paul Newman", "text": "Russo) in which he played the dissolute father of the protagonist, Miles Roby, and for which he won a Golden Globe and a Primetime Emmy. In 2006, in keeping with his strong interest in car racing, he provided the voice of Doc Hudson, a retired anthropomorphic race car, in Disney/Pixar's \"Cars\" \u2014 this was his final role for a major feature film. While not in the sequel \"Cars 2\" (2011), his voice was later used in the third film, \"Cars 3\" (2017), for which he received billing, almost nine years after his death. Newman retired from acting in May 2007,"}, "final_score": 1.9921606771693907, "relevance_score": 0.9994023305743112, "ground_score": 0.9927583465950796, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.385580433856099, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000597590655577323, "[Relevant]": 0.9992706138933842}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9871772755560679, "[Partially supported]": 0.010546427545479395, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0019661939742839484}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.004819613455256473, "[Utility:2]": 0.009039464887954832, "[Utility:3]": 0.0016462088521109427, "[Utility:4]": 0.0122115378330166, "[Utility:5]": 0.7408882469040229}], "n_tokens": 106}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 Disney film Cars, the voice of Doc Hudson, the town's judge and doctor, was performed by Paul Newman.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 Disney film Cars, the voice of Doc Hudson, the town's judge and doctor, was performed by Paul Newman.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.97856498588569, "ctx": {"title": "Fabulous Hudson Hornet", "text": "himself stuck in the Route 66 town of Radiator Springs. The town's judge and doctor is Doc Hudson (voiced by Paul Newman), a Hornet that turns out to be the real Fabulous Hudson Hornet himself. Doc Hudson in \"Cars\" closely resembles the \"Fabulous Hudson Hornet.\" He shares many of the same records as the real Hornet, although their fates differ. His number is 51, in reference to the year his model was created. Fabulous Hudson Hornet The Fabulous Hudson Hornet is a famous NASCAR Grand National (now Monster Energy Cup Series) and AAA stock car campaigned during the early 1950s"}, "final_score": 1.97856498588569, "relevance_score": 0.9985449373018224, "ground_score": 0.9800200485838675, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3974024347297362, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0014550051509905119, "[Relevant]": 0.9985054452219231}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9640373933954248, "[Partially supported]": 0.030271074879016143, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.004827143542726694}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0006825379678616332, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005636378335136936, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002570462765070698, "[Utility:4]": 0.001378764594849953, "[Utility:5]": 0.6061487974679955}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 film Cars, the voice of Doc Hudson was performed by actor Michael Wallis.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2006 film Cars, the voice of Doc Hudson was performed by actor Michael Wallis.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8652848779689637, "ctx": {"title": "Doc Hudson", "text": "number 51 as a second tribute. The car is based on the real-life Fabulous Hudson Hornet in NASCAR competition, with Doc's racing career most closely resembling that of Herb Thomas. Newman, a racing enthusiast and former driver, drew upon his experiences for the grumpy old race car's personality. The character has strong parallels to the Doc Hollywood of a 1991 film and shares the \"Doc\" moniker with the late Walter \"Doc\" Mason, interviewed on Route 66 as research for the film. A close friend of Michael Wallis (the voice of \"Sheriff\"), country veterinarian Dr. Walter S. Mason Jr. owned the"}, "final_score": 1.8652848779689637, "relevance_score": 0.9585377298055215, "ground_score": 0.906747148163442, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3893618003611924, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.041452363731926026, "[Relevant]": 0.9583087091059594}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8360649933829902, "[Partially supported]": 0.13755431855673625, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.024279776570292982}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004210993953064908, "[Utility:2]": 0.00032795269519558405, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015889760499395453, "[Utility:4]": 0.0013807317609652904, "[Utility:5]": 0.5724683725272457}], "n_tokens": 28}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth. It comprises stratified squamous epithelium, termed \"oral epithelium\", and an underlying connective tissue termed \"lamina propria\". The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual. Changes indicative of disease are seen as alterations in the oral mucosa lining the mouth, which can reveal systemic conditions, such as diabetes or vitamin deficiency, or the local effects of chronic tobacco or alcohol use.", "question": "What is the type of lining in the mouth called?", "short_answers": ["mucous membrane"], "wikipage": "Oral mucosa"}, {"context": "The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth. It comprises stratified squamous epithelium, termed \"oral epithelium\", and an underlying connective tissue termed \"lamina propria\". The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual. Changes indicative of disease are seen as alterations in the oral mucosa lining the mouth, which can reveal systemic conditions, such as diabetes or vitamin deficiency, or the local effects of chronic tobacco or alcohol use.", "question": "What is the mucous membrane lining of the mouth called?", "short_answers": ["Oral mucosa"], "wikipage": "Oral mucosa"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Oral mucosa", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oral%20mucosa"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The lining of the mouth is a mucous membrane called the oral mucosa."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth. It comprises stratified squamous epithelium, termed \"oral epithelium\", and an underlying connective tissue termed \"lamina propria\". The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual."}], "sample_id": "-2578916277348572724", "question": "What is the lining of the mouth called?", "docs": [{"id": "6275517", "title": "Oral mucosa", "text": "Oral mucosa The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria. The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual. Changes indicative of disease are seen as alterations in the oral mucosa lining the mouth, which can reveal systemic conditions, such as diabetes or vitamin deficiency, or the local effects of chronic tobacco or alcohol use. Oral mucosa can be divided into three main categories based on function and histology: Oral", "score": 0.76123046875, "summary": "The lining of the mouth is called oral mucosa, which consists of stratified squamous epithelium and lamina propria. It can reveal systemic conditions or local effects of chronic tobacco or alcohol use.", "extraction": "The lining of the mouth is called oral mucosa."}], "answer": "The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth. It comprises stratified squamous epithelium, termed \"oral epithelium\", and an underlying connective tissue termed \"lamina propria\". The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual.", "output": "The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 628, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9963427360290682, "ctx": {"title": "Oral mucosa", "text": "Oral mucosa The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria. The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual. Changes indicative of disease are seen as alterations in the oral mucosa lining the mouth, which can reveal systemic conditions, such as diabetes or vitamin deficiency, or the local effects of chronic tobacco or alcohol use. Oral mucosa can be divided into three main categories based on function and histology: Oral"}, "final_score": 1.9963427360290682, "relevance_score": 0.9984870617953329, "ground_score": 0.9978556742337352, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.037789227928057036, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0015129082530063028, "[Relevant]": 0.9984672947316625}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9967092444189279, "[Partially supported]": 0.0021464899007949223, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0010709235464399964}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000158865027972428, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003833367551790115, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005260099396348056, "[Utility:4]": 0.00203221703021525, "[Utility:5]": 0.5293381483255167}], "n_tokens": 59}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.995728709255682, "ctx": {"title": "Oral mucosa", "text": "basal lamina (basement membrane without aid of the microscope) is at the interface between the oral epithelium and lamina propria similar to the epidermis and dermis. Oral mucosa The oral mucosa is the mucous membrane lining the inside of the mouth and consists of stratified squamous epithelium termed oral epithelium and an underlying connective tissue termed lamina propria. The oral cavity has sometimes been described as a mirror that reflects the health of the individual. Changes indicative of disease are seen as alterations in the oral mucosa lining the mouth, which can reveal systemic conditions, such as diabetes or vitamin"}, "final_score": 1.995728709255682, "relevance_score": 0.9986222674579065, "ground_score": 0.9971064417977755, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.0357799232052068, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0013776960016565671, "[Relevant]": 0.9985957818427137}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9955426940003212, "[Partially supported]": 0.0028850371927563556, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.001450687804138532}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014486582247439986, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003984281491056993, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005467181631514239, "[Utility:4]": 0.0022135900391047434, "[Utility:5]": 0.5788375681525926}], "n_tokens": 59}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The floor of the mouth is formed by the mylohyoid muscles and is occupied mainly by the tongue.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]A mucous membrane, the oral mucus, lines the sides and under surface of the tongue to the gums, lining the inner aspect of the jaw (mandible).[Continue to Use Evidence]It receives the secretions from the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The floor of the mouth is formed by the mylohyoid muscles and is occupied mainly by the tongue.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]A mucous membrane, the oral mucus, lines the sides and under surface of the tongue to the gums, lining the inner aspect of the jaw (mandible).[Continue to Use Evidence]It receives the secretions from the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9636683911548287, "ctx": {"title": "Human mouth", "text": "The uvula projects downwards from the middle of the soft palate at its back. The floor is formed by the mylohyoid muscles and is occupied mainly by the tongue. A mucous membrane \u2013 the oral mucus, lines the sides and under surface of the tongue to the gums, lining the inner aspect of the jaw (mandible). It receives the secretions from the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands. While shut, the orifice of the mouth forms a line between the upper and lower lip. In facial expression, this \"mouth line\" is iconically shaped like an up-open parabola in a smile, and"}, "final_score": 1.9636683911548287, "relevance_score": 0.9959927112120663, "ground_score": 0.9676756799427623, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.0679681271380458, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0040069808900071455, "[Relevant]": 0.9959161846359129}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.952329636886042, "[Partially supported]": 0.0261843310395564, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.01915686582205213}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00019459909953134033, "[Utility:2]": 4.5504742098050155e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012225362649079092, "[Utility:4]": 0.0003824977582485626, "[Utility:5]": 0.6296835141475193}], "n_tokens": 92}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The oral cavity is lined by a stratified squamous epithelium containing about three layers of cells.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The inner oral cavity is lined by a stratified squamous epithelium containing about three layers of cells.[Continue to Use Evidence]The cells line the oral, nasal, and external auditory meatus, providing lubrication and protection against pathogens.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The oral cavity is lined by a stratified squamous epithelium containing about three layers of cells.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The inner oral cavity is lined by a stratified squamous epithelium containing about three layers of cells.[Continue to Use Evidence]The cells line the oral, nasal, and external auditory meatus, providing lubrication and protection against pathogens.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9908177206056297, "ctx": {"title": "Head and neck anatomy", "text": "lined by epithelium. The protective tissues of the oral cavity are continuous with the digestive tract are called mucosa or mucous membranes. The cells of the inner oral cavity are called the buccal mucosa. The oral cavity is lined by a stratified squamous epithelium containing about three layers of cells. They line the oral, nasal, and external auditory meatus, (ear), providing lubrication and protection against pathogens. The lips are also protected by specialized sensory cells called Meissner's corpuscles. Blood circulates from the upper systemic loop originating at the aortic arch, and includes: the brachiocephalic artery, left common carotid artery and"}, "final_score": 1.9908177206056297, "relevance_score": 0.9979966664899166, "ground_score": 0.9928210541157132, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14695096430199753, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002003311800817543, "[Relevant]": 0.997985851628192}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9878140746137012, "[Partially supported]": 0.009097451916465145, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.002626906360756157}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.738823756498041e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00010786344722761348, "[Utility:3]": 0.00022568659737926905, "[Utility:4]": 0.0012538706585426302, "[Utility:5]": 0.7564067212586596}], "n_tokens": 87}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The mouth is the first part of the upper gastrointestinal tract and is equipped with several structures that begin the first processes of digestion.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]These include salivary glands, teeth and the tongue.[Continue to Use Evidence]The mouth consists of two regions; the vestibule and the oral cavity proper.[Continue to Use Evidence]The vestibule is the area between the teeth, lips and cheeks, and the rest is the oral cavity proper.[Continue to Use Evidence]Most of the oral cavity is lined with oral mucosa, a mucous membrane that produces a lubricating mucus, of which only a small amount is needed.[Continue to Use Evidence]Mucous membranes vary in structure in the different regions of the mouth.[Continue to Use Evidence]The oral mucosa is a stratified squamous epithelium, which is a type of epithelium that is composed of layers of cells.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The mouth is the first part of the upper gastrointestinal tract and is equipped with several structures that begin the first processes of digestion.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]These include salivary glands, teeth and the tongue.[Continue to Use Evidence]The mouth consists of two regions; the vestibule and the oral cavity proper.[Continue to Use Evidence]The vestibule is the area between the teeth, lips and cheeks, and the rest is the oral cavity proper.[Continue to Use Evidence]Most of the oral cavity is lined with oral mucosa, a mucous membrane that produces a lubricating mucus, of which only a small amount is needed.[Continue to Use Evidence]Mucous membranes vary in structure in the different regions of the mouth.[Continue to Use Evidence]The oral mucosa is a stratified squamous epithelium, which is a type of epithelium that is composed of layers of cells.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9680546302047182, "ctx": {"title": "Human digestive system", "text": "intestine. The mouth is the first part of the upper gastrointestinal tract and is equipped with several structures that begin the first processes of digestion. These include salivary glands, teeth and the tongue. The mouth consists of two regions; the vestibule and the oral cavity proper. The vestibule is the area between the teeth, lips and cheeks, and the rest is the oral cavity proper. Most of the oral cavity is lined with oral mucosa, a mucous membrane that produces a lubricating mucus, of which only a small amount is needed. Mucous membranes vary in structure in the different regions"}, "final_score": 1.9680546302047182, "relevance_score": 0.9973887526396721, "ground_score": 0.970665877565046, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14492289379539638, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002611119787332107, "[Relevant]": 0.9973400250188795}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9532923989948833, "[Partially supported]": 0.03137022196464675, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.013597987941161346}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.785229548873607e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00013320945764040384, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002213478085825104, "[Utility:4]": 0.0008551678443842817, "[Utility:5]": 0.7162372510779816}], "n_tokens": 196}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "On January 7, 2007, Cowher resigned from coaching the Steelers, citing a need to spend more time with his family. He did not use the term \"retire\", leaving open a possible return to the NFL as coach of another team. A three-man committee consisting of Art Rooney II, Dan Rooney, and Kevin Colbert was set up to conduct interviews for the head coaching vacancy. The candidates interviewed included: offensive coordinator Ken Whisenhunt, offensive line coach Russ Grimm, former offensive coordinator Chan Gailey, Minnesota Vikings defensive coordinator Mike Tomlin, and Chicago Bears defensive coordinator Ron Rivera. On January 22, 2007, Mike Tomlin was announced as Cowher's successor as head coach. Tomlin is the first African-American to be named head coach of the Pittsburgh Steelers in its 75-year history. Tomlin became the third consecutive Steelers Head Coach to go to the Super Bowl, equaling the Dallas Cowboys (Tom Landry, Jimmy Johnson and Barry Switzer) in this achievement. He was named the Motorola 2008 Coach of the Year. On February 1, 2009, Tomlin led the Steelers to their second Super Bowl of this decade, and went on to win 27\u201323 against the Arizona Cardinals. At age 36, he was the youngest head coach to ever win the Super Bowl, and he is only the second African-American coach to ever win the Super Bowl (Tony Dungy was the first). The 2010 season made Tomlin the only coach to reach the Super Bowl twice before the age of 40. Tomlin led the team to his second Super Bowl (Super Bowl XLV) on February 6, 2011. However, the Steelers were defeated in their eighth Super Bowl appearance by the Green Bay Packers by the score of 31\u201325. The Steelers recorded their 400th victory in 2012 after defeating the Washington Redskins.", "question": "When was the date last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl in 2009?", "short_answers": ["February 1, 2009"], "wikipage": "Pittsburgh Steelers"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When was the edition last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl in 2009?", "short_answers": ["Super Bowl XLIII", "Superbowl 43"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When was the date last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl in 2006?", "short_answers": ["February 5, 2006"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When was the edition last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl in 2006?", "short_answers": ["Super Bowl XL", "Superbowl 40"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When was the date last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl in 1980?", "short_answers": ["January 20, 1980"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When was the edition last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl in 1980?", "short_answers": ["Superbowl 14", "Super Bowl XIV"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Pittsburgh Steelers", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pittsburgh%20Steelers"}, {"title": "List of Super Bowl champions", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20Super%20Bowl%20champions"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": " The team is tied with the New England Patriots for the most Super Bowl titles at six, and they have both played in (16 times) and hosted (11 times) more conference championship games than any other team in the NFL.", "wikipage": "Pittsburgh Steelers"}], "long_answer": "The Pittsburgh Steelers have won the Super Bowl a total of six times. Some of their most resent wins have bee, 1980 at the Super Bowl XIV on January 20, 1980, in 2006 at the Super Bowl XL on February 5, 2006 and in 2009 at the Super Bowl XLIII on February 1, 2009."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Super Bowl XLIII was an American football game between the American Football Conference (AFC) champions Pittsburgh Steelers and the National Football Conference (NFC) champions Arizona Cardinals to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 2008 season. The Steelers defeated the Cardinals by the score of 27\u201323.", "wikipage": "Super Bowl XLIII"}], "long_answer": "The Pittsburgh Steelers have won the Super Bowl a number of times. They last won in 2009 on February 1, 2009, in the Super Bowl XLIII edition, defeating the Arizona Cardinals 27-23. They last won in 2006 on February 5, 2006, in the Super Bowl XL edition, while they last won in 1980 on January 20, 1980, in the Super Bowl XIV edition."}], "sample_id": "6127342290326352089", "question": "When was the last time pittsburgh steelers won the superbowl?", "docs": [{"id": "4245732", "title": "Super Bowl XLIII", "text": "Super Bowl XLIII Super Bowl XLIII was an American football game between the American Football Conference (AFC) champions Pittsburgh Steelers and the National Football Conference (NFC) champions Arizona Cardinals to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 2008 season. The Steelers defeated the Cardinals by the score of 27\u201323. The game was played on February 1, 2009 at Raymond James Stadium in Tampa, Florida. With this victory, the Steelers became the first team to win six Super Bowl championships. The win was also Pittsburgh's second Super Bowl victory in four years, after winning Super Bowl XL at the", "score": 0.7353515625, "summary": "The Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl XLIII on February 1, 2009, making it their sixth Super Bowl championship and second victory in four years.", "extraction": "The last time Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 1, 2009, when they defeated the Arizona Cardinals with a score of 27-23."}], "answer": "The Pittsburgh Steelers have won the Super Bowl a number of times. They last won in 2009 on February 1, 2009, in the Super Bowl XLIII edition, defeating the Arizona Cardinals 27-23. They last won in 2006 on February 5, 2006, in the Super Bowl XL edition, while they last won in 1980 on January 20, 1980, in the Super Bowl XIV edition.", "output": "The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 631, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9381790690851677, "ctx": {"title": "Super Bowl XLIII", "text": "Super Bowl XLIII Super Bowl XLIII was an American football game between the American Football Conference (AFC) champions Pittsburgh Steelers and the National Football Conference (NFC) champions Arizona Cardinals to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 2008 season. The Steelers defeated the Cardinals by the score of 27\u201323. The game was played on February 1, 2009 at Raymond James Stadium in Tampa, Florida. With this victory, the Steelers became the first team to win six Super Bowl championships. The win was also Pittsburgh's second Super Bowl victory in four years, after winning Super Bowl XL at the"}, "final_score": 1.9381790690851677, "relevance_score": 0.9987156919039067, "ground_score": 0.9394633771812609, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11703586550588782, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0012842812482895725, "[Relevant]": 0.9986948142633106}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9247294195561774, "[Partially supported]": 0.022613872742316618, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04900885794262812}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002607435626633195, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003454290977530306, "[Utility:3]": 0.00044876804729340454, "[Utility:4]": 0.0017203037547319716, "[Utility:5]": 0.7330312595486653}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8225863287609871, "ctx": {"title": "Ben Roethlisberger", "text": "a miraculous reception and scored the game-winning touchdown against the Oakland Raiders. The victory marked the first time in playoff history that a sixth-seeded NFL playoff team defeated the top-seeded team. On January 22, 2006, the Steelers defeated the Broncos 34\u201317 in Denver to win the AFC Championship and advance to Super Bowl XL. Roethlisberger completed 21 of 29 passes for 275 yards and threw two touchdown passes and scored one touchdown himself on a four-yard play-action bootleg. His run was the last touchdown of the game, sealing the win for the Steelers. The Pittsburgh Steelers won Super Bowl XL"}, "final_score": 1.8225863287609871, "relevance_score": 0.9988926611938982, "ground_score": 0.8236936675670888, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1158585116381487, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0011073144561120861, "[Relevant]": 0.9988706958967195}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8038176409331077, "[Partially supported]": 0.035042441585563563, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.15828108059339346}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003475408938968538, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005382818664818376, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004768913028805219, "[Utility:4]": 0.0017997689748829387, "[Utility:5]": 0.7432970402163274}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Pittsburgh Steelers have won six Super Bowl titles, five of which came between 1974 and 1986.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Pittsburgh Steelers have won six Super Bowl titles, five of which came between 1974 and 1986.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.354325285964454, "ctx": {"title": "History of the Pittsburgh Steelers", "text": "Dallas defeated the Steelers in Super Bowl XXX to become the second team behind San Francisco to win five Super Bowl Championships. Ten years later, Pittsburgh would join Dallas and San Francisco to become the third team to reach that milestone when they defeated the Seattle Seahawks in Super Bowl XL. Three years later, the Steelers would become the first team to win a sixth Super Bowl, achieving that milestone in the 2008\u201309 season. They played against the Arizona Cardinals in Super Bowl XLIII, with a 27\u201323 comeback victory. The Broncos have competed in seven playoff games against the Steelers,"}, "final_score": 1.354325285964454, "relevance_score": 0.9973477404629681, "ground_score": 0.35697754550148575, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3037194013178284, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002652150414279837, "[Relevant]": 0.9973067062697738}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.28068292492483987, "[Partially supported]": 0.14224318328383065, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5625827032000039}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001188393693143225, "[Utility:2]": 0.001389372987965246, "[Utility:3]": 0.0008263980734603991, "[Utility:4]": 0.01419757687693985, "[Utility:5]": 0.7691291699961164}], "n_tokens": 34}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8785024188731492, "ctx": {"title": "Pittsburgh Pirates", "text": "the city's other 2 more relevant sports franchises. While the team's recent struggles compared to Pittsburgh's other two teams can be partly to blame (since the Pirates last World Series championship in 1979, the Steelers have won the Super Bowl 3 times (XIV, XL, and XLIII) and the Penguins the Stanley Cup five times in 1991, 1992, 2009, 2016, and 2017, including both in 2009), distractions off the field have also caused the team's popularity to slip in the city. While the team was ranked first in Pittsburgh as recent as the late 1970s, the Pittsburgh drug trials in 1985"}, "final_score": 1.8785024188731492, "relevance_score": 0.9988664310629292, "ground_score": 0.8796359878102201, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11205600598425698, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0011335563648732698, "[Relevant]": 0.9988553528252213}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.859239993895388, "[Partially supported]": 0.03464352519123234, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.10262159057030826}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00034684159536173454, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003001663821855333, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005146049033153003, "[Utility:4]": 0.001674193654756482, "[Utility:5]": 0.7989517200175934}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The last time the Pittsburgh Steelers won the Super Bowl was on February 6, 2011.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.808656555101167, "ctx": {"title": "Pittsburgh sports lore", "text": "6-yard line, quarterback Ben Roethlisberger threw high to the right corner of the endzone where receiver Santonio Holmes made an incredible diving catch on his toes and miraculously kept both feet in bounds while maintaining control of the ball. The Steelers went ahead 27-23 and proceeded to win their record sixth Super Bowl title. On a rain-soaked vicious battle between two AFC North rivals at Paul Brown Stadium on January 9, 2016, filled with injuries and personal fouls on both sides, the Steelers held a 15-0 lead entering the fourth quarter before the Cincinnati Bengals scored three times to take"}, "final_score": 1.808656555101167, "relevance_score": 0.9991373922702658, "ground_score": 0.8095191628309013, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11794602864505703, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0008625899891506688, "[Relevant]": 0.999116843787181}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7865478220840151, "[Partially supported]": 0.039159907858578875, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.17010288582376165}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002765423164128204, "[Utility:2]": 0.000350950302632473, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004910677615485922, "[Utility:4]": 0.0022180742101746455, "[Utility:5]": 0.7162170157981099}], "n_tokens": 29}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The character has received generally positive feedback from television critics. Weeks after Hunt's first appearance on the show, Matt Roush of \"TV Guide\" commented that \"Hunt/McKidd is the most encouraging thing to happen to \"Grey's Anatomy\" in quite a while.\" He also added: \"The instant sparks between him [Hunt] and Yang were electrifying.\" On the other hand, Robert Rorke of the New York Post states that McKidd was brought in as Hunt to \"boost the sagging fortunes\" of the show's ratings. Kelley L. Carter of \"USA Today\", describes Hunt as \"hardcore\" and \"the antithesis of the other males on the show.\" Chris Monfette of IGN said that the fifth season of Grey's Anatomy was an improvement on the previous two seasons, attributing this in part to the introduction of \"fresh, new characters\", Owen and Arizona Robbins (Jessica Capshaw). He also referred to McKidd as \"the season [five]'s best, most effective addition\", adding: Margaret Lyons of \"New York Magazine\" judged Hunt \"too sad\" for the first part of the ninth season. In 2010, Kevin McKidd was nominated for the Prism Award for Best Performance in a Drama Series' Multi-Episode Storyline, and won the award. In 2011, McKidd was nominated for the Prism Award for Best Performance in a Drama Series, for his work on \"Grey's Anatomy\". McKidd was nominated, along with the rest of the \"Grey's Anatomy\" cast, for Best Drama Series at the 21st GLAAD Media Awards, in 2010. Also in 2010, McKidd, and the rest of the cast, were nominated for Outstanding Drama Series, at the NAACP Image Awards. The same nomination was received at the 2011 NAACP Image Awards, with the cast winning the award. At the 43rd NAACP Image Awards, in 2012, McKidd and the cast were nominated yet again for Outstanding Drama Series.", "question": "Who plays dr owen hunt on grey's anatomy?", "short_answers": ["Kevin McKidd"], "wikipage": "Owen Hunt"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who plays dr megan hunt on grey's anatomy?", "short_answers": ["Abigail Leigh Spencer", "Abigail Spencer"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Owen Hunt", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owen%20Hunt"}, {"title": "Abigail Spencer", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abigail%20Spencer"}, {"title": "List of Grey's Anatomy characters", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20Grey%27s%20Anatomy%20characters"}, {"title": "Nathan Riggs", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nathan%20Riggs"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Family\tMegan Hunt (sister)", "wikipage": "Owen Hunt"}], "long_answer": "The character Dr. Owen Hunt on the television series Grey's Anatomy is played by Kevin McKidd and the character's sister is played by Abigail Spencer."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Grey's Anatomy is an American television medical drama series created by Shonda Rhimes, broadcast by ABC.", "wikipage": "List of Grey's Anatomy characters"}, {"content": "He was introduced in season five as a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq, and subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, head of trauma surgery, and eventual chief of surgery, sometimes with unorthodox methods.", "wikipage": "Owen Hunt"}], "long_answer": "Grey's Anatomy is an American television medical drama series created by Shonda Rhimes, broadcast by ABC. Dr. Owen Hunt, a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq that subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, is played by Kevin McKidd. Dr. Megan Hunt is played by Abigail Spencer."}], "sample_id": "2048938326690984839", "question": "Who plays dr hunt on grey's anatomy?", "docs": [{"id": "12402431", "title": "Kevin McKidd", "text": "a strong audience, the show lost about half of its viewership throughout its run and suffered from the fractious situation in the United States due to the writer's strike at the time. Thirteen episodes were produced. His role in \"Rome\" led to McKidd's casting in the medical drama series \"Grey's Anatomy\" as Dr. Owen Hunt. He made his directorial debut in the series's seventh season, directing the episode \"Don't Deceive Me (Please Don't Go)\". He won the award for \"Best Performance in a Drama Series Multi-Episode Storyline\" at the 14th Prism Awards for his work in \"Grey's Anatomy\". He played", "score": 0.78369140625, "summary": "Kevin McKidd played Dr. Owen Hunt on Grey's Anatomy.", "extraction": "Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt in \"Grey's Anatomy\"."}], "answer": "Grey's Anatomy is an American television medical drama series created by Shonda Rhimes, broadcast by ABC. Dr. Owen Hunt, a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq that subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, is played by Kevin McKidd. Dr. Megan Hunt is played by Abigail Spencer.", "output": "Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt on Grey's Anatomy [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt on Grey's Anatomy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 632, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt, a trauma surgeon and Cristina Yang's love interest.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt, a trauma surgeon and Cristina Yang's love interest.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.971862667753622, "ctx": {"title": "Grey's Anatomy (season 5)", "text": "episodes as cardiothoracic surgeon Dr. Erica Hahn, Callie's love interest, who eventually resigns and moves away. Patrick Dempsey portrayed neurosurgeon Dr. Derek Shepherd whose relationship with Meredith Grey is the series' main storyline. Although originally introduced as a recurring character in the season premiere, trauma surgeon Dr. Owen Hunt was promoted to a series regular in the fourteenth episode of the season \"Beat Your Heart Out\". He was portrayed by Kevin McKidd and was conceived as a love interest to resident Cristina Yang. Originally only signed onto the show until December 2008, Kevin McKidd was upgraded to regular status after"}, "final_score": 1.971862667753622, "relevance_score": 0.9967013481481439, "ground_score": 0.975161319605478, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.38811657063019994, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0032984581213939706, "[Relevant]": 0.9966428116849421}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9657106603759691, "[Partially supported]": 0.017687605683811707, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.015979420097983785}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005881648657831985, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004492035126315655, "[Utility:3]": 0.00035258387689432333, "[Utility:4]": 0.001450046701146804, "[Utility:5]": 0.6475254029651276}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt on Grey's Anatomy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Owen Hunt on Grey's Anatomy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9887347862586375, "ctx": {"title": "Kevin McKidd", "text": "a strong audience, the show lost about half of its viewership throughout its run and suffered from the fractious situation in the United States due to the writer's strike at the time. Thirteen episodes were produced. His role in \"Rome\" led to McKidd's casting in the medical drama series \"Grey's Anatomy\" as Dr. Owen Hunt. He made his directorial debut in the series's seventh season, directing the episode \"Don't Deceive Me (Please Don't Go)\". He won the award for \"Best Performance in a Drama Series Multi-Episode Storyline\" at the 14th Prism Awards for his work in \"Grey's Anatomy\". He played"}, "final_score": 1.9887347862586375, "relevance_score": 0.9990526909611184, "ground_score": 0.9896820952975192, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1984537961231146, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0009472396747564306, "[Relevant]": 0.998979538047852}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9876776482784977, "[Partially supported]": 0.003268787297838296, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.00867965307186671}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00040760323807173993, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005033228159987497, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017737452763553303, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010287250464946706, "[Utility:5]": 0.848240475500069}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]In the American television medical drama Grey's Anatomy, the character of Dr. Hunt was first played by Brooke Smith, who was fired from the show after the fifth season.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The character was then recast with Kevin McKidd, who played the role from the fifth season onwards.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the American television medical drama Grey's Anatomy, the character of Dr. Hunt was first played by Brooke Smith, who was fired from the show after the fifth season.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The character was then recast with Kevin McKidd, who played the role from the fifth season onwards.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.890154314580347, "ctx": {"title": "Grey's Anatomy", "text": "from \"Grey's Anatomy\" on November 6, 2008. \"E! Online\" Kristin Dos Santos asserted that Smith's dismissal from the show had been forced by the ABC network, as part of an attempt to \"de-gay\" \"Grey's Anatomy\". Rhimes countered these claims, saying that \"we did not find that the magic and chemistry with Brooke's character would sustain in the long run\". Season five introduced actor Kevin McKidd (Dr. Owen Hunt), who was signed as a series regular after originally being cast for a specific story arc. In addition, Jessica Capshaw (Dr. Arizona Robbins) was originally introduced for a three-episode arc, but received"}, "final_score": 1.890154314580347, "relevance_score": 0.9969005839895417, "ground_score": 0.8932537305908052, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.44894836902795815, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0030991584268231904, "[Relevant]": 0.9968177344219664}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8744826812388956, "[Partially supported]": 0.03286415106712572, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.09003468009270145}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 4.685519643870944e-05, "[Utility:2]": 4.7038582492497087e-05, "[Utility:3]": 1.7237063481850583e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0007187622741530542, "[Utility:5]": 0.9733197669298865}], "n_tokens": 66}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Hunt on Grey's Anatomy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Kevin McKidd plays Dr. Hunt on Grey's Anatomy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8960967348129247, "ctx": {"title": "Grey's Anatomy (season 5)", "text": "appearing in five episodes. Shonda Rhimes said, \"I am excited to have Kevin McKidd joining us for the season, he\u2019s been a delight to collaborate with and brings incredible passion, talent and creativity to his work.\" Weeks after Hunt's first appearance on the show, Matt Roush of TVGuide comments that \"Hunt/McKidd is the most encouraging thing to happen to \"Grey's Anatomy\" in quite a while.\" Robert Rorke of the New York Post states that McKidd was brought in as Hunt to \"boost the sagging fortunes\" of the show's ratings. Kelley L. Carter of USA Today, describes Hunt as \"hardcore\" and"}, "final_score": 1.8960967348129247, "relevance_score": 0.9962927469579211, "ground_score": 0.8998039878550036, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14308175772696682, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003706821778848027, "[Relevant]": 0.9961768486299656}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8921375582782312, "[Partially supported]": 0.011954651989739235, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.09403059913863218}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00046383794522467906, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006241621779992216, "[Utility:3]": 0.00013818553772186888, "[Utility:4]": 0.0007647394864497692, "[Utility:5]": 0.9067844902837853}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Owen Hunt, M.D. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The character was created by series' producer Shonda Rhimes, and is portrayed by actor Kevin McKidd.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Owen Hunt, M.D. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series Grey's Anatomy, which airs on the American Broadcasting Company in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The character was created by series' producer Shonda Rhimes, and is portrayed by actor Kevin McKidd.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7698165222571465, "ctx": {"title": "Owen Hunt", "text": "Owen Hunt Owen Hunt, M.D. is a fictional character from the medical drama television series \"Grey's Anatomy\", which airs on the American Broadcasting Company (ABC) in the United States. The character was created by series' producer Shonda Rhimes, and is portrayed by actor Kevin McKidd. He was introduced in season five as a U.S. Army trauma surgeon who served in war-torn Iraq, and subsequently joins the fictional Seattle Grace Hospital to teach medicine as a surgical attending, head of trauma surgery, and eventual chief of surgery, sometimes with unorthodox methods. Originally contracted to appear for a multi-episode story arc, he"}, "final_score": 1.7698165222571465, "relevance_score": 0.99882132798551, "ground_score": 0.7709951942716364, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07421790629919944, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0011786345560905033, "[Relevant]": 0.9987895852717724}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7603344402081249, "[Partially supported]": 0.008059757337017854, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.22300539165558267}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023434280223894646, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002860034175356323, "[Utility:3]": 5.352894363700878e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006271341587298716, "[Utility:5]": 0.8693798561218528}], "n_tokens": 69}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Whats the most consecutive Superbowls the Patriots have won?", "short_answers": ["2"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many Superbowl winning streaks have the Patriots had?", "short_answers": ["1"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of Super Bowl champions", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20Super%20Bowl%20champions"}, {"title": "List of New England Patriots seasons", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20New%20England%20Patriots%20seasons"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The New England Patriots are a professional American football team based in the Greater Boston area. ", "wikipage": "New England Patriots"}], "long_answer": "The New England Patriots have won 2 consecutive Super Bowls and have had 1 winning streak.  "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The New England Patriots are a professional American football team based in the Greater Boston town of Foxborough, Massachusetts.", "wikipage": "List of New England Patriots seasons"}, {"content": "The Super Bowl is the annual championship game of the National Football League (NFL).", "wikipage": "Super Bowl"}], "long_answer": "The New England Patriots are a professional American football team based in the Greater Boston town of Foxborough, Massachusetts. The Super Bowl is the annual championship game of the National Football League (NFL). The most consecutive Super Bowls the Patriots have won is 2. The Patriots have 1 Super Bowl winning streak. "}], "sample_id": "-8503037420106361302", "question": "How many times has the patriots won the superbowl in a row?", "docs": [{"id": "8915770", "title": "History of the Green Bay Packers", "text": "XIII and XIV following the 1978 and 1979 seasons and remain, to date, the only team to win back-to-back Super Bowl titles more than once, the San Francisco 49ers won Super Bowls XXIII and XXIV following the 1988 and 1989 seasons, the Dallas Cowboys won Super Bowls XXVII and XXVIII following the 1992 and 1993 seasons, the Denver Broncos won Super Bowls XXXII and XXXIII following the 1997 and 1998 seasons, and most recently, the New England Patriots won Super Bowls XXXVIII and XXXIX following the 2003 and 2004 seasons. After the franchise's victory in Super Bowl II, Vince Lombardi", "score": 0.7275390625, "summary": "The New England Patriots won the Super Bowl twice in a row, following the 2003 and 2004 seasons.", "extraction": "The New England Patriots won Super Bowls XXXVIII and XXXIX following the 2003 and 2004 seasons. Therefore, the Patriots won the Super Bowl twice in a row."}], "answer": "The New England Patriots are a professional American football team based in the Greater Boston town of Foxborough, Massachusetts. The Super Bowl is the annual championship game of the National Football League (NFL). The most consecutive Super Bowls the Patriots have won is 2. The Patriots have 1 Super Bowl winning streak. ", "output": "The Green Bay Packers have won the Super Bowl the most times in a row, with four consecutive wins from 1997 to 2000 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Green Bay Packers have won the Super Bowl the most times in a row, with four consecutive wins from 1997 to 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 633, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The New England Patriots have won 11 Super Bowls, the most of any team in the NFL.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The Patriots have won 11 Super Bowls in 18 appearances, which is the most times a team has won the Super Bowl in a row.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The New England Patriots have won 11 Super Bowls, the most of any team in the NFL.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The Patriots have won 11 Super Bowls in 18 appearances, which is the most times a team has won the Super Bowl in a row.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0513732582160886, "ctx": {"title": "Vince Lombardi Trophy", "text": "(1976, 1980, 1983), the Washington Redskins (1982, 1987, 1991) and the Denver Broncos (1997, 1998, 2015) are tied for seventh with three each. Although none of these teams have ever won three straight Super Bowls, two of them have won three Lombardi trophies in four years and one twice in three years: The Dallas Cowboys (1992, 1993, 1995) and the New England Patriots (2001, 2003, 2004) and (2014, 2016). The Pittsburgh Steelers won four Super Bowls in six years (1974, 1975, 1978, 1979). The trophy has been presented on a stage constructed on the field since Super Bowl XXX in"}, "final_score": 1.0513732582160886, "relevance_score": 0.9469902330333763, "ground_score": 0.10438302518271245, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.39820722099865946, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.05300446510150542, "[Relevant]": 0.9468955181389108}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.06297663327201915, "[Partially supported]": 0.0814978188365369, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8492267987291736}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00046145759395699646, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005168079897047364, "[Utility:3]": 0.00021334387712051913, "[Utility:4]": 0.004065009716670392, "[Utility:5]": 0.9235243370524608}], "n_tokens": 61}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The New England Patriots have won the Super Bowl in 2014, 2016, and 2018, for a total of 11 times.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The New England Patriots have won the Super Bowl in 2014, 2016, and 2018, for a total of 11 times.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3178309792951506, "ctx": {"title": "Super Bowl XXXIX", "text": "13-3 record, when they beat the Falcons and Vikings in the reverse order that they did in the 2004-05 playoffs. They exacted revenge on New England in Super Bowl LII, edging them 41\u201333. This is the last time that a team has won back-to-back Super Bowls, with the Patriots losing their second playoff game a year later and then, for the next eight years, every team either losing their first playoff game or missing them altogether. The streak was broken in 2014 when the Seahawks defeated the Panthers and ultimately advanced to the Super Bowl, only to lose to a"}, "final_score": 1.3178309792951506, "relevance_score": 0.9669140238756562, "ground_score": 0.3509169554194944, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3314990775296221, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.033080364706215615, "[Relevant]": 0.9667500341882573}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.23230582030159455, "[Partially supported]": 0.23049800206550275, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5276151812369625}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0015446468122209042, "[Utility:2]": 0.0009666152755745474, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005616235652207898, "[Utility:4]": 0.00844871225343442, "[Utility:5]": 0.8001468940828534}], "n_tokens": 43}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Denver Broncos were the last team to win three consecutive Super Bowls, doing so from 1997 to 1999.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Denver Broncos were the last team to win three consecutive Super Bowls, doing so from 1997 to 1999.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1940560143413357, "ctx": {"title": "2005 Denver Broncos season", "text": "final score of 23\u20137. The Broncos entered their third consecutive year in the playoffs with the momentum of a four-game winning streak. With a record of 13\u20133, they were tied with the Seattle Seahawks for second overall in the league, behind the 14\u20132 Indianapolis Colts. They were seeded number two in the AFC behind the Colts. On January 14, 2006, the Broncos defeated the two-time defending champions, the New England Patriots, 27\u201313, ending the Patriots chance of becoming the first NFL team ever to win three consecutive Super Bowl championships. The last team with a chance of winning three consecutive"}, "final_score": 1.1940560143413357, "relevance_score": 0.8962513842182777, "ground_score": 0.297804630123058, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.26672236825121826, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.10372706914264054, "[Relevant]": 0.8960652496375232}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.165860163171684, "[Partially supported]": 0.2568892460567054, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5654984433265723}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0022370254853605123, "[Utility:2]": 0.0018837643243449541, "[Utility:3]": 0.000928895368221556, "[Utility:4]": 0.04107104307319922, "[Utility:5]": 0.7810317214667356}], "n_tokens": 36}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The New England Patriots have won 11 Super Bowls in 18 appearances, including 10 times in a row from 2011 to 2019.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The New England Patriots have won 11 Super Bowls in 18 appearances, including 10 times in a row from 2011 to 2019.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3014089881827275, "ctx": {"title": "Bill Belichick", "text": "round. They opened the season at 6\u20130, which combined with the 15 straight wins to end the previous regular season, those 21 straight wins broke the record for most wins in a row (18 regular season wins in a row), formerly held by the Miami Dolphins during and just after their perfect 1972 season with 18 straight wins (16 regular season, 1971\u201373). They defeated the Pittsburgh Steelers in the AFC Championship game. In Super Bowl XXXIX, the Patriots beat the Philadelphia Eagles and became only the second team to win three Super Bowls in four years. Belichick is the only"}, "final_score": 1.3014089881827275, "relevance_score": 0.9412234439458431, "ground_score": 0.3601855442368843, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3971787606192679, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0587676686993851, "[Relevant]": 0.941081125524564}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.1937921593043798, "[Partially supported]": 0.3296515676256837, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.4722040686443428}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.002507345548510294, "[Utility:2]": 0.0016637405206802968, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006314846738956829, "[Utility:4]": 0.017540932384605266, "[Utility:5]": 0.8517980720786685}], "n_tokens": 44}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Green Bay Packers have won the Super Bowl the most times in a row, with four consecutive wins from 1997 to 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Green Bay Packers have won the Super Bowl the most times in a row, with four consecutive wins from 1997 to 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4863099177031256, "ctx": {"title": "History of the Green Bay Packers", "text": "XIII and XIV following the 1978 and 1979 seasons and remain, to date, the only team to win back-to-back Super Bowl titles more than once, the San Francisco 49ers won Super Bowls XXIII and XXIV following the 1988 and 1989 seasons, the Dallas Cowboys won Super Bowls XXVII and XXVIII following the 1992 and 1993 seasons, the Denver Broncos won Super Bowls XXXII and XXXIII following the 1997 and 1998 seasons, and most recently, the New England Patriots won Super Bowls XXXVIII and XXXIX following the 2003 and 2004 seasons. After the franchise's victory in Super Bowl II, Vince Lombardi"}, "final_score": 1.4863099177031256, "relevance_score": 0.9693241988431797, "ground_score": 0.5169857188599459, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.37041483362832206, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.030672674375720795, "[Relevant]": 0.9692253957322585}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.34771307051851735, "[Partially supported]": 0.32664620055607846, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3141325811249015}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0015305426830210327, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006430292070379467, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005086779709454513, "[Utility:4]": 0.009825660355967375, "[Utility:5]": 0.5343714077343749}], "n_tokens": 38}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen in most of his incarnations, who voiced him in the original series. Following the production of the 2007 film, Cullen reprised his role for the sequels and supporting media and would even voice Optimus in later series such as \"\". Optimus has been voiced by a number of other voice actors in other series such as Neil Kaplan, Garry Chalk, and David Kaye in the series \"\", the Unicron Trilogy, and \"Transformers Animated\", respectively.", "question": "Who voiced Optimus Prime in the 2007 movie Transformers?", "short_answers": ["Peter Cullen"], "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}, {"context": "Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen in most of his incarnations, who voiced him in the original series. Following the production of the 2007 film, Cullen reprised his role for the sequels and supporting media and would even voice Optimus in later series such as \"\". Optimus has been voiced by a number of other voice actors in other series such as Neil Kaplan, Garry Chalk, and David Kaye in the series \"\", the Unicron Trilogy, and \"Transformers Animated\", respectively.", "question": "Who voiced Optimus Prime in the 1986 animated film The Transformers: The Movie?", "short_answers": ["Peter Cullen"], "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}, {"context": "Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen in most of his incarnations, who voiced him in the original series. Following the production of the 2007 film, Cullen reprised his role for the sequels and supporting media and would even voice Optimus in later series such as \"\". Optimus has been voiced by a number of other voice actors in other series such as Neil Kaplan, Garry Chalk, and David Kaye in the series \"\", the Unicron Trilogy, and \"Transformers Animated\", respectively.", "question": "Who voiced Optimus Prime in the 2009 movie Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen?", "short_answers": ["Peter Cullen"], "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}, {"context": "Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen in most of his incarnations, who voiced him in the original series. Following the production of the 2007 film, Cullen reprised his role for the sequels and supporting media and would even voice Optimus in later series such as \"\". Optimus has been voiced by a number of other voice actors in other series such as Neil Kaplan, Garry Chalk, and David Kaye in the series \"\", the Unicron Trilogy, and \"Transformers Animated\", respectively.", "question": "Who voiced Optimus Prime in the 2011 movie Transformers: Dark of the Moon?", "short_answers": ["Peter Cullen"], "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}, {"context": "Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen in most of his incarnations, who voiced him in the original series. Following the production of the 2007 film, Cullen reprised his role for the sequels and supporting media and would even voice Optimus in later series such as \"\". Optimus has been voiced by a number of other voice actors in other series such as Neil Kaplan, Garry Chalk, and David Kaye in the series \"\", the Unicron Trilogy, and \"Transformers Animated\", respectively.", "question": "Who voiced Optimus Prime in the 2014 movie Transformers: Age of Extinction?", "short_answers": ["Peter Cullen"], "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}, {"context": "Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen in most of his incarnations, who voiced him in the original series. Following the production of the 2007 film, Cullen reprised his role for the sequels and supporting media and would even voice Optimus in later series such as \"\". Optimus has been voiced by a number of other voice actors in other series such as Neil Kaplan, Garry Chalk, and David Kaye in the series \"\", the Unicron Trilogy, and \"Transformers Animated\", respectively.", "question": "Who voiced Optimus Prime in the 2017 movie Transformers: The Last Knight?", "short_answers": ["Peter Cullen"], "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Peter Cullen", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter%20Cullen"}, {"title": "Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformers%3A%20Revenge%20of%20the%20Fallen"}, {"title": "Optimus Prime", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimus%20Prime"}, {"title": "The Return of Optimus Prime", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Return%20of%20Optimus%20Prime"}, {"title": "Bumblebee (film)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bumblebee%20%28film%29"}, {"title": "Transformers (film)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformers%20%28film%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Peter Cullen voices Optimus Prime in the following movies: the 1986 film The Transformers: The Movie, the 2007 film Transformers, the 2009 film Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen, the 2011 film Transformers: Dark of the Moon and the 2014 film Transformers: Age of Extinction."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": " a Cybertronian, a fictional extraterrestrial species of sentient self-configuring modular robotic lifeforms (e.g.: cars and other objects), a synergistic blend of biological evolution and technological engineering. ", "wikipage": "Optimus Prime"}], "long_answer": "Optimus Prime, known in Japan as Convoy  is a Cybertronian, a fictional extraterrestrial species of sentient self-configuring modular robotic lifeforms like cars and other objects, a synergistic blend of biological evolution and technological engineering, created by the Transformer franchise. In almost every version of the mythos, Optimus is the leader of the Autobots. Since 1986 and over the history of the Transformers franchise, Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen. "}], "sample_id": "-2089902327121633962", "question": "Who voiced optimus prime in the transformers movie?", "docs": [{"id": "3425117", "title": "Peter Cullen", "text": "Peter Cullen Peter Claver Cullen (born July 28, 1941) is a Canadian voice actor. He is best known as the voice of Optimus Prime (as well as Ironhide) in the original 1980s \"Transformers\" animated series, and most other incarnations of the character. He has also voiced several other characters, including Eeyore in the \"Winnie the Pooh\" franchise, Monterey Jack in \"Chip 'n Dale Rescue Rangers\", and KARR in \"Knight Rider\". In 2007, Cullen returned to the role of Optimus Prime in various \"Transformers\" media, starting with the first live-action film. Cullen was born on July 28, 1941 in Montreal, Quebec,", "score": 0.75830078125, "summary": "Peter Cullen voiced Optimus Prime in the original 1980s \"Transformers\" animated series and most other incarnations of the character. He returned to the role in various \"Transformers\" media, starting with the first live-action film in 2007.", "extraction": "Peter Cullen voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movie."}], "answer": "Optimus Prime, known in Japan as Convoy  is a Cybertronian, a fictional extraterrestrial species of sentient self-configuring modular robotic lifeforms like cars and other objects, a synergistic blend of biological evolution and technological engineering, created by the Transformer franchise. In almost every version of the mythos, Optimus is the leader of the Autobots. Since 1986 and over the history of the Transformers franchise, Optimus Prime is primarily voiced by Peter Cullen. ", "output": "Peter Claver Cullen is a Canadian voice actor who voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movie [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Peter Claver Cullen is a Canadian voice actor who voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movie.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 635, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Peter Claver Cullen is a Canadian voice actor.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is best known as the voice of Optimus Prime in the original 1980s Transformers animated series, and most other incarnations of the character.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Peter Claver Cullen is a Canadian voice actor.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is best known as the voice of Optimus Prime in the original 1980s Transformers animated series, and most other incarnations of the character.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9891627887431462, "ctx": {"title": "Peter Cullen", "text": "actor Frank Welker. At BotCon 2010, Hasbro named Cullen as one of the first four human inductees in the Transformers Hall of Fame for his role as the voice of Optimus Prime. Peter Cullen Peter Claver Cullen (born July 28, 1941) is a Canadian voice actor. He is best known as the voice of Optimus Prime (as well as Ironhide) in the original 1980s \"Transformers\" animated series, and most other incarnations of the character. He has also voiced several other characters, including Eeyore in the \"Winnie the Pooh\" franchise, Monterey Jack in \"Chip 'n Dale Rescue Rangers\", and KARR in"}, "final_score": 1.9891627887431462, "relevance_score": 0.9995557816390368, "ground_score": 0.9896070071041093, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12160890354090481, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00044420306587769707, "[Relevant]": 0.9995213654768257}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.984799405502753, "[Partially supported]": 0.005587672539129436, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0075780077777052755}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00045087907948629266, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007580356658550571, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020886078658145408, "[Utility:4]": 0.0036022869517156383, "[Utility:5]": 0.7307482197094245}], "n_tokens": 54}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Peter Cullen voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movies from 1984 to 1990.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Cullen also voiced the character in the 2007 movie.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Peter Cullen voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movies from 1984 to 1990.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Cullen also voiced the character in the 2007 movie.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9858442137959287, "ctx": {"title": "Transformers: Prime", "text": "personal website on February 4, 2010. On May 17, 2010, it was announced that Peter Cullen, who had originated the role of Optimus Prime in the original series and voiced him in the live-action film series, would return as Optimus' voice in \"Prime\". During San Diego Comic Con on July 8, 2010, it was revealed that Frank Welker would also reprise the role of Megatron from the original series. Besides Optimus and Ratchet, Autobots Bumblebee, Arcee and Bulkhead were also announced. It was also revealed that Starscream and Soundwave would be part of the Decepticons. On September 8, 2010, the"}, "final_score": 1.9858442137959287, "relevance_score": 0.9982178035972769, "ground_score": 0.9876264101986517, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3409767073060965, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001782164769822115, "[Relevant]": 0.9982000858390069}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9771657071715328, "[Partially supported]": 0.019966010111787807, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0023845998520941564}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010126906680535978, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008169046212416962, "[Utility:3]": 0.00048023345266149026, "[Utility:4]": 0.0029881129459234526, "[Utility:5]": 0.7004152517890386}], "n_tokens": 52}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Peter Cullen voiced the character in the first three films, and Mark Ryan voiced him in the fourth and fifth films.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Peter Cullen voiced the character in the first three films, and Mark Ryan voiced him in the fourth and fifth films.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8905285987714175, "ctx": {"title": "Optimus Prime", "text": "that extend from his wrists. The trailer contains an energy axe, a shield, and flight gear. In the later IDW comics, he displays the ability to produce a holographic driver. At San Diego ComicCon 06, it was announced that original Optimus Prime voice actor, Peter Cullen, would reprise his role for the third film. Cullen has commented that Prime is basically the same in the third film as the previous two, and retains the same basic personality. The 2000s film series incarnation of Optimus Prime was met with warm reception. It was named the 30th greatest movie superhero of all"}, "final_score": 1.8905285987714175, "relevance_score": 0.9964063981747403, "ground_score": 0.8941222005966771, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5109752767572401, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003593443060206124, "[Relevant]": 0.9963623770163417}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.800791801048852, "[Partially supported]": 0.1843527458436981, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.013564772006806142}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0016294896195696788, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005208161643447779, "[Utility:3]": 0.000294442902482438, "[Utility:4]": 0.005599301450392418, "[Utility:5]": 0.8811568607074949}], "n_tokens": 31}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Peter Cullen voiced optimus prime in the transformers movie.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Peter Cullen voiced optimus prime in the transformers movie.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.989751124922365, "ctx": {"title": "Transformers (toy line)", "text": "DeSanto and Don Murphy serving as co-executive producers. In practice, this meant that they were the primary financial sponsors of the film. Shia LaBeouf portrays the lead human character, Sam Witwicky, in the film with Josh Duhamel, Tyrese Gibson, and Megan Fox in supporting roles. Peter Cullen reprises his role as the voice of Optimus Prime. Megatron's voice is provided by Hugo Weaving. It was expected that the release of a new movie would involve the production and release of associated toys from Takara and Hasbro. Images surfaced, on-line, of some toy prototypes, notably Starscream and a new Decepticon who"}, "final_score": 1.989751124922365, "relevance_score": 0.9990889488965162, "ground_score": 0.9906621760258488, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.28021615900898295, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000911010135263858, "[Relevant]": 0.9990440217835959}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9841004448887861, "[Partially supported]": 0.012101027040981665, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003282491797783934}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0018506026105133732, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008341319476403776, "[Utility:3]": 0.00036015981029885127, "[Utility:4]": 0.0028108307253788605, "[Utility:5]": 0.6931819038425114}], "n_tokens": 19}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Peter Claver Cullen is a Canadian voice actor who voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movie.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Peter Claver Cullen is a Canadian voice actor who voiced Optimus Prime in the Transformers movie.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9984431027128031, "ctx": {"title": "Peter Cullen", "text": "Peter Cullen Peter Claver Cullen (born July 28, 1941) is a Canadian voice actor. He is best known as the voice of Optimus Prime (as well as Ironhide) in the original 1980s \"Transformers\" animated series, and most other incarnations of the character. He has also voiced several other characters, including Eeyore in the \"Winnie the Pooh\" franchise, Monterey Jack in \"Chip 'n Dale Rescue Rangers\", and KARR in \"Knight Rider\". In 2007, Cullen returned to the role of Optimus Prime in various \"Transformers\" media, starting with the first live-action film. Cullen was born on July 28, 1941 in Montreal, Quebec,"}, "final_score": 1.9984431027128031, "relevance_score": 0.9997997262143598, "ground_score": 0.9986433764984435, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.291331872067907, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00020026937830905622, "[Relevant]": 0.999777724091091}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9976831832327456, "[Partially supported]": 0.0016092181550181879, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0005518030681509419}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007623725270568031, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003629437330489225, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002280133242352968, "[Utility:4]": 0.0021717972224707, "[Utility:5]": 0.695816799865697}], "n_tokens": 29}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is Mary Warren's role in the crucible, in terms of the accusations of witchcraft?", "short_answers": ["oldest accuser during the Salem witch trials"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is Mary Warren's role in the crucible, in terms of her relationships to the other characters?", "short_answers": ["The Proctor family's servant"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Crucible", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Crucible"}, {"title": "Mary Warren", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary%20Warren"}, {"title": "Mary Anne Warren", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary%20Anne%20Warren"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "True to the historical record, she is a maid for John Proctor, and becomes involved in the Salem witch hunt as one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams.", "wikipage": "Mary Warren (Salem witch trials)"}], "long_answer": "In the play the Crucible is the Proctor family's servant and the oldest accuser during the Salem witch trials, which is true to the historical record. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Crucible is a 1953 play by American playwright Arthur Miller.", "wikipage": "The Crucible"}, {"content": "Mary Ann Warren (c. 1674 \u2014 unknown) was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old when the trials began.[1] ", "wikipage": "Mary Warren (Salem witch trials)"}, {"content": "True to the historical record, she is a maid for John Proctor, and becomes involved in the Salem witch hunt as one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams.", "wikipage": "Mary Warren (Salem witch trials)"}], "long_answer": "The Crucible is a 1953 play by American playwright Arthur Miller. Mary Ann Warren was the oldest accuser during the Salem witch trials in 1691, being 18 years old when the trials began. She was the Proctor family's servant, and in the Crucible, true to historical record, she was a maid for John Proctor, and becomes involved in the Salem witch hunt as one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams."}], "sample_id": "926074333625643859", "question": "What is mary warrens role in the crucible?", "docs": [{"id": "8465547", "title": "Mary Warren", "text": "Mary Warren Mary Ann Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began. She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor. Renouncing her claims after being threatened to be hanged, she was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself, but did not confess. Her life after the trials is unknown. In early March 1692, Warren began having fits, claiming that she saw the ghost of Giles Corey. John Proctor told her she was just seeing his shadow, and put her to work at the spinning wheel, threatening to beat", "score": 0.77783203125, "summary": "Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the Salem witch trials and a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor. She eventually renounced her claims after being threatened to be hanged and was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself. Her life after the trials is unknown.", "extraction": "Mary Warren was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor and the oldest accuser during the Salem witch trials. Her role in the crucible was as an accuser during the trials."}], "answer": "The Crucible is a 1953 play by American playwright Arthur Miller. Mary Ann Warren was the oldest accuser during the Salem witch trials in 1691, being 18 years old when the trials began. She was the Proctor family's servant, and in the Crucible, true to historical record, she was a maid for John Proctor, and becomes involved in the Salem witch hunt as one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams.", "output": "Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began [1]. She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor [1]. Renouncing her claims after being threatened to be hanged, she was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself, but did not confess [1]. Her life after the trials is unknown [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor.[Continue to Use Evidence]Renouncing her claims after being threatened to be hanged, she was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself, but did not confess.[Continue to Use Evidence]Her life after the trials is unknown.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 636, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the play The Crucible by Arthur Miller, Mary Warren is a maidservant for John Proctor, who becomes involved in the Salem witch trials.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She is one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams, who falsely accuse people of witchcraft.[Continue to Use Evidence]She is a weak character who gives in to pressure and is easily influenced by Abigail.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the play The Crucible by Arthur Miller, Mary Warren is a maidservant for John Proctor, who becomes involved in the Salem witch trials.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She is one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams, who falsely accuse people of witchcraft.[Continue to Use Evidence]She is a weak character who gives in to pressure and is easily influenced by Abigail.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9334696060493712, "ctx": {"title": "Mary Warren", "text": "witchcraft and began to accuse various people, including the Proctors, of witchcraft. Mary Warren is a character in the play \"The Crucible\" by Arthur Miller. True to the historical record, she is a maid for John Proctor, and becomes involved in the Salem witch hunt as one of the accusers, led by Abigail Williams. Mary Warren has a very weak character, giving in to pressure a number of times. Proctor manages to convince her to reveal that she and the other accusers have been fabricating their stories and \"supernatural experiences\" that have resulted in the arrest of many innocents. However,"}, "final_score": 1.9334696060493712, "relevance_score": 0.9982178034763274, "ground_score": 0.9352518025730437, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.38163679672080675, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0017821146321867162, "[Relevant]": 0.9981719356171256}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8755767018689625, "[Partially supported]": 0.11757426747112541, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005899589059184206}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005214821530548191, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007676959847024442, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017062838905958157, "[Utility:4]": 0.003734708509408672, "[Utility:5]": 0.5351321050554312}], "n_tokens": 87}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor.[Continue to Use Evidence]She was the first to accuse Elizabeth Proctor of witchcraft.[Continue to Use Evidence]She was the first to recant her accusations.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor.[Continue to Use Evidence]She was the first to accuse Elizabeth Proctor of witchcraft.[Continue to Use Evidence]She was the first to recant her accusations.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9542972369972835, "ctx": {"title": "Mary Warren", "text": "Warren\u2019s confession comes to nothing. Williams accuses Warren of witchcraft, which leads to Warren to renounce her confession and accuse Proctor of forcing her to make it. Proctor is later hanged as he renounces his confession to save his heart and soul. In the 1957 and 1996 film adaptations of Miller's play, she was depicted by Pascale Petit and Karron Graves, respectively. Mary Warren Mary Ann Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began. She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor. Renouncing her claims after being threatened"}, "final_score": 1.9542972369972835, "relevance_score": 0.9974090200663658, "ground_score": 0.9568882169309176, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.33770083148133784, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002590874016377019, "[Relevant]": 0.9973682467565169}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9310417579847691, "[Partially supported]": 0.04090711839728841, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.02241525136862024}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010629103315139138, "[Utility:2]": 0.001672192670587549, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023122133122143978, "[Utility:4]": 0.015989755591244235, "[Utility:5]": 0.7467259434870693}], "n_tokens": 79}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor.[Continue to Use Evidence]Renouncing her claims after being threatened to be hanged, she was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself, but did not confess.[Continue to Use Evidence]Her life after the trials is unknown.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mary Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor.[Continue to Use Evidence]Renouncing her claims after being threatened to be hanged, she was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself, but did not confess.[Continue to Use Evidence]Her life after the trials is unknown.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9733255287005758, "ctx": {"title": "Mary Warren", "text": "Mary Warren Mary Ann Warren was the oldest accuser during the 1692 Salem witch trials, being 18 years old, when the trials began. She was a servant for John and Elizabeth Proctor. Renouncing her claims after being threatened to be hanged, she was later arrested for allegedly practicing witchcraft herself, but did not confess. Her life after the trials is unknown. In early March 1692, Warren began having fits, claiming that she saw the ghost of Giles Corey. John Proctor told her she was just seeing his shadow, and put her to work at the spinning wheel, threatening to beat"}, "final_score": 1.9733255287005758, "relevance_score": 0.9986954931375628, "ground_score": 0.9746300355630129, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08050075933321299, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0013044528630738257, "[Relevant]": 0.9986541526721475}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.959702180506837, "[Partially supported]": 0.02383870612149229, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.013372299005548979}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0014346484784723411, "[Utility:2]": 0.0014290553141145194, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016381746400801663, "[Utility:4]": 0.0045415283958453925, "[Utility:5]": 0.7757950912546586}], "n_tokens": 96}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 1996 film The Crucible, Mary Warren is a young girl who is one of the \"afflicted\" girls in Salem, Massachusetts, who claims to have seen witchcraft.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She is played by Chloe Aarons.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 1996 film The Crucible, Mary Warren is a young girl who is one of the \"afflicted\" girls in Salem, Massachusetts, who claims to have seen witchcraft.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]She is played by Chloe Aarons.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7592656720948567, "ctx": {"title": "The Crucible (1996 film)", "text": "save herself and the other girls from punishment, Abigail claims that Tituba was working with the devil. After being whipped, Tituba confesses to being a witch, and is saved from being hanged. Struck by their new power, the other girls begin naming other women whom they \"saw\" with the devil, including Elizabeth Proctor, John Proctor's wife. John, determined not to give his former lover her vengeance, insists that his servant, Mary Warren, one of the \"afflicted\" girls, testify in court that the witchcraft was faked. Although Mary Warren is frightened of Abigail, she eventually agrees. In the court, Francis Nurse"}, "final_score": 1.7592656720948567, "relevance_score": 0.9969956569203361, "ground_score": 0.7622700151745208, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.48195559591298054, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003004247102747772, "[Relevant]": 0.9969638068399835}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5449652473316142, "[Partially supported]": 0.43110299378994243, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.02163169339897701}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0012135251970778588, "[Utility:2]": 0.0017795170547582238, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023525259022399056, "[Utility:4]": 0.004932634664141745, "[Utility:5]": 0.8864985743987649}], "n_tokens": 60}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 1996 film The Crucible, Mary Warren is played by Karron Graves.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 1996 film The Crucible, Mary Warren is played by Karron Graves.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2186854166517813, "ctx": {"title": "Karron Graves", "text": "Mary Warren in 20th Century Fox\u2019s film \"The Crucible\", starring Daniel Day-Lewis, Joan Allen, Paul Scofield, and Winona Ryder. It was adapted for the screen by the original playwright Arthur Miller, directed by Nicholas Hytner, and produced by Robert Miller and David V. Picker. Other film and television appearances include NBC\u2019s \"Law & Order\", CBS\u2019s \"Guiding Light\", USA\u2019s \"Monk\", Robert De Niro's \"The Good Shepherd\", Michael Goorjian's \"5 Wishes\" , Adrian Garcia Bogliano\u2019s \"Late Phases\", Graves' stage credits include Broadway, Off-Broadway, and Regional Theatre productions. While at Yale, she originated the roles of Dora Hand in Roberto Aguirre-Sacasa\u2019s \"The Muckle"}, "final_score": 1.2186854166517813, "relevance_score": 0.9529258238447563, "ground_score": 0.2657595928070251, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.34674455198770515, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.04706708140934497, "[Relevant]": 0.9527822044097992}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.1251452805608343, "[Partially supported]": 0.2798245520370199, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5923885460018954}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005814639037595247, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003016594993448723, "[Utility:3]": 5.734826817637819e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.005603652496731038, "[Utility:5]": 0.8992342135412986}], "n_tokens": 26}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the only president to have had a child born in the White House?", "short_answers": ["Grover Cleveland"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the only child of a president born in the White House?", "short_answers": ["Esther Cleveland"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Esther Cleveland", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esther%20Cleveland"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Esther Clevland, daughter of President Grover Cleveland was the only child of a president born in the White House."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Esther Cleveland (September 9, 1893 \u2013 June 25, 1980) was the second child of Grover Cleveland, 22nd and 24th President of the United States, and his wife Frances Folsom Cleveland.", "wikipage": "Esther Cleveland"}], "long_answer": "Grover Cleveland was the only president to have a child born in the White House. The name of his child was Esther Cleveland, who was born on September 9, 1893."}], "sample_id": "-5700241817712358648", "question": "The only child of a president to be born in the whitehouse?", "docs": [{"id": "9706417", "title": "Esther Cleveland", "text": "Esther Cleveland Esther Cleveland (September 9, 1893 \u2013 June 25, 1980) was the second child of Grover Cleveland, 22nd and 24th President of the United States, and his wife Frances Folsom Cleveland. She was born on September 9, 1893 in the White House. She was the only child of a President to be born there. In April, 1896, she contracted measles when it spread through the White House, leading to a quarantine. Five years later, she contracted diphtheria. She made her debut in 1912 and was rumored to be engaged to Randolph D. West shortly after (which was denied by", "score": 0.73046875, "summary": "Esther Cleveland was the only child of a US President to be born in the White House. She was the second child of Grover Cleveland and his wife Frances Folsom Cleveland, born on September 9, 1893.", "extraction": "\"She was the only child of a President to be born there.\""}], "answer": "Grover Cleveland was the only president to have a child born in the White House. The name of his child was Esther Cleveland, who was born on September 9, 1893.", "output": "The only child of a president to be born in the White House is Esther Cleveland, daughter of Grover Cleveland [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House is Esther Cleveland, daughter of Grover Cleveland.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 638, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House is Esther Cleveland, daughter of Grover Cleveland.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House is Esther Cleveland, daughter of Grover Cleveland.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9808025607795365, "ctx": {"title": "Esther Cleveland", "text": "Esther Cleveland Esther Cleveland (September 9, 1893 \u2013 June 25, 1980) was the second child of Grover Cleveland, 22nd and 24th President of the United States, and his wife Frances Folsom Cleveland. She was born on September 9, 1893 in the White House. She was the only child of a President to be born there. In April, 1896, she contracted measles when it spread through the White House, leading to a quarantine. Five years later, she contracted diphtheria. She made her debut in 1912 and was rumored to be engaged to Randolph D. West shortly after (which was denied by"}, "final_score": 1.9808025607795365, "relevance_score": 0.998312490104439, "ground_score": 0.9824900706750975, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2008612244142114, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0016873196908658915, "[Relevant]": 0.9981999670767024}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9695674058972021, "[Partially supported]": 0.023896333383395883, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005544395136955983}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00019864853212849632, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006462746660080833, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017124573864338072, "[Utility:4]": 0.005024121254951735, "[Utility:5]": 0.7369586495822278}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House is Sasha Obama, daughter of Barack Obama.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House is Sasha Obama, daughter of Barack Obama.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6520991971499286, "ctx": {"title": "Family of Barack Obama", "text": "both delivered at University of Chicago Medical Center by their parents' friend and physician Anita Blanchard. Sasha was the youngest child to reside in the White House since John F. Kennedy Jr. arrived as an infant in 1961. In 2014 Malia and Sasha were named two of \"The 25 Most Influential Teens of 2014\" by \"Time\" magazine. Before his inauguration, President Obama published an open letter to his daughters in \"Parade\" magazine, describing what he wants for them and every child in America: \"to grow up in a world with no limits on your dreams and no achievements beyond your"}, "final_score": 1.6520991971499286, "relevance_score": 0.9901401429053045, "ground_score": 0.6619590542446241, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24065063100903597, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.009857491788454107, "[Relevant]": 0.9899026156635342}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.533773198492102, "[Partially supported]": 0.24629586753664326, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.21232030918729541}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014395507957417228, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005306954555492249, "[Utility:3]": 0.00021907698079019997, "[Utility:4]": 0.002759006214528312, "[Utility:5]": 0.759042823460818}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House was Joseph Davis, the son of Jefferson Davis, who was born in 1864.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House was Joseph Davis, the son of Jefferson Davis, who was born in 1864.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8190020547824242, "ctx": {"title": "White House of the Confederacy", "text": "during their stay at the White House of the Confederacy. When they moved in, the family consisted of the President and First Lady, six-year-old Margaret, four-year-old Jefferson Davis, Jr., and two-year-old Joseph. The two youngest Davis children, William and Varina Anne (\"Winnie\"), were born in the White House, in 1861 and 1864, respectively. Among their neighborhood playmates was George Smith Patton, Sr., whose father commanded the 22nd Virginia Infantry, and whose son commanded the U.S. Third Army in World War Two. Joseph Davis died in the spring of 1864, after a 15-foot fall from the railing on the White House's"}, "final_score": 1.8190020547824242, "relevance_score": 0.9664104942289684, "ground_score": 0.8525915605534559, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3050950580766988, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.03357434985074317, "[Relevant]": 0.9659744401674337}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7891553696983773, "[Partially supported]": 0.11914463692689697, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.087168078480891}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003061946582274583, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004687186825711227, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002508869119743276, "[Utility:4]": 0.004771019365631611, "[Utility:5]": 0.7025717179148511}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House was Eleanor Axson Sayre, daughter of President Woodrow Wilson.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House was Eleanor Axson Sayre, daughter of President Woodrow Wilson.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7387631139421287, "ctx": {"title": "Jessie Woodrow Wilson Sayre", "text": "Their November 25, 1913, wedding was the thirteenth White House wedding, and the first since Alice Roosevelt and Nicholas Longworth were wed in 1906. Upon their return from their honeymoon in Europe, they moved to Williamstown, Massachusetts, where her husband began his service as an assistant to the president of Williams College. On January 17, 1915, she gave birth in the White House to a son, Francis B. Sayre, Jr. (January 17, 1915 \u2013 October 3, 2008), who became a noted clergyman and was a social activist like his mother. The following year, a daughter, Eleanor Axson Sayre (March 26,"}, "final_score": 1.7387631139421287, "relevance_score": 0.9848109515960162, "ground_score": 0.7539521623461126, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.20553470404510418, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.015185942991046336, "[Relevant]": 0.9846096062194882}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6684368745525963, "[Partially supported]": 0.16509225908238823, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.16253274084378497}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.213396988802553e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002985761359924516, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003304936554422879, "[Utility:4]": 0.001977622093544181, "[Utility:5]": 0.8103936122698155}], "n_tokens": 33}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House was Rebecca Van Buren, daughter of Martin Van Buren.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The only child of a president to be born in the White House was Rebecca Van Buren, daughter of Martin Van Buren.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.660148057370022, "ctx": {"title": "Angelica Singleton Van Buren", "text": "Washington, she hoped to bring some European style to the White House. Angelica and other honored female guests began standing on a dais in the Blue Room to greet guests at the beginning of White House functions. Although the French Ambassador enjoyed the reception, Americans did not. The dais was soon removed. In March 1840, Angelica gave birth to the couple's first child, a daughter named Rebecca. Rebecca died a few months later. After leaving the White House, the couple had four sons, including: After Martin Van Buren was defeated for re-election in 1840, Angelica and her husband lived at"}, "final_score": 1.660148057370022, "relevance_score": 0.9640516762741995, "ground_score": 0.6960963810958224, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2244788769489503, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.03594275169460545, "[Relevant]": 0.9639022471643808}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6257170416959862, "[Partially supported]": 0.13322284188669023, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.23564719004847862}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002152777332060833, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007905352335275059, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003263415726968271, "[Utility:4]": 0.005256609293404609, "[Utility:5]": 0.8272276654322733}], "n_tokens": 32}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which actor died while the movie fast and furious 7 was being filmed?", "short_answers": ["Paul Walker"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which character was killed by Shaw in the movie fast and furious 7?", "short_answers": ["Han"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which character was killed by Hobbs in the movie fast and furious 7?", "short_answers": ["Jakande", "Mose Jakande"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "Sung Kang (Korean name: Kang Sung-Ho; \uac15\uc131\ud638; born April 8, 1972) is an American actor. He is best known for his role as Han Lue in \"The Fast and the Furious\" film franchise.", "question": "Which actor was killed by Shaw in the movie fast and furious 7?", "short_answers": ["Sung Kang"], "wikipage": "Sung Kang"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which actor was killed by Hobbs in the movie fast and furious 7?", "short_answers": ["Djimon Gaston Hounsou", "Djimon Hounsou"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Sung Kang", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sung%20Kang"}, {"title": "Furious 7", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Furious%207"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "His first major role was as Han Lue in the Fast & Furious franchise, a character he first portrayed in Better Luck Tomorrow (2002)", "wikipage": "Sung Kang"}, {"content": "In a 45 mph (72 km/h) speed zone on Hercules Street near Kelly Johnson Parkway in Valencia, Santa Clarita, California, the car crashed into a concrete lamp post and two trees subsequently setting fire to the vehicle", "wikipage": "Paul Walker"}], "long_answer": "In the movie Fast and Furious 7, the character that was killed by Shaw was Han, played by Sung Kang. The character was killed by Hobbs in the movie was Mose Jakande played by Djimon Gaston Hounsou. Sadly, during the filming of the movie the actor Paul Walker died in a car accident while the movie was still in production. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Furious 7 (also known as Fast & Furious 7), is a 2015 American action film directed by James Wan and written by Chris Morgan.", "wikipage": "Furious 7"}, {"content": "Paul William Walker IV[3] (September 12, 1973[4] \u2013 November 30, 2013) was an American actor.", "wikipage": "Paul Walker"}, {"content": "Walker died from injuries sustained from a single-vehicle collision on November 30, 2013.", "wikipage": "Paul Walker"}], "long_answer": "Furious 7, also known as Fast & Furious 7, is a 2015 American action film directed by James Wan and written by Chris Morgan. American actor Paul Walker died from injuries sustained from a single-vehicle collision on November 30, 2013 while the movie was being filmed. The character of Han, played by Sung Kang, was killed by Shaw in the movie. The character of Mose Jakande, played by Djimon Hounsou, was killed by Hobbs in the movie. "}], "sample_id": "582604295108025620", "question": "Who died in the movie fast and furious 7?", "docs": [{"id": "18391976", "title": "The Fate of the Furious", "text": "cyberterrorist Cipher (Theron) coerces him into working for her and turns him against his team, forcing them to find Dom and take down Cipher. \"The Fate of the Furious\" marks the first installment in the franchise since \"\" (2006) not to feature both Paul Walker, who died in a single-vehicle crash during the filming of \"Furious 7\" (2015) on November 30, 2013, and Jordana Brewster. Script rewrites to the seventh installment after Walker's death were intended to complete the story arcs for both of their characters (Brian O'Conner and Mia Toretto, respectively). Plans for an eighth installment were first announced", "score": 0.75732421875, "summary": "Paul Walker died in the movie \"Fast and Furious 7\" during its filming on November 30, 2013.", "extraction": "Paul Walker died in the movie \"Fast and Furious 7\"."}], "answer": "Furious 7, also known as Fast & Furious 7, is a 2015 American action film directed by James Wan and written by Chris Morgan. American actor Paul Walker died from injuries sustained from a single-vehicle collision on November 30, 2013 while the movie was being filmed. The character of Han, played by Sung Kang, was killed by Shaw in the movie. The character of Mose Jakande, played by Djimon Hounsou, was killed by Hobbs in the movie. ", "output": "Paul Walker died in the movie Fast and Furious 7 [1]. The movie was released in 2015, after Walker's death [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Paul Walker died in the movie Fast and Furious 7.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The movie was released in 2015, after Walker's death.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 639, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Furious 7 is the seventh installment of The Fast and the Furious franchise.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]In the movie, Deckard Shaw, a rogue special forces assassin, puts the team in danger once again.[Continue to Use Evidence]With the previous three installments set between 2 Fast 2 Furious (2003) and The Fast and the Furious: Tokyo Drift (2006), Fast & Furious 7 is the first installment in the franchise to take place after Tokyo Drift.[Continue to Use Evidence]Furious 7 marks the final film appearance of Paul Walker, who died in a single-vehicle crash on November 30, 2013, with filming only half-completed.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Furious 7 is the seventh installment of The Fast and the Furious franchise.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]In the movie, Deckard Shaw, a rogue special forces assassin, puts the team in danger once again.[Continue to Use Evidence]With the previous three installments set between 2 Fast 2 Furious (2003) and The Fast and the Furious: Tokyo Drift (2006), Fast & Furious 7 is the first installment in the franchise to take place after Tokyo Drift.[Continue to Use Evidence]Furious 7 marks the final film appearance of Paul Walker, who died in a single-vehicle crash on November 30, 2013, with filming only half-completed.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.076599915098517, "ctx": {"title": "Furious 7", "text": "\"Fast & Furious 6\" (2013), until Deckard Shaw (Statham), a rogue special forces assassin seeking to avenge his comatose younger brother, puts the team in danger once again. With the previous three installments set between \"2 Fast 2 Furious\" (2003) and \"\" (2006), \"Furious 7\" is the first installment in the franchise to take place after \"Tokyo Drift\". The film also marks the final film appearance of Walker, who died in a single-vehicle crash on November 30, 2013 with filming only half-completed. Following Walker's death, filming was delayed for script rewrites, and his brothers, Caleb and Cody, were used as"}, "final_score": 1.076599915098517, "relevance_score": 0.9866748498329446, "ground_score": 0.08992506526557233, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.160188819065197, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.013324650301425427, "[Relevant]": 0.9866378367532246}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.04058335785173972, "[Partially supported]": 0.09435913405324932, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8410137069498845}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023667072863425547, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011114220077289394, "[Utility:3]": 6.59783128247474e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006395792059848617, "[Utility:5]": 0.656320168661718}], "n_tokens": 159}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Paul Walker died in the movie Fast and Furious 7.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The movie was released in 2015, after Walker's death.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Paul Walker died in the movie Fast and Furious 7.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The movie was released in 2015, after Walker's death.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9928877097967599, "ctx": {"title": "The Fate of the Furious", "text": "cyberterrorist Cipher (Theron) coerces him into working for her and turns him against his team, forcing them to find Dom and take down Cipher. \"The Fate of the Furious\" marks the first installment in the franchise since \"\" (2006) not to feature both Paul Walker, who died in a single-vehicle crash during the filming of \"Furious 7\" (2015) on November 30, 2013, and Jordana Brewster. Script rewrites to the seventh installment after Walker's death were intended to complete the story arcs for both of their characters (Brian O'Conner and Mia Toretto, respectively). Plans for an eighth installment were first announced"}, "final_score": 1.9928877097967599, "relevance_score": 0.9996288144879089, "ground_score": 0.993258895308851, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4508493590096935, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0003711827204292054, "[Relevant]": 0.9996212963450881}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9883217305025761, "[Partially supported]": 0.006764128277456024, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0033484863087543055}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003271034021873531, "[Utility:2]": 0.00032203213398579624, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020310312105473972, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009031614547002449, "[Utility:5]": 0.6833807406086816}], "n_tokens": 35}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]In the movie, actor Paul Walker died in a single-vehicle collision while filming only half-completed.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the movie, actor Paul Walker died in a single-vehicle collision while filming only half-completed.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9898765180189817, "ctx": {"title": "Jordana Brewster", "text": "for the fourth time in \"Fast & Furious 6\" (2013), which follows the remaining wanted fugitives on the heist from \"Fast Five\". The film earned a worldwide total of US$789 million. Her last portrayal of Mia came in the next installment, \"Furious 7\" (2015), also the final film appearance of Walker, who died in a single-vehicle collision while filming only half-completed. After Walker's death, filming was delayed for script rewrites to the story arcs for both Walker and Brewster's characters, causing them to be retired. The highest-grossing film in the franchise, it grossed US$397.6 million worldwide during its opening weekend"}, "final_score": 1.9898765180189817, "relevance_score": 0.9970420966906679, "ground_score": 0.9928344213283139, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3211253699489658, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002957829939809488, "[Relevant]": 0.997017365489227}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9861738756842737, "[Partially supported]": 0.012317488571840394, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0010032127374508342}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0024233128606906003, "[Utility:2]": 0.0018799201540848286, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005000788738546312, "[Utility:4]": 0.004991776231832907, "[Utility:5]": 0.7096862671347371}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]In the movie, Gisele Yashar, played by Gal Gadot, dies after being shot by Deckard Shaw, played by Jason Statham.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the movie, Gisele Yashar, played by Gal Gadot, dies after being shot by Deckard Shaw, played by Jason Statham.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5142261639291834, "ctx": {"title": "Gisele Yashar", "text": "kill him. Yashar lets go of Lue's hand to shoot Adolfson, and she falls to her death. Lue later kills Adolfson in revenge for Yashar's death. At the end of the film, Roman Pearce (Tyrese Gibson) says a blessing in Yashar's honor during grace. In the 2015 film \"Furious 7\", a photo of Yashar is shown in Lue's personal belongings, and it is later placed in his coffin as part of his memorial service. In a deleted scene, it is revealed that Yashar had found Letty Ortiz (Michelle Rodriguez) after she was nearly killed by Fenix Calderon (Laz Alonso) and"}, "final_score": 1.5142261639291834, "relevance_score": 0.9309620742195172, "ground_score": 0.5832640897096661, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3981288932892569, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0690321812981746, "[Relevant]": 0.9308846110700294}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2756152693922942, "[Partially supported]": 0.6114792011954298, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.10963219276539389}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008091965719980267, "[Utility:2]": 0.00015204353115151438, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017094713710964142, "[Utility:4]": 0.002632605631158807, "[Utility:5]": 0.952032307636033}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Paul Walker died in the movie Fast and Furious 7.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Paul Walker died in the movie Fast and Furious 7.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9704048803141325, "ctx": {"title": "The Fast and the Furious", "text": "Paul Walker in a car accident in November 2013 while half way filming \"Furious 7\" forced Universal to major re-shoots, script changes, hiring Weta Digital to use CGI to create Paul's likeness and ultimately delayed releasing the movie from July 2014 to April 2015. \"Furious 7\" was the most successful film in the franchise and producers decided to retire the Paul Walker character instead of killing him off. However, the toll of directing the movie with additional re-shoots forced Director James Wan not to return to the franchise. F. Gary Gray stepped in to helm the eighth movie, \"The Fate"}, "final_score": 1.9704048803141325, "relevance_score": 0.995461567850241, "ground_score": 0.9749433124638915, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3050057997605857, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.004538279039008618, "[Relevant]": 0.9954279844754815}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9600918922790415, "[Partially supported]": 0.024035432460628087, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.012966143241176863}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0017134947907009393, "[Utility:2]": 0.00038533103698264415, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002695304586294286, "[Utility:4]": 0.0008416403634099142, "[Utility:5]": 0.540471189254374}], "n_tokens": 17}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race. This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System.", "question": "When did the third party system begin in American Politics?", "short_answers": ["1854"], "wikipage": "Third Party System"}, {"context": "The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race. This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System.", "question": "When did the third party system end in American Politics?", "short_answers": ["mid-1890s"], "wikipage": "Third Party System"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Third Party System", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third%20Party%20System"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The third party system took place in American politics between 1854 and the mid-1890s."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States beginning in 1854 until the end in the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race."}], "sample_id": "2861046199766206511", "question": "During which time period did the third party system take place in american politics answers.com?", "docs": [{"id": "7196410", "title": "Third Party System", "text": "Third Party System The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race. This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System. It was dominated by the new Republican Party, which claimed success in saving the Union, abolishing slavery and enfranchising the freedmen, while adopting", "score": 0.75439453125, "summary": "The Third Party System in American politics took place from 1854 until the mid-1890s during the Gilded Age.", "extraction": "The Third Party System took place in American politics from 1854 until the mid-1890s, during the period often termed the Gilded Age."}], "answer": "The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States beginning in 1854 until the end in the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race.", "output": "The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race [1]. This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System [1]. It was dominated by the new Republican Party, which claimed success in saving the Union, abolishing slavery and enfranchising the freedmen, while adopting a protectionist stance on economic issues [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System.[Continue to Use Evidence]It was dominated by the new Republican Party, which claimed success in saving the Union, abolishing slavery and enfranchising the freedmen, while adopting a protectionist stance on economic issues.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 641, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System.[Continue to Use Evidence]It was dominated by the new Republican Party, which claimed success in saving the Union, abolishing slavery and enfranchising the freedmen, while adopting a protectionist stance on economic issues.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System.[Continue to Use Evidence]It was dominated by the new Republican Party, which claimed success in saving the Union, abolishing slavery and enfranchising the freedmen, while adopting a protectionist stance on economic issues.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9655465305880664, "ctx": {"title": "Third Party System", "text": "Third Party System The Third Party System is a term of periodization used by historians and political scientists to describe the history of political parties in the United States from 1854 until the mid-1890s, which featured profound developments in issues of American nationalism, modernization, and race. This period, the later part of which is often termed the Gilded Age, is defined by its contrast with the eras of the Second Party System and the Fourth Party System. It was dominated by the new Republican Party, which claimed success in saving the Union, abolishing slavery and enfranchising the freedmen, while adopting"}, "final_score": 1.9655465305880664, "relevance_score": 0.9992083704576361, "ground_score": 0.9663381601304303, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07746562985254918, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0007916227466735628, "[Relevant]": 0.9991997928208456}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9579903485091119, "[Partially supported]": 0.012837076052457654, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.02717609669559661}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003043181356501015, "[Utility:2]": 0.00020114200538398592, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016005148233253454, "[Utility:4]": 0.0023064498385443463, "[Utility:5]": 0.61022900033157}], "n_tokens": 152}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The third party system in American politics took place from 1824 to 1860.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The third party system in American politics took place from 1824 to 1860.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.190329636493159, "ctx": {"title": "Two-party system", "text": "but they are often unlikely to exert much influence by gaining control of legislatures or by winning elections. While there are occasional opinions in the media expressed about the possibility of third parties emerging in the United States, for example, political insiders such as the 1980 presidential candidate John Anderson think the chances of one appearing in the early twenty-first century is remote. A report in \"The Guardian\" suggested that American politics has been \"stuck in a two-way fight between Republicans and Democrats\" since the Civil War, and that third-party runs had little meaningful success. Third parties in a two-party"}, "final_score": 1.190329636493159, "relevance_score": 0.9912208893906415, "ground_score": 0.19910874710251764, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.26902200534487536, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008778846891779157, "[Relevant]": 0.991191113894555}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.12341193441300347, "[Partially supported]": 0.14886322617440126, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7213705248949004}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010472800397001445, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006180738160550753, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005124011906945341, "[Utility:4]": 0.010251728902728371, "[Utility:5]": 0.8805717211977216}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The third party system in American politics took place from 1824 to 1860.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The third party system in American politics took place from 1824 to 1860.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2810186341113372, "ctx": {"title": "Third party (United States)", "text": "Third party (United States) Third party is a term used in the United States for American political parties other than the Republican and Democratic parties. \"This list does not include political organizations that do not run candidates for office but otherwise function similarly to third parties. For non-electoral political \"parties\", see here.\" This section includes only parties that have actually run candidates under their name in recent years. \"This section includes any party that advocates positions associated with American conservatism, including both Old Right and New Right tendencies.\" State-only parties \"This section includes any party that is independent, populist, or"}, "final_score": 1.2810186341113372, "relevance_score": 0.9915543730276772, "ground_score": 0.28946426108365997, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2882172889915707, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00844534496492898, "[Relevant]": 0.9915212640985936}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.21535832542710642, "[Partially supported]": 0.1434595724950694, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.6329733187201713}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000916175008713614, "[Utility:2]": 0.00048657687421457287, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003491018292404717, "[Utility:4]": 0.009509543516055211, "[Utility:5]": 0.8763217085162354}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Third Party System is the term used in political science and history for the period in American political history from about 1824 to 1860 that was dominated by the Democratic-Republican Party, excepting the 1824, 1828, and 1844 elections in which the Republican Party held the White House for eight years.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Third Party System is the term used in political science and history for the period in American political history from about 1824 to 1860 that was dominated by the Democratic-Republican Party, excepting the 1824, 1828, and 1844 elections in which the Republican Party held the White House for eight years.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7656871125009215, "ctx": {"title": "Fourth Party System", "text": "Fourth Party System The Fourth Party System is the term used in political science and history for the period in American political history from about 1896 to 1932 that was dominated by the Republican Party, excepting the 1912 split in which Democrats held the White House for eight years. American history texts usually call the period the Progressive Era. The concept was introduced under the name \"System of 1896\" by E.E. Schattschneider in 1960, and the numbering scheme was added by political scientists in the mid-1960s. The period featured a transformation from the issues of the Third Party System, which"}, "final_score": 1.7656871125009215, "relevance_score": 0.9936610772245954, "ground_score": 0.7720260352763261, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.22370481450639892, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006338728276729737, "[Relevant]": 0.9936305884854877}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7274824372348003, "[Partially supported]": 0.08355686990246101, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.18537899617901796}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004612646591091299, "[Utility:2]": 0.00038464904653506436, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005413836764599991, "[Utility:4]": 0.004586383005226988, "[Utility:5]": 0.5621032246255242}], "n_tokens": 85}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Third party officeholders in the United States have been rare at any point of the country's existence thus-far.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Although the current two-party system in the U.S., with the Republican Party and Democratic Party, started in 1856, many of the older parties still existed and had an influence.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Third party officeholders in the United States have been rare at any point of the country's existence thus-far.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Although the current two-party system in the U.S., with the Republican Party and Democratic Party, started in 1856, many of the older parties still existed and had an influence.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8049220906653514, "ctx": {"title": "Third party officeholders in the United States", "text": "Third party officeholders in the United States Third-party officeholders in the United States have been rare at any point of the country's existence thus-far. Although the current two-party system in the U.S., with the Republican Party and Democratic Party, started in 1856, many of the older parties still existed and had an influence. Therefore, this article includes any politician since the end of Reconstruction or since the 45th United States Congress. These galleries and lists include anyone who at one time, while in office, was an independent or affiliated with a third party. They are listed from the first year"}, "final_score": 1.8049220906653514, "relevance_score": 0.9969246296288498, "ground_score": 0.8079974610365016, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07653265345032022, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003075282532568512, "[Relevant]": 0.9968961555151823}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7936214418736881, "[Partially supported]": 0.022867840057029423, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.1798694948276591}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003611805164713288, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003333854330136384, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004005719913264166, "[Utility:4]": 0.0033539482199910894, "[Utility:5]": 0.490052783037167}], "n_tokens": 73}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who began their term of mayor of warner robins ga in 2014?", "short_answers": ["Randy Toms"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who began their term of mayor of warner robins ga in 2010?", "short_answers": ["Chuck Shaheen"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who began their term of mayor of warner robins ga in 2009?", "short_answers": ["John Havrilla"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the mayor of Warner Robins, GA in 2014?", "short_answers": ["Randy Toms"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the mayor of Warner Robins, GA in 2010?", "short_answers": ["Chuck Shaheen"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the mayor of Warner Robins, GA in December 2009?", "short_answers": ["John Havrilla"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of mayors of Warner Robins, Georgia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20mayors%20of%20Warner%20Robins%2C%20Georgia"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The mayor of Warner Robins, GA in December 2009 was John Havrilla. In 2010, the mayor was Chuck Shaheen. And in 2014 the mayor was  Randy Toms."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Warner Robins (typically /\u02c8w\u0251\u02d0rn\u025c\u02d0r \u02c8r\u0251\u02d0b\u0259nz/) is a city in the U.S. state of Georgia, located in Houston and Peach counties in the central part of the state.", "wikipage": "Warner Robins, Georgia"}, {"content": "It is currently Georgia's tenth-largest incorporated city, with an estimated population of 74,762 in 2019.[5]", "wikipage": "Warner Robins, Georgia"}], "long_answer": "There have been several Mayors of Warner Robins, Georgia, which is Georgia's tenth-largest incorporated city and is located in Houston and Peach counties in the central part of the state. John Havrilla was the Mayor in 2009. Chuck Shaheen was the Mayor in 2010. Randy Toms was the Mayor in 2014."}], "sample_id": "7994278059308814702", "question": "Who is the mayor of warner robins ga?", "docs": [{"id": "13852877", "title": "Donald Walker (politician)", "text": "political family that has participated in the politics of central Georgia throughout the second half of the 20th century. Donald Walker (politician) Donald Walker (died September 28, 2009) was a United States politician who served from 1994 to 2009 as mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia. He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound. He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem; after Walker's death, John Havrilla served as mayor pro tem", "score": 0.78125, "summary": "Donald Walker served as the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 until his death on September 28, 2009.", "extraction": "Donald Walker served from 1994 to 2009 as mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia."}], "answer": "There have been several Mayors of Warner Robins, Georgia, which is Georgia's tenth-largest incorporated city and is located in Houston and Peach counties in the central part of the state. John Havrilla was the Mayor in 2009. Chuck Shaheen was the Mayor in 2010. Randy Toms was the Mayor in 2014.", "output": "Donald Walker was the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 to 2009 [1]. He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound [1]. He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Donald Walker was the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 to 2009.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound.[Continue to Use Evidence]He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 642, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Donald Walker was the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 to 2009.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound.[Continue to Use Evidence]He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Donald Walker was the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 to 2009.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound.[Continue to Use Evidence]He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9617543938476134, "ctx": {"title": "Donald Walker (politician)", "text": "political family that has participated in the politics of central Georgia throughout the second half of the 20th century. Donald Walker (politician) Donald Walker (died September 28, 2009) was a United States politician who served from 1994 to 2009 as mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia. He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound. He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem; after Walker's death, John Havrilla served as mayor pro tem"}, "final_score": 1.9617543938476134, "relevance_score": 0.9990526911418083, "ground_score": 0.9627017027058051, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.10670996616140825, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0009471750868508317, "[Relevant]": 0.9989116129528668}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9569477725653944, "[Partially supported]": 0.008475557487388204, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03300177775324365}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014993019079365144, "[Utility:2]": 0.00027576382450030044, "[Utility:3]": 5.356377050147436e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010906670833046935, "[Utility:5]": 0.5671907701729063}], "n_tokens": 94}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Donald Walker was the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 to 2009.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound.[Continue to Use Evidence]He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem; after Walker's death, John Havrilla served as mayor pro tem until a successor, Chuck Shaheen, was elected in the November 2009 election.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Donald Walker was the mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia from 1994 to 2009.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound.[Continue to Use Evidence]He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem; after Walker's death, John Havrilla served as mayor pro tem until a successor, Chuck Shaheen, was elected in the November 2009 election.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9480578534921162, "ctx": {"title": "Donald Walker (politician)", "text": "Donald Walker (politician) Donald Walker (died September 28, 2009) was a United States politician who served from 1994 to 2009 as mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia. He was in the midst of a campaign for a fourth term in office when he committed suicide by a self-inflicted gunshot wound. He had previously temporarily stepped down as mayor during foot surgery in 2008, during which Clifford Holmes served as mayor pro tem; after Walker's death, John Havrilla served as mayor pro tem until a successor, Chuck Shaheen, was elected in the November 2009 election. Walker hailed from the Walker family, a"}, "final_score": 1.9480578534921162, "relevance_score": 0.9990149917249063, "ground_score": 0.94904286176721, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07116606507637376, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0009847832149058142, "[Relevant]": 0.9987867312041576}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9427766608645448, "[Partially supported]": 0.007783099485609659, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.046938085895231574}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00011432638099681427, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005255516384618216, "[Utility:3]": 6.425666630037436e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011169473925593993, "[Utility:5]": 0.8822489241202855}], "n_tokens": 132}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The current mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia is Lee Robinson.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The current mayor of Warner Robins, Georgia is Lee Robinson.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0410707149199019, "ctx": {"title": "Lee Robinson (politician)", "text": "establish a Mental Health Court in Bibb County when he was later appointed Circuit Public Defender for the Macon Judicial Circuit. While in private practice, Robinson was active in the Macon Sertoma Club and served as president of M.A.C.D.L., the Macon Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers. Lee Robinson served as Macon's mayor from 1987 to 1991. During his term, Macon developed its model War-on-Drugs program, which is now emulated by communities around the nation. Mayor Robinson introduced strategic planning and strategic management systems to community problems. Noteworthy programs included the Quality Labor Force Council and the Mayor's Housing Council. Additionally,"}, "final_score": 1.0410707149199019, "relevance_score": 0.9525741263175448, "ground_score": 0.08849658860235705, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3780945451615159, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.04739946828140566, "[Relevant]": 0.952043759665718}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.07996885440224898, "[Partially supported]": 0.016631932639255866, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9010062633112303}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 4.852971095429839e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00012011218816132354, "[Utility:3]": 3.6347074066312114e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009521653506608197, "[Utility:5]": 0.8690404944614687}], "n_tokens": 17}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]William Lee Robinson was the mayor of Macon, Georgia from 1987 to 1991.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]William Lee Robinson was the mayor of Macon, Georgia from 1987 to 1991.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3563312203792157, "ctx": {"title": "Lee Robinson (politician)", "text": "Lee Robinson (politician) William Lee Robinson (September 24, 1943 \u2013 November 4, 2015) was an American politician who was the Mayor of Macon, Georgia from 1987\u20131991, and a four-term State Senator of Georgia. At the time of his death, Lee Robinson was serving as the Circuit Public Defender of the Macon (Georgia) Judicial Circuit, which includes Bibb, Peach and Crawford Counties. Born on September 24, 1943, in Rome, Georgia, Lee Robinson attended Bibb County Public Schools, and after graduation from Lanier High School, Robinson left temporarily for Atlanta and Georgia Tech. He was the husband of Irene Scales Robinson. The"}, "final_score": 1.3563312203792157, "relevance_score": 0.9086178944582997, "ground_score": 0.44771332592091617, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2188612789004451, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.09113462666138152, "[Relevant]": 0.9061571967338901}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.4256795194114193, "[Partially supported]": 0.03990490550384394, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5297667213717191}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001094049656234497, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006687791403144778, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004185112919659054, "[Utility:4]": 0.004770935191399462, "[Utility:5]": 0.6248691222270307}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Warner Robins is a city in the U.S. state of Georgia, located in Houston County in the central part of the state.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]As of the 2010 census, the city had a total population of 66,588.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Warner Robins is a city in the U.S. state of Georgia, located in Houston County in the central part of the state.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]As of the 2010 census, the city had a total population of 66,588.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9868513519965855, "ctx": {"title": "Warner Robins, Georgia", "text": "Warner Robins, Georgia Warner Robins is a city in the U.S. state of Georgia, located in Houston County in the central part of the state. As of the 2010 census, the city had a total population of 66,588. From 2000 to 2010, the Warner Robins city population grew by 36.4% (from 48,804 people to 66,588). Warner Robins is a part of the larger Macon-Warner Robins Combined Statistical Area, with an estimated 2017 population of 420,693. Warner Robins Air Depot was built in 1942 just outside the city limits. Its expansion has attracted more residents. Warner Robins was founded in 1942"}, "final_score": 0.9868513519965855, "relevance_score": 0.9207896758909756, "ground_score": 0.0660616761056099, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08303758123529406, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.07908501055128626, "[Relevant]": 0.9193329537842009}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.04848746739066911, "[Partially supported]": 0.03492421262796945, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9148913845179493}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00019431499218836829, "[Utility:2]": 0.00033837948873458626, "[Utility:3]": 0.00011290104910023723, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006970270808217935, "[Utility:5]": 0.6312253015935991}], "n_tokens": 60}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "On what date did the financial crisis of 2008 actually start happening?", "short_answers": ["April 2007"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Upon what 2007 event did the financial crisis of 2008 start happening?", "short_answers": ["depreciation in the subprime mortgage market"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008. Excessive risk-taking by banks such as Lehman Brothers helped to magnify the financial impact globally. Massive bail-outs of financial institutions and other palliative monetary and fiscal policies were employed to prevent a possible collapse of the world financial system. The crisis was nonetheless followed by a global economic downturn, the Great Recession. The Asian markets (China, Hong Kong, Japan, India, etc.) were immediately impacted and volatilized after the U.S. sub-prime crisis. The European debt crisis, a crisis in the banking system of the European countries using the euro, followed later.", "question": "On what date did the financial crisis in 2008 start developing into a full-blown international banking crisis?", "short_answers": ["September 15, 2008"], "wikipage": "Financial crisis of 2007\u201308"}, {"context": "It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008. Excessive risk-taking by banks such as Lehman Brothers helped to magnify the financial impact globally. Massive bail-outs of financial institutions and other palliative monetary and fiscal policies were employed to prevent a possible collapse of the world financial system. The crisis was nonetheless followed by a global economic downturn, the Great Recession. The Asian markets (China, Hong Kong, Japan, India, etc.) were immediately impacted and volatilized after the U.S. sub-prime crisis. The European debt crisis, a crisis in the banking system of the European countries using the euro, followed later.", "question": "Upon what event did the financial crisis in 2008 start developing into a full-blown international banking crisis?", "short_answers": ["collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers"], "wikipage": "Financial crisis of 2007\u201308"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Financial crisis of 2007\u201308", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial%20crisis%20of%202007%E2%80%9308"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In April 2007 the depreciation in the subprime mortgage market initiated the financial crisis. This crisis later turned into a full-blown international banking crisis by September 15, 2008, because of the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers. "}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The 2008 financial crisis began and started developing into an international banking crisis on different dates and after different events. It actually started happening in April 2007 when the 2007 depreciation in the subprime mortgage market occurred. It started developing into a full-blown international banking crisis on September 15, 2008, upon the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers."}], "sample_id": "-859070820016775385", "question": "When did the financial crisis start in 2008?", "docs": [{"id": "13327939", "title": "Global financial crisis in September 2008", "text": "Global financial crisis in September 2008 The subprime mortgage crisis reached a critical stage during the first week of September 2008, characterized by severely contracted liquidity in the global credit markets and insolvency threats to investment banks and other institutions. The United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA), James B. Lockhart III, on September 7, 2008 announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises (GSEs), Fannie Mae (Federal National Mortgage Association) and Freddie Mac (Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation), into conservatorship run by FHFA. United States Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, at the same press conference", "score": 0.70068359375, "summary": "The financial crisis started in September 2008, with the subprime mortgage crisis reaching a critical stage characterized by contracted liquidity and insolvency threats to investment banks and other institutions. On September 7, 2008, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac were placed into conservatorship run by FHFA.", "extraction": "The financial crisis started during the first week of September 2008."}], "answer": "The 2008 financial crisis began and started developing into an international banking crisis on different dates and after different events. It actually started happening in April 2007 when the 2007 depreciation in the subprime mortgage market occurred. It started developing into a full-blown international banking crisis on September 15, 2008, upon the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers.", "output": "The financial crisis started in 2008 on September 7, 2008, when the United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, James B. Lockhart III, announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, into conservatorship run by the FHFA [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The financial crisis started in 2008 on September 7, 2008, when the United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, James B. Lockhart III, announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, into conservatorship run by the FHFA.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 643, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis of 2007\u20132008, also known as the global financial crisis and the 2008 financial crisis, is considered by many economists to have been the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression of the 1930s.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis of 2007\u20132008, also known as the global financial crisis and the 2008 financial crisis, is considered by many economists to have been the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression of the 1930s.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.897599116386809, "ctx": {"title": "Financial crisis of 2007\u20132008", "text": "subsequent material were adapted from the Wikinfo article Financial crisis of 2007\u20132008 released under the Financial crisis of 2007\u20132008 The financial crisis of 2007\u20132008, also known as the global financial crisis and the 2008 financial crisis, is considered by many economists to have been the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression of the 1930s. It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008. Excessive risk-taking by banks such as Lehman Brothers"}, "final_score": 1.897599116386809, "relevance_score": 0.9990960323207408, "ground_score": 0.898503084066068, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.03459015271712172, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0009039447357519997, "[Relevant]": 0.9990706743709258}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.814102159341952, "[Partially supported]": 0.16030622237212433, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.020863962239568355}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.715573850118317e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001408067604504586, "[Utility:3]": 0.00029404022630159505, "[Utility:4]": 0.001583369313050359, "[Utility:5]": 0.84624171865868}], "n_tokens": 125}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis of 2007\u20132008, also known as the global financial crisis and the 2008 financial crisis, is considered by many economists to have been the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression of the 1930s.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis of 2007\u20132008, also known as the global financial crisis and the 2008 financial crisis, is considered by many economists to have been the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression of the 1930s.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.876612400929214, "ctx": {"title": "Financial crisis of 2007\u20132008", "text": "Financial crisis of 2007\u20132008 The financial crisis of 2007\u20132008, also known as the global financial crisis and the 2008 financial crisis, is considered by many economists to have been the worst financial crisis since the Great Depression of the 1930s. It began in 2007 with a crisis in the subprime mortgage market in the United States, and developed into a full-blown international banking crisis with the collapse of the investment bank Lehman Brothers on September 15, 2008. Excessive risk-taking by banks such as Lehman Brothers helped to magnify the financial impact globally. Massive bail-outs of financial institutions and other palliative"}, "final_score": 1.876612400929214, "relevance_score": 0.9987648321238553, "ground_score": 0.8778475688053587, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.0306268809681614, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0012351430457705579, "[Relevant]": 0.9987447541207357}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7886995098215003, "[Partially supported]": 0.1679238132526644, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.037468871810439355}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.021138155229063e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001401785095009531, "[Utility:3]": 0.00033300285158582247, "[Utility:4]": 0.0013170511989541616, "[Utility:5]": 0.8165456679083545}], "n_tokens": 125}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis of 2008 started in the United States in September 2008, when Lehman Brothers, an American investment bank, filed for bankruptcy.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The crisis then spread globally, with many countries experiencing a severe economic downturn.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis of 2008 started in the United States in September 2008, when Lehman Brothers, an American investment bank, filed for bankruptcy.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The crisis then spread globally, with many countries experiencing a severe economic downturn.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9585331401112973, "ctx": {"title": "Global financial crisis in October 2008", "text": "Global financial crisis in October 2008 The policy response to the subprime crisis started in earnest after Lehman\u2019s failure in mid September 2008, accelerated after February 2009, and had become very large by September 2009. Governments have relied on a portfolio of intervention tools, but the biggest commitments and outlays have been in the form of debt and asset guarantees, while purchases of bad assets have been very limited. Announcements directed at the banking system as a whole (general) and at specific banks (specific) were priced by the markets. General announcements tend to be associated with positive returns and specific"}, "final_score": 1.9585331401112973, "relevance_score": 0.9993638089246493, "ground_score": 0.959169331186648, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.43722629132455143, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0006361804764301471, "[Relevant]": 0.9993471595592444}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9218005409387506, "[Partially supported]": 0.07391319742466779, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.00385652360997723}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023582089205999993, "[Utility:2]": 0.00040985644323805715, "[Utility:3]": 0.00036453386671896364, "[Utility:4]": 0.0025802735771311798, "[Utility:5]": 0.8629833400949046}], "n_tokens": 65}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis started in 2008 on September 7, 2008, when the United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, James B. Lockhart III, announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, into conservatorship run by FHFA.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis started in 2008 on September 7, 2008, when the United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, James B. Lockhart III, announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, into conservatorship run by FHFA.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9918698646511022, "ctx": {"title": "Global financial crisis in September 2008", "text": "of recession. Global financial crisis in September 2008 The subprime mortgage crisis reached a critical stage during the first week of September 2008, characterized by severely contracted liquidity in the global credit markets and insolvency threats to investment banks and other institutions. The United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA), James B. Lockhart III, on September 7, 2008 announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises (GSEs), Fannie Mae (Federal National Mortgage Association) and Freddie Mac (Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation), into conservatorship run by FHFA. United States Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, at the same"}, "final_score": 1.9918698646511022, "relevance_score": 0.998725673738097, "ground_score": 0.9931441909130052, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.15114975549515894, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0012742826389398867, "[Relevant]": 0.9986914851832877}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9873766661054928, "[Partially supported]": 0.011228878343684232, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0012403131442408574}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001414034354382534, "[Utility:2]": 0.00018334739269642867, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004887501528583292, "[Utility:4]": 0.0012977845846493428, "[Utility:5]": 0.6217473520976241}], "n_tokens": 84}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis started in 2008 on September 7, 2008, when the United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, James B. Lockhart III, announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, into conservatorship run by the FHFA.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The financial crisis started in 2008 on September 7, 2008, when the United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, James B. Lockhart III, announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, into conservatorship run by the FHFA.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9923631373975015, "ctx": {"title": "Global financial crisis in September 2008", "text": "Global financial crisis in September 2008 The subprime mortgage crisis reached a critical stage during the first week of September 2008, characterized by severely contracted liquidity in the global credit markets and insolvency threats to investment banks and other institutions. The United States director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA), James B. Lockhart III, on September 7, 2008 announced his decision to place two United States government-sponsored enterprises (GSEs), Fannie Mae (Federal National Mortgage Association) and Freddie Mac (Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation), into conservatorship run by FHFA. United States Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, at the same press conference"}, "final_score": 1.9923631373975015, "relevance_score": 0.9989266936986982, "ground_score": 0.9934364436988031, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14554467722244205, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0010732766781327424, "[Relevant]": 0.9988991234009147}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9881154395108662, "[Partially supported]": 0.010311910031055334, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0014065108187928225}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00012125574563631653, "[Utility:2]": 0.00014943870225457022, "[Utility:3]": 0.000420751559855776, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011085327137917759, "[Utility:5]": 0.6233253605974203}], "n_tokens": 85}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which soundstages was It Came From Outer Space filmed on?", "short_answers": ["Universal's sound stages"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "At which outside locations was It Came From Outer Space filmed at?", "short_answers": ["Palmdale, CA, Victorville, CA, and the Mojave Desert"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "It Came from Outer Space", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/It%20Came%20from%20Outer%20Space"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International.", "wikipage": "It Came from Outer Space"}], "long_answer": "The 1953 black-and-white science fiction horror film, It Came From Outer Space was filmed at Universal's sound stages with the outside scenes filmed at Palmdale, CA, Victorville, CA, and the Mojave Desert."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International.[1]", "wikipage": "It Came from Outer Space"}, {"content": "Filming took place on location in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert,[2] as well as on Universal's sound stages.", "wikipage": "It Came from Outer Space Production"}, {"content": "The film was nominated for AFI's Top 10 Science Fiction Films list.[10]", "wikipage": "It Came from Outer Space Reception"}], "long_answer": "It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International. Filming took place on location in and around the California towns of Palmdale, CA, Victorville, CA, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages. The film was nominated for AFI's Top 10 Science Fiction Films list."}], "sample_id": "-2447485837780724019", "question": "Where was it came from outer space filmed?", "docs": [{"id": "5274862", "title": "It Came from Outer Space", "text": "his screen treatment for \"It Came From Outer Space\". Filming took place on location in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages. The film's uncredited music score was composed by Irving Gertz, Henry Mancini, and Herman Stein. Universal's make-up department submitted two alien designs for consideration by studio executives; the rejected design was saved and then later used as the \"Metaluna Mutant\" in Universal's 1955 science fiction film \"This Island Earth\". The special effects created for the in-flight alien spacecraft consisted of a wire-mounted iron ball, with hollowed", "score": 0.81298828125, "summary": "\"It Came from Outer Space\" was filmed on location in Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert in California, as well as on Universal's sound stages.", "extraction": "Filming took place on location in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert."}], "answer": "It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International. Filming took place on location in and around the California towns of Palmdale, CA, Victorville, CA, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages. The film was nominated for AFI's Top 10 Science Fiction Films list.", "output": "It Came from Outer Space was filmed in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space was filmed in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 644, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space was filmed in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space was filmed in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.995298584016499, "ctx": {"title": "It Came from Outer Space", "text": "his screen treatment for \"It Came From Outer Space\". Filming took place on location in and around the California towns of Palmdale, Victorville, and the Mojave Desert, as well as on Universal's sound stages. The film's uncredited music score was composed by Irving Gertz, Henry Mancini, and Herman Stein. Universal's make-up department submitted two alien designs for consideration by studio executives; the rejected design was saved and then later used as the \"Metaluna Mutant\" in Universal's 1955 science fiction film \"This Island Earth\". The special effects created for the in-flight alien spacecraft consisted of a wire-mounted iron ball, with hollowed"}, "final_score": 1.995298584016499, "relevance_score": 0.9997219690140704, "ground_score": 0.9955766150024286, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11705234558036429, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0002780297911520545, "[Relevant]": 0.9997176729270929}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9921661461343178, "[Partially supported]": 0.006429059780799533, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0012079845427957715}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0006287627530029744, "[Utility:2]": 0.00018658935774063928, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002268349712780133, "[Utility:4]": 0.0021521502859337635, "[Utility:5]": 0.6709234818926026}], "n_tokens": 46}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was produced by William Alland, directed by Jack Arnold, and stars Richard Carlson, Barbara Rush, and Charles Drake.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was produced by William Alland, directed by Jack Arnold, and stars Richard Carlson, Barbara Rush, and Charles Drake.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8799113184670102, "ctx": {"title": "It Came from Outer Space", "text": "\"II\" anyway, is a clue about how clearly the makers of this new version were thinking when they made it. In other words, not very.\" It Came from Outer Space It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International. It was produced by William Alland, directed by Jack Arnold, and stars Richard Carlson, Barbara Rush, and Charles Drake. The film's script is based on Ray Bradbury's original story treatment (not, as sometimes claimed, a published short story) \"The Meteor.\" \"It Came from Outer Space\" tells the story"}, "final_score": 1.8799113184670102, "relevance_score": 0.9829546844942233, "ground_score": 0.8969566339727869, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07760291277908167, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.017044145131209807, "[Relevant]": 0.9828871923341158}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8557668146789124, "[Partially supported]": 0.07419415338857223, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.06547611055530135}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002947196230277078, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003935025117026184, "[Utility:3]": 6.411214714272956e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006933214197432875, "[Utility:5]": 0.6157251894818841}], "n_tokens": 72}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was produced by William Alland, directed by Jack Arnold, and stars Richard Carlson, Barbara Rush, and Charles Drake.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was produced by William Alland, directed by Jack Arnold, and stars Richard Carlson, Barbara Rush, and Charles Drake.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.881648625698213, "ctx": {"title": "It Came from Outer Space", "text": "It Came from Outer Space It Came from Outer Space is a 1953 American black-and-white science fiction horror film, the first in the 3D process from Universal-International. It was produced by William Alland, directed by Jack Arnold, and stars Richard Carlson, Barbara Rush, and Charles Drake. The film's script is based on Ray Bradbury's original story treatment (not, as sometimes claimed, a published short story) \"The Meteor.\" \"It Came from Outer Space\" tells the story of an astronomer and his fianc\u00e9e who are stargazing in the desert when a large fiery object crashes to Earth. At the crash site, he"}, "final_score": 1.881648625698213, "relevance_score": 0.988038902487619, "ground_score": 0.893609723210594, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07619883882135357, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.011960656041621966, "[Relevant]": 0.9880024350745195}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8642890470654466, "[Partially supported]": 0.05109988481869094, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08039140304523834}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00021438446248740777, "[Utility:2]": 0.00029997779547362126, "[Utility:3]": 5.052450453100433e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00042138743500690274, "[Utility:5]": 0.5773528100136789}], "n_tokens": 72}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space was filmed in 1953 at the Universal Studios Hollywood.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]It Came from Outer Space was filmed in 1953 at the Universal Studios Hollywood.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8774016392547308, "ctx": {"title": "It Came from Outer Space", "text": "\"It Came from Outer Space\" comes with a documentary, \"The Universe According to Universal\". It was written and directed by David J. Skal and has audio commentary by Tom Weaver, in which Weaver notes the extra's similarity to Morey Amsterdam. \"It Came from Outer Space\" was released in June 1953; by the end of the year it had accrued US$1.6 million in distributors' US and Canadian rentals, making it the year's 75th biggest earner. Barbara Rush won the Golden Globe award in 1954 as most promising female newcomer for her role in the film. The film was nominated for AFI's"}, "final_score": 1.8774016392547308, "relevance_score": 0.9724147149252496, "ground_score": 0.9049869243294814, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.33497921271501024, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.027576949645481956, "[Relevant]": 0.9721208809462294}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8730049866300337, "[Partially supported]": 0.05081498683384017, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.06891534225640704}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0013546956957560274, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005473465395349578, "[Utility:3]": 0.00028120009683907143, "[Utility:4]": 0.0035831170298909498, "[Utility:5]": 0.8431717633508118}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The 1965 film It Came from Outer Space was filmed in and around Hollywood, California.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 1965 film It Came from Outer Space was filmed in and around Hollywood, California.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.957566204594956, "ctx": {"title": "Teenagers from Outer Space", "text": "his ground location, causing a massive explosion. Derek does not survive the blast but is remembered by Betty for declaring, \"I shall make the Earth my home. And I shall never, never leave it.\" \"Teenagers from Outer Space\" was filmed on location in and around Hollywood, California, with a number of tell-tale landmarks like Bronson Canyon in Griffith Park and Hollywood High School, which gives away the film's otherwise sketchy location. One notable aspect of the film is that it was largely the work of a single person, Tom Graeff, who, in addition to playing the role of reporter Joe"}, "final_score": 1.957566204594956, "relevance_score": 0.9839708514002211, "ground_score": 0.9735953531947348, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.297184992360688, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.01602725107675401, "[Relevant]": 0.9838543693965284}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9669317863554169, "[Partially supported]": 0.011345432827715565, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.02070505777391263}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005407799380101547, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002084892387435143, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016364507849970201, "[Utility:4]": 0.003198240766565761, "[Utility:5]": 0.7674472881710934}], "n_tokens": 28}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "In the 21st century, scroll wheels patterned after the mouse's started appearing on keyboards as well, particularly on Logitech and Microsoft models. It was usually located to the left of the caps lock key. The implementation of scroll wheels on laptop computers has generally faded, while touchpads are often programmed with a pointing device gesture to substitute for them; such as allowing the edges to scroll the page (rather than to move the pointer), partly making up for the lack of a scroll wheel; touchpads with multitouch capability usually achieve scroll capability by touching and dragging two fingers on the touchpad at the same time; many Linux distributions offer a third method of scrolling using the touchpad, where the user will first activate scroll-mode by pressing in a corner of the pad, and then dragging in a circle around the center of the pad, letting go of the touchpad will switch back to the default mouse-mode.", "question": "Some computer mice have a wheel in the middle. Generally, what is this used for?", "short_answers": ["scrolling"], "wikipage": "Scroll wheel"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Some mouse cages have a wheel in the middle. Generally, what is this used for?", "short_answers": ["running", "exercise"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Hamster wheel", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamster%20wheel"}, {"title": "Mouse wheel", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mouse%20wheel"}, {"title": "Scroll wheel", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scroll%20wheel"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In computer mice, the wheel in the middle is generally used for scrolling. But for mice, as in the animal, they often have a wheel in the middle of their cage for running. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "A scroll wheel is a wheel used for scrolling.", "wikipage": "Scroll wheel"}, {"content": "It is usually located between the left and right mouse buttons and is positioned perpendicular to the mouse surface.", "wikipage": "Scroll wheel"}, {"content": "Hamster wheels or running wheel are exercise devices used primarily by hamsters and other rodents, but also by other cursorial animals when given the opportunity.", "wikipage": "Hamster wheel"}], "long_answer": "There are several reasons why a mouse has a wheel in the middle. This could refer to a computer mouse, which has a scroll wheel used for scrolling. It is usually located between the left and right mouse buttons and is positioned perpendicular to the mouse surface. This could also refer to a wheel in a mouse cage, or a hamster wheel or running wheel which are exercise devices used primarily by hamsters and other rodents."}], "sample_id": "2998178262284903248", "question": "Some mice have a wheel in the middle. generally what is this wheel for?", "docs": [{"id": "4378504", "title": "Scroll wheel", "text": "Scroll wheel A scroll wheel (or mouse wheel) is a hard plastic or rubbery disc (the \"wheel\") on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface. It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons. It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling. It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it. The wheel is oftenbut not alwaysengineered with detents to turn in discrete steps, rather than continuously as an analog axis, to allow the operator to more easily intuit how far they are scrolling. Some modern mice can", "score": 0.7578125, "summary": "A scroll wheel is a disc on a computer mouse used for scrolling and can also be used as a third mouse button. It is often engineered with detents to turn in discrete steps.", "extraction": "Irrelevant. The passage talks about a scroll wheel on a computer mouse and not a wheel in the middle of some mice."}], "answer": "There are several reasons why a mouse has a wheel in the middle. This could refer to a computer mouse, which has a scroll wheel used for scrolling. It is usually located between the left and right mouse buttons and is positioned perpendicular to the mouse surface. This could also refer to a wheel in a mouse cage, or a hamster wheel or running wheel which are exercise devices used primarily by hamsters and other rodents.", "output": "A scroll wheel, or mouse wheel, is a hard plastic or rubbery disc on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface [1]. It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons [1]. It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling [1]. It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]A scroll wheel, or mouse wheel, is a hard plastic or rubbery disc on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling.[Continue to Use Evidence]It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 645, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]A scroll wheel, or mouse wheel, is a hard plastic or rubbery disc on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling.[Continue to Use Evidence]It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]A scroll wheel, or mouse wheel, is a hard plastic or rubbery disc on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling.[Continue to Use Evidence]It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.990498682880093, "ctx": {"title": "Scroll wheel", "text": "Scroll wheel A scroll wheel (or mouse wheel) is a hard plastic or rubbery disc (the \"wheel\") on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface. It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons. It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling. It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it. The wheel is oftenbut not alwaysengineered with detents to turn in discrete steps, rather than continuously as an analog axis, to allow the operator to more easily intuit how far they are scrolling. Some modern mice can"}, "final_score": 1.990498682880093, "relevance_score": 0.9982992776516161, "ground_score": 0.9921994052284769, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08241555894138297, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0017007043349089395, "[Relevant]": 0.9982887040038918}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9831454582073434, "[Partially supported]": 0.010668756192207224, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.002436973713011005}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00013116650057448542, "[Utility:2]": 0.00012614159084795528, "[Utility:3]": 2.393389458936747e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.000812963540509406, "[Utility:5]": 0.8640934514310339}], "n_tokens": 79}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]A scroll wheel, also known as a mouse wheel, is a hard plastic or rubbery disc on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling.[Continue to Use Evidence]It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]A scroll wheel, also known as a mouse wheel, is a hard plastic or rubbery disc on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling.[Continue to Use Evidence]It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9900065393528585, "ctx": {"title": "Scroll wheel", "text": "orbit. Scroll wheel A scroll wheel (or mouse wheel) is a hard plastic or rubbery disc (the \"wheel\") on a computer mouse that is perpendicular to the mouse surface. It is normally located between the left and right mouse buttons. It is used, as the name suggests, for scrolling. It can often also be used as a third mouse button by pressing on it. The wheel is oftenbut not alwaysengineered with detents to turn in discrete steps, rather than continuously as an analog axis, to allow the operator to more easily intuit how far they are scrolling. Some modern mice"}, "final_score": 1.9900065393528585, "relevance_score": 0.9976768380002865, "ground_score": 0.992329701352572, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1009565386944903, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0023231400272102597, "[Relevant]": 0.9976674019568114}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9843367104759324, "[Partially supported]": 0.011194310457348616, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0020546245989450374}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014494708028611674, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011646825100021545, "[Utility:3]": 2.508982692683685e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0008673401348638066, "[Utility:5]": 0.876244185346531}], "n_tokens": 82}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The scroll wheel is a common feature on computer mice, and is often used for scrolling horizontally as well as vertically.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The scroll wheel is a common feature on computer mice, and is often used for scrolling horizontally as well as vertically.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9402696768620098, "ctx": {"title": "Scroll wheel", "text": "scroll horizontally as well as vertically, using a tilting scroll-wheel (introduced by Microsoft), scroll ball (found, for example, on Apple's Mighty Mouse), pointing stick, touchpad (as on Apple's Magic Mouse), optical sensor, or a second scroll wheel. Logitech mice with 'Free Spinning' scroll wheels use a 14 gram scroll wheel with high rotational inertia like a flywheel that can be used to quickly scroll through long pages and lists. A peculiar early example was a mouse by Saitek which had a joystick-style hatswitch on it. Scroll wheels are prevalent on modern computer mice and have become an integral part of"}, "final_score": 1.9402696768620098, "relevance_score": 0.9929882030557491, "ground_score": 0.9472814738062608, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5322834809972817, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0070116836982916965, "[Relevant]": 0.992972165526088}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9047335418892666, "[Partially supported]": 0.08415332701660225, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.010615635448196577}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00032829597251779084, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001547734495241726, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017098431151091434, "[Utility:4]": 0.0021075702730292693, "[Utility:5]": 0.5554365192570759}], "n_tokens": 31}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The wheel in the middle of a mouse is generally used to scroll up and down.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The wheel in the middle of a mouse is generally used to scroll up and down.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7260594655722241, "ctx": {"title": "Scroll wheel", "text": "less common use is to use the mouse wheel to adjust the player's speed of movement. Real time strategy games often use the mouse wheel to change the altitude of the camera; many also allow the view to either tilt or pan with mouse movement when the scroll wheel button is held down. CAD applications such as AutoDesk's AutoCAD use the mouse wheel to navigate the space in which the user is drawing. This has also become a de facto standard in 3D applications such as Trimble's SketchUp where the wheelscroll is zoom, and wheelclick with a mouse drag is"}, "final_score": 1.7260594655722241, "relevance_score": 0.9643214519420273, "ground_score": 0.7617380136301967, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3779358405819429, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.035677177322655275, "[Relevant]": 0.9642844036442849}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6279317443202227, "[Partially supported]": 0.26381391130314447, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.10576094950278347}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00032333508249978284, "[Utility:2]": 7.97023098838614e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002047230197971009, "[Utility:4]": 0.0015915102955240256, "[Utility:5]": 0.644574149622493}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The wheel in the middle of a mouse is generally used for scrolling.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The wheel in the middle of a mouse is generally used for scrolling.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9459441149417047, "ctx": {"title": "Personal computer", "text": "allow a user to signal the computer to carry out some operation, such as selecting an item from a menu of choices on the screen. A mouse may have a scroll wheel, to allow users to move the displayed image. The scroll wheel can also be pressed down, and used as a third button. Some mouse wheels may be tilted from side to side to allow sideways scrolling. Different programs make use of these functions differently, and may scroll horizontally by default with the scroll wheel, open different menus with different buttons, etc. These functions may be also user-defined through"}, "final_score": 1.9459441149417047, "relevance_score": 0.9985449367152269, "ground_score": 0.9473991782264778, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.41630269596741226, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0014550509425454773, "[Relevant]": 0.998536466795791}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8995118231017898, "[Partially supported]": 0.09407004179974607, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0055184781804471575}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00027689224533121914, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011363634852072954, "[Utility:3]": 8.058023728566023e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0015204375351335123, "[Utility:5]": 0.6062420181876379}], "n_tokens": 18}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What team won the 1967 Superbowl?", "short_answers": ["Green Bay Packers"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "The Packers were an NFL dynasty, turning around what had been a losing team just eight years earlier. The team had posted an NFL-worst 1\u201310\u20131 record in 1958 before legendary head coach Vince Lombardi was hired in January 1959. \"Their offense was like a conga dance,\" one sportswriter quipped. \"1, 2, 3and kick.\"", "question": "What head coach won the 1967 Superbowl?", "short_answers": ["Vince Lombardi"], "wikipage": "Super Bowl I"}, {"context": "The 1967 Pro Bowl was the seventeenth annual National Football League (NFL) all-star game which featured the outstanding performers from the 1966 season. The game was played on January 22, 1967, in a heavy rainstorm at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum in Los Angeles, California before a sparse crowd of 15,062. This was the second-lowest attendance in the history of the Pro Bowl next to the inaugural game in 1939. The final score was East 20, West 10. For the second year in a row, the East dominated the West on the strength of turnovers. They recovered two fumbles and intercepted four passes.", "question": "What league won the 1967 Superbowl?", "short_answers": ["National Football League", "National", "NFL", "National League"], "wikipage": "1967 Pro Bowl"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Super Bowl I", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super%20Bowl%20I"}, {"title": "1967 Pro Bowl", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1967%20Pro%20Bowl"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In the 1967 Super Bowl, the National Football League champion Green Bay Packers won the game, led by coach Vince Lombardi."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The First AFL-NFL World Championship Game in professional American football, known retroactively as Super Bowl I and referred to in contemporaneous reports, including the game's radio broadcast, as the Super Bowl,[6] was played on January 15, 1967 at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum in Los Angeles, California.", "wikipage": "Super Bowl I"}, {"content": "The National Football League (NFL) champion Green Bay Packers defeated the American Football League (AFL) champion Kansas City Chiefs by the score of 35\u201310.", "wikipage": "Super Bowl I"}], "long_answer": "The First AFL-NFL World Championship Game in professional American football, known retroactively as Super Bowl I and referred to in contemporaneous reports, including the game's radio broadcast, as the Super Bowl, was played on January 15, 1967 at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum in Los Angeles, California. The National Football League (NFL) champion Green Bay Packers defeated the American Football League (AFL) champion Kansas City Chiefs by the score of 35\u201310. Vince Lombardi was the Head Coach of the Green Bay Packers."}], "sample_id": "1616810287777418755", "question": "Who won the first super bowl in 1967?", "docs": [{"id": "8996718", "title": "1968 NFL Championship Game", "text": "1968 NFL Championship Game The 1968 National Football League championship game was the 36th annual championship game. The winner of the game represented the NFL in the third AFL-NFL World Championship Game also called the Super Bowl. The NFL title game was held December 29 at Cleveland Municipal Stadium in Cleveland, Ohio. The Baltimore Colts (13\u20131) won the Coastal Division and defeated the Minnesota Vikings 24\u201314 in the Western Conference championship game. The Colts were led by head coach Don Shula and reserve quarterback Earl Morrall. This was the Colts' fourth championship game appearance since joining the NFL in 1953,", "score": 0.7392578125, "summary": "Irrelevant. The document provides information about the 1968 NFL Championship Game but does not mention who won the first Super Bowl in 1967.", "extraction": "irrelevant"}], "answer": "The First AFL-NFL World Championship Game in professional American football, known retroactively as Super Bowl I and referred to in contemporaneous reports, including the game's radio broadcast, as the Super Bowl, was played on January 15, 1967 at the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum in Los Angeles, California. The National Football League (NFL) champion Green Bay Packers defeated the American Football League (AFL) champion Kansas City Chiefs by the score of 35\u201310. Vince Lombardi was the Head Coach of the Green Bay Packers.", "output": "The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, and was won by the Green Bay Packers [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, and was won by the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 646, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Green Bay Packers won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35\u201310.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Green Bay Packers won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35\u201310.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6029343972355274, "ctx": {"title": "Super Bowl VI", "text": "Super Bowl VI Super Bowl VI was an American football game between the National Football Conference (NFC) champion Dallas Cowboys and the American Football Conference (AFC) champion Miami Dolphins to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 1971 season. The Cowboys defeated the Dolphins by the score of 24\u20133, to win their first Super Bowl. The game was played on January 16, 1972, at Tulane Stadium in New Orleans, Louisiana, the second time the Super Bowl was played in that city. Despite the southerly location, it was unseasonably cold at the time, with the kickoff air temperature of"}, "final_score": 1.6029343972355274, "relevance_score": 0.9989098098967658, "ground_score": 0.6040245873387617, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.18864938852892552, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0010901637711390468, "[Relevant]": 0.9988856825559039}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.40676637137315486, "[Partially supported]": 0.37914794856495887, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.20136394348989822}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023819098594472443, "[Utility:2]": 0.00047185255996131786, "[Utility:3]": 9.987627688379118e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.002834541044025219, "[Utility:5]": 0.9296877641410309}], "n_tokens": 57}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Green Bay Packers won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35\u201310.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Green Bay Packers won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35\u201310.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6292981010390597, "ctx": {"title": "Super Bowl XVI", "text": "Super Bowl XVI Super Bowl XVI was an American football game between the National Football Conference (NFC) champion San Francisco 49ers and the American Football Conference (AFC) champion Cincinnati Bengals to decide the National Football League (NFL) champion for the 1981 season. The 49ers defeated the Bengals by the score of 26\u201321 to win their first Super Bowl. The game was played on January 24, 1982, at the Pontiac Silverdome in Pontiac, Michigan, a suburb of Detroit. It marked the first time that a Super Bowl was held at a cold-weather city. The domed stadium saved the crowd at the"}, "final_score": 1.6292981010390597, "relevance_score": 0.9988027888405677, "ground_score": 0.630495312198492, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.17784311817778967, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0011971816488749309, "[Relevant]": 0.9987781689339735}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.43668764679492544, "[Partially supported]": 0.3706119922974494, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.1792162491460371}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002074511511463529, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004730091087915185, "[Utility:3]": 9.133950567263823e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.002886235670165563, "[Utility:5]": 0.9356140551538191}], "n_tokens": 57}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967 between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Green Bay Packers.[Partially supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Green Bay Packers won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35-10.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967 between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Green Bay Packers.[Partially supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Green Bay Packers won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35-10.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.466502084885617, "ctx": {"title": "Indianapolis Colts", "text": "to win the Colts' third NFL Championship and made an appearance in Super Bowl III. Leading up to the Super Bowl and following the 34\u20130 trouncing of the Cleveland Browns in the NFL Championship, many were calling the 1968 Colts team one of the \"greatest pro football teams of all time\" and were favored by 18 points against their counterparts from the American Football League, the New York Jets. The Colts, however, were stunned by the Jets, who won the game 16\u20137 in the first Super Bowl victory for the young AFL. The result of the game surprised many in"}, "final_score": 1.466502084885617, "relevance_score": 0.9964063967557931, "ground_score": 0.4700956881298239, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.19100804147347258, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0035934824706273065, "[Relevant]": 0.996372909595666}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.19410752453075417, "[Partially supported]": 0.5401515861655606, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2531639381305942}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000248530763340667, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005666746808848035, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012088788055094291, "[Utility:4]": 0.004042542618534841, "[Utility:5]": 0.9184203395909057}], "n_tokens": 56}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967 between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Baltimore Colts.[Partially supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Kansas City Chiefs won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35-20.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967 between the Kansas City Chiefs and the Baltimore Colts.[Partially supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Kansas City Chiefs won the first Super Bowl with a score of 35-20.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6014767392099265, "ctx": {"title": "1967 NFL season", "text": "time, it was officially the \"AFL-NFL World Championship Game,\" though the more succinct \"Super Bowl\" was commonly used. The Baltimore Colts had tied for the NFL's best record in 1967 at 11\u20131\u20132, but were excluded from the postseason because of new rules introduced for breaking ties within a division. The L.A. Rams won the division title over Baltimore as a result of the Rams' 34\u201310 win over Baltimore on the last game of the regular season and a 24\u201324 tie in Baltimore in mid-October. L.A. had a 24-point edge over Baltimore in head-to-head meetings, giving them the tiebreaker and the"}, "final_score": 1.6014767392099265, "relevance_score": 0.9960851765014977, "ground_score": 0.6053915627084288, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.23139327123142045, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003914750976441232, "[Relevant]": 0.9960667240348586}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.3690438008306682, "[Partially supported]": 0.4628846275300152, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.15996863724953642}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003893596915212449, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005118036591414558, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016943558076381714, "[Utility:4]": 0.0034567956397191816, "[Utility:5]": 0.9217551655150581}], "n_tokens": 55}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, and was won by the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The first Super Bowl was played on January 15, 1967, and was won by the Green Bay Packers.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.799857820593397, "ctx": {"title": "1968 NFL Championship Game", "text": "1968 NFL Championship Game The 1968 National Football League championship game was the 36th annual championship game. The winner of the game represented the NFL in the third AFL-NFL World Championship Game also called the Super Bowl. The NFL title game was held December 29 at Cleveland Municipal Stadium in Cleveland, Ohio. The Baltimore Colts (13\u20131) won the Coastal Division and defeated the Minnesota Vikings 24\u201314 in the Western Conference championship game. The Colts were led by head coach Don Shula and reserve quarterback Earl Morrall. This was the Colts' fourth championship game appearance since joining the NFL in 1953,"}, "final_score": 1.799857820593397, "relevance_score": 0.9984752132729086, "ground_score": 0.8013826073204886, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.28519067565963496, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0015247421488000922, "[Relevant]": 0.9984460220960606}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6602403013615373, "[Partially supported]": 0.2730872718844179, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.06093400671006796}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00043363594759778315, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006848751793050009, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023668652863730565, "[Utility:4]": 0.0028609931582566565, "[Utility:5]": 0.6811790690254069}], "n_tokens": 32}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\" (King James Version). As a biblical reference, the metaphor may refer to physical armour worn by God in metaphorical battles, or it may refer to vigilant righteousness in general as bestowed by the grace of God (Romans , King James Version): \"The night is far spent, the day is at hand: let us therefore cast off the works of darkness, and let us put on the armour of light.\"", "question": "What scripture in Ephesians talks about the whole armor of god?", "short_answers": ["Ephesians 6:11"], "wikipage": "Armor of God"}, {"context": "Given the many points of contact between the Book of Wisdom and Paul's writings (particularly his Epistle to the Romans), it is perhaps unsurprising that the imagery of the Armor of God would be used here. Compare the various elements of the whole armor of God mentioned in the epistle to the Ephesians to Wisdom 5:17\u201320:", "question": "What scripture in Wisdom talks about the whole armor of god?", "short_answers": ["Wisdom 5:17\u201320"], "wikipage": "Armor of God"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Armor of God", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armor%20of%20God"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": " The Armor of God is mentioned in multiple Biblical scriptures. It is derived from Ephesians 6:11 and is also mentioned in Wisdom 5:17\u201320."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "It is one of the seven Sapiential or wisdom books in the Septuagint, the others being Psalms, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Song of Songs (Song of Solomon), Job, and Sirach.", "wikipage": "Book of Wisdom"}, {"content": "Ephesians 6 10-11is the sixth (and the last) chapter of the Epistle to the Ephesians in the New Testament of the Christian Bible.", "wikipage": "Ephesians 6"}, {"content": "The Epistle to the Ephesians, also called the Letter to the Ephesians and often shortened to Ephesians, is the tenth book of the New Testament.", "wikipage": "Epistle to the Ephesians"}], "long_answer": "Scripture in both Ephesians and Wisdom talks about the whole armor of God. The Scripture in Ephesians that does is Ephesians 6:11. Ephesians 6:10\u201311 is the sixth and last chapter of the Epistle to the Ephesians, the tenth book in the New Testament of the Christian Bible. The Scripture Wisdom 5:17\u201320 in The Book of Wisdom, one of the seven Sapiential or wisdom books in the Septuagint, also talks about the whole armor of God."}], "sample_id": "-1580949668050894237", "question": "What scripture talks about the whole armor of god?", "docs": [{"id": "12330376", "title": "Armor of God", "text": "six pieces of armor: These pieces are described in Ephesians as follows: loins girt with truth (belt of truth), breastplate of righteousness, feet shod with the preparation of the gospel of peace (peace), shield of faith, helmet of salvation, and the sword of the spirit/word of God. The helmet of Salvation and the breastplate of Righteousness also appear in Isaiah 59:17 Armor of God The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\" (King James Version). As a biblical", "score": 0.7998046875, "summary": "The scripture that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:11.", "extraction": "The scripture that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:11, which says \"Put on the whole armor of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\""}], "answer": "Scripture in both Ephesians and Wisdom talks about the whole armor of God. The Scripture in Ephesians that does is Ephesians 6:11. Ephesians 6:10\u201311 is the sixth and last chapter of the Epistle to the Ephesians, the tenth book in the New Testament of the Christian Bible. The Scripture Wisdom 5:17\u201320 in The Book of Wisdom, one of the seven Sapiential or wisdom books in the Septuagint, also talks about the whole armor of God.", "output": "The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\"[Continue to Use Evidence]The whole armor of God is described in Ephesians 6:14-17 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\"[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The whole armor of God is described in Ephesians 6:14-17.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 647, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\"[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\"[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.99053291290197, "ctx": {"title": "Armor of God", "text": "Armor of God The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\" (King James Version). As a biblical reference, the metaphor may refer to physical armour worn by God in metaphorical battles, or it may refer to vigilant righteousness in general as bestowed by the grace of God (Romans , King James Version): \"The night is far spent, the day is at hand: let us therefore cast off the works of darkness, and let us put on the armour"}, "final_score": 1.99053291290197, "relevance_score": 0.9985104823736681, "ground_score": 0.992022430528302, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.0747546164094365, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0014894939783035366, "[Relevant]": 0.998494629721934}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9876705544478399, "[Partially supported]": 0.005062760594867225, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005431549251682972}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005976992326967163, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001801641617946968, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023360556424967147, "[Utility:4]": 0.0023824869833448275, "[Utility:5]": 0.5246209928398228}], "n_tokens": 50}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The passage in the Bible that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:10-18.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The passage in the Bible that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:10-18.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8650057145015948, "ctx": {"title": "Armor of God", "text": "of light.\" The full Armour of God quote outlines these aspects of belief: truth, righteousness, preparation of the Gospel of peace, faith, salvation and the Word of God and Prayer. The armour that is available for believers to put on is God's own armour (Isaiah 59:16\u201319) for He is the Divine Warrior. The full quote as outlined in the King James Bible, is from Paul the Apostle's letter to the Ephesians : Given the many points of contact between the Book of Wisdom and Paul's writings (particularly his Epistle to the Romans), it is perhaps unsurprising that the imagery of"}, "final_score": 1.8650057145015948, "relevance_score": 0.9935117317601263, "ground_score": 0.8714939827414684, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24572592896145745, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006487954718668903, "[Relevant]": 0.993463724035483}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.830018444821815, "[Partially supported]": 0.0802791831380294, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08816943391654826}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004235881559093432, "[Utility:2]": 4.874739699018845e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001510349362495813, "[Utility:4]": 0.0017903526718039118, "[Utility:5]": 0.7423019975768927}], "n_tokens": 33}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\"[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The whole armor of God is described in Ephesians 6:14-17.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\"[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The whole armor of God is described in Ephesians 6:14-17.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9928199328619862, "ctx": {"title": "Armor of God", "text": "six pieces of armor: These pieces are described in Ephesians as follows: loins girt with truth (belt of truth), breastplate of righteousness, feet shod with the preparation of the gospel of peace (peace), shield of faith, helmet of salvation, and the sword of the spirit/word of God. The helmet of Salvation and the breastplate of Righteousness also appear in Isaiah 59:17 Armor of God The phrase \"Armor of God\" is derived from Ephesians 6:11: \"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.\" (King James Version). As a biblical"}, "final_score": 1.9928199328619862, "relevance_score": 0.9982725410123123, "ground_score": 0.994547391849674, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.18367632719596322, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0017273769785819204, "[Relevant]": 0.9982251491847871}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9904669159839425, "[Partially supported]": 0.006172176766434006, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0023610680356619725}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007869149644801104, "[Utility:2]": 0.00014274915689637516, "[Utility:3]": 0.00011270353842058021, "[Utility:4]": 0.0019025163665698772, "[Utility:5]": 0.5703797764166705}], "n_tokens": 73}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The passage in the Bible that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:10-18.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The passage in the Bible that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:10-18.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9229489831983562, "ctx": {"title": "Armor of God", "text": "the Armor of God would be used here. Compare the various elements of the whole armor of God mentioned in the epistle to the Ephesians to Wisdom 5:17\u201320: Other related passages, by Paul and references by later theologians, indicate a metaphorical context for the Armor of God. For instance, Paul's letter to the Romans indicates not a literal, but a figurative, application of the concept (Romans , New International Version): In terms of the parts of the Armor of God, the various pieces (the belt of truth, the breastplate of righteousness, the shoes of the gospel of peace, the shield"}, "final_score": 1.9229489831983562, "relevance_score": 0.9979651829552812, "ground_score": 0.9249838002430749, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2256779590232079, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0020347691356858142, "[Relevant]": 0.9979416862252358}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8852303123325211, "[Partially supported]": 0.077350559192444, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03625347745060769}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003843340686703126, "[Utility:2]": 5.150848337738401e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018368657250890098, "[Utility:4]": 0.0017090614597576265, "[Utility:5]": 0.748428611963127}], "n_tokens": 33}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The passage in the Bible that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:10-18.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The passage in the Bible that talks about the whole armor of God is Ephesians 6:10-18.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9378630369479688, "ctx": {"title": "Armor of God", "text": "of faith, the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit) are correlated to what Paul would have witnessed firsthand as the arms and armor of Roman legionaries during his life in the Roman Empire. In biblical exegesis, the vast majority of biblical scholars, Catholic, Protestant, and otherwise, agree that Paul used the concept as a reference to spiritual battle with the Devil. Christians are to put on this armor and deal well with its upkeeping. This armor seems to be in direct correlation of that of the Roman Empire's soldiers. The biblical text of Ephesians chapter 6 mentions"}, "final_score": 1.9378630369479688, "relevance_score": 0.997603269489496, "ground_score": 0.940259767458473, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21361402880816197, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002396602122278681, "[Relevant]": 0.997549829808715}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9045686704451105, "[Partially supported]": 0.06921003072552583, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.02506621202443196}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00039297954099718336, "[Utility:2]": 4.66605090815533e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014570296339062503, "[Utility:4]": 0.0014544037502619385, "[Utility:5]": 0.7622804969507571}], "n_tokens": 33}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do you pronounce the capital of burkina faso in English?", "short_answers": ["/\u02ccw\u0251\u02d0\u0261\u0259\u02c8du\u02d0\u0261u\u02d0/"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do you pronounce the capital of burkina faso in Mossi?", "short_answers": ["[\u02c8w\u0254\u0263\u0259d\u0259\u0263\u028a]"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do you pronounce the capital of burkina faso in French?", "short_answers": ["[wa\u0261adu\u0261u]"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Burkina Faso", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burkina%20Faso"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Its citizens are known as Burkinab\u00e9 or Burkinab\u00e8 (/b\u025c\u02d0r\u02c8ki\u02d0n\u0259be\u026a/ bur-KEE-n\u0259-bay), and its capital and largest city is Ouagadougou.", "wikipage": "Burkina Faso"}], "long_answer": "The pronunciation of Ouagadougou, the capital of Burkina Faso is /\u02ccw\u0251\u02d0\u0261\u0259\u02c8du\u02d0\u0261u\u02d0/ in English,  [\u02c8w\u0254\u0263\u0259d\u0259\u0263\u028a] in Mossi and [wa\u0261adu\u0261u] in French."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Burkina Faso (UK: /b\u025c\u02d0r\u02ccki\u02d0n\u0259 \u02c8f\u00e6so\u028a/, US: /- \u02c8f\u0251\u02d0so\u028a/ (About this soundlisten);[9] French: [bu\u0281kina faso]) is a landlocked country in West Africa that covers an area of around 274,200 square kilometres (105,900 sq mi) and is bordered by Mali to the northwest, Niger to the northeast, Benin to the southeast, Togo and Ghana to the south, and the Ivory Coast to the southwest.", "wikipage": "Burkina Faso"}, {"content": "Ouagadougou[2] (/\u02ccw\u0251\u02d0\u0261\u0259\u02c8du\u02d0\u0261u\u02d0/, Mossi: [\u02c8w\u0254\u0263\u0259d\u0259\u0263\u028a], French: [wa\u0261adu\u0261u]) is the capital of Burkina Faso[3] and the administrative, communications, cultural, and economic centre of the nation.", "wikipage": "Ouagadougou"}], "long_answer": "Burkina Faso is a landlocked country in West Africa that covers an area of around 274,200 square kilometres and is bordered by Mali to the northwest, Niger to the northeast, Benin to the southeast, Togo and Ghana to the south, and the Ivory Coast to the southwest. Ouagadougou is the capital of Burkina Faso and the administrative, communications, cultural, and economic centre of the nation. In English, Ouagadougou is pronounced /\u02ccw\u0251\u02d0\u0261\u0259\u02c8du\u02d0\u0261u\u02d0/. In Mossi, Ouagadougou is pronounced [\u02c8w\u0254\u0263\u0259d\u0259\u0263\u028a]. In French, Ouagadougou is pronounced [wa\u0261adu\u0261u]."}], "sample_id": "-1910862419822972998", "question": "How do you pronounce the capital of burkina faso?", "docs": [{"id": "665376", "title": "Ouagadougou", "text": "Ouagadougou Ouagadougou (, , ) is the capital of Burkina Faso and the administrative, communications, cultural, and economic centre of the nation. It is also the country's largest city, with a population of 2,200,000 in 2015. The city's name is often shortened to \"Ouaga\". The inhabitants are called \"ouagalais\". The spelling of the name \"Ouagadougou\" is derived from the French orthography common in former French African colonies. Ouagadougou's primary industries are food processing and textiles. It is served by an international airport and it is linked by rail to Abidjan in the Ivory Coast. There is no rail service to", "score": 0.720703125, "summary": "The document provides information about Ouagadougou, the capital of Burkina Faso, its population, industries, and transportation links. However, it does not provide information on how to pronounce the city's name. Therefore, the answer is \"irrelevant\".", "extraction": "The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced \"Ouagadougou\"."}], "answer": "Burkina Faso is a landlocked country in West Africa that covers an area of around 274,200 square kilometres and is bordered by Mali to the northwest, Niger to the northeast, Benin to the southeast, Togo and Ghana to the south, and the Ivory Coast to the southwest. Ouagadougou is the capital of Burkina Faso and the administrative, communications, cultural, and economic centre of the nation. In English, Ouagadougou is pronounced /\u02ccw\u0251\u02d0\u0261\u0259\u02c8du\u02d0\u0261u\u02d0/. In Mossi, Ouagadougou is pronounced [\u02c8w\u0254\u0263\u0259d\u0259\u0263\u028a]. In French, Ouagadougou is pronounced [wa\u0261adu\u0261u].", "output": "The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Ouagadougou\" [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Ouagadougou\".[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 648, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Wa-gadu-dougou\" in French, and \"Wa-gadu-doug-oo\" in English.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Wa-gadu-dougou\" in French, and \"Wa-gadu-doug-oo\" in English.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0136245817051164, "ctx": {"title": "Culture of Burkina Faso", "text": "for disabled artisans in Burkina Faso. The Popular Theatre in Ouagadougou, the capital of Burkina Faso, is a center of social and cultural events for the country. Ouagadougou, is a major hub of activity for those involved in African cinema, and hosts a Pan-African film festival every year called FESPACO, the Pan-African film and television Festival of Ouagadougou, which is a world-renowned affair. Culture and art are further displayed at Laongo, an area of exposed granites where artists from the entire world are invited to sculpt on the rock. Idrissa Ouedraogo, probably the most commercially successful African director, is from"}, "final_score": 1.0136245817051164, "relevance_score": 0.9671630461023408, "ground_score": 0.046461535602775585, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.41483298273037095, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.03283161882746982, "[Relevant]": 0.9670059096410365}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.029383995424164772, "[Partially supported]": 0.034086031017895986, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9357868928737796}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001670656856639458, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004224082043611488, "[Utility:3]": 0.00039372774007813947, "[Utility:4]": 0.03685410007761862, "[Utility:5]": 0.9069541983960796}], "n_tokens": 46}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Ouagadougou\".[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Ouagadougou\".[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3783308044781428, "ctx": {"title": "Ouagadougou", "text": "Ouagadougou Ouagadougou (, , ) is the capital of Burkina Faso and the administrative, communications, cultural, and economic centre of the nation. It is also the country's largest city, with a population of 2,200,000 in 2015. The city's name is often shortened to \"Ouaga\". The inhabitants are called \"ouagalais\". The spelling of the name \"Ouagadougou\" is derived from the French orthography common in former French African colonies. Ouagadougou's primary industries are food processing and textiles. It is served by an international airport and it is linked by rail to Abidjan in the Ivory Coast. There is no rail service to"}, "final_score": 1.3783308044781428, "relevance_score": 0.9906599773384119, "ground_score": 0.3876708271397308, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24232059379270746, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.009338842001652178, "[Relevant]": 0.9905347493183361}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.36719209239691386, "[Partially supported]": 0.03931122587056204, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5913734324449547}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001169361420160244, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002888120298897279, "[Utility:3]": 0.000461520089584811, "[Utility:4]": 0.016525345695658392, "[Utility:5]": 0.8344480711723152}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Wagadu\" in the local language, Moore, and as \"Wagadu-Dougou\" in French.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Wagadu\" in the local language, Moore, and as \"Wagadu-Dougou\" in French.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0044253937228222, "ctx": {"title": "Centre-Ouest Region", "text": "in the region. The main languages spoken in Centre-Ouest as of 2006 were Moore, Lyele, and Numi. French is the official language throughout the country. The main road connecting the national capital, Ouagadougou, and Burkina Faso's second largest city Bobo-Dioulasso traverses the province (east to west). Burkina Faso's only railway connects the region's largest city, Koudougou with Ouagadougou, Bobo-Dioulasso and Ivory Coast. There is no commercial airport in the province but Ouagadougou International Airport is approximately 141 km southeast of Koudougou and as of June 2014 the airport had regularly scheduled flights to most major cities in West Africa as"}, "final_score": 1.0044253937228222, "relevance_score": 0.9504109724510764, "ground_score": 0.0540144212717458, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3303305600534908, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.04958326052378544, "[Relevant]": 0.9503004430811629}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.0284281831925769, "[Partially supported]": 0.05107619406214803, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9196043598758149}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007401647848058494, "[Utility:2]": 0.00018861065554877626, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002402962090567922, "[Utility:4]": 0.024415332463333484, "[Utility:5]": 0.9379050778287616}], "n_tokens": 42}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Wagadu\" in the local language, Mossi.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Wagadu\" in the local language, Mossi.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3305909547110089, "ctx": {"title": "Languages of Burkina Faso", "text": "Languages of Burkina Faso Burkina Faso is a multilingual country. An estimated 70 languages are spoken there, of which about 66 are indigenous. The Mossi language () is spoken by about 40% of the population, mainly in the central region around the capital, Ouagadougou. French is the official language. English is also widely spoken. In the west, Mande languages are widely spoken, the most predominant being Dyula (also spelled Jula or Dioula), others including Bobo, Samo, and Marka. The Fula language (, ) is widespread, particularly in the north. The Gourmanch\u00e9 language is spoken in the east, while the Bissa"}, "final_score": 1.3305909547110089, "relevance_score": 0.9796676455442832, "ground_score": 0.3509233091667256, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.36617864129647354, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.02032880993703906, "[Relevant]": 0.979496861079791}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2817895823724248, "[Partially supported]": 0.13415195239967564, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5781946467219441}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0012682063361815626, "[Utility:2]": 0.00019832162815975888, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005412040325814017, "[Utility:4]": 0.014457268789147293, "[Utility:5]": 0.5865877765146345}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Ouagadougou\" in French, which is the official language of Burkina Faso.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The capital of Burkina Faso is pronounced as \"Ouagadougou\" in French, which is the official language of Burkina Faso.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0302753912164302, "ctx": {"title": "Burkina Faso", "text": "Burkina Faso Burkina Faso (, ; ) is a landlocked country in West Africa. It covers an area of around and is surrounded by six countries: Mali to the north; Niger to the east; Benin to the southeast; Togo and Ghana to the south; and Ivory Coast to the southwest. In 2017, its population was estimated at just over 20 million. Burkina Faso is a francophone country, with French as the official language of government and business. Formerly called the Republic of Upper Volta (1958\u20131984), the country was renamed \"Burkina Faso\" on 4 August 1984 by then-President Thomas Sankara. Its"}, "final_score": 1.0302753912164302, "relevance_score": 0.9136765292901317, "ground_score": 0.11659886192629859, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.25072767678876773, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.08631218327896995, "[Relevant]": 0.9135570593406144}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.10108075304563251, "[Partially supported]": 0.030587983400099647, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8664091420437}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004940630940484827, "[Utility:2]": 0.00010747654146005276, "[Utility:3]": 0.00027284755422339737, "[Utility:4]": 0.00935877308088977, "[Utility:5]": 0.7730753275669778}], "n_tokens": 39}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "In December 2016, advocate and singer-songwriter Paul Congemi announced his bid for mayor. In January 2017, current incumbent mayor Rick Kriseman filed for re-election. In March 2017, African People's Socialist Party activist Jesse Nevel filed for election, as well as Anthony Cates II, a salesman. In May 2017, former St. Petersburg mayor Rick Baker filed for re-election, and Ernisa Barnwell. In June 2017, activist Theresa Lassiter announced her bid for mayoral office.", "question": "Who won the 2017 mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "short_answers": ["Kriseman", "Rick Kriseman"], "wikipage": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election"}, {"context": "The 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election took place on August 27 and November 5, 2013 to elect the mayor of St. Petersburg, Florida. A non-partisan primary election was held on August 27, 2013. No candidate won a majority of the vote, so the top two finishers, incumbent Mayor Bill Foster and former State Representative Rick Kriseman, advanced to a runoff.", "question": "Who won the 2013 mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "short_answers": ["Kriseman", "Rick Kriseman"], "wikipage": "2013 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who won the 2009 mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "short_answers": ["Foster", "Bill Foster"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who won the 2013 mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "short_answers": ["Richard David \"Rick\" Kriseman"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who won the 2017 mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "short_answers": ["Richard David \"Rick\" Kriseman"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who won the 2009 mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "short_answers": ["Bill Foster"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "2013 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2013%20St.%20Petersburg%2C%20Florida%20mayoral%20election"}, {"title": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017%20St.%20Petersburg%2C%20Florida%20mayoral%20election"}, {"title": "List of mayors of St. Petersburg, Florida", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20mayors%20of%20St.%20Petersburg%2C%20Florida"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In 2009, Bill Foster won the St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election. Then in both 2013 and 2017 elections, Richard David \"Rick\" Kriseman won. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "St. Petersburg is a city in Pinellas County, Florida, United States.", "wikipage": "St. Petersburg, Florida"}], "long_answer": "St. Petersburg is a city in Pinellas County, Florida, United States. The 2009 Mayor race was won by Bill Foster. The 2013 and 2017 Mayor races were both won by Richard David \"Rick\" Kriseman. "}], "sample_id": "-1422989764069069118", "question": "Who won the mayor race in st petersburg florida?", "docs": [{"id": "20007340", "title": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "text": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election The 2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election was held on November 7, 2017, to elect the next mayor of St. Petersburg, Florida, in a general election, with a primary election which took place on August 29, 2017. Incumbent mayor Rick Kriseman, a Democrat, opted to seek re-election. His opponent in the runoff was Republican former mayor Rick Baker (2001\u20132010). The election was officially non-partisan. Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the re-election. In the 2013 election, then incumbent mayor Bill Foster opted to seek re-election for that year's mayoral election. With no candidates having a majority", "score": 0.818359375, "summary": "Rick Kriseman won the 2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election.", "extraction": "Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the re-election."}], "answer": "St. Petersburg is a city in Pinellas County, Florida, United States. The 2009 Mayor race was won by Bill Foster. The 2013 and 2017 Mayor races were both won by Richard David \"Rick\" Kriseman. ", "output": "In the 2017 election, Rick Kriseman won the re-election [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]In the 2017 election, Rick Kriseman won the re-election.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 649, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election, incumbent Mayor Bill Foster lost to Rick Kriseman by 56% to 44%.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election, incumbent Mayor Bill Foster lost to Rick Kriseman by 56% to 44%.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8880033707227866, "ctx": {"title": "2013 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "text": "2013 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election The 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election took place on November 5, 2013 to elect the mayor of St. Petersburg, Florida. A non-partisan primary election was held on August 27, 2013. No candidate won a majority of the vote, so the top two finishers, incumbent Mayor Bill Foster and former State Representative Rick Kriseman, advanced to a runoff. After a campaign described as \"nasty\", \"partisan\", \"contentious\" and \"the costliest in [St. Petersburg] history\", Foster lost to Kriseman by 56% to 44%, becoming the first incumbent mayor of St. Petersburg to lose a race for re-election"}, "final_score": 1.8880033707227866, "relevance_score": 0.9980882308656799, "ground_score": 0.8899151398571067, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1826675389216334, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001911695620498403, "[Relevant]": 0.9980498510849072}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8269116827460511, "[Partially supported]": 0.12100392720415001, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04927345516742087}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00011107671611243067, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003090980680066832, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002847544838251129, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018067467329210862, "[Utility:5]": 0.682062804237297}], "n_tokens": 41}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 election, Rick Kriseman won the re-election.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2017 election, Rick Kriseman won the re-election.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9538474603194633, "ctx": {"title": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "text": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election The 2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election was held on November 7, 2017, to elect the next mayor of St. Petersburg, Florida, in a general election, with a primary election which took place on August 29, 2017. Incumbent mayor Rick Kriseman, a Democrat, opted to seek re-election. His opponent in the runoff was Republican former mayor Rick Baker (2001\u20132010). The election was officially non-partisan. Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the re-election. In the 2013 election, then incumbent mayor Bill Foster opted to seek re-election for that year's mayoral election. With no candidates having a majority"}, "final_score": 1.9538474603194633, "relevance_score": 0.9990746149850563, "ground_score": 0.954772845334407, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.22515860855727018, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0009253568986379591, "[Relevant]": 0.9990442597416722}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9328886031182114, "[Partially supported]": 0.040988265854783615, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.024667172894558432}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016423747506593388, "[Utility:2]": 0.00032662522057321007, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014607583419438852, "[Utility:4]": 0.002048270175625831, "[Utility:5]": 0.6460661898330027}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election, Rick Kriseman won the election with 53% of the vote.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election, Rick Kriseman won the election with 53% of the vote.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6076999291316398, "ctx": {"title": "2013 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "text": "\"safe, credible alternative for those unimpressed with the incumbent\", promising to govern like popular former Mayor Rick Baker. 2013 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election The 2013 St. Petersburg mayoral election took place on November 5, 2013 to elect the mayor of St. Petersburg, Florida. A non-partisan primary election was held on August 27, 2013. No candidate won a majority of the vote, so the top two finishers, incumbent Mayor Bill Foster and former State Representative Rick Kriseman, advanced to a runoff. After a campaign described as \"nasty\", \"partisan\", \"contentious\" and \"the costliest in [St. Petersburg] history\", Foster lost to Kriseman"}, "final_score": 1.6076999291316398, "relevance_score": 0.9978172834238246, "ground_score": 0.6098826457078151, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3631631770630894, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0021826628355448973, "[Relevant]": 0.9977927161801601}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.49844587387631517, "[Partially supported]": 0.2177548884111973, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.27960281132798026}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 8.705301114278317e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00021842421921688173, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018037407368271284, "[Utility:4]": 0.0012817356071932438, "[Utility:5]": 0.6261695454311328}], "n_tokens": 34}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the 2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the 2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8072133459687674, "ctx": {"title": "2017 St. Petersburg, Florida mayoral election", "text": "in the primary election, Foster and former Florida House of Representatives member Rick Kriseman faced off in the general election, where Kriseman was elected on November 5, 2013, by a 10 point margin. He assumed office on January 2, 2014. In December 2016, advocate and singer-songwriter Paul Congemi announced his bid for mayor. In January 2017, current incumbent mayor Rick Kriseman filed for re-election. In March 2017, African People's Socialist Party activist Jesse Nevel filed for election, as well as Anthony Cates II, a salesman. In May 2017, former St. Petersburg mayor Rick Baker filed for re-election, and Ernisa Barnwell."}, "final_score": 1.8072133459687674, "relevance_score": 0.9950945942147211, "ground_score": 0.8121187517540461, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2207733226568962, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.004904904457418523, "[Relevant]": 0.994992896482547}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7539367041360371, "[Partially supported]": 0.11294160528075035, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.13101455418975066}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.797690520413126e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00031757358991013976, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002473265605048141, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009667987383791616, "[Utility:5]": 0.7696398545825394}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the 2017 mayoral election with 51.62% of the vote.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Incumbent Rick Kriseman won the 2017 mayoral election with 51.62% of the vote.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9462151766805316, "ctx": {"title": "Rick Kriseman", "text": "re-election in the 2017 St. Petersburg mayoral election. In August 2017, Barack Obama endorsed Kriseman for re-election as mayor of St. Petersburg. On November 7, 2017, Kriseman was re-elected for a second term, winning the general election with 51.62% of the vote. He defeated former Republican Mayor Rick Baker, who had 48.38% of the vote in a nonpartisan election. Rick Kriseman Rick Kriseman (born August 2, 1962) is an American politician who currently serves as the Mayor of St. Petersburg, Florida. A member of the Democratic Party, he previously served as a member of the Florida House of Representatives, representing"}, "final_score": 1.9462151766805316, "relevance_score": 0.9985335417259597, "ground_score": 0.9476816349545718, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.23313023797249222, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0014663763423465826, "[Relevant]": 0.9984777532007441}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9277603772017198, "[Partially supported]": 0.03799524762969594, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03326975045925515}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.293383759531701e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00034732022439401176, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001940694221654021, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010050043926353382, "[Utility:5]": 0.7341708252383401}], "n_tokens": 34}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "When does the new season 1 of the expanse start?", "short_answers": ["December 14, 2015"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When does the new season 2 of the expanse start?", "short_answers": ["February 1, 2017"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When does the new season 3 of the expanse start?", "short_answers": ["April 11, 2018"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When does the new season 4 of the expanse start?", "short_answers": ["December 12, 2019"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did season 2 of the Expanse start?", "short_answers": ["February 1, 2017"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did season 1 of the Expanse start?", "short_answers": ["December 14, 2015"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Expanse (TV series)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Expanse%20%28TV%20series%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Expanse is an American science fiction television series developed by Mark Fergus and Hawk Ostby, based on the series of novels of the same name by James S. A. Corey. ", "wikipage": "The Expanse (TV series)"}], "long_answer": "There has been multiple seasons of the American science fiction television series The Expanse. Season one came out on December 14, 2015, season two came out February 1, 2017, season three came out April 11, 2018 and season four came out December 12, 2019."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Expanse is an American science fiction television series developed by Mark Fergus and Hawk Ostby, based on the series of novels of the same name by James S. A. Corey.", "wikipage": "The Expanse (TV series)"}], "long_answer": "The Expanse is an American science fiction television series developed by Mark Fergus and Hawk Ostby, based on the series of novels of the same name by James S. A. Corey. Season 1 started on December 14, 2015. Season 2 started on February 1, 2017. Season 3 started on April 11, 2018 and Season 4 started on December 12, 2019."}], "sample_id": "8369552384426926962", "question": "When does the new season of the expanse start?", "docs": [{"id": "18371626", "title": "The Expanse (TV series)", "text": "serve as writers and showrunners alongside Naren Shankar. Produced by Alcon Television and The Sean Daniel Company, principal photography started on October 29, 2014, in Toronto. The pilot episode was screened at San Diego Comic-Con in July 2015. In May 2015, before the first season aired, writing commenced for a second season, which was ordered in December 2015. The second season of \"The Expanse\" premiered on February 1, 2017. On March 16, 2017, \"The Expanse\" was renewed by Syfy for a 13-episode third season to air in 2018. Filming for season 3 began on July 12, 2017. Four digital comics", "score": 0.76318359375, "summary": "The third season of \"The Expanse\" will air in 2018.", "extraction": "The new season of The Expanse starts in 2018."}], "answer": "The Expanse is an American science fiction television series developed by Mark Fergus and Hawk Ostby, based on the series of novels of the same name by James S. A. Corey. Season 1 started on December 14, 2015. Season 2 started on February 1, 2017. Season 3 started on April 11, 2018 and Season 4 started on December 12, 2019.", "output": "The third season of the science fiction television series The Expanse premiered on December 13, 2018 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The third season of the science fiction television series The Expanse premiered on December 13, 2018.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 650, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The third season of the science fiction television series The Expanse premiered on December 13, 2018.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The third season of the science fiction television series The Expanse premiered on December 13, 2018.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5443041787234928, "ctx": {"title": "The Expanse (TV series)", "text": "serve as writers and showrunners alongside Naren Shankar. Produced by Alcon Television and The Sean Daniel Company, principal photography started on October 29, 2014, in Toronto. The pilot episode was screened at San Diego Comic-Con in July 2015. In May 2015, before the first season aired, writing commenced for a second season, which was ordered in December 2015. The second season of \"The Expanse\" premiered on February 1, 2017. On March 16, 2017, \"The Expanse\" was renewed by Syfy for a 13-episode third season to air in 2018. Filming for season 3 began on July 12, 2017. Four digital comics"}, "final_score": 1.5443041787234928, "relevance_score": 0.9899864300877278, "ground_score": 0.554317748635765, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2744803927971295, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.010013292870917944, "[Relevant]": 0.9899590405369728}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5175646190257985, "[Partially supported]": 0.06842217340143572, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.4094273127218345}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002659243066847971, "[Utility:2]": 7.784316379896325e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017439741811016313, "[Utility:4]": 0.00024307449422352522, "[Utility:5]": 0.7927094222063623}], "n_tokens": 31}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Expanse is an American science fiction media franchise that began with a series of novels written by Daniel Abraham and Ty Franck under the pen name James S. A. Corey.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Expanse is an American science fiction media franchise that began with a series of novels written by Daniel Abraham and Ty Franck under the pen name James S. A. Corey.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1413724091298358, "ctx": {"title": "Daniel Abraham (author)", "text": "a television series. Syfy gave a straight-to-series order for a 10-episode first season of the show in April 2014. The series premiered on demand on November 23, 2015, and on Syfy on December 14, 2015. \"The Expanse\" was developed by Mark Fergus & Hawk Ostby, who wrote the pilot and serve as writers, executive producers, and showrunners alongside Naren Shankar. Writing for a second season commenced in May 2015, before the first season aired, and was officially ordered for in December 2015, with an increased order of 13 episodes. SyFy aired the second-season premiere on February 1, 2017. Abraham and"}, "final_score": 1.1413724091298358, "relevance_score": 0.976129166523221, "ground_score": 0.16524324260661483, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.25158996535621597, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.023869394511688877, "[Relevant]": 0.9760703241792605}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.06649985222945345, "[Partially supported]": 0.1954539350944455, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7318951422062583}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0033103638403696642, "[Utility:2]": 0.0010789241151245247, "[Utility:3]": 0.00046950943011661567, "[Utility:4]": 0.002170989609648643, "[Utility:5]": 0.5417010900347647}], "n_tokens": 44}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Expanse is an American science fiction media franchise that was adapted from the book series of the same name by James S.A. Corey.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The series premiered on streaming services on November 23, 2015 and on Syfy on December 14, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Expanse is an American science fiction media franchise that was adapted from the book series of the same name by James S.A. Corey.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The series premiered on streaming services on November 23, 2015 and on Syfy on December 14, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1277572490874523, "ctx": {"title": "The Expanse (novel series)", "text": "more novellas. The series was adapted for television by the Syfy Network, also under the title of \"The Expanse\". The series premiered on streaming services on November 23, 2015 and on Syfy on December 14, 2015. Syfy did not renew \"The Expanse\" for a fourth season, but on May 26, 2018, Jeff Bezos announced that Amazon would be picking up the series. Four digital comics based on the books and tying into the television series were published by ComiXology. The comics were written by James S.A. Corey, Hallie Lambert and Georgia Lee and illustrated by Huang Danlan, Triona Farrell, Juan"}, "final_score": 1.1277572490874523, "relevance_score": 0.9676556656367313, "ground_score": 0.1601015834507209, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21085755241172066, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.03234200665220268, "[Relevant]": 0.967586026769669}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.06964485460329042, "[Partially supported]": 0.17784436652328772, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7429259543344506}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00011459939081387602, "[Utility:2]": 0.000135824602178486, "[Utility:3]": 0.00024024923496253952, "[Utility:4]": 0.0008550915937084339, "[Utility:5]": 0.5469682472077041}], "n_tokens": 72}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Expanse is a science fiction media franchise that began with a series of novels written by James S.A. Corey.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The first season of the television series premiered on December 14, 2015, on Syfy, and was released on Amazon Prime Video on December 23, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Expanse is a science fiction media franchise that began with a series of novels written by James S.A. Corey.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The first season of the television series premiered on December 14, 2015, on Syfy, and was released on Amazon Prime Video on December 23, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.8415517168727615, "ctx": {"title": "The Expanse (TV series)", "text": "The Original Television Soundtrack\", consisting of nineteen tracks, was released by Lakeshore Records, on May 20, 2016 via iTunes, and on May 26, 2016 via Amazon. In the United States, \"The Expanse\" is broadcast by Syfy and streamed on Amazon Prime. In Canada, the series airs on Space and streams on CraveTV. In New Zealand, the series airs on Sky. In all other countries where Netflix is available, seasons 1 and 2 were streamed until September 30, 2018. The first season received a rating of 65 out of 100 on Metacritic based on reviews from 23 critics, indicating \"generally favorable"}, "final_score": 0.8415517168727615, "relevance_score": 0.7879311981328135, "ground_score": 0.053620518739947956, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.31586138376274336, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.21204799055660417, "[Relevant]": 0.7878538747324008}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.011500020996331078, "[Partially supported]": 0.0836568958634464, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8993964776474619}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014287214083645835, "[Utility:2]": 0.00016316510630622752, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003257625886130329, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006230375794474035, "[Utility:5]": 0.6294334501518072}], "n_tokens": 79}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The seventh and latest book in the series, \"Persepolis Rising\", was released on December 5, 2017.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The seventh and latest book in the series, \"Persepolis Rising\", was released on December 5, 2017.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.7825362415695504, "ctx": {"title": "James S. A. Corey", "text": "The seventh and latest book in the series, \"Persepolis Rising\", was released on December 5, 2017. As other authors in recent years have done, between each pair of full-length books they published shorter works in the series. The first, a short story entitled \"The Butcher of Anderson Station: A Story of The Expanse\" was released as an eBook in October 2011. A 69-page novella, \"Gods of Risk\" followed, and was released as an eBook on September 2012. A short story entitled \"Drive\" was released in November 2012 as a part of the anthology \"Edge of Infinity\". Another novella, \"The Churn\","}, "final_score": 0.7825362415695504, "relevance_score": 0.6548947357776795, "ground_score": 0.1276415057918708, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1174947608176613, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.3449681176178305, "[Relevant]": 0.6546344772460903}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.11052685376579828, "[Partially supported]": 0.032165148186912136, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8492222431338422}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0018450885566530892, "[Utility:2]": 0.00012385489496743828, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003000281499763713, "[Utility:4]": 0.0007691473422121107, "[Utility:5]": 0.7385692904774632}], "n_tokens": 35}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League?", "short_answers": ["Cristiano Ronaldo"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Women's Champions League?", "short_answers": ["Ada Hegerberg"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the most successful career goal scorer in the history of the eufa champions league?", "short_answers": ["Cristiano Ronaldo dos Santos Aveiro", "Cristiano Ronaldo", "Ronaldo"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the most successful single season goal scorer in the history of the eufa champions league?", "short_answers": ["Cristiano Ronaldo dos Santos Aveiro", "Cristiano Ronaldo", "Ronaldo"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the most successful goal scorer in terms of tournament championships in the history of the eufa champions league?", "short_answers": ["Francisco \"Paco\" Gento L\u00f3pez", "Gento", "Francisco Gento"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the most successful goal scorer in terms of matches won in the history of the eufa champions league?", "short_answers": ["Cristiano Ronaldo dos Santos Aveiro", "Cristiano Ronaldo", "Ronaldo"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of UEFA Champions League top scorers", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20UEFA%20Champions%20League%20top%20scorers"}, {"title": "UEFA Champions League", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA%20Champions%20League"}, {"title": "European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics ...", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European%20Cup%20and%20UEFA%20Champions%20League%20records%20and%20statistics"}, {"title": "UEFA Women's Champions League", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA%20Women%27s%20Champions%20League"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Multiple players have scored record high goals. Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League, in a single season and throughout his career. The player in the  the UEFA Women's Champions that is the most successful goal scorer is Ada Hegerberg. And the most successful goal scorer in terms of tournament championships in the history of the eufa champions league is Francisco \"Paco\" Gento L\u00f3pez. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Ronaldo holds the records for most goals (134) and assists (42) in the Champions League, most goals in the European Championship (14), and is currently tied with Ali Daei for most international goals (109).", "wikipage": "Cristiano Ronaldo"}, {"content": "She holds the record for most goals in a UEFA Women's Champions League season (15), and is currently the all-time highest goalscorer in UEFA Women's Champions League (53).", "wikipage": "Ada Hegerberg"}], "long_answer": "Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League with 134 goals. He is also the most successful single-season goal scorer and goal scorer in terms of matches won in the league, while Francisco Gento is the most successful goal scorer in the league in terms of tournament championships. In the history of the UEFA Women's Champions League, Ada Hegerberg is the most successful goal scorer with 53 goals."}], "sample_id": "-9116893482082868404", "question": "Who is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the eufa champions league?", "docs": [{"id": "6619814", "title": "2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League", "text": "on away goals. The top scorers and assists from the 2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League (excluding qualifying rounds) are as follows: 2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League The 2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League was the 11th season of UEFA's premier European club football tournament, the UEFA Champions League, since its rebranding in 1992, and the 48th European Cup tournament overall. The competition was won by Milan, who beat Juventus on penalties in the European Cup's first ever all-Italian final, to win their sixth European title, and its first in nine years. Manchester United's Ruud van Nistelrooy was again the top scorer, scoring 12 goals", "score": 0.73095703125, "summary": "The document mentions that Ruud van Nistelrooy was the top scorer of the 2002-03 UEFA Champions League with 12 goals, but it does not provide information on the most successful goal scorer in the history of the tournament.", "extraction": "Manchester United's Ruud van Nistelrooy was again the top scorer, scoring 12 goals."}], "answer": "Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League with 134 goals. He is also the most successful single-season goal scorer and goal scorer in terms of matches won in the league, while Francisco Gento is the most successful goal scorer in the league in terms of tournament championships. In the history of the UEFA Women's Champions League, Ada Hegerberg is the most successful goal scorer with 53 goals.", "output": "Ruud van Nistelrooy is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Ruud van Nistelrooy is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 652, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6168257836400262, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "quarter-final against Bayern Munich, Ronaldo scored two goals in a 2\u20131 away win to give Madrid the win and the lead going into the second leg. The two goals saw him make history in becoming the first player to reach 100 goals in UEFA club competition. During the second leg of the quarter-finals, Ronaldo scored a perfect hat-trick and reached his 100th UEFA Champions League goal, becoming the first player to do so as Real Madrid again defeated Bayern 4\u20132 after extra-time. On 2 May, Ronaldo scored another hat-trick in the following Champions League match, as Real Madrid defeated Atl\u00e9tico"}, "final_score": 1.6168257836400262, "relevance_score": 0.9936116792853608, "ground_score": 0.6232141043546655, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1317652299053057, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006387660701191197, "[Relevant]": 0.9935090236580566}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5656140678705935, "[Partially supported]": 0.11224954652477272, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.31976920037667234}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010220046992206375, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007775001747532021, "[Utility:3]": 0.0007134742658743393, "[Utility:4]": 0.006359126911319979, "[Utility:5]": 0.646119738184883}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He has won the title five times with Manchester United and Real Madrid, and is the competition's all-time top goalscorer with 117 goals.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He has won the title five times with Manchester United and Real Madrid, and is the competition's all-time top goalscorer with 117 goals.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6591129995582534, "ctx": {"title": "Messi\u2013Ronaldo rivalry", "text": "itself has been described as one about records and reputation of the players, rather than one based in loathing. Ronaldo is the UEFA Champions League all-time top goalscorer while Messi is second. The two had broken each other's record over the course of 2015 after Messi surpassed the previous record holder Ra\u00fal in November 2014. Ronaldo opened a gap in the 2015\u201316 season when he became the first player to score double figures in the group stage of the Champions League, setting the record at 11 goals. They are the first two players to score 100 goals in UEFA Champions"}, "final_score": 1.6591129995582534, "relevance_score": 0.9969005846715352, "ground_score": 0.6622124148867181, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3393361883129087, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0030992914311278688, "[Relevant]": 0.9968607341466443}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5629878215056836, "[Partially supported]": 0.19154697064827095, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2402537258814714}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018094694172927117, "[Utility:2]": 0.00039677132162755946, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004602627852605242, "[Utility:4]": 0.005673243895160744, "[Utility:5]": 0.689896882574257}], "n_tokens": 61}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Ruud van Nistelrooy is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Ruud van Nistelrooy is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7125499739281869, "ctx": {"title": "2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League", "text": "on away goals. The top scorers and assists from the 2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League (excluding qualifying rounds) are as follows: 2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League The 2002\u201303 UEFA Champions League was the 11th season of UEFA's premier European club football tournament, the UEFA Champions League, since its rebranding in 1992, and the 48th European Cup tournament overall. The competition was won by Milan, who beat Juventus on penalties in the European Cup's first ever all-Italian final, to win their sixth European title, and its first in nine years. Manchester United's Ruud van Nistelrooy was again the top scorer, scoring 12 goals"}, "final_score": 1.7125499739281869, "relevance_score": 0.9940889313927582, "ground_score": 0.7184610425354286, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.17190968678105806, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.005910874918581788, "[Relevant]": 0.9940563579672275}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6714579431435089, "[Partially supported]": 0.09016477172299436, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2357039132779478}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0006253681223192395, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008784721646678693, "[Utility:3]": 0.000488943393009489, "[Utility:4]": 0.00525663686548993, "[Utility:5]": 0.6699128128654971}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4475628971498498, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "120th minute of the 4\u20131 final victory over Atl\u00e9tico Madrid, becoming the first player to score in two European Cup finals for two different winning teams. His overall performance in the final was subdued as a result of patellar tendinitis and related hamstring problems, which had plagued him in the last months of the campaign. Ronaldo played the final against medical advice, later commenting: \"In your life you do not win without sacrifices and you must take risks.\" As the Champions League top goalscorer for the third time, with a record 17 goals, he was named the UEFA Best Player"}, "final_score": 1.4475628971498498, "relevance_score": 0.9838471620105507, "ground_score": 0.4637157351392991, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1542465124863996, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.016151321553715084, "[Relevant]": 0.9837547979941228}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.3507391671625091, "[Partially supported]": 0.22294337907403122, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.42307219308814037}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001060538964924904, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007147989149890786, "[Utility:3]": 0.00040253518748550085, "[Utility:4]": 0.010180722553759928, "[Utility:5]": 0.8279340498560753}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Patrick Kluivert is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Patrick Kluivert is the most successful goal scorer in the history of the UEFA Champions League.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.5946610976659644, "ctx": {"title": "Patrick Kluivert", "text": "credited with four goals, but the third was later re-attributed as an own goal by Yugoslavia's Dejan Govedarica after Kluivert admitted not getting a touch on Paul Bosvelt's cross. Had all four goals stood, Kluivert would have been the first player to score four times in a European Championship finals match. The semi-final against Italy would provide much heartache for Kluivert, as the Dutch yet again crashed out on penalties. Neither Kluivert nor his Dutch side could find the back of the net, despite twice having a chance from the penalty spot \u2013 Kluivert himself would miss one of those"}, "final_score": 0.5946610976659644, "relevance_score": 0.5640984800226174, "ground_score": 0.030562617643347, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.20144997914982155, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.4358019855755413, "[Relevant]": 0.5639696729361188}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.017495723864101188, "[Partially supported]": 0.02605979648087069, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.955233996337068}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0009841987597765524, "[Utility:2]": 0.0019045177735984879, "[Utility:3]": 0.00038996095483244506, "[Utility:4]": 0.019691091775088508, "[Utility:5]": 0.891286922749247}], "n_tokens": 26}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "It rises in northeastern Pitkin County, in the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide. It flows westward along the county line between Pitkin and Eagle County. Below Meredith, it is dammed to form the Ruedi Reservoir. It joins the Roaring Fork below Basalt. A portion of the river's water is diverted to the east side of the continental divide for irrigation and drinking water via the Fryingpan-Arkansas Project.", "question": "Where is the source of Fryingpan River located in Colorado?|What county in Colorado is the location of the Fryingpan River?", "short_answers": ["Pitkin", "Hunter-Fryingpan Wilderness, Pitkin County"], "wikipage": "Fryingpan River"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where in Colorado is the Fryingpan River?", "short_answers": ["west central Colorado", "in west central Colorado"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the coordinates of the location of the Fryingpan River in Colorado?", "short_answers": ["39\u00b009\u203252\u2033N 106\u00b031\u203240\u2033W"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "It rises in northeastern Pitkin County, in the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide. It flows westward along the county line between Pitkin and Eagle County. Below Meredith, it is dammed to form the Ruedi Reservoir. It joins the Roaring Fork below Basalt. A portion of the river's water is diverted to the east side of the continental divide for irrigation and drinking water via the Fryingpan-Arkansas Project.", "question": "What forest is the Fryingpan river in Colorado?", "short_answers": ["White River National Forest"], "wikipage": "Fryingpan River"}, {"context": "It rises in northeastern Pitkin County, in the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide. It flows westward along the county line between Pitkin and Eagle County. Below Meredith, it is dammed to form the Ruedi Reservoir. It joins the Roaring Fork below Basalt. A portion of the river's water is diverted to the east side of the continental divide for irrigation and drinking water via the Fryingpan-Arkansas Project.", "question": "What Mountain range is the Fryingpan River in Colorado?", "short_answers": ["Sawatch Mountains"], "wikipage": "Fryingpan River"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Fryingpan River", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fryingpan%20River"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Frying Pan River is located in west central Colorado, in northeastern Pitkin County, in the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide, with the coordinates of 39\u00b009\u203252\u2033N 106\u00b031\u203240\u2033W. "}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Fryingpan River in Colorado is located in west central Colorado in Pitkin County, where its source is, and its coordinates are 39\u00b009\u203252\u2033N 106\u00b031\u203240\u2033W. It rises in northeastern Pitkin County in the forest called White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains mountain range."}], "sample_id": "-1468534904392028006", "question": "Where is the frying pan river in colorado?", "docs": [{"id": "2774324", "title": "Fraser River (Colorado)", "text": "downstream from the town of Fraser. Some of the best fishing is in the canyon downstream from Tabernash, however this is on private land. Fraser River (Colorado) The Fraser River is a tributary of the Colorado River, approximately long, in north central Colorado in the United States. It drains large portion of the Middle Park basin in Grand County in the Rocky Mountains west of Boulder and southwest of Rocky Mountain National Park. It rises at the continental divide on the north side of Berthoud Pass in the Arapaho National Forest. It flows NNW past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash,", "score": 0.7470703125, "summary": "The document mentions the Fraser River in Colorado, which is approximately long and a tributary of the Colorado River. It flows past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash, and some of the best fishing is in the canyon downstream from Tabernash. However, there is no mention of the Frying Pan River in Colorado, so the answer is irrelevant.", "extraction": "Irrelevant."}], "answer": "The Fryingpan River in Colorado is located in west central Colorado in Pitkin County, where its source is, and its coordinates are 39\u00b009\u203252\u2033N 106\u00b031\u203240\u2033W. It rises in northeastern Pitkin County in the forest called White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains mountain range.", "output": "The Fraser River is a tributary of the Colorado River, approximately 10 miles long, in north central Colorado in the United States [1]. It drains a large portion of the Middle Park basin in Grand County in the Rocky Mountains west of Boulder and southwest of Rocky Mountain National Park [1]. It rises at the continental divide on the north side of Berthoud Pass in the Arapaho National Forest [1]. It flows NNW past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash, and joins the Colorado River at Granby [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Fraser River is a tributary of the Colorado River, approximately 10 miles long, in north central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It drains a large portion of the Middle Park basin in Grand County in the Rocky Mountains west of Boulder and southwest of Rocky Mountain National Park.[Continue to Use Evidence]It rises at the continental divide on the north side of Berthoud Pass in the Arapaho National Forest.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows NNW past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash, and joins the Colorado River at Granby.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 653, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Fryingpan River is a tributary of the Roaring Fork River, approximately 70 miles long, in west central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The river rises in northeastern Pitkin County, in the southern Rocky Mountains, approximately 10 miles west of Aspen.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows generally west-southwest through the valleys of the Elk Mountains and the Gore Range, and joins the Roaring Fork River near Glenwood Springs.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Fryingpan River is a tributary of the Roaring Fork River, approximately 70 miles long, in west central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The river rises in northeastern Pitkin County, in the southern Rocky Mountains, approximately 10 miles west of Aspen.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows generally west-southwest through the valleys of the Elk Mountains and the Gore Range, and joins the Roaring Fork River near Glenwood Springs.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8805011204317552, "ctx": {"title": "Fryingpan River", "text": "Fryingpan River The Fryingpan River is a tributary of the Roaring Fork River, approximately long, in west central Colorado in the United States. The reason for the unusual name of the river is that when a group of trappers were attacked by a band of Ute Indians, only two men survived, one of whom was injured. Leaving his wounded friend in a cave close by, the last man left to summon help, but not before hanging a frying pan in a tree so that he could find the cave again on his return. It rises in northeastern Pitkin County, in"}, "final_score": 1.8805011204317552, "relevance_score": 0.9988575502776509, "ground_score": 0.8816435701541043, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2890847299660272, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0011424289806550962, "[Relevant]": 0.9988394155647784}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8641355569631461, "[Partially supported]": 0.029569091844254738, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.1032062714771369}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00017153030216092454, "[Utility:2]": 0.00036383958319282847, "[Utility:3]": 0.000333877969348276, "[Utility:4]": 0.0019784568279348097, "[Utility:5]": 0.7645964684145042}], "n_tokens": 108}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Fryingpan River is a tributary of the Roaring Fork River, approximately 100 miles long, in west central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The river rises in the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows westward along the county line between Pitkin and Eagle County.[Continue to Use Evidence]Below Meredith, it is dammed to form the Ruedi Reservoir.[Continue to Use Evidence]It joins the Roaring Fork below Basalt.[Continue to Use Evidence]A portion of the river's water is diverted to the east side of the continental divide for irrigation and drinking water via the Fryingpan-Arkansas Project.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Fryingpan River is a tributary of the Roaring Fork River, approximately 100 miles long, in west central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The river rises in the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows westward along the county line between Pitkin and Eagle County.[Continue to Use Evidence]Below Meredith, it is dammed to form the Ruedi Reservoir.[Continue to Use Evidence]It joins the Roaring Fork below Basalt.[Continue to Use Evidence]A portion of the river's water is diverted to the east side of the continental divide for irrigation and drinking water via the Fryingpan-Arkansas Project.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9208676258033486, "ctx": {"title": "Fryingpan River", "text": "the White River National Forest in the Sawatch Mountains along the western side of the continental divide. It flows westward along the county line between Pitkin and Eagle County. Below Meredith, it is dammed to form the Ruedi Reservoir. It joins the Roaring Fork below Basalt. A portion of the river's water is diverted to the east side of the continental divide for irrigation and drinking water via the Fryingpan-Arkansas Project. Fryingpan River The Fryingpan River is a tributary of the Roaring Fork River, approximately long, in west central Colorado in the United States. The reason for the unusual name"}, "final_score": 1.9208676258033486, "relevance_score": 0.9992145262197853, "ground_score": 0.9216530995835633, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08342897253843826, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0007854652858909168, "[Relevant]": 0.9992037204464813}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8973953884775039, "[Partially supported]": 0.04036382870229667, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.057818515096309815}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.930243462283798e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00019748647255518376, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001742812003323074, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009366514148427519, "[Utility:5]": 0.815733053773704}], "n_tokens": 159}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Canadian River is a tributary of the North Platte River, approximately 100 miles long, located in Jackson County in north central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It drains the eastern edge of the North Park basin along the western side of Medicine Bow Mountains.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Canadian River is a tributary of the North Platte River, approximately 100 miles long, located in Jackson County in north central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It drains the eastern edge of the North Park basin along the western side of Medicine Bow Mountains.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9242172990862123, "ctx": {"title": "Canadian River (Platte River)", "text": "Canadian River (Platte River) The Canadian River is a tributary of the North Platte River, approximately long, located in Jackson County in north central Colorado in the United States. It drains the eastern edge of the North Park basin along the western side of Medicine Bow Mountains. It rises in several short forks that descend from the southwest edge of the Medicine Bow Mountains near Clark Peak in southeastern Jackson County, north of Gould and northwest of Cameron Pass. It descends to the northwest through the Colorado State Forest, and in the valley floor, where it becomes a braided stream"}, "final_score": 1.9242172990862123, "relevance_score": 0.9946981049488688, "ground_score": 0.9295191941373436, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14268364572404973, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00530119578250097, "[Relevant]": 0.9945669138983875}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9019914538549054, "[Partially supported]": 0.04455804181126701, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.047803800737054813}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.36414700804175e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00040045396601445674, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002555401631916202, "[Utility:4]": 0.0010427639057248173, "[Utility:5]": 0.6266024133608359}], "n_tokens": 62}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Fryingpan River is located in the state of Colorado in the United States.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Fryingpan River is located in the state of Colorado in the United States.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.435774995404877, "ctx": {"title": "Fryingpan-Arkansas Project", "text": "from the West Slope, the second highest diversion amount in the project's 50-year operating history. The following year, 2012, snowpack was scarce and drought returned to the state. As a result, the project was only able to import roughly of water. Before the Fry-Ark Project could be built in its entirety, a compromise had to be struck between East and West Slope water politics. The result was the construction of Ruedi Reservoir, upstream on the Fryingpan River from Basalt, Colorado. Ruedi provides water to Colorado's West Slope, in part to compensate for what is diverted further upstream. Water is diverted"}, "final_score": 1.435774995404877, "relevance_score": 0.9959614090053848, "ground_score": 0.4398135863994924, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.34701946258163924, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0040385409035653365, "[Relevant]": 0.9959490559959601}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2700738447450302, "[Partially supported]": 0.31329114084774584, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.38686292087599866}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00011662830567859121, "[Utility:2]": 0.00031889164628817333, "[Utility:3]": 0.00028032373403548807, "[Utility:4]": 0.0014291738872748714, "[Utility:5]": 0.6507920420486694}], "n_tokens": 22}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Fraser River is a tributary of the Colorado River, approximately 10 miles long, in north central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It drains a large portion of the Middle Park basin in Grand County in the Rocky Mountains west of Boulder and southwest of Rocky Mountain National Park.[Continue to Use Evidence]It rises at the continental divide on the north side of Berthoud Pass in the Arapaho National Forest.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows NNW past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash, and joins the Colorado River at Granby.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Fraser River is a tributary of the Colorado River, approximately 10 miles long, in north central Colorado in the United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It drains a large portion of the Middle Park basin in Grand County in the Rocky Mountains west of Boulder and southwest of Rocky Mountain National Park.[Continue to Use Evidence]It rises at the continental divide on the north side of Berthoud Pass in the Arapaho National Forest.[Continue to Use Evidence]It flows NNW past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash, and joins the Colorado River at Granby.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9373357986772952, "ctx": {"title": "Fraser River (Colorado)", "text": "downstream from the town of Fraser. Some of the best fishing is in the canyon downstream from Tabernash, however this is on private land. Fraser River (Colorado) The Fraser River is a tributary of the Colorado River, approximately long, in north central Colorado in the United States. It drains large portion of the Middle Park basin in Grand County in the Rocky Mountains west of Boulder and southwest of Rocky Mountain National Park. It rises at the continental divide on the north side of Berthoud Pass in the Arapaho National Forest. It flows NNW past Winter Park, Fraser, and Tabernash,"}, "final_score": 1.9373357986772952, "relevance_score": 0.9987551565455021, "ground_score": 0.9385806421317932, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11899164460692736, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0012447894363004804, "[Relevant]": 0.9987118169970005}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9033025870923086, "[Partially supported]": 0.055533980554956015, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03316085439103056}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00028539065090389166, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008177799753536113, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005468910316154388, "[Utility:4]": 0.002933457591489778, "[Utility:5]": 0.7463870894259894}], "n_tokens": 125}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The history of the Brisbane Broncos Rugby League Football Club stretches back from their inception in the mid-1980s to the present day. They were introduced to the NSWRL's Winfield Cup premiership in 1988, taking the competition by storm in winning their first six games. The Broncos participated in 18 consecutive finals series from 1992\u20132009, winning premierships six times, including the 1992 and 1993 NSWRL premierships, the Superleague premiership in 1997 and then the 1998, 2000 and 2006 National Rugby League premierships.", "question": "When did the Brisbane Broncos last win a  men's Minor premiership?", "short_answers": ["2000"], "wikipage": "History of the Brisbane Broncos"}, {"context": "In 2017, the Brisbane Broncos launched a bid to enter a team in the inaugural NRL Women's Premiership in 2018. On 27 March 2018, the club won a license to participate in the inaugural NRL Women's season, on the back of a strong bid which included the NRL's desire for a geographical spread. Paul Dyer was named as the coach of the women's side, but stepped down after the inaugural season to concentrate on his role as game development manager. Kelvin Wright was named his replacement in May 2019.", "question": "When did the Brisbane Broncos last win an NRL Women's Premiership?", "short_answers": ["2019"], "wikipage": "Brisbane Broncos"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the Brisbane Broncos last win a men's premiership?", "short_answers": ["1 October 2006", "2006 NRL season", "Sunday, 1 October"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "The history of the Brisbane Broncos Rugby League Football Club stretches back from their inception in the mid-1980s to the present day. They were introduced to the NSWRL's Winfield Cup premiership in 1988, taking the competition by storm in winning their first six games. The Broncos participated in 18 consecutive finals series from 1992\u20132009, winning premierships six times, including the 1992 and 1993 NSWRL premierships, the Superleague premiership in 1997 and then the 1998, 2000 and 2006 National Rugby League premierships.", "question": "When did the Brisbane Broncos last win a Minor premiership?", "short_answers": ["2000"], "wikipage": "History of the Brisbane Broncos"}, {"context": "In 2017, the Brisbane Broncos launched a bid to enter a team in the inaugural NRL Women's Premiership in 2018. On 27 March 2018, the club won a license to participate in the inaugural NRL Women's season, on the back of a strong bid which included the NRL's desire for a geographical spread. Paul Dyer was named as the coach of the women's side, but stepped down after the inaugural season to concentrate on his role as game development manager. Kelvin Wright was named his replacement in May 2019.", "question": "When did the Brisbane Broncos last win an NRL Women's Premiership?", "short_answers": ["2019"], "wikipage": "Brisbane Broncos"}, {"context": "The history of the Brisbane Broncos Rugby League Football Club stretches back from their inception in the mid-1980s to the present day. They were introduced to the NSWRL's Winfield Cup premiership in 1988, taking the competition by storm in winning their first six games. The Broncos participated in 18 consecutive finals series from 1992\u20132009, winning premierships six times, including the 1992 and 1993 NSWRL premierships, the Superleague premiership in 1997 and then the 1998, 2000 and 2006 National Rugby League premierships.", "question": "When did the Brisbane Broncos last win a premiership?|When was the last time the  Brisbane Broncos won a premiership?", "short_answers": ["2006", "1 October 2006", "2006 NRL season"], "wikipage": "History of the Brisbane Broncos"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "1999 Brisbane Broncos season", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1999%20Brisbane%20Broncos%20season"}, {"title": "Brisbane Broncos", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisbane%20Broncos"}, {"title": "History of the Brisbane Broncos", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History%20of%20the%20Brisbane%20Broncos"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Brisbane Broncos Rugby League Football Club Ltd., commonly referred to as the Broncos, are an Australian professional rugby league football club based in the city of Brisbane, the capital of the state of Queensland.", "wikipage": "Brisbane Broncos"}], "long_answer": "The Australian professional rugby league football club has had multiple wins over the years. The Brisbane Broncos last won an NRL Women's Premiership was in 2019. And the last win for the Brisbane Broncos for the a men's premiership was on 1 October 2006. The men's league also won a Minor premiership in 2000."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Brisbane Broncos last won the NRL Women's Premiership in 2019, which was the last time they won a premiership overall, while the last time they won a men's premiership was on October 1, 2006, during the 2006 NRL season. They last won a men's minor premiership in 2000, which was also the year of their last minor premiership win."}], "sample_id": "-1118314332714070461", "question": "When did the brisbane broncos last won a premiership?", "docs": [{"id": "8916148", "title": "History of the Brisbane Broncos", "text": "week saw a return to form. The Broncos then defeated the injury-struck Parramatta Eels 23-0 in Round 25 and in round 26 the New Zealand Warriors 36-12. Brisbane was heading into the finals with momentum not seen since their last premiership-winning season in 2000, but lost to the St George Illawarra Dragons 20-4 in what was at the time the highest attended Broncos game ever played at Suncorp Stadium. Brisbane bounced back in the semi-final, defeating the Newcastle Knights 50-6 and captain Darren Lockyer became the club's highest ever point-scorer with 1,077 (breaking Michael De Vere's record of 1,062 points).", "score": 0.7998046875, "summary": "The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000 according to the document.", "extraction": "The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000."}], "answer": "The Brisbane Broncos last won the NRL Women's Premiership in 2019, which was the last time they won a premiership overall, while the last time they won a men's premiership was on October 1, 2006, during the 2006 NRL season. They last won a men's minor premiership in 2000, which was also the year of their last minor premiership win.", "output": "The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 654, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9414319720254758, "ctx": {"title": "History of the Brisbane Broncos", "text": "week saw a return to form. The Broncos then defeated the injury-struck Parramatta Eels 23-0 in Round 25 and in round 26 the New Zealand Warriors 36-12. Brisbane was heading into the finals with momentum not seen since their last premiership-winning season in 2000, but lost to the St George Illawarra Dragons 20-4 in what was at the time the highest attended Broncos game ever played at Suncorp Stadium. Brisbane bounced back in the semi-final, defeating the Newcastle Knights 50-6 and captain Darren Lockyer became the club's highest ever point-scorer with 1,077 (breaking Michael De Vere's record of 1,062 points)."}, "final_score": 1.9414319720254758, "relevance_score": 0.9987744323562494, "ground_score": 0.9426575396692265, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11746529187194424, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0012255264113097734, "[Relevant]": 0.9987408300432155}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9299810943875613, "[Partially supported]": 0.021871058050987376, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.046301030257277054}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003530406321210317, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003303581544036167, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018066812005157536, "[Utility:4]": 0.0031099577052039803, "[Utility:5]": 0.9143405272735785}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2006.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2006.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9312916087766312, "ctx": {"title": "History of the Brisbane Broncos", "text": "Golden Point period to win the match for the North Queensland Cowboys 17-16. As of 2017, the Broncos are currently experiencing their longest premiership drought, having endured ten completed seasons without winning the premiership since their most recent success in 2006. History of the Brisbane Broncos The history of the Brisbane Broncos Rugby League Football Club stretches back from their inception in the mid-1980s to the present day. They were introduced to the NSWRL's Winfield Cup premiership in 1988, taking the competition by storm in winning their first six games. The Broncos participated in 18 consecutive finals series from 1992-2009,"}, "final_score": 1.9312916087766312, "relevance_score": 0.9973269935854845, "ground_score": 0.9339646151911468, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14796873965520302, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002672920898457994, "[Relevant]": 0.9972950866390015}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9258075526378622, "[Partially supported]": 0.012503129164334636, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05964909318271812}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002634058357740401, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003055561151192208, "[Utility:3]": 0.000182812597292073, "[Utility:4]": 0.00341589885275892, "[Utility:5]": 0.907298901502243}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership on 24 September 2006.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership on 24 September 2006.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9059740021680194, "ctx": {"title": "Brisbane Broncos", "text": "to the Cowboys and Johnathan Thurston kicked a field goal 2 minutes into the Golden Point period to win the match for the North Queensland Cowboys 17-16. , the Broncos are currently experiencing their longest premiership drought, having endured twelve completed seasons without winning the premiership since their most recent success in 2006. In the 2017 NRL season, the Brisbane Broncos highest try-scorer of 2017 is former Gold Coast Titans Centre, James Roberts with 15 tries, 2888 run metres, 4 try-assists and 289 tackles. In December 2017, the Brisbane Broncos expressed their interest in applying for a licence to participate"}, "final_score": 1.9059740021680194, "relevance_score": 0.9975845167452388, "ground_score": 0.9083894854227805, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21945320763655604, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002415428851425225, "[Relevant]": 0.9975620483942527}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8930776717437307, "[Partially supported]": 0.026758738297693412, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.07803625959950222}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016912674785375353, "[Utility:2]": 0.00021379624197047882, "[Utility:3]": 0.00019089855687624016, "[Utility:4]": 0.002446768421203624, "[Utility:5]": 0.9309209683567736}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2000.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9382488791216472, "ctx": {"title": "Brisbane Broncos", "text": "third consecutive premiership losing 8 of their first 10 matches. Club legend Allan Langer retired mid-season, perhaps as a result of the team's form. Despite the club's mid-season turnaround, which resulted in qualification for the finals after an 11-match winning streak, the team was eliminated by the Cronulla Sharks in the first week of the finals. However, the Broncos' rebounded in 2000 with their fifth premiership, defeating the Sydney Roosters. The game marked the retirement of veterans Kevin Walters and Michael Hancock. Not long after the disappointment of the previous year, in 2000, the Broncos rested on the very top"}, "final_score": 1.9382488791216472, "relevance_score": 0.9981469407652331, "ground_score": 0.9401019383564141, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12928566679273276, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0018530247163763105, "[Relevant]": 0.9981283474978004}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9266674525739028, "[Partially supported]": 0.022839005643100756, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04835017532586127}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003137761780224298, "[Utility:2]": 0.00039202895272537114, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020377965710932823, "[Utility:4]": 0.004382603337329311, "[Utility:5]": 0.9030410114088411}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2015.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Brisbane Broncos last won a premiership in 2015.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4292577797443196, "ctx": {"title": "Brisbane Broncos honours", "text": "Brisbane Broncos honours Honours won by the Brisbane Broncos include six National Rugby League Premierships and two World Club Challenge titles. In addition the club and its players have won several more honours and awards since the Broncos were founded in 1988. The Broncos qualified for the NSWRL/ARL/SL/NRL finals in the following years. 1990, 1992, 1993, 1994, 1995, 1996, 1997, 1998, 1999, 2000, 2001, 2002, 2003, 2004, 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2011, 2012, 2014 and 2015 The Rothmans Medal is awarded to Player of the Year from the years 1968-1996. The Dally M. Medal is a different system to"}, "final_score": 1.4292577797443196, "relevance_score": 0.9500414775247253, "ground_score": 0.4792163022195942, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.17808638998882959, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.04995250405612346, "[Relevant]": 0.9499270276263011}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.46127633326160783, "[Partially supported]": 0.03188457480502781, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.502670449201012}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00015375026447887697, "[Utility:2]": 0.00025448346844151134, "[Utility:3]": 0.00013728353378826292, "[Utility:4]": 0.0026106678627905713, "[Utility:5]": 0.9589662389547683}], "n_tokens": 23}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the position of the moon during a solar eclipse?", "short_answers": ["between Earth and the sun"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where is the moon in terms of phases, during a solar eclipse?", "short_answers": ["new moon phase"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Solar eclipse", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar%20eclipse"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "A solar eclipse occurs when a portion of the Earth is engulfed in a shadow cast by the Moon which fully or partially blocks sunlight", "wikipage": "Solar eclipse"}], "long_answer": "A solar eclipse is when a part of the Earth is covered by a shadow that is cast by the moon. The moon would be in a new moon phase,  positioned between Earth and the sun. "}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "During a solar eclipse, the moon is between Earth and the sun. In terms of phases, the moon is in the new moon phase during a solar eclipse."}], "sample_id": "-7140472960783987194", "question": "Where is the moon located during a solar eclipse?", "docs": [{"id": "7745006", "title": "Solar eclipse", "text": "Solar eclipse A solar eclipse occurs when an observer (on Earth) passes through the shadow cast by the Moon which fully or partially blocks (\"occults\") the Sun. This can only happen when the Sun, Moon and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions (syzygy) during a new moon when the Moon is close to the ecliptic plane. In a total eclipse, the disk of the Sun is fully obscured by the Moon. In partial and annular eclipses, only part of the Sun is obscured. If the Moon were in a perfectly circular orbit, a little closer", "score": 0.748046875, "summary": "During a solar eclipse, the Moon passes in front of the Sun, blocking part or all of it. The Moon must be close to the ecliptic plane and aligned with the Sun and Earth in syzygy during a new moon for this to happen.", "extraction": "During a solar eclipse, the Moon fully or partially blocks the Sun when the Sun, Moon and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions. So, the Moon is located between the Sun and the Earth during a solar eclipse."}], "answer": "During a solar eclipse, the moon is between Earth and the sun. In terms of phases, the moon is in the new moon phase during a solar eclipse.", "output": "A solar eclipse occurs when an observer on Earth passes through the shadow cast by the Moon, which fully or partially blocks the Sun [1]. This can only happen when the Sun, Moon, and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions during a new moon, when the Moon is close to the ecliptic plane [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]A solar eclipse occurs when an observer on Earth passes through the shadow cast by the Moon, which fully or partially blocks the Sun.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]This can only happen when the Sun, Moon, and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions during a new moon, when the Moon is close to the ecliptic plane.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 655, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the moon is located in the Earth's shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the moon is located in the Earth's shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9100080155019978, "ctx": {"title": "Solar eclipses on the Moon", "text": "Solar eclipses on the Moon Solar eclipses on the Moon are caused when the Earth passes in front of the Sun, blocking its light. Viewers on Earth will see a lunar eclipse. The solar eclipses are only seen in the Nearside portion and smaller parts of the Farside where the Earth is seen during librations, making up the visible portion of the Moon and eclipses there are seen during the lunar sunrise and sunset as well as furthermost areas of the Nearside but mainly not in the polar areas of the Moon. As the Moon revolves around the Earth, the"}, "final_score": 1.9100080155019978, "relevance_score": 0.9962637793706557, "ground_score": 0.9137442361313421, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.304736256577685, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0037360960475277347, "[Relevant]": 0.99623055961098}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.860646603234461, "[Partially supported]": 0.1027895707945413, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03470023282066495}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0013265564889218336, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003771071096637813, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004711542370509803, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018131877401686592, "[Utility:5]": 0.5946990427324979}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]A solar eclipse occurs when an observer on Earth passes through the shadow cast by the Moon, which fully or partially blocks the Sun.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]This can only happen when the Sun, Moon, and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions during a new moon, when the Moon is close to the ecliptic plane.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]A solar eclipse occurs when an observer on Earth passes through the shadow cast by the Moon, which fully or partially blocks the Sun.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]This can only happen when the Sun, Moon, and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions during a new moon, when the Moon is close to the ecliptic plane.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9404648965789857, "ctx": {"title": "Solar eclipse", "text": "Solar eclipse A solar eclipse occurs when an observer (on Earth) passes through the shadow cast by the Moon which fully or partially blocks (\"occults\") the Sun. This can only happen when the Sun, Moon and Earth are nearly aligned on a straight line in three dimensions (syzygy) during a new moon when the Moon is close to the ecliptic plane. In a total eclipse, the disk of the Sun is fully obscured by the Moon. In partial and annular eclipses, only part of the Sun is obscured. If the Moon were in a perfectly circular orbit, a little closer"}, "final_score": 1.9404648965789857, "relevance_score": 0.9945307263815333, "ground_score": 0.9459341701974525, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1162638678001583, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.005469022626703488, "[Relevant]": 0.9944850861305564}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9230221413874633, "[Partially supported]": 0.031094417831438424, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0380976771134928}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003735584669860866, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001619256097084322, "[Utility:3]": 0.00011549721365103818, "[Utility:4]": 0.0022353140918441984, "[Utility:5]": 0.4941401846147244}], "n_tokens": 70}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the Moon is located in the Earth's umbra, the darkest part of its shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the Moon is located in the Earth's umbra, the darkest part of its shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9050263714524998, "ctx": {"title": "Solar eclipse", "text": "eclipse, the Moon's umbra (or antumbra, in the case of an annular eclipse) moves rapidly from west to east across the Earth. The Earth is also rotating from west to east, at about 28 km/min at the Equator, but as the Moon is moving in the same direction as the Earth's spin at about 61 km/min, the umbra almost always appears to move in a roughly west-east direction across a map of the Earth at the speed of the Moon's orbital velocity minus the Earth's rotational velocity. The width of the track of a central eclipse varies according to the"}, "final_score": 1.9050263714524998, "relevance_score": 0.9912886152527669, "ground_score": 0.9137377561997327, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3085045574981147, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008710832574337652, "[Relevant]": 0.9912257822221092}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8498866309812251, "[Partially supported]": 0.12436589973941135, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.023921808319768423}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007765383266290706, "[Utility:2]": 0.00031869068712620945, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004691580707712832, "[Utility:4]": 0.0023640398834457793, "[Utility:5]": 0.603858888155406}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the moon is located in the Earth's shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the moon is located in the Earth's shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8936875883867232, "ctx": {"title": "Solar eclipse", "text": "shadow covers only a small fraction of the Earth. The Earth continues to receive at least 92 percent of the amount of sunlight it receives without an eclipse \u2013 more if the penumbra of the Moon's shadow partly misses the Earth. Seen from the Moon, the Earth during a total solar eclipse is mostly brilliantly illuminated, with only a small dark patch showing the Moon's shadow. The brilliantly-lit Earth reflects a lot of light to the Moon. If the corona of the eclipsed Sun were not present, the Moon, illuminated by earthlight, would be easily visible from Earth. This would"}, "final_score": 1.8936875883867232, "relevance_score": 0.9915543738075945, "ground_score": 0.9021332145791289, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.284745667200609, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008445369127289302, "[Relevant]": 0.991524193210625}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8447948012393571, "[Partially supported]": 0.1082459750917915, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.043394976458004504}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010078242581993567, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003297588357067196, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004048188614612475, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018609275708510488, "[Utility:5]": 0.5689150854133188}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the Moon is located inside the Earth's umbral shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]During a solar eclipse, the Moon is located inside the Earth's umbral shadow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9183160255650473, "ctx": {"title": "Solar eclipses on the Moon", "text": "of the Moon (notably especially at its poles) having totality. Unlike the Earth which receives the smallest portion being inside the center of the Moon's shadow during total solar eclipses. During its total eclipses on the Moon, the center of the Earth's shadow covers the whole Nearside part of the Moon and lasts much longer than on Earth with up to around 1.8 hours. Some total-partial eclipses have a most, half or a part of the Moon being in the center of the Earth's shadow. Unlike the Earth which it umbral shadow shows black, as the Moon has no atmosphere,"}, "final_score": 1.9183160255650473, "relevance_score": 0.9933588878463661, "ground_score": 0.9249571377186813, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.23430464011196742, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0066408779131450655, "[Relevant]": 0.9933238508124816}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8708672593338237, "[Partially supported]": 0.10482603527611054, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.022493798955354555}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0011379468558051383, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003110972104444716, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006408286881032217, "[Utility:4]": 0.002574439614434947, "[Utility:5]": 0.6705724601824868}], "n_tokens": 24}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The first season of the reality television series \"\" aired on VH1 from October 26, 2015 until December 28, 2015. It chronicles the daily operations and staff drama at an African American owned and operated tattoo shop \"9MAG\" located in Chicago, Illinois.", "question": "When is Black Ink Crew: Chicago coming back in 2015?", "short_answers": ["October 26, 2015", "October 26"], "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 1)"}, {"context": "The second season of the reality television series \"\" aired on VH1 from October 3, 2016, until November 28, 2016. It chronicles the daily operations and staff drama at an African American owned and operated tattoo shop \"9MAG\" located in Chicago, Illinois.", "question": "When is Black Ink Crew: Chicago coming back in 2016?", "short_answers": ["October 3, 2016", "October 3"], "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 2)"}, {"context": "The third season of the reality television series \"\" aired on VH1 from July 19, 2017, until December 6, 2017. It chronicles the daily operations and staff drama at an African American owned and operated tattoo shop, \"9MAG\", located in Chicago, Illinois.", "question": "When is Black Ink Crew: Chicago coming back in 2017?", "short_answers": ["July 19", "July 19, 2017"], "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 3)"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Ink%20Crew%3A%20Chicago"}, {"title": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 3)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Ink%20Crew%3A%20Chicago%20%28season%203%29"}, {"title": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 2)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Ink%20Crew%3A%20Chicago%20%28season%202%29"}, {"title": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 6)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Ink%20Crew%3A%20Chicago%20%28season%206%29"}, {"title": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 5)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Ink%20Crew%3A%20Chicago%20%28season%205%29"}, {"title": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 1)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black%20Ink%20Crew%3A%20Chicago%20%28season%201%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago is an American reality television series that airs on VH1 and debuted on October 26, 2015. ", "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago"}], "long_answer": "The first season of the American reality television series, Black Ink Crew: Chicago, aired on October 26, 2015 and returned for a second season on October 3, 2016. And the third season of the series came out July 19, 2017. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago is an American reality television series that airs on VH1 and debuted on October 26, 2015. It is the Chicago-based spin-off of Black Ink Crew.", "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago"}, {"content": "The fourth season of the reality television series Black Ink Crew: Chicago aired on VH1 from May 30, 2018 until September 19, 2018.", "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 4)"}, {"content": "The fifth season of the reality television series Black Ink Crew: Chicago aired on VH1 from January 2, 2019 until August 6, 2019.", "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 5)"}, {"content": "The sixth season of the reality television series Black Ink Crew: Chicago aired on VH1 from December 4, 2019 until July 27, 2020.", "wikipage": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago (season 6)"}], "long_answer": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago had six seasons from October 26, 2015, to July 27, 2020, with different start dates for each. The VH1 reality television series, the Chicago-based spin-off of Black Ink Crew, debuted on October 26, 2015. Following the first season, the show returned with a second and third season on October 3, 2016, and July 19, 2017, respectively, while the show came back for a fourth and fifth season on September 19, 2018, and August 6, 2019, respectively. Finally, the sixth season aired from December 4, 2019, until July 27, 2020."}], "sample_id": "272628974012850734", "question": "When is black ink crew chicago coming back?", "docs": [{"id": "17005584", "title": "Black Ink Crew", "text": "Black Ink Crew Black Ink Crew is an American reality television series that airs on VH1 and debuted on January 7, 2013. It chronicles the daily operations and staff drama at an African American-owned and operated tattoo shop in Harlem, New York. The spinoff \"\" premiered October 26, 2015. On August 20, 2018, the series was renewed for a seventh season, which premiered on September 19, 2018. \"Black Ink Crew\" second season premiered on September 23, 2013, with the season returning on March 31, 2014, for five more episodes. The third season premiered on January 26, 2015, with the season", "score": 0.779296875, "summary": "Irrelevant.", "extraction": "irrelevant."}], "answer": "Black Ink Crew: Chicago had six seasons from October 26, 2015, to July 27, 2020, with different start dates for each. The VH1 reality television series, the Chicago-based spin-off of Black Ink Crew, debuted on October 26, 2015. Following the first season, the show returned with a second and third season on October 3, 2016, and July 19, 2017, respectively, while the show came back for a fourth and fifth season on September 19, 2018, and August 6, 2019, respectively. Finally, the sixth season aired from December 4, 2019, until July 27, 2020.", "output": "The show premiered on October 26, 2015 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The show premiered on October 26, 2015.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 656, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The show premiered on September 19, 2018.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The show premiered on September 19, 2018.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9898051531569936, "ctx": {"title": "Black Ink Crew", "text": "returning August 24, 2015 for eight more episodes and a reunion special on October 19, 2015. The show was renewed for a fourth season, which premiered on April 4, 2016. Black Ink Crew Black Ink Crew is an American reality television series that airs on VH1 and debuted on January 7, 2013. It chronicles the daily operations and staff drama at an African American-owned and operated tattoo shop in Harlem, New York. The spinoff \"\" premiered October 26, 2015. On August 20, 2018, the series was renewed for a seventh season, which premiered on September 19, 2018. \"Black Ink Crew\""}, "final_score": 0.9898051531569936, "relevance_score": 0.8766343643466801, "ground_score": 0.11317078881031345, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2951594260357524, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.12335104238519572, "[Relevant]": 0.8765306648013562}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.0942797081995839, "[Partially supported]": 0.037501876923705886, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8669800904791353}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005384695269996889, "[Utility:2]": 0.00038482681669626525, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017277888111988325, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018430915968928535, "[Utility:5]": 0.9697802634539764}], "n_tokens": 20}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The show premiered on October 26, 2015.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The show premiered on October 26, 2015.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1077438274631257, "ctx": {"title": "Black Ink Crew", "text": "Black Ink Crew Black Ink Crew is an American reality television series that airs on VH1 and debuted on January 7, 2013. It chronicles the daily operations and staff drama at an African American-owned and operated tattoo shop in Harlem, New York. The spinoff \"\" premiered October 26, 2015. On August 20, 2018, the series was renewed for a seventh season, which premiered on September 19, 2018. \"Black Ink Crew\" second season premiered on September 23, 2013, with the season returning on March 31, 2014, for five more episodes. The third season premiered on January 26, 2015, with the season"}, "final_score": 1.1077438274631257, "relevance_score": 0.8832359848650174, "ground_score": 0.22450784259810827, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.33271072456518597, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.1167363111153195, "[Relevant]": 0.8830264238366169}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.20264284009590486, "[Partially supported]": 0.04314515550864394, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7529094897728109}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000931691367120325, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004022838197102647, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002587213486735399, "[Utility:4]": 0.0033682811835663165, "[Utility:5]": 0.8807809559044186}], "n_tokens": 20}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Irrelevant]The show came back on October 11, 2017.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]The show came back on October 11, 2017.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.07004895456767256, "ctx": {"title": "Chicago (band)", "text": "Thursday, June 15 at the Marriott Marquis Hotel in New York City. Chicago's website stated that in 2017, the band was working on a new album, \"Chicago XXXVII\".<ref name= \"Chicago News 2/22/17\"></ref> On September 17, 2017, former percussionist Laudir de Oliveira died of a heart attack while performing onstage in his native Rio de Janeiro. Chicago began their 2018 touring schedule on Saturday, January 13 by performing the grand opening concert at the new Xcite Center in Bensalem, Pennsylvania. On Wednesday, January 17, 2018, drummer Tris Imboden announced he was leaving the band after 27 years to spend more time"}, "final_score": 0.07004895456767256, "relevance_score": 0.07004895456767256, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5407981215577916, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.9297379426085745, "[Relevant]": 0.0700329025076285}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0013438912503842895, "[Utility:2]": 0.0011450096603236857, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015345421533150932, "[Utility:4]": 0.003638833174977151, "[Utility:5]": 0.9222526383746137}], "n_tokens": 19}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Irrelevant]The show came back on June 26, 2017.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]The show came back on June 26, 2017.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.006145090707614902, "ctx": {"title": "Fraternal Order of Moai", "text": "The third annual event was held June 23\u201326, 2011. The fourth annual event was held June 21\u201324, 2012, the fifth annual event was held June 27\u201330, 2013, the sixth annual event was held June 27\u201329, 2014. The most recent events were held June 25\u201328, 2015, and June 23\u201326, 2016. The next will be held June 15\u201318, 2017. Chicago Area Tiki Tour The Red Palms Chapter of the Order sponsored a bus tour of tiki bars in the Chicago area September 30-October 2, 2011. The event, called CATT, was similar to the already established NorthEast Tiki Tour (NETT). All proceeds from"}, "final_score": 0.006145090707614902, "relevance_score": 0.006145090707614902, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5548177680159968, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.9938129321479439, "[Relevant]": 0.006144831159306767}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008907990191836402, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005016487052228746, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001099825766693155, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018565845853930187, "[Utility:5]": 0.9617346109841777}], "n_tokens": 19}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Irrelevant]Black Ink Crew Chicago came back on January 1, 2018.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]Black Ink Crew Chicago came back on January 1, 2018.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.005343257030235743, "ctx": {"title": "In a World Like This Tour", "text": "FirstMerit Bank Pavilion was cut short due to the city's curfew. Towards the end of the show, with 5 songs left, the group's microphone's were turned off and lights were cut. According to a statement by Live Nation, the concert started late and they hoped to finish by the curfew. However, the show was cut off at 10:55pm, five minutes short of the curfew. The group promised they would return to Chicago to give their fans a full show, and they announced in 2014 that another show will be held at the same venue on June 11, 2014. In May"}, "final_score": 0.005343257030235743, "relevance_score": 0.005343257030235743, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.35175932151295536, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.994619389912611, "[Relevant]": 0.005343056371077048}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0019417430396834353, "[Utility:2]": 0.0009244082681798405, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001874384824047893, "[Utility:4]": 0.002416538257000894, "[Utility:5]": 0.9412226197467446}], "n_tokens": 22}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which band sang there's a meeting in the ladies room", "short_answers": ["Klymaxx"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the lead vocabl of there's a meeting in the ladies room", "short_answers": ["Lorena Porter"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Meeting in the Ladies Room (song)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meeting%20in%20the%20Ladies%20Room%20%28song%29"}, {"title": "Meeting in the Ladies Room", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meeting%20in%20the%20Ladies%20Room"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Lead vocals Bernadette Cooper, Lorena Porter and Lynn Malsby", "wikipage": "Meeting in the Ladies Room (song)"}, {"content": "Klymaxx is an American all-female pop/R&B band from Los Angeles, California. ", "wikipage": "Klymaxx"}], "long_answer": "The song \"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is by American all-female pop/R&B band Klymaxx, with lead vocals sang by band members Bernadette Cooper, Lorena Porter and Lynn Malsby. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a song recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and the title track from their fourth album.", "wikipage": "Meeting in the Ladies Room (song)"}], "long_answer": "\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a song recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and the title track from their fourth album. Lead vocals were sung by Lorena Porter."}], "sample_id": "5489044326175665193", "question": "Who sang there's a meeting in the ladies room?", "docs": [{"id": "12911997", "title": "Meeting in the Ladies Room (song)", "text": "Casale of Devo and featured an appearance from a then-unknown Vivica A. Fox. The song was also included on the MCA Records motion picture soundtrack for 1985's \"Secret Admirer\". \"Billboard\" named the song #85 on their list of 100 Greatest Girl Group Songs of All Time. Meeting in the Ladies Room (song) \"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a hit single recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and was the title track from their fourth album and was written by: Reggie Calloway; Vincent Calloway and Boaz Watson. The single reached number 4 on the \"Billboard\" R&B chart, number", "score": 0.83837890625, "summary": "Klymaxx sang \"Meeting in the Ladies Room\", which was a hit single released in 1984. The song was written by Reggie Calloway, Vincent Calloway, and Boaz Watson.", "extraction": "irrelevant"}], "answer": "\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a song recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and the title track from their fourth album. Lead vocals were sung by Lorena Porter.", "output": "\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a hit single recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and was the title track from their fourth album [1]. The single reached number 4 on the Billboard R&B chart, number 11 on the Billboard Hot 100, and number 2 on the Billboard Dance chart [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a hit single recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and was the title track from their fourth album.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The single reached number 4 on the Billboard R&B chart, number 11 on the Billboard Hot 100, and number 2 on the Billboard Dance chart.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 657, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a hit single recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and was the title track from their fourth album.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was written by Reggie Calloway, Vincent Calloway, and Boaz Watson.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a hit single recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and was the title track from their fourth album.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was written by Reggie Calloway, Vincent Calloway, and Boaz Watson.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.97424318054426, "ctx": {"title": "Meeting in the Ladies Room (song)", "text": "Meeting in the Ladies Room (song) \"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" is a hit single recorded by Klymaxx for the MCA Records label, and was the title track from their fourth album and was written by: Reggie Calloway; Vincent Calloway and Boaz Watson. The single reached number 4 on the \"Billboard\" R&B chart, number 59 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 chart In addition to peaking at number 22 on the \"Billboard\" Hot Dance Club Play chart. The success of this song helped their \"Meeting in the Ladies Room\" album reach Platinum status. The song's music video was directed by Gerald"}, "final_score": 1.97424318054426, "relevance_score": 0.9996566999802688, "ground_score": 0.9745864805639911, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12297999437445395, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00034329870234543075, "[Relevant]": 0.999652863879262}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9716053058111089, "[Partially supported]": 0.005469909361872891,