of romantic longing with some gospel elements.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"How Come U Don't Call Me Anymore?\" is a song by Prince.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is a ballad of romantic longing with some gospel elements.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9544278932044934, "ctx": {"title": "How Come U Don't Call Me Anymore?", "text": "How Come U Don't Call Me Anymore? \"How Come U Don't Call Me Anymore?\" is a song by Prince. It is a ballad of romantic longing with some gospel elements. On his original recording of the song, which was released as the non-album B-side to his 1982 single \"1999\", Prince performs most of the song in his falsetto range, with his own bluesy piano playing providing the only instrumental accompaniment. The song's first album appearance was on his 1993 compilation \"The Hits/The B-Sides\". It was later included on the soundtrack to the 1996 film \"Girl 6\". Prince also performs the"}, "final_score": 1.9544278932044934, "relevance_score": 0.9983386095143482, "ground_score": 0.9560892836901453, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08569769592051539, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0016613495471323435, "[Relevant]": 0.9983140093346091}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9513793939207176, "[Partially supported]": 0.006665702979397084, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04051462100152919}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00011143215247362303, "[Utility:2]": 4.896718024076964e-05, "[Utility:3]": 6.892005141681965e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0005793190764273943, "[Utility:5]": 0.5164957794749238}], "n_tokens": 40}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]\"How Come You Don't Call Me Anymore\" is a song by American singer-songwriter Alicia Keys from her second studio album, \"The Diary of Alicia Keys\" (2003).[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was written by Alicia Keys, Kerry \"Krucial\" Brothers, and Daft Punk member Thomas Bangalter, who also produced the song.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"How Come You Don't Call Me Anymore\" is a song by American singer-songwriter Alicia Keys from her second studio album, \"The Diary of Alicia Keys\" (2003).[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was written by Alicia Keys, Kerry \"Krucial\" Brothers, and Daft Punk member Thomas Bangalter, who also produced the song.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3045046805247218, "ctx": {"title": "How Come U Don't Call Me Anymore?", "text": "\"Remixed & Unplugged in A minor\" reissue, released in 2002. It features vocals from Justin Timberlake towards the end of the track. Keys' cover of \"How Come You Don't Call Me\" was inspired by a long-term relationship with a partner. The song's music video, directed by Little X. It contains references to Japanese popular culture such as San-X's Buru Buru Dog and \"Cardcaptor Sakura\"'s Kero-chan, besides Korean character Mashimaro. It starts with Keys waking up in the morning, and following her daily routine throughout the video, ending with a performance on stage. The video ends with a phone call from"}, "final_score": 1.3045046805247218, "relevance_score": 0.9477691377350302, "ground_score": 0.3567355427896915, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2876405962809596, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.05222224810667767, "[Relevant]": 0.9476128272124227}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.3295671673393092, "[Partially supported]": 0.053381683388445574, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.6157125280840531}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00015446423435666335, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011591454072425997, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010390512277000085, "[Utility:4]": 0.001172984138858235, "[Utility:5]": 0.8535418879669782}], "n_tokens": 90}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The song How Come You Don't Call Me Anymore was written by Alicia Keys, Kerry \"Krucial\" Brothers, and Daft Punk member Thomas Bangalter.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song How Come You Don't Call Me Anymore was written by Alicia Keys, Kerry \"Krucial\" Brothers, and Daft Punk member Thomas Bangalter.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3112256014708386, "ctx": {"title": "How Come U Don't Call Me Anymore?", "text": "song on his 2002 live album \"One Nite Alone... Live!\". Artists who have covered the song include Stephanie Mills (1983), Joshua Redman (1998), and Alicia Keys (2001). Bilal recorded the song which appears on his 2001 single \"Fast Lane\". Roger Cicero recorded the song with Soulounge for the 2004 album \"Home\"; a live version by Cicero is included on his 2008 single \"Alle M\u00f6bel verr\u00fcckt\". American Idol season 11 finalist Jessica Sanchez performed the song on the American Idols LIVE! Tour 2012. Keys recorded a cover of the song\u2014retitled \"How Come You Don't Call Me\"\u2014for her debut album, \"Songs in"}, "final_score": 1.3112256014708386, "relevance_score": 0.9372107935951599, "ground_score": 0.37401480787567865, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.41629746166264814, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.06278071025441828, "[Relevant]": 0.9370839774696623}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.27545589865802955, "[Partially supported]": 0.1953274807272582, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5268232157677106}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00022171224493235453, "[Utility:2]": 7.339909435728986e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010805391841082595, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011846008012729346, "[Utility:5]": 0.9033632881627469}], "n_tokens": 44}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The song \"Why Don't You Call Me\" was written by Yahzarah and Phonte Coleman.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song \"Why Don't You Call Me\" was written by Yahzarah and Phonte Coleman.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9377883852605382, "ctx": {"title": "Why Dontcha Call Me No More", "text": "Exchange Music's website. The song received positive feedback from fans and reviewers of the song. Andy Kellman of Allmusic.com stated, Why Dontcha Call Me No More \"Why Dontcha Call Me No More\" is a song by American singer Yahzarah. The song was released in March 2010 as the lead single for her third studio album, \"The Ballad of Purple St. James\". The song was written by YahZarah and Phonte Coleman and was produced by Nicolay and Phonte. The song features several instruments including the keyboard, tambourine, live handclaps, drums, the acoustic, bass, and electric guitars. In March 2010, \"Why Dontcha"}, "final_score": 1.9377883852605382, "relevance_score": 0.9936116800952471, "ground_score": 0.9441767051652911, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.270426074358381, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006388120644719944, "[Relevant]": 0.9935806880505463}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9268049532229278, "[Partially supported]": 0.033235485465083134, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.039160972228296634}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001771045886710161, "[Utility:2]": 4.7019790121891124e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017031981842611084, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009243443555186862, "[Utility:5]": 0.6832070989501786}], "n_tokens": 28}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who were the main writers of the song \"Superstar\" by The Carpenters?", "short_answers": ["Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "\"Superstar\" is a 1969 song written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett that has been a hit for many artists in different genres and interpretations in the years since; the best-known version is by the Carpenters in 1971.", "question": "Who only has a songwriting credit for the song \"Superstar\" by The Carpenters?", "short_answers": ["Delaney Bramlett"], "wikipage": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song)"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Superstar: The Karen Carpenter Story", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superstar%3A%20The%20Karen%20Carpenter%20Story"}, {"title": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superstar%20%28Delaney%20and%20Bonnie%20song%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "\"Superstar\" is a 1969 song written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett that has been a hit for many artists in different genres and interpretations in the years since. The best-known versions are by the Carpenters in 1971, and by Luther Vandross in 1983."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell were the main writers for the Carpenters' song Superstar, but Delaney Bramlett also received credit for writing the song."}], "sample_id": "-8662129582734887891", "question": "Who wrote the song superstar by the carpenters?", "docs": [{"id": "9026335", "title": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song)", "text": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song) \"Superstar\" is a 1969 song written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett that has been a hit for many artists in different genres and interpretations in the years since; the best-known version is by the Carpenters in 1971. In its first recorded incarnation, the song was called \"Groupie (Superstar),\" and was recorded and released as a B-side to the Delaney & Bonnie single \"Comin' Home\" in December 1969. Released by Atlantic Records, the full credit on the single was to Delaney & Bonnie and Friends Featuring", "score": 0.83349609375, "summary": "The song \"Superstar\" was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett. The Carpenters released the best-known version in 1971.", "extraction": "Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett wrote the song \"Superstar\" that has been a hit for many artists in different genres and interpretations in the years since; the best-known version is by the Carpenters in 1971."}], "answer": "Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell were the main writers for the Carpenters' song Superstar, but Delaney Bramlett also received credit for writing the song.", "output": "The song \"Superstar\" was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett [1]. The Carpenters recorded a version of the song in 1971 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The song \"Superstar\" was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Carpenters recorded a version of the song in 1971.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 850, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The song \"Superstar\" was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Carpenters recorded a version of the song in 1971.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song \"Superstar\" was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Carpenters recorded a version of the song in 1971.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9966733287706493, "ctx": {"title": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song)", "text": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song) \"Superstar\" is a 1969 song written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell with a songwriting credit also given to Delaney Bramlett that has been a hit for many artists in different genres and interpretations in the years since; the best-known version is by the Carpenters in 1971. In its first recorded incarnation, the song was called \"Groupie (Superstar),\" and was recorded and released as a B-side to the Delaney & Bonnie single \"Comin' Home\" in December 1969. Released by Atlantic Records, the full credit on the single was to Delaney & Bonnie and Friends Featuring"}, "final_score": 1.9966733287706493, "relevance_score": 0.9986541398891072, "ground_score": 0.9980191888815422, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24160979674194724, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001345836255348865, "[Relevant]": 0.9986364386158392}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.996229494715315, "[Partially supported]": 0.003428431181344697, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0002664457222473202}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00010251894454237461, "[Utility:2]": 0.00012269911099107645, "[Utility:3]": 3.9216991374122726e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009957389315458514, "[Utility:5]": 0.9267349160707369}], "n_tokens": 54}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The song \"Superstar\" by the Carpenters was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song \"Superstar\" by the Carpenters was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9864046224054115, "ctx": {"title": "The Carpenters", "text": "demo was written by Williams about his mother, which led to the line, \"Talking to myself and feeling old\". Richard rearranged the song to include a saxophone solo, played by Bob Messenger. The single peaked at No. 2 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100, kept from the top slot by Carole King's \"It's Too Late\". \"Superstar\", written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell, became the duo's next hit. The song had originally appeared on Joe Cocker's 1970 album \"Mad Dogs & Englishmen\", sung by Rita Coolidge. Karen was familiar with the album, but Richard first heard the song when it was"}, "final_score": 1.9864046224054115, "relevance_score": 0.998338609106349, "ground_score": 0.9880660132990625, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.19557066110202734, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0016613574690831992, "[Relevant]": 0.9983185241061243}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9784312824754754, "[Partially supported]": 0.01848945314808339, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0026845496060756975}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018019973161781603, "[Utility:2]": 8.629207221845042e-05, "[Utility:3]": 9.797289470938022e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0005550545141702583, "[Utility:5]": 0.6403994707331125}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The song Superstar by the Carpenters was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song Superstar by the Carpenters was written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9763815980336816, "ctx": {"title": "Carpenters (album)", "text": "on to win an Academy Award for \"Best Song\". James [Arthur] Griffin and Robb [Wilson] Royer of the popular '70s soft-rock group Bread petitioned the National Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences to use their real names on their awards, but to no avail, as the published composer credits are under their respective pseudonyms. Written by Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell, \"Superstar\" had been made popular by Rita Coolidge in 1970, but the Carpenters' version, released as a single in August 1971, became an international hit. In the liner notes for \"\", Richard writes that he saw Bette Midler"}, "final_score": 1.9763815980336816, "relevance_score": 0.9971550907993504, "ground_score": 0.9792265072343312, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1900427309634688, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0028448740023418275, "[Relevant]": 0.9971427536141043}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9612877106338386, "[Partially supported]": 0.03501489838705847, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0032568928882355285}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001635346224052271, "[Utility:2]": 8.550605787311298e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010724862377058717, "[Utility:4]": 0.0005299623550149806, "[Utility:5]": 0.7669290530117736}], "n_tokens": 25}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]\"Superstar\" is a song written by Delaney and Bonnie and first recorded by them in 1969.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was later recorded by the Carpenters in 1971.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]\"Superstar\" is a song written by Delaney and Bonnie and first recorded by them in 1969.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was later recorded by the Carpenters in 1971.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8550770369514362, "ctx": {"title": "Superstar (Delaney and Bonnie song)", "text": "as a single in August 1971, rising to number 2 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 pop singles chart (held out of the top spot by Rod Stewart's \"Maggie May\"), and spending two weeks at number one on the Easy Listening chart that autumn and earned gold record status. It also reached number 18 on the UK pop singles chart and did well in Australia and New Zealand as well. Richard would be nominated for a Grammy Award for Best Arrangement Accompanying Vocalist for his efforts. \"Superstar\" would go on to appear on two mid-1970s Carpenters live albums as well as"}, "final_score": 1.8550770369514362, "relevance_score": 0.9881308775446219, "ground_score": 0.8669461594068142, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3010140524197027, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.011868460299392121, "[Relevant]": 0.9880757515841351}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7825381648906782, "[Partially supported]": 0.16275243198520634, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05121232491385237}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000235324170150196, "[Utility:2]": 0.00021342975476325754, "[Utility:3]": 7.162977833427958e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0016271044192841348, "[Utility:5]": 0.7265916908212262}], "n_tokens": 48}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The song Superstar by the Carpenters was written by Todd Haynes, Cynthia Schneider, and Barry Ellsworth.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song Superstar by the Carpenters was written by Todd Haynes, Cynthia Schneider, and Barry Ellsworth.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9284535355394885, "ctx": {"title": "Superstar: The Karen Carpenter Story", "text": "Superstar: The Karen Carpenter Story Superstar: The Karen Carpenter Story is a 1987 American short biographical film portraying the last 17 years of singer Karen Carpenter's life. Directed by Todd Haynes, the film uses Barbie dolls as actors, as well as documentary and artistic footage. \"Superstar\" was co-written and co-produced by Haynes and Cynthia Schneider, with an unauthorized soundtrack consisting mostly of the hit songs of The Carpenters. \"Superstar\" was filmed over a ten day period at Bard College in the summer of 1985. Barry Ellsworth collaborated on the film and was the cinematographer for the Barbie themed interior segments"}, "final_score": 1.9284535355394885, "relevance_score": 0.9961456366459905, "ground_score": 0.932307898893498, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.18176996796158645, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003854198060227603, "[Relevant]": 0.996102917092985}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8752103895857982, "[Partially supported]": 0.11214321646785086, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.011546005249663935}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014310600677169662, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001038838202499199, "[Utility:3]": 9.025582713523325e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00032249436451918734, "[Utility:5]": 0.8583816804830822}], "n_tokens": 34}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Starbucks Corporation is an American coffee company and coffeehouse chain. Starbucks was founded in Seattle, Washington, in 1971. As of early 2019, the company operates over 30,000 locations worldwide.", "question": "How many starbucks are there around the world, as of early 2019?", "short_answers": ["30,000", "over 30,000"], "wikipage": "Starbucks"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many starbucks are there around the world, as of 2018?", "short_answers": ["28,218"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many starbucks are there around the world as of November 2019?", "short_answers": ["31,256"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "Independently operated Starbucks locations exist. Stores that independently operate locations include Ahold Delhaize, Barnes & Noble, Target, Albertsons and, more recently, Publix stores. As of 2015, 4,962 licensed locations exist.", "question": "How many independently operated and licensed starbucks locations are there around the world as of 2015?", "short_answers": ["4,962"], "wikipage": "Starbucks"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Starbucks", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starbucks"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Starbucks Corporation is an American multinational chain of coffeehouses and roastery reserves headquartered in Seattle, Washington.", "wikipage": "Starbucks"}, {"content": "As the world's largest coffeehouse chain, Starbucks is seen to be the main representation of the United States' second wave of coffee culture.[4][5]", "wikipage": "Starbucks"}], "long_answer": "Starbucks Corporation, the world's largest coffeehouse chain, is an American multinational chain of coffeehouses and roastery reserves headquartered in Seattle, Washington. In 2018, there were 28,218 Starbucks around the world. It increased to over 30,000 in early 2019 and by November 2019, there were 31,256 locations. There are 4,962 independently operated Starbucks locations that operate in locations like Ahold Delhaize, Barnes & Noble, Target, Albertsons and, more recently, Publix stores."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "As of September 2020, the company had 32,660 stores in 83 countries, including 16,637 company operated stores and 16,023 licensed stores.", "wikipage": "Starbucks"}, {"content": "In December 2020, Starbucks announced that it is planning to increase its store count to about 55,000 by 2030, up from roughly 33,000.", "wikipage": "Starbucks"}], "long_answer": "Starbucks Corporation is an American coffee company and coffeehouse chain that has company operated stores as well as independently operated and licensed stores around the world, with the number of stores changing over time. There were 28,218 Starbucks around the world in 2018, while in early 2019 there were 30,000, and by November 2019, there were 31,256. In 2015 there were 4,962 independently operated and licensed Starbucks, in 2020 that number grew to 16,023. In December 2020, Starbucks announced that its planning to increase its store count to about 55,000 by 2030."}], "sample_id": "-2403867946266681362", "question": "How many starbucks are there around the world?", "docs": [{"id": "1516450", "title": "Starbucks", "text": "Starbucks Starbucks Corporation is an American coffee company and coffeehouse chain. Starbucks was founded in Seattle, Washington in 1971. As of 2018, the company operates 28,218 locations worldwide. Starbucks is considered the main representative of \"second wave coffee\", initially distinguishing itself from other coffee-serving venues in the US by taste, quality, and customer experience while popularizing darkly roasted coffee. Since the 2000s, third wave coffee makers have targeted quality-minded coffee drinkers with hand-made coffee based on lighter roasts, while Starbucks nowadays uses automated espresso machines for efficiency and safety reasons. Starbucks locations serve hot and cold drinks, whole-bean coffee, microground", "score": 0.7275390625, "summary": "As of 2018, Starbucks operates 28,218 locations worldwide.", "extraction": "As of 2018, the company operates 28,218 locations worldwide."}], "answer": "Starbucks Corporation is an American coffee company and coffeehouse chain that has company operated stores as well as independently operated and licensed stores around the world, with the number of stores changing over time. There were 28,218 Starbucks around the world in 2018, while in early 2019 there were 30,000, and by November 2019, there were 31,256. In 2015 there were 4,962 independently operated and licensed Starbucks, in 2020 that number grew to 16,023. In December 2020, Starbucks announced that its planning to increase its store count to about 55,000 by 2030.", "output": "As of 2018, there are 28,218 Starbucks locations worldwide [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 28,218 Starbucks locations worldwide.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 851, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]As of 2019, there are 2,800 Starbucks locations around the world.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]As of 2019, there are 2,800 Starbucks locations around the world.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.8921052021671457, "ctx": {"title": "Starbucks", "text": "announced ongoing losses in the Australian market, which resulted in the remaining stores being sold to the Withers Group. In January 2009, Starbucks announced the closure of an additional 300 underperforming stores and the elimination of 7,000 positions. CEO Howard Schultz also announced that he had received board approval to reduce his salary. Altogether, from February 2008 to January 2009, Starbucks terminated an estimated 18,400 U.S. jobs and began closing 977 stores worldwide. In August 2009, Ahold announced closures and rebranding for 43 of their licensed store Starbucks kiosks for their US based Stop & Shop and Giant supermarkets. In"}, "final_score": 0.8921052021671457, "relevance_score": 0.8407825958435005, "ground_score": 0.05132260632364529, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3849316931635777, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.15918595255866366, "[Relevant]": 0.840616508748864}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.0068938198943221465, "[Partially supported]": 0.0887046970952305, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9029121221384395}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001883422960490088, "[Utility:2]": 0.00014553964605701721, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017350905374378635, "[Utility:4]": 0.004026937771138826, "[Utility:5]": 0.9587807871639947}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]As of September 2018, Starbucks is present on 6 continents and in 76 countries and territories, with a total of 27,340 locations.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]As of September 2018, Starbucks is present on 6 continents and in 76 countries and territories, with a total of 27,340 locations.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3362555276671426, "ctx": {"title": "Starbucks", "text": "brew strength\u2014the Verismo has buttons for coffee, espresso, and latte with no strength variation for any type. And since Starbucks has limited its coffee selection to its own brand, there are only eight varieties so far plus a milk pod for the latte.\" The company's headquarters is located in Seattle, Washington, United States, where 3,501 people worked as of January 2015. The main building in the Starbucks complex was previously a Sears distribution center. As of September 2018, Starbucks is present on 6 continents and in 76 countries and territories, with a total of 27,340 locations In 2008, Starbucks continued"}, "final_score": 1.3362555276671426, "relevance_score": 0.9372107922798332, "ground_score": 0.3990447353873094, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.0848144160082118, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.06278362909937678, "[Relevant]": 0.9371275240909147}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.26674620267135235, "[Partially supported]": 0.2626106861146689, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.46815419312899614}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005170798770764604, "[Utility:2]": 0.00030516530509016736, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005928349997717808, "[Utility:4]": 0.008443617535327467, "[Utility:5]": 0.9240105930103737}], "n_tokens": 46}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 28,218 Starbucks locations worldwide.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 28,218 Starbucks locations worldwide.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.827861926821842, "ctx": {"title": "Starbucks", "text": "Starbucks Starbucks Corporation is an American coffee company and coffeehouse chain. Starbucks was founded in Seattle, Washington in 1971. As of 2018, the company operates 28,218 locations worldwide. Starbucks is considered the main representative of \"second wave coffee\", initially distinguishing itself from other coffee-serving venues in the US by taste, quality, and customer experience while popularizing darkly roasted coffee. Since the 2000s, third wave coffee makers have targeted quality-minded coffee drinkers with hand-made coffee based on lighter roasts, while Starbucks nowadays uses automated espresso machines for efficiency and safety reasons. Starbucks locations serve hot and cold drinks, whole-bean coffee, microground"}, "final_score": 1.827861926821842, "relevance_score": 0.9968763509818163, "ground_score": 0.8309855758400256, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.15201737306012678, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0031234401081911395, "[Relevant]": 0.9968096797809598}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7761537036360877, "[Partially supported]": 0.10669514561946028, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.11536495921795363}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023207849430355776, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002064148857644098, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004177847204292648, "[Utility:4]": 0.002442045476050154, "[Utility:5]": 0.8459768999934735}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 28,218 Starbucks locations worldwide.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 28,218 Starbucks locations worldwide.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7689744023393805, "ctx": {"title": "Starbucks", "text": "Video of the week on the Food Consumer website. Starbucks Starbucks Corporation is an American coffee company and coffeehouse chain. Starbucks was founded in Seattle, Washington in 1971. As of 2018, the company operates 28,218 locations worldwide. Starbucks is considered the main representative of \"second wave coffee\", initially distinguishing itself from other coffee-serving venues in the US by taste, quality, and customer experience while popularizing darkly roasted coffee. Since the 2000s, third wave coffee makers have targeted quality-minded coffee drinkers with hand-made coffee based on lighter roasts, while Starbucks nowadays uses automated espresso machines for efficiency and safety reasons. Starbucks"}, "final_score": 1.7689744023393805, "relevance_score": 0.9960237717747117, "ground_score": 0.7729506305646687, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.15794370339101857, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003975898796444428, "[Relevant]": 0.9959412516221928}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7057203090662503, "[Partially supported]": 0.13054513460463854, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.16120172419380513}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00024550039780195604, "[Utility:2]": 0.00022616556455941478, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005674707422998862, "[Utility:4]": 0.002749884507780074, "[Utility:5]": 0.8308893285455083}], "n_tokens": 28}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Irrelevant]Starbucks has 30,271 outlets in 77 countries.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]Starbucks has 30,271 outlets in 77 countries.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.16344544925827645, "ctx": {"title": "Borders Group", "text": "franchise store in the Mall of the Emirates in Dubai, UAE in October 2006. Despite financial difficulties in the domestic market, Borders continued to expand its franchises, adding stores in Malaysia, Oman, and Sharjah. In 2003, Borders had 1,249 stores using the Borders and Waldenbooks names. In 2004, Borders reached an agreement with Starbucks subsidiary Seattle's Best Coffee to operate cafes in its domestic superstores under the Seattle's Best brand name. In March 2007, Borders Group announced it would scale down the number of Waldenbooks outlets it had by half, to about 300, in the next year. Also in March"}, "final_score": 0.16344544925827645, "relevance_score": 0.16344544925827645, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.6349758144880814, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.8362971244622421, "[Relevant]": 0.1633951534899211}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0011626112178092325, "[Utility:2]": 0.0013071589284386503, "[Utility:3]": 0.00043698906815499194, "[Utility:4]": 0.006163453632949662, "[Utility:5]": 0.9364301767110071}], "n_tokens": 22}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where could I previously use a 'Golden Age Passport'?", "short_answers": ["United States National Parks", "United States national parks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where could I previously use a \"Golden Age Passport\" according to the National Park Service's description of the pass?", "short_answers": ["Federal recreation entrance or standard amenity fee sites"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Golden Age Passport", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden%20Age%20Passport"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Senior Pass \u2013 a lifetime pass for $80 (or annual pass for $20) available to U.S. citizens or permanent residents aged 62 or older. It replaced the Golden Age Passport that functioned similarly and is still valid for those possessing one.", "wikipage": "America the Beautiful Pass Series"}, {"content": "The Senior and Access passes additionally provide a 50% discount on some amenity fees such as camping, swimming, boat launching, and specialized interpretive services at some sites.[1]", "wikipage": "America the Beautiful Pass Series"}], "long_answer": "The Golden Age Passport, now known as the Senior Pass, is a lifetime pass available to U.S. citizens and permanent residents aged 62 or older. It can be used at United States National Parks at the federal recreation entrance or standard amenity fee sites. It also provides a 50% discount on some amenity fees such as camping, swimming, boat launching, and specialized interpretive services at some sites."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Senior Pass \u2013 a lifetime pass for $80 (or annual pass for $20) available to U.S. citizens or permanent residents aged 62 or older. It replaced the Golden Age Passport that functioned similarly and is still valid for those possessing one.", "wikipage": "America the Beautiful Pass"}], "long_answer": "A Golden Age Passport could previously be used at United States national parks and, according to the National Park Service's description of the pass, at a federal recreation entrance or standard amenity fee sites. The Golden Age Passport was replaced by the Senior Pass but is still valid for those possessing one."}], "sample_id": "8690134227082764044", "question": "Where can i use my golden age passport?", "docs": [{"id": "12001187", "title": "Golden Age Passport", "text": "Golden Age Passport Golden Age Passport was a pass issued by the National Park Service until January 1, 2007. It has been replaced with the Senior Pass of the new pass series now called America the Beautiful \u2013 National Parks and Federal Recreational Lands Pass, created by the Federal Lands Recreation Enhancement Act and authorized by Congress in December 2004. The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older. To obtain this a senior citizen need only go to any National Park Service facility that charges an entrance fee with", "score": 0.76708984375, "summary": "The Golden Age Passport was replaced by the Senior Pass, which is a lifetime entrance pass for those 62 years and older to United States national parks. The Senior Pass can be obtained by going to any National Park Service facility that charges an entrance fee.", "extraction": "The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older."}], "answer": "A Golden Age Passport could previously be used at United States national parks and, according to the National Park Service's description of the pass, at a federal recreation entrance or standard amenity fee sites. The Golden Age Passport was replaced by the Senior Pass but is still valid for those possessing one.", "output": "The Golden Age Passport was a pass issued by the National Park Service until January 1, 2007 [1]. It has been replaced with the Senior Pass of the new pass series now called America the Beautiful \u2013 National Parks and Federal Recreational Lands Pass, created by the Federal Lands Recreation Enhancement Act and authorized by Congress in December 2004 [1]. The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Golden Age Passport was a pass issued by the National Park Service until January 1, 2007.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It has been replaced with the Senior Pass of the new pass series now called America the Beautiful \u2013 National Parks and Federal Recreational Lands Pass, created by the Federal Lands Recreation Enhancement Act and authorized by Congress in December 2004.[Continue to Use Evidence]The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 853, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Golden Age Passport was a pass issued by the National Park Service until January 1, 2007.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It has been replaced with the Senior Pass of the new pass series now called America the Beautiful \u2013 National Parks and Federal Recreational Lands Pass, created by the Federal Lands Recreation Enhancement Act and authorized by Congress in December 2004.[Continue to Use Evidence]The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Golden Age Passport was a pass issued by the National Park Service until January 1, 2007.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It has been replaced with the Senior Pass of the new pass series now called America the Beautiful \u2013 National Parks and Federal Recreational Lands Pass, created by the Federal Lands Recreation Enhancement Act and authorized by Congress in December 2004.[Continue to Use Evidence]The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8226646145074836, "ctx": {"title": "Golden Age Passport", "text": "Golden Age Passport Golden Age Passport was a pass issued by the National Park Service until January 1, 2007. It has been replaced with the Senior Pass of the new pass series now called America the Beautiful \u2013 National Parks and Federal Recreational Lands Pass, created by the Federal Lands Recreation Enhancement Act and authorized by Congress in December 2004. The Senior Pass is a lifetime entrance pass to United States national parks for those 62 years and older. To obtain this a senior citizen need only go to any National Park Service facility that charges an entrance fee with"}, "final_score": 1.8226646145074836, "relevance_score": 0.9597620580957161, "ground_score": 0.8629025564117676, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.04931531032631634, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.04023170323377063, "[Relevant]": 0.9596132522928295}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8066972949943343, "[Partially supported]": 0.0912187970513284, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08980458068495227}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 6.303979611610941e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00010152739985329372, "[Utility:3]": 7.69368504502775e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0006517778032912575, "[Utility:5]": 0.6283153451297506}], "n_tokens": 111}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Gold Age was a digital currency exchange and one of the first independent e-gold exchanges.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Gold Age was a digital currency exchange and one of the first independent e-gold exchanges.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.6801695967979849, "ctx": {"title": "Gold Age", "text": "Gold Age The Gold Age was a digital currency exchange and one of the first independent e-gold exchanges. Gold Age was a registered legal corporate entity of Panama. The Gold Age was launched in 1999 and closed down by regulators in 2006. Gold Age used to buy and sell and trade the following digital gold currencies: e-gold, e-Bullion, and 1mdc. Gold Age's fees for exchanging fiat currency into digital gold currency ranged from 4 percent to 1.75 percent, depending on the amount exchanged and if the client was an \"Inner Circle Member\". Gold Age was an accredited and gold awarded"}, "final_score": 0.6801695967979849, "relevance_score": 0.653126900716748, "ground_score": 0.02704269608123696, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1757409604190616, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.34646855101998864, "[Relevant]": 0.6523651773259128}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.021464884341411583, "[Partially supported]": 0.010963192787666042, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9640140177824903}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.009467380224757915, "[Utility:2]": 0.0018210526800479115, "[Utility:3]": 0.0008304882314349069, "[Utility:4]": 0.047149385659950145, "[Utility:5]": 0.6114425730569247}], "n_tokens": 25}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Irrelevant]The Golden Age Passport is a discount card offered by AARP to its members.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is accepted at many locations, including restaurants, movie theaters, and retail stores.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]The Golden Age Passport is a discount card offered by AARP to its members.[Continue to Use Evidence]It is accepted at many locations, including restaurants, movie theaters, and retail stores.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.12002561673739197, "ctx": {"title": "Gold Age", "text": "five years probation. Budovsky subsequently fled the country and founded Liberty Reserve to perform a similar service as Gold Age. Gold Age The Gold Age was a digital currency exchange and one of the first independent e-gold exchanges. Gold Age was a registered legal corporate entity of Panama. The Gold Age was launched in 1999 and closed down by regulators in 2006. Gold Age used to buy and sell and trade the following digital gold currencies: e-gold, e-Bullion, and 1mdc. Gold Age's fees for exchanging fiat currency into digital gold currency ranged from 4 percent to 1.75 percent, depending on"}, "final_score": 0.12002561673739197, "relevance_score": 0.12002561673739197, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.6100439979494864, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.8797143836813446, "[Relevant]": 0.11999015364814054}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008012280319356987, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008868785853801583, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003156092714057731, "[Utility:4]": 0.0178831523768646, "[Utility:5]": 0.815801831754932}], "n_tokens": 43}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The United States, Canada, and the United Kingdom all offer discounts to seniors with a valid Golden Age Passport.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The United States, Canada, and the United Kingdom all offer discounts to seniors with a valid Golden Age Passport.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9589091490330541, "ctx": {"title": "EPassport gates", "text": "Singapore, South Korea, Taiwan and the United States who are enrolled in the Registered Traveller Service can use ePassport gates, provided that they are aged either 18 and over or 12 and over travelling with an adult and holding valid biometric passports. On 29 October 2018, Chancellor of the Exchequer Philip Hammond announced that visitors from the United States, Canada, New Zealand, Australia and Japan will be able to use the gates in 2019 if they meet unspecified eligibility guidelines. To use the ePassport gates, the traveller must have a biometric EEA or Swiss passport or be an eligible citizen"}, "final_score": 0.9589091490330541, "relevance_score": 0.7461389873857265, "ground_score": 0.21277016164732765, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.6774002012167936, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.25371419437100734, "[Relevant]": 0.7457074645842048}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.006709035241857098, "[Partially supported]": 0.4118432109998216, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5807920184593511}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0011406446765310902, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005022190541086702, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004886725519595816, "[Utility:4]": 0.02535957439041627, "[Utility:5]": 0.9223329469599377}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Queanbeyan Age is a weekly newspaper based in Queanbeyan, New South Wales, Australia.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It has had a number of title changes throughout its publication history.[Continue to Use Evidence]First published on 15 September 1860 by John Gale and his brother, Peter Francis Gale, \"The Golden Age\", as it was known at the time, was the first newspaper of the small township on the banks of the Queanbeyan River.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Queanbeyan Age is a weekly newspaper based in Queanbeyan, New South Wales, Australia.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It has had a number of title changes throughout its publication history.[Continue to Use Evidence]First published on 15 September 1860 by John Gale and his brother, Peter Francis Gale, \"The Golden Age\", as it was known at the time, was the first newspaper of the small township on the banks of the Queanbeyan River.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.872316602286455, "ctx": {"title": "The Queanbeyan Age", "text": "The Queanbeyan Age The Queanbeyan Age is a weekly newspaper based in Queanbeyan, New South Wales, Australia. It has had a number of title changes throughout its publication history. First published on 15 September 1860 by John Gale and his brother, Peter Francis Gale, \"The Golden Age\", as it was known at the time, was the first newspaper of the small township on the banks of the Queanbeyan River. It was named due to the short-lived Kiandra goldrush, which generated large amounts of gold-based traffic through the region. The \"Age\" published in 32 to 48 page editions on Fridays. Previously"}, "final_score": 0.872316602286455, "relevance_score": 0.5907792048683922, "ground_score": 0.2815373974180629, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.059596889391792224, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.40786951634191526, "[Relevant]": 0.5888284061347323}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2587805968920083, "[Partially supported]": 0.034479177339469015, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.6871434975650313}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00046769861132129465, "[Utility:2]": 0.00022178985746111585, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002454989930414308, "[Utility:4]": 0.003171267393239059, "[Utility:5]": 0.7065409537728432}], "n_tokens": 101}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The following is a listing of all 30 current Major League Baseball (MLB) teams ranked by win-loss record percentage, accurate as of the end of April 10th, 2019. The records do not count wins and losses recorded by a team's playing time in the National Association of Professional Base Ball Players (NAPP). Counting MLB statistics, the New York Yankees have the highest win-loss record percentage, with . The San Diego Padres have the lowest win-loss record percentage, with . The San Francisco Giants and Tampa Bay Rays have recorded the most and least overall wins, with 11,092 and 1,599, respectively. The Chicago Cubs lead the association with the most played games, with 21,395. Conversely, the Rays have played the fewest overall games, with 3,412.", "question": "Which National League team has the most wins in a single MLB season?|Who has the highest win percentage in a Major League Baseball season?", "short_answers": ["Chicago Cubs", "Chicago", "Cubs"], "wikipage": "List of all-time Major League Baseball win\u2013loss records"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who has the highest number of wins in a Major League Baseball season?", "short_answers": ["Cubs and Mariners", "Chicago Cubs and Seattle Mariners", "Chicago and Seattle"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which American League team has the most wins in a single MLB season?", "short_answers": ["Seattle Mariners"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of Major League Baseball wins records", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20Major%20League%20Baseball%20wins%20records"}, {"title": "List of all-time Major League Baseball win\u2013loss records", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20all-time%20Major%20League%20Baseball%20win%E2%80%93loss%20records"}, {"title": "List of best Major League Baseball season win-loss records ...", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20best%20Major%20League%20Baseball%20season%20win-loss%20records"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": " Although they fell to the \"Hitless Wonders\" White Sox in the 1906 World Series, the Cubs recorded a record 116 victories and the best winning percentage (.763) in Major League history.", "wikipage": "Chicago Cubs"}, {"content": "The Mariners finished with a 116\u201346 (.716) record, tying the major league record for wins set by the 1906 Chicago Cubs,[1] and setting the record for wins by an American League team. ", "wikipage": "2001 Seattle Mariners season"}], "long_answer": " Although they fell to the \"Hitless Wonders\" White Sox in the 1906 World Series, the Cubs recorded a record 116 victories and the best winning percentage (.763) in Major League history. The Mariners finished with a 116\u201346 (.716) record, tying the major league record for wins set by the 1906 Chicago Cubs,[1] and setting the record for wins by an American League team. "}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Seattle Mariners have the most major league baseball wins in a single season for the American League teams, and the Chicago Cubs have the most wins in a single season for the National League teams. So, both the Cubs and Mariners have the highest number of wins in a MLB season."}], "sample_id": "8092204619038306144", "question": "Who has the most wins in a major league baseball season?", "docs": [{"id": "10270424", "title": "1998 Major League Baseball season", "text": "1998 Major League Baseball season The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games. The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record. The 1998 season was also marked by an expansion to 30 teams (16 in the NL, 14 in the AL), with two new teams\u2013the Arizona Diamondbacks in the National League, and the Tampa Bay Devil Rays in the American League\u2013added to the MLB.", "score": 0.748046875, "summary": "The New York Yankees won a record-breaking 125 games in the 1998 Major League Baseball season, which remains the MLB record.", "extraction": "The New York Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record. Therefore, the answer to the question \"Who has the most wins in a major league baseball season?\" is the New York Yankees with 125 wins."}], "answer": "Seattle Mariners have the most major league baseball wins in a single season for the American League teams, and the Chicago Cubs have the most wins in a single season for the National League teams. So, both the Cubs and Mariners have the highest number of wins in a MLB season.", "output": "The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games [1]. The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 854, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The 2009 Major League Baseball season was the 135th season of Major League Baseball, and the Cincinnati Reds won the most games in a major league baseball season with 103.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 2009 Major League Baseball season was the 135th season of Major League Baseball, and the Cincinnati Reds won the most games in a major league baseball season with 103.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.344812073593061, "ctx": {"title": "2009 Major League Baseball season", "text": "was played June 20 at Great American Ball Park in Cincinnati, Ohio, when the host Cincinnati Reds lost to the Chicago White Sox in an interleague game, 10\u20138. Both teams wore replicas of their 1965 uniforms in the contest. The New York Yankees, with 103 wins, clinched Major League Baseball's best record in the 2009 season, and the #1 seed in the American League by winning the AL East. The Los Angeles Angels of Anaheim won the second seed with a 97\u201365 record, while a 95\u201367 mark was enough to win the wild card spot for the Boston Red Sox."}, "final_score": 1.344812073593061, "relevance_score": 0.9252299586383206, "ground_score": 0.41958211495474057, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.313853843179851, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.07476096655157632, "[Relevant]": 0.9251176638472058}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.3438259185000555, "[Partially supported]": 0.14903750391704235, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5041873467838311}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002585923379711653, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003618357346000114, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020616778694898305, "[Utility:4]": 0.008974368394578545, "[Utility:5]": 0.9518763127211792}], "n_tokens": 51}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In Major League Baseball, the pitcher with the most wins in a season is Christy Mathewson, who won 37 games in 1916.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In Major League Baseball, the pitcher with the most wins in a season is Christy Mathewson, who won 37 games in 1916.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.8932472791149287, "ctx": {"title": "Win\u2013loss record (pitching)", "text": "pitching record of 12\u201310 indicates 12 wins and 10 losses. In the early years of Major League Baseball before 1900 it was common for an exceptional pitcher to win 30 or more games in one season with Old Hoss Radbourn of the defunct Providence Grays holding the record with 59 wins in 1884. Since 1900, however, pitchers have made fewer and fewer starts and the standard has changed. Gradually, as hitting improved, better pitching was needed. This meant, among other things, throwing the ball much harder, and it became unrealistic to ask a pitcher to throw nearly as hard as"}, "final_score": 0.8932472791149287, "relevance_score": 0.7786493222753043, "ground_score": 0.11459795683962445, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.31890316089639104, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.22127417035903418, "[Relevant]": 0.778380190917615}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.045048594481577296, "[Partially supported]": 0.1376795888024952, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.811080013856889}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016332561537134587, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004269568147317814, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002822013571236368, "[Utility:4]": 0.010756283470217225, "[Utility:5]": 0.8478280264177624}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8514502528492354, "ctx": {"title": "1998 Major League Baseball season", "text": "1998 Major League Baseball season The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games. The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record. The 1998 season was also marked by an expansion to 30 teams (16 in the NL, 14 in the AL), with two new teams\u2013the Arizona Diamondbacks in the National League, and the Tampa Bay Devil Rays in the American League\u2013added to the MLB."}, "final_score": 1.8514502528492354, "relevance_score": 0.991812089652078, "ground_score": 0.8596381631971574, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.052336469309943824, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008187410073765347, "[Relevant]": 0.991751490801384}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8055876082674424, "[Partially supported]": 0.10004642767411731, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08968103816162135}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00021837214903239102, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005117126377043551, "[Utility:3]": 0.00023983488633936106, "[Utility:4]": 0.004040684805867968, "[Utility:5]": 0.9397679108825905}], "n_tokens": 80}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Jack Chesbro holds the record for most wins in a single season in the modern era of Major League Baseball with 41 wins in 1904.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Jack Chesbro holds the record for most wins in a single season in the modern era of Major League Baseball with 41 wins in 1904.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7487046282532446, "ctx": {"title": "Jack Chesbro", "text": "Chesbro and his 1904 win-total as the modern era major league record and its holder. Some view Chesbro's 41 wins in a season as an unbreakable record. Chesbro's 1904 pitching totals of 51 games started and 48 complete games also fall into the same historical category as his 1904 wins total, as they are all-time American League single-season records. These 1904 single-season totals for games started and complete games, as the wins total, are also the most recorded by a pitcher in either the American or National League, since the beginning of the twentieth century and the co-existence of the"}, "final_score": 1.7487046282532446, "relevance_score": 0.9669140245907174, "ground_score": 0.7817906036625271, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2683931394524878, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.03308091679897108, "[Relevant]": 0.9667661903136667}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7435481309837858, "[Partially supported]": 0.07024986665662034, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.18221431136429908}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002566413819054824, "[Utility:2]": 0.0008580916905922854, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004946892112235333, "[Utility:4]": 0.006909471698931737, "[Utility:5]": 0.6777855355864285}], "n_tokens": 38}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.548043379454049, "ctx": {"title": "1998 Major League Baseball season", "text": "that a team went from winning the World Series one year to finishing with 100 or more losses and last in their division the following year. American League Triple Crown pitching winner 1998 Major League Baseball season The 1998 Major League Baseball season ended with the New York Yankees sweeping the San Diego Padres in the World Series, after they had won a then AL record 114 regular season games. The Yankees finished with 125 wins for the season (regular season and playoffs combined), which remains the MLB record. The 1998 season was also marked by an expansion to 30"}, "final_score": 1.548043379454049, "relevance_score": 0.9348724472631125, "ground_score": 0.6131709321909365, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.059853283165205086, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.06512141569713777, "[Relevant]": 0.9347843532210566}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5541463660885515, "[Partially supported]": 0.11525147381504572, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3283208562683429}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023702806627663958, "[Utility:2]": 0.0006029123504723073, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002503515317601202, "[Utility:4]": 0.0062579978482863625, "[Utility:5]": 0.9545157506962209}], "n_tokens": 80}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Fort Davis has the highest elevation above sea level of any county seat in Texas; the elevation is .", "question": "What unincorporated city in texas has the highest elevation?", "short_answers": ["Fort Davis, Texas", "Fort Davis"], "wikipage": "Fort Davis, Texas"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What incorporated city in texas has the highest elevation?", "short_answers": ["Marfa, Texas", "Marfa"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Fort Davis, Texas", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort%20Davis%2C%20Texas"}, {"title": "List of highest United States cities by state or territory", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20highest%20United%20States%20cities%20by%20state%20or%20territory"}, {"title": "List of cities in the United States by elevation", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20cities%20in%20the%20United%20States%20by%20elevation"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Fort Davis is an unincorporated community and census-designated place (CDP) in Jeff Davis County, Texas, United States.", "wikipage": "Fort Davis, Texas"}, {"content": "Fort Davis has the highest elevation above sea level of any county seat in Texas; the elevation is 4,900 feet (1,500 m).", "wikipage": "Fort Davis, Texas"}, {"content": "Marfa is a city in the high desert of the Trans-Pecos in far West Texas, between the Davis Mountains and Big Bend National Park.", "wikipage": "Marfa, Texas"}, {"content": "highest incorporated city is Marfa, elevation 4,685 feet", "wikipage": "List of highest United States cities by state or territory"}], "long_answer": "Fort Davis is an unincorporated community and census-designated place (CDP) in Jeff Davis County, Texas, United States. Fort Davis has the highest elevation above sea level of any county seat in Texas; the elevation is 4,900 feet (1,500 m). Marfa is a city in the high desert of the Trans-Pecos in far West Texas, between the Davis Mountains and Big Bend National Park. Marfa is the highest incorporated city in Texas with an elevation 4,685 feet."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Fort Davis has the highest elevation above sea level of any county seat in Texas; the elevation is 4,900 feet (1,500 m).", "wikipage": "Fort Davis, Texas"}, {"content": "Texas (/\u02c8t\u025bks\u0259s/, also locally /\u02c8t\u025bks\u026az/)[9] is a state in the South Central region of the United States.", "wikipage": "Texas"}], "long_answer": "The unincorporated city with the highest elevation in the state of Texas is Fort Davis, which has an elevation of 4,900 feet, while the incorporated city with the highest elevation in Texas is Marfa."}], "sample_id": "3643093465010420057", "question": "What city in texas has the highest elevation?", "docs": [{"id": "3663239", "title": "Guadalupe Peak", "text": "Guadalupe Peak Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of above sea level. It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas. The mountain is about east of El Paso and about southwest of Carlsbad, New Mexico. The peak rises more than above the arid floor of the Chihuahuan Desert. The peak can be climbed by a maintained stony trail ( each way) with a 3,000 feet elevation gain at any time of the year. The", "score": 0.72216796875, "summary": "Guadalupe Peak in the Guadalupe Mountains range has the highest elevation in Texas.", "extraction": "\"Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of above sea level.\""}], "answer": "The unincorporated city with the highest elevation in the state of Texas is Fort Davis, which has an elevation of 4,900 feet, while the incorporated city with the highest elevation in Texas is Marfa.", "output": "Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of 8,749 feet above sea level [1]. It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of 8,749 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 856, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of 8,749 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of 8,749 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9932330858790044, "ctx": {"title": "Guadalupe Peak", "text": "Guadalupe Peak Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of above sea level. It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas. The mountain is about east of El Paso and about southwest of Carlsbad, New Mexico. The peak rises more than above the arid floor of the Chihuahuan Desert. The peak can be climbed by a maintained stony trail ( each way) with a 3,000 feet elevation gain at any time of the year. The"}, "final_score": 1.9932330858790044, "relevance_score": 0.9991169540068304, "ground_score": 0.994116131872174, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07205255077903305, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0008830061416512963, "[Relevant]": 0.9990718642517696}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9898847435098721, "[Partially supported]": 0.004548402779817629, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0035980827492082274}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.585820038170619e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00029022680052292224, "[Utility:3]": 0.00020025047101714138, "[Utility:4]": 0.0013525205275495005, "[Utility:5]": 0.5477814195384351}], "n_tokens": 77}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The city in Texas with the highest elevation is El Paso, which is located at 3,800 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The city in Texas with the highest elevation is El Paso, which is located at 3,800 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9390060097739417, "ctx": {"title": "El Paso, Texas", "text": "in the nation. The Franklin Mountains State Park, with its more than , is completely located within the city limits. It is considered a small range (23 miles long, wide) that extends from city north into New Mexico. It is home to the highest peak in the county North Franklin Mountain at 7,192 feet.The park is open year-round for recreation including hiking, mountain biking, picnicking, scenic driving and views of the city. The Wyler Aerial Tramway is operated by the Texas Parks and Wildlife Department and is in the Franklin Mountains State Park. The tramway complex covers on the east"}, "final_score": 1.9390060097739417, "relevance_score": 0.9955316165103048, "ground_score": 0.9434743932636368, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2973164698375485, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.004468121943280127, "[Relevant]": 0.99547334538698}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9167077094977869, "[Partially supported]": 0.04635892195353333, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03313122742572422}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00019015921032003523, "[Utility:2]": 0.00025588718668213476, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003443340105397465, "[Utility:4]": 0.0022629502180181423, "[Utility:5]": 0.8814011830685053}], "n_tokens": 33}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The highest point in Texas is Guadalupe Peak, located in the Guadalupe Mountains, which rises to an elevation of 8,749 feet (2,666 m) above sea level.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The highest point in Texas is Guadalupe Peak, located in the Guadalupe Mountains, which rises to an elevation of 8,749 feet (2,666 m) above sea level.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.988634082903857, "ctx": {"title": "Guadalupe Mountains", "text": "as summer homes by Pratt and his family until 1960. Wallace Pratt donated about of McKittrick Canyon which became part of Guadalupe Mountains National Park. The Guadalupe Mountains reach their highest point at Guadalupe Peak, the highest point in Texas, with an elevation of . The range lies southeast of the Sacramento Mountains and east of the Brokeoff Mountains. The mountain range extends north-northwest and northeast from Guadalupe Peak in Texas into New Mexico. The northeastern extension ends about 10 miles (16 km) southwest of Carlsbad, near White's City and Carlsbad Caverns National Park; the southwest tip ends with El"}, "final_score": 1.988634082903857, "relevance_score": 0.9986645994587752, "ground_score": 0.9899694834450817, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.27290408324174487, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0013353705155476577, "[Relevant]": 0.9986421450864899}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9845253641960111, "[Partially supported]": 0.009958334541302946, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005046638322368783}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00010857277149474545, "[Utility:2]": 0.00020724569632511448, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002934071627061967, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009471299230999546, "[Utility:5]": 0.711072604469577}], "n_tokens": 53}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The highest city in Texas is San Antonio, with an elevation of 724 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The highest city in Texas is San Antonio, with an elevation of 724 feet above sea level.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6256259907165767, "ctx": {"title": "Friedrich Wilderness Park", "text": "spend summers in the park. The park is in the transition zone between the flat South Texas region and the Texas Hill Country. The parking lot is at elevation above sea level and above the San Antonio River in Downtown San Antonio. The highest hill in the park is higher (1425 feet above sea level). The skyscrapers of Downtown San Antonio can be seen from the highest points in the park. Friedrich Wilderness Park Friedrich Wilderness Park lies at the southern end of the Texas Hill Country. The park is hilly due to its location on the Balcones escarpment that"}, "final_score": 1.6256259907165767, "relevance_score": 0.9811668711077101, "ground_score": 0.6444591196088667, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.38358287897696114, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.01883084083941925, "[Relevant]": 0.9810476683088024}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5776855754666455, "[Partially supported]": 0.12591312360867907, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2904785321690742}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00023213737483321329, "[Utility:2]": 0.00021469197561762261, "[Utility:3]": 0.00047076572886258427, "[Utility:4]": 0.0016495648903926184, "[Utility:5]": 0.8249768539696648}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The highest city in Texas is El Paso, with an elevation of 3,800 feet.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The highest city in Texas is El Paso, with an elevation of 3,800 feet.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.4582694824486202, "ctx": {"title": "Davis Mountains", "text": "since 1996, The Nature Conservancy has acquired 33,000 acres (130 km\u00b2) in the Davis Mountains range, along with conservation easements on 70,000 adjoining acres (280 km\u00b2) of private ranchland. The Davis Mountain Preserve is open to the public at specified times. McDonald Observatory is accessed by Spur 78 from State Highway 118. Spur 78 is the highest state maintained road in Texas at 6,791 feet near the summit of Mt. Locke where the older telescopes of the observatory are located. Spur 77 branches off from Spur 78 providing access to the newer research equipment atop Mt. Fowlkes. The Texas separatist"}, "final_score": 1.4582694824486202, "relevance_score": 0.9473810505521219, "ground_score": 0.5108884318964984, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.30416044790450136, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.05259950496298081, "[Relevant]": 0.9470309611504366}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.4730747086943009, "[Partially supported]": 0.07031627380941896, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.45141104053753384}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00022190368188535015, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002461051902907241, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004894388467724162, "[Utility:4]": 0.001928221345197731, "[Utility:5]": 0.8345647125439064}], "n_tokens": 27}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Originally the brothers competed as teenagers in the World Wrestling Federation (WWF) as jobbers\u2014wrestlers who consistently lose to make their opponents look stronger \u2013 both in singles and tag team competition. After being signed to a contract in 1998, they were trained by Dory Funk, Jr. in his Funkin' Dojo.", "question": "When did the hardy boyz sign to a contract in wwe?", "short_answers": ["1998"], "wikipage": "The Hardy Boyz"}, {"context": "The Hardy Boyz started their run as a popular tag team with a win over Kaientai (Men's Teioh and Shoichi Funaki) on the September 27, 1998 episode of \"Sunday Night Heat\". Soon after their \"Sunday Night Heat\" win, they built up a fan base following through their high-flying tag team tactics. They started to become serious contenders for the Tag Team Championship after they became managed by Michael Hayes, formerly a member of The Fabulous Freebirds. On the June 29, 1999, episode of \"Raw Is War\" (aired July 5), they defeated The Acolytes (Faarooq and Bradshaw) to win their first WWF Tag Team Championship, but they re-lost the title to The Acolytes a little less than a month later at Fully Loaded.", "question": "When did the hardy boyz debut as a popular tag team in wwe?", "short_answers": ["September 27, 1998"], "wikipage": "The Hardy Boyz"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Hardy Boyz", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Hardy%20Boyz"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The Hardy Boyz are an American professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Matt and Jeff Hardy.[8][9] They first began teaming together in 1993 on the independent circuit, winning the NWA 2000 Tag Team Championship in NWA and founding OMEGA Championship Wrestling, where they held the OMEGA Tag Team Championship.", "wikipage": "The Hardy Boyz"}], "long_answer": "The Hardy Boyz are an American professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Matt and Jeff Hardy. They first began teaming together in 1993 on the independent circuit, winning the NWA 2000 Tag Team Championship in NWA and founding OMEGA Championship Wrestling, where they held the OMEGA Tag Team Championship. After being signed to a contract in 1998, they were trained by Dory Funk, Jr. in his Funkin' Dojo. The Hardy Boyz started their run as a popular tag team with a win over Kaientai (Men's Teioh and Shoichi Funaki) on the September 27, 1998 episode of Sunday Night Heat."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Hardy Boyz are an American professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Matt and Jeff Hardy.", "wikipage": "The Hardy Boyz"}, {"content": "They signed contracts with the WWF (now WWE) in 1997.", "wikipage": "The Hardy Boyz"}], "long_answer": "The Hardy Boyz, real-life brothers Matt and Jeff Hardy, are an American professional wrestling tag team. They signed contracts with the WWF, which is now the WWE, in 1998. They became a popular tag team in WWE on the September 27, 1998 episode of Sunday Night Heat."}], "sample_id": "8147972648703985410", "question": "When did the hardy boyz debut in wwe?", "docs": [{"id": "4279461", "title": "The Hardy Boyz", "text": "face Montel Vontavious Porter (MVP) and Mr. Kennedy, a match that was originally scheduled for the November 16 episode of \"SmackDown!\". The Hardys were unsuccessful in their return as Porter pinned Jeff for the win. The Hardys, announced and referred to once again as the Hardy Boyz, reunited once again in 2008, on the June 23 episode of \"Raw\" in a losing effort to John Morrison and The Miz. The match occurred after Jeff was drafted to the SmackDown brand (the same brand as Matt). This loss, however, caused Matt to be drafted to the ECW brand. On the July", "score": 0.7529296875, "summary": "The Hardy Boyz reunited on June 23, 2008, in a match against John Morrison and The Miz on the Raw episode. No information is given on when they debuted in WWE.", "extraction": "The passage does not contain an answer to the question \"When did the Hardy Boyz debut in WWE?\""}], "answer": "The Hardy Boyz, real-life brothers Matt and Jeff Hardy, are an American professional wrestling tag team. They signed contracts with the WWF, which is now the WWE, in 1998. They became a popular tag team in WWE on the September 27, 1998 episode of Sunday Night Heat.", "output": "The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on the June 23, 2008 episode of Raw [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on the June 23, 2008 episode of Raw.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 857, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz, also known as The Hardy Brothers, are a professional wrestling tag team in WWE, where they are currently signed to the SmackDown brand.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]They made their debut in WWE on the June 29, 1998 episode of Raw is War.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz, also known as The Hardy Brothers, are a professional wrestling tag team in WWE, where they are currently signed to the SmackDown brand.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]They made their debut in WWE on the June 29, 1998 episode of Raw is War.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3308503624084205, "ctx": {"title": "The Hardy Boyz", "text": "also appear on \"The Hardy Show\", an internet web show which features the Hardys, Shannon Moore, and many of their friends. The Hardy Boyz The Hardy Boyz, are a professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Jeff and Matt Hardy who are currently signed to WWE. They first began teaming together in 1993 in independent promotions in North Carolina, winning the NWA 2000 Tag Team Championship in NWA 2000 and founded the Organization of Modern Extreme Grappling Arts, where they held the OMEGA Tag Team Championship. They both signed contracts with the World Wrestling Entertainment (WWE) in 1998. In"}, "final_score": 1.3308503624084205, "relevance_score": 0.9940889308509737, "ground_score": 0.3367614315574467, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.35899410573044555, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0059107311755648845, "[Relevant]": 0.9940320924570244}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.18803705530804446, "[Partially supported]": 0.2822770530058316, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.507160005300015}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0009257426267496487, "[Utility:2]": 0.0010327405285501176, "[Utility:3]": 0.00021817189871935634, "[Utility:4]": 0.014368358695797908, "[Utility:5]": 0.938906445365245}], "n_tokens": 67}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz, are a professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Jeff and Matt Hardy, who are currently signed to WWE.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]They first began teaming together in 1993 in independent promotions in North Carolina, winning the NWA 2000 Tag Team Championship in NWA 2000 and founded the Organization of Modern Extreme Grappling Arts, where they held the OMEGA Tag Team Championship.[Continue to Use Evidence]They both signed contracts with the World Wrestling Entertainment (WWE) in 1998.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz, are a professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Jeff and Matt Hardy, who are currently signed to WWE.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]They first began teaming together in 1993 in independent promotions in North Carolina, winning the NWA 2000 Tag Team Championship in NWA 2000 and founded the Organization of Modern Extreme Grappling Arts, where they held the OMEGA Tag Team Championship.[Continue to Use Evidence]They both signed contracts with the World Wrestling Entertainment (WWE) in 1998.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6054456877096865, "ctx": {"title": "The Hardy Boyz", "text": "The Hardy Boyz The Hardy Boyz, are a professional wrestling tag team consisting of real-life brothers Jeff and Matt Hardy who are currently signed to WWE. They first began teaming together in 1993 in independent promotions in North Carolina, winning the NWA 2000 Tag Team Championship in NWA 2000 and founded the Organization of Modern Extreme Grappling Arts, where they held the OMEGA Tag Team Championship. They both signed contracts with the World Wrestling Entertainment (WWE) in 1998. In 2000, they were joined by Lita, and the trio became Team Xtreme. After splitting as a team in 2002, the brothers"}, "final_score": 1.6054456877096865, "relevance_score": 0.9956684981742389, "ground_score": 0.6097771895354477, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.06864567754500842, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0043313565108042, "[Relevant]": 0.995635095088928}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.513351655231523, "[Partially supported]": 0.16153326523053338, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.29943536905125334}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004150639705340872, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007060475978111986, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015003288887313526, "[Utility:4]": 0.003922664433114835, "[Utility:5]": 0.659690665046366}], "n_tokens": 126}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on October 11, 1993, at the WWE's first-ever pay-per-view, In Your House: Badd Blood.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on October 11, 1993, at the WWE's first-ever pay-per-view, In Your House: Badd Blood.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5483957379806177, "ctx": {"title": "Jeff Hardy", "text": "their autobiography \"The Hardy Boyz: Exist 2 Inspire\". As part of WWE, Hardy has appeared in several of their DVDs, including \"The Hardy Boyz: Leap of Faith\" (2001) and \"The Ladder Match\" (2007). He is also featured in the Total Nonstop Action Wrestling release \"Enigma: The Best of Jeff Hardy\" (2005) and \"Pro Wrestling's Ultimate Insiders: Hardy Boys \u2013 From the Backyard to the Big Time\" (2005). On April 29, 2008, WWE released \"\"Twist of Fate: The Matt and Jeff Hardy Story\"\". The DVD features footage of the brothers in OMEGA and WWE, and also briefly mentions Hardy's first run"}, "final_score": 1.5483957379806177, "relevance_score": 0.974434784823889, "ground_score": 0.5739609531567288, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.48528648941557584, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.025563469249901712, "[Relevant]": 0.9743682377121835}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.4975970785431884, "[Partially supported]": 0.14940572780841413, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3501032499050157}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016762777082693085, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004116538780898497, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003928028745454022, "[Utility:4]": 0.002571421517288788, "[Utility:5]": 0.7185760492419883}], "n_tokens": 49}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on the June 23, 2008 episode of Raw.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on the June 23, 2008 episode of Raw.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9381993194596765, "ctx": {"title": "The Hardy Boyz", "text": "face Montel Vontavious Porter (MVP) and Mr. Kennedy, a match that was originally scheduled for the November 16 episode of \"SmackDown!\". The Hardys were unsuccessful in their return as Porter pinned Jeff for the win. The Hardys, announced and referred to once again as the Hardy Boyz, reunited once again in 2008, on the June 23 episode of \"Raw\" in a losing effort to John Morrison and The Miz. The match occurred after Jeff was drafted to the SmackDown brand (the same brand as Matt). This loss, however, caused Matt to be drafted to the ECW brand. On the July"}, "final_score": 1.9381993194596765, "relevance_score": 0.9875683473516846, "ground_score": 0.9506309721079919, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.25555745679139363, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.012430752636950575, "[Relevant]": 0.9874968506037296}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9266731830863713, "[Partially supported]": 0.04649819743285018, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.026083124829342885}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00039129069873486053, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005004684244583995, "[Utility:3]": 0.00038222645946139465, "[Utility:4]": 0.0028242927514558875, "[Utility:5]": 0.830353493802642}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on October 17, 1999 at No Mercy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Hardy Boyz made their WWE debut on October 17, 1999 at No Mercy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.861309455100256, "ctx": {"title": "Jeff Hardy", "text": "WWF Tag Team Championship by defeating the Acolytes, but lost it back to them a month later. After the dissolution of the Brood, the Hardys joined forces with Gangrel as The New Brood and feuded with Edge and Christian. This stable did not last long, however, and on October 17, 1999 at No Mercy, the Hardy Boyz won the managerial services of Terri Runnels in the finals of the Terri Invitational Tournament in the WWF's first ever tag team ladder match against Edge and Christian. They competed in the first ever tag team triple threat ladder match against The Dudley"}, "final_score": 1.861309455100256, "relevance_score": 0.9594592653040042, "ground_score": 0.9018501897962519, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.24154174685129323, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.04053697910884547, "[Relevant]": 0.9593703835184335}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8663769309392801, "[Partially supported]": 0.06628791076681494, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.06475235296778917}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004816525549240916, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005697466359711899, "[Utility:3]": 0.00038400689713434657, "[Utility:4]": 0.0037590085335020623, "[Utility:5]": 0.830969079783734}], "n_tokens": 30}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 4], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The Battle of Kings Mountain was a military engagement between Patriot and Loyalist militias in South Carolina during the Southern Campaign of the American Revolutionary War, resulting in a decisive victory for the Patriots. The battle took place on October 7, 1780, south of the present-day town of Kings Mountain, North Carolina in what is now rural Cherokee County, South Carolina, where the Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot. The battle has been described as \"the war\u2019s largest all-American fight\".", "question": "For which militia was the battle of king's mountain a victory?", "short_answers": ["Patriot militia", "Patriot"], "wikipage": "Battle of Kings Mountain"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "For which commanders and leaders was the battle of king's mountain a victory?", "short_answers": ["James Johnston", "Joseph Winston", "Benjamin Cleveland", "Frederick Hambright", "Joseph McDowell", "Isaac Shelby", "John Sevier", "James Williams", "William Campbell", "William Chronicle"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Battle of Kings Mountain", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle%20of%20Kings%20Mountain"}, {"title": "Benjamin Cleveland", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin%20Cleveland"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Ferguson challenged Patriot militias to lay down their arms or suffer the consequences. In response, the Patriot militias led by Benjamin Cleveland, James Johnston, William Campbell, John Sevier, Joseph McDowell and Isaac Shelby rallied to attack Ferguson and his forces.", "wikipage": "Battle of Kings Mountain"}], "long_answer": "The Battle of Kings Mountain was a military engagement between Patriot and Loyalist militias in South Carolina during the Southern Campaign of the American Revolutionary War, resulting in a decisive victory for the Patriots. The battle took place on October 7, 1780, south of the present-day town of Kings Mountain, North Carolina in what is now rural Cherokee County, South Carolina, where the Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot. Ferguson challenged Patriot militias to lay down their arms or suffer the consequences. In response, the Patriot militias led by Benjamin Cleveland, James Johnston, Joseph Winston, Fredrick Hambright, William Campbell, John Sevier, Joseph McDowell, James Williams, William Chronicle, and Isaac Shelby rallied to attack Ferguson and his forces."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "In what is now rural Cherokee County, South Carolina, the Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot.", "wikipage": "Battle of Kings Mountain"}, {"content": "In response, the Patriot militias led by Benjamin Cleveland, James Johnston, William Campbell, John Sevier, Joseph McDowell and Isaac Shelby rallied to attack Ferguson and his forces.", "wikipage": "Battle of Kings Mountain"}], "long_answer": "The Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot during the Battle of King's Mountain. The Patriot militia was commanded by Benjamin Cleveland, James Johnston, William Campbell, John Sevier, Joseph McDowell and Isaac Shelby."}], "sample_id": "-62521546794442829", "question": "For whom was the battle of king's mountain a victory?", "docs": [{"id": "5599636", "title": "South Carolina in the American Revolution", "text": "the state. On October 8, 1780, at Kings Mountain, American Colonel Isaac Shelby led a body of North and South Carolinians and attacked British Major Patrick Ferguson and his body of American loyalists on a hilltop. America's first major poet, William Cullen Bryant, described the homefield advantage that led to the Patriot victory in one of his poems. This was a major victory for the Patriots, especially because it was won by militiamen and not trained Continentals. It provided a great swing of momentum for the moderate \"Overmountain Men\" who had grown tired of British brutality. Kings Mountain is considered", "score": 0.81787109375, "summary": "The battle of King's Mountain was a victory for the Patriots, led by American Colonel Isaac Shelby, against British Major Patrick Ferguson and his loyalists.", "extraction": "The battle of King's Mountain was a victory for the Patriots."}], "answer": "The Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot during the Battle of King's Mountain. The Patriot militia was commanded by Benjamin Cleveland, James Johnston, William Campbell, John Sevier, Joseph McDowell and Isaac Shelby.", "output": "The Battle of Kings Mountain was a victory for the Patriots, especially because it was won by militiamen and not trained Continentals [1]. It provided a great swing of momentum for the moderate \"Overmountain Men\" who had grown tired of British brutality [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a victory for the Patriots, especially because it was won by militiamen and not trained Continentals.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It provided a great swing of momentum for the moderate \"Overmountain Men\" who had grown tired of British brutality.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 858, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a victory for the Patriots, especially because it was won by militiamen and not trained Continentals.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It provided a great swing of momentum for the moderate \"Overmountain Men\" who had grown tired of British brutality.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a victory for the Patriots, especially because it was won by militiamen and not trained Continentals.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It provided a great swing of momentum for the moderate \"Overmountain Men\" who had grown tired of British brutality.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9924665481955346, "ctx": {"title": "South Carolina in the American Revolution", "text": "the state. On October 8, 1780, at Kings Mountain, American Colonel Isaac Shelby led a body of North and South Carolinians and attacked British Major Patrick Ferguson and his body of American loyalists on a hilltop. America's first major poet, William Cullen Bryant, described the homefield advantage that led to the Patriot victory in one of his poems. This was a major victory for the Patriots, especially because it was won by militiamen and not trained Continentals. It provided a great swing of momentum for the moderate \"Overmountain Men\" who had grown tired of British brutality. Kings Mountain is considered"}, "final_score": 1.9924665481955346, "relevance_score": 0.9995592369927564, "ground_score": 0.9929073112027782, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12809795515214925, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0004407565483596378, "[Relevant]": 0.9995445895813119}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9862006579330933, "[Partially supported]": 0.012414433248258226, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0008819045201397142}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008901570043485364, "[Utility:2]": 0.0007703666778886311, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004915913866812536, "[Utility:4]": 0.013443373762188567, "[Utility:5]": 0.599059633799648}], "n_tokens": 60}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a military engagement between Patriot and Loyalist militias in South Carolina during the Southern Campaign of the American Revolutionary War, resulting in a decisive victory for the Patriots.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The battle took place on October 7, 1780, south of the present-day town of Kings Mountain, North Carolina in what is now rural Cherokee County, South Carolina, where the Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot.[Continue to Use Evidence]The battle has been described as \"the war's largest all-American fight\".[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a military engagement between Patriot and Loyalist militias in South Carolina during the Southern Campaign of the American Revolutionary War, resulting in a decisive victory for the Patriots.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The battle took place on October 7, 1780, south of the present-day town of Kings Mountain, North Carolina in what is now rural Cherokee County, South Carolina, where the Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot.[Continue to Use Evidence]The battle has been described as \"the war's largest all-American fight\".[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.988663444161644, "ctx": {"title": "Battle of Kings Mountain", "text": "Battle of Kings Mountain The Battle of Kings Mountain was a military engagement between Patriot and Loyalist militias in South Carolina during the Southern Campaign of the American Revolutionary War, resulting in a decisive victory for the Patriots. The battle took place on October 7, 1780, south of the present-day town of Kings Mountain, North Carolina in what is now rural Cherokee County, South Carolina, where the Patriot militia defeated the Loyalist militia commanded by British Major Patrick Ferguson of the 71st Foot. The battle has been described as \"the war\u2019s largest all-American fight\". Ferguson had arrived in North Carolina"}, "final_score": 1.988663444161644, "relevance_score": 0.9989596816030469, "ground_score": 0.989703762558597, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.053584790356770266, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0010402822123313103, "[Relevant]": 0.9989249355308146}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9823260860553338, "[Partially supported]": 0.013266419285329564, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003655282847315409}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003307345432374705, "[Utility:2]": 0.0016503429953746462, "[Utility:3]": 0.00035552023635858316, "[Utility:4]": 0.005896863753013826, "[Utility:5]": 0.56505352630374}], "n_tokens": 139}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a decisive victory for Patriot forces in the American Revolutionary War.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The battle was led by Patriot militia commander, General Isaac R. Harris, against a Loyalist force led by British commander, Major General Patrick Ferguson.[Continue to Use Evidence]The victory of the Patriot militia at Kings Mountain was a major boost to Patriot morale and a significant setback for the British.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a decisive victory for Patriot forces in the American Revolutionary War.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The battle was led by Patriot militia commander, General Isaac R. Harris, against a Loyalist force led by British commander, Major General Patrick Ferguson.[Continue to Use Evidence]The victory of the Patriot militia at Kings Mountain was a major boost to Patriot morale and a significant setback for the British.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9520891853371678, "ctx": {"title": "Battle of Kings Mountain", "text": "in single file through woodlands. The column finally made camp at Salem, North Carolina. Kings Mountain was a pivotal moment in the history of the American Revolution. Coming after a series of disasters and humiliations in the Carolinas\u2014the fall of Charleston and capture of the American army there, the destruction of another American army at the Battle of Camden, the Waxhaws Massacre\u2014the surprising decisive victory at Kings Mountain was a great boost to Patriot morale. The Tories of the Carolina back country were broken as a military force. Additionally, the destruction of Ferguson's command and the looming threat of Patriot"}, "final_score": 1.9520891853371678, "relevance_score": 0.9979809865156779, "ground_score": 0.9541081988214899, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.49693857092225974, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0020189440636983027, "[Relevant]": 0.997946672498847}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9247350209570226, "[Partially supported]": 0.0546736707812041, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.018457019429143486}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00014958128608524951, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004066042857270535, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001249798425504976, "[Utility:4]": 0.004270144479248012, "[Utility:5]": 0.8696177900598732}], "n_tokens": 92}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a victory for the Patriot militia, who were led by General Daniel Morgan.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Patriot militia were American Revolutionaries who fought in the American Revolutionary War.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of Kings Mountain was a victory for the Patriot militia, who were led by General Daniel Morgan.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Patriot militia were American Revolutionaries who fought in the American Revolutionary War.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9739871671053142, "ctx": {"title": "Southern theater of the American Revolutionary War", "text": "large numbers in North Carolina were effectively crushed when Patriot militia defeated a larger force of Loyalists in the Battle of Kings Mountain on October 7, 1780. Many of the Patriot men had crossed the Appalachian Mountains from the Washington District of North Carolina to fight the British, and were so named the Overmountain Men. The British plan to raise large Loyalist armies failed\u2014not enough Loyalists enlisted, and those who did were at high risk once the British army moved on. The defeat at Kings Mountain and the continuing harassment of his communications and supply lines by militia forces in"}, "final_score": 1.9739871671053142, "relevance_score": 0.9994385212188441, "ground_score": 0.9745486458864699, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5365998514292226, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0005614610323319111, "[Relevant]": 0.9994069281132798}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9552404159358256, "[Partially supported]": 0.035899125567625825, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.007466308319883292}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010602067691813642, "[Utility:2]": 0.0014951311883040334, "[Utility:3]": 0.00032843657887873725, "[Utility:4]": 0.01246977485212624, "[Utility:5]": 0.5578492561150717}], "n_tokens": 46}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of King's Mountain was a victory for the Patriots.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The battle was a clear victory for the Patriots, and was one of the most shocking and decisive British defeats in the war.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Battle of King's Mountain was a victory for the Patriots.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The battle was a clear victory for the Patriots, and was one of the most shocking and decisive British defeats in the war.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9624943881374137, "ctx": {"title": "Johan Martin Shultz", "text": "unit engaged the British Army at the Battle of King's Mountain. The battle lasted a little more than an hour, and was a clear victory for the Patriots. King's Mountain served as one of the most shocking and decisive British defeats in the war. In 1785, Governor John Sevier signed the Treaty of Dumplin Creek, which \"Ceded the territory south of the French Broad and Holston Rivers and west of the Big Pigeon River and east of the ridge dividing Little River from the Tennessee River to the State of Franklin.\" Upon signing this treaty, Sevier began conscripting trusted men"}, "final_score": 1.9624943881374137, "relevance_score": 0.9986645994884286, "ground_score": 0.9638297886489853, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2789938968739989, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001335348866077573, "[Relevant]": 0.9986259769733012}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9484061717009352, "[Partially supported]": 0.022655620395757803, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.024688695387292774}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005374370499444073, "[Utility:2]": 0.001183077220221592, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004025217516482954, "[Utility:4]": 0.00884486980403336, "[Utility:5]": 0.7686849356335368}], "n_tokens": 49}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults. Aquatic animals may breathe air or extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialised organs called gills, or directly through the skin. Natural environments and the animals that live in them can be categorized as aquatic (water) or terrestrial (land). This designation is paraphyletic.", "question": "What do you call animals live in water for most of its lifetime?", "short_answers": ["aquatic"], "wikipage": "Aquatic animal"}, {"context": "Marine life, or sea life or ocean life, is the plants, animals and other organisms that live in the salt water of the sea or ocean, or the brackish water of coastal estuaries. At a fundamental level, marine life affects the nature of the planet. Marine organisms produce oxygen and sequester carbon. Shorelines are in part shaped and protected by marine life, and some marine organisms even help create new land. The term \"marine\" comes from the Latin \"mare\", meaning sea or ocean.", "question": "What do you call animals live in salt water?", "short_answers": ["marine life"], "wikipage": "Marine life"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Marine life", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine%20life"}, {"title": "Aquatic animal", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquatic%20animal"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The term aquatic can be applied to animals that live in either fresh water or salt water. However, the adjective marine is most commonly used for animals that live in saltwater, i.e. in oceans, seas, etc.", "wikipage": "Aquatic animal Description"}], "long_answer": "Marine life, or sea life or ocean life, is the plants, animals and other organisms that live in the salt water of the sea or ocean, or the brackish water of coastal estuaries. An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. The term aquatic can be applied to animals that live in either fresh water or salt water. However, the adjective marine is most commonly used for animals that live in saltwater, i.e. in oceans, seas, etc."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. Aquatic animals may breathe air or extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialised organs called gills, or directly through the skin. Marine life, or sea life or ocean life, is the plants, animals and other organisms that live in the salt water of the sea or ocean, or the brackish water of coastal estuaries."}], "sample_id": "8140946730436258527", "question": "What do you call animals live in water?", "docs": [{"id": "2105269", "title": "Aquatic animal", "text": "Aquatic animal An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults. Aquatic animals may breathe air or extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialised organs called gills, or directly through the skin. Natural environments and the animals that live in them can be categorized as aquatic (water) or terrestrial (land). The term aquatic can be applied to animals that live in either fresh water (fresh water animals) or salt water (marine", "score": 0.76611328125, "summary": "An aquatic animal is an animal that lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. It can include both vertebrate and invertebrate animals, with examples such as insects, fish, and mammals.", "extraction": "\"An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime.\""}], "answer": "An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. Aquatic animals may breathe air or extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialised organs called gills, or directly through the skin. Marine life, or sea life or ocean life, is the plants, animals and other organisms that live in the salt water of the sea or ocean, or the brackish water of coastal estuaries.", "output": "Aquatic animals are animals that live in water for most or all of their lifetime [1]. Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies, and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Aquatic animals are animals that live in water for most or all of their lifetime.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies, and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 860, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Some animals that live in water are fish, amphibians, reptiles, and aquatic plants.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Some animals that live in water are fish, amphibians, reptiles, and aquatic plants.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9203840218641872, "ctx": {"title": "Water", "text": "pH values less than 7 while bases have values greater than 7. Earth surface waters are filled with life. The earliest life forms appeared in water; nearly all fish live exclusively in water, and there are many types of marine mammals, such as dolphins and whales. Some kinds of animals, such as amphibians, spend portions of their lives in water and portions on land. Plants such as kelp and algae grow in the water and are the basis for some underwater ecosystems. Plankton is generally the foundation of the ocean food chain. Aquatic vertebrates must obtain oxygen to survive, and"}, "final_score": 1.9203840218641872, "relevance_score": 0.9968519295980783, "ground_score": 0.9235320922661088, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3232170746232441, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003147991590971499, "[Relevant]": 0.9968269737242377}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8533307317117287, "[Partially supported]": 0.1382183526157123, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.007268299220277339}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016128432344637074, "[Utility:2]": 0.00022217853971636775, "[Utility:3]": 0.0009538558330255122, "[Utility:4]": 0.007591096236196348, "[Utility:5]": 0.8114727841724338}], "n_tokens": 27}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Aquatic animals are animals that live in water for most or all of their lifetime.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies, and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Aquatic animals are animals that live in water for most or all of their lifetime.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies, and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9892798430022047, "ctx": {"title": "Aquatic animal", "text": "Aquatic animal An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults. Aquatic animals may breathe air or extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialised organs called gills, or directly through the skin. Natural environments and the animals that live in them can be categorized as aquatic (water) or terrestrial (land). The term aquatic can be applied to animals that live in either fresh water (fresh water animals) or salt water (marine"}, "final_score": 1.9892798430022047, "relevance_score": 0.9993833702513923, "ground_score": 0.9898964727508124, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.13841963950821234, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0006166049087155475, "[Relevant]": 0.9993431117734396}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9825682909811707, "[Partially supported]": 0.013269690189996766, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.00346160550912772}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 6.604630340097588e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00024876979175679297, "[Utility:3]": 0.0007573380086612173, "[Utility:4]": 0.0029604332787505775, "[Utility:5]": 0.4978682365456508}], "n_tokens": 60}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Aquatic animals are animals that live in water for most or all of their lifetime.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Some aquatic animals are able to breathe air, while others extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialized organs called gills, or directly through the skin.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Aquatic animals are animals that live in water for most or all of their lifetime.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Some aquatic animals are able to breathe air, while others extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialized organs called gills, or directly through the skin.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.989085683358597, "ctx": {"title": "Aquatic animal", "text": "gills, while some fresh water ones have a lung instead (e.g. \"Planorbidae\") and some amphibious ones have both (e.g. \"Ampullariidae\"). Aquatic animal An aquatic animal is an animal, either vertebrate or invertebrate, which lives in the water for most or all of its lifetime. Many insects such as mosquitoes, mayflies, dragonflies and caddisflies have aquatic larvae, with winged adults. Aquatic animals may breathe air or extract oxygen that dissolved in water through specialised organs called gills, or directly through the skin. Natural environments and the animals that live in them can be categorized as aquatic (water) or terrestrial (land). The"}, "final_score": 1.989085683358597, "relevance_score": 0.9988304900027081, "ground_score": 0.990255193355889, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.25339351713338754, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.001169489116381676, "[Relevant]": 0.9988126564743142}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9831838291607999, "[Partially supported]": 0.013072144452879726, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.003203466768718116}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 6.837823341471512e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00018298964179990428, "[Utility:3]": 0.0013734135319936052, "[Utility:4]": 0.0030115518782604855, "[Utility:5]": 0.7692561903739675}], "n_tokens": 60}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Animals that live in water are called aquatic animals.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The term aquatic animal is applied to air-breathing aquatic or sea mammals such as whales, as well as the pinnipeds, which cannot survive on land.[Continue to Use Evidence]Molluscs, crustaceans, and fish are also aquatic animals.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Animals that live in water are called aquatic animals.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The term aquatic animal is applied to air-breathing aquatic or sea mammals such as whales, as well as the pinnipeds, which cannot survive on land.[Continue to Use Evidence]Molluscs, crustaceans, and fish are also aquatic animals.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9684253431944763, "ctx": {"title": "Aquatic animal", "text": "animals). However, the adjective marine is most commonly used for animals that live in saltwater, i.e. in oceans, seas, etc. Aquatic animals (especially freshwater animals) are often of special concern to conservationists because of the fragility of their environments. Aquatic animals are subject to pressure from overfishing, destructive fishing, marine pollution and climate change. In addition to water breathing animals, e.g., fish, most mollusks etc., the term \"aquatic animal\" can be applied to air-breathing aquatic or sea mammals such as those in the orders Cetacea (whales) and Sirenia (sea cows), which cannot survive on land, as well as the pinnipeds"}, "final_score": 1.9684253431944763, "relevance_score": 0.9991306325403537, "ground_score": 0.9692947106541225, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3002590886657988, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0008693462464319908, "[Relevant]": 0.9991062530078143}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9447313651775114, "[Partially supported]": 0.04384181551592511, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.008700674523553864}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 4.881531841003401e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00012058332894454633, "[Utility:3]": 0.00036211086268336297, "[Utility:4]": 0.0040323582820836566, "[Utility:5]": 0.7477042761475653}], "n_tokens": 77}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Amphibians, like frogs, are separated into their own environmental classification.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The majority of amphibians have an aquatic larval stage, like a tadpole, but then live as terrestrial adults, and may return to the water to mate.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Amphibians, like frogs, are separated into their own environmental classification.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The majority of amphibians have an aquatic larval stage, like a tadpole, but then live as terrestrial adults, and may return to the water to mate.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9594800220671864, "ctx": {"title": "Aquatic animal", "text": "(true seals, eared seals, and the walrus). The term \"aquatic mammal\" is also applied to four-footed mammals like the river otter (\"Lontra canadensis\") and beavers (family Castoridae), although these are technically amphibious or semiaquatic. Amphibians, like frogs (the order Anura), while requiring water, are separated into their own environmental classification. The majority of amphibians (class Amphibia) have an aquatic larval stage, like a tadpole, but then live as terrestrial adults, and may return to the water to mate. Certain fish also evolved to breathe air to survive oxygen-deprived water, such as \"Arapaima\" (family Osteoglossidae) and walking catfish. Most mollusks have"}, "final_score": 1.9594800220671864, "relevance_score": 0.9949396669716312, "ground_score": 0.9645403550955554, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.16812796980216485, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0050601902179026785, "[Relevant]": 0.9949115882272384}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9369280235781066, "[Partially supported]": 0.052446518155503975, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.009185318999682175}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00010214614382364532, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005008207839465528, "[Utility:3]": 0.0016103664977316077, "[Utility:4]": 0.008146232426674747, "[Utility:5]": 0.5020247147441698}], "n_tokens": 61}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many times is the f word used in the movie Scarface in total?", "short_answers": ["207"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many times is the f word used in the movie Scarface per minute?", "short_answers": ["1.21"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many times is the f word used total in scarface?", "short_answers": ["207"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many times is the f word used per minute in scarface?", "short_answers": ["1.21"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of films that most frequently use the word \"fuck\"", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20films%20that%20most%20frequently%20use%20the%20word%20%22fuck%22"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In the movie Scarface, the F word is used a total of 207 times and is used 1.21 times per minute."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The use of profanity in films has always been controversial, but has increased significantly in recent years.[1][2]", "wikipage": "List of films that most frequently use the word \"fuck\""}, {"content": "Scarface was among the earliest films in which the expletive \"fuck\" is used persistently, 226 times in total.[86]", "wikipage": "Scarface (1983 film) Film industry"}, {"content": "Initial critical reception was negative due to its excessive violence, profanity, and graphic drug usage.", "wikipage": "Scarface (1983 film)"}, {"content": "In the years that followed, critics have reappraised it, and it is now considered one of the greatest gangster films ever made,[9] as well as one of the greatest remakes ever made.", "wikipage": "Scarface (1983 film)"}], "long_answer": "The use of profanity in films has always been controversial, but has increased significantly in recent years. Scarface was among the earliest films in which the f word is used persistently, 207 times in total, or 1.21 times per minute. Initial critical reception was negative due to its excessive violence, profanity, and graphic drug usage. In the years that followed, critics have reappraised it, and it is now considered one of the greatest gangster films ever made, as well as one of the greatest remakes ever made."}], "sample_id": "9137853598452853384", "question": "How many times is the f word used in scarface?", "docs": [{"id": "4763194", "title": "Eddie Murphy Raw", "text": "a large-scale brawl after which \"everybody sued me\" for millions of dollars. After the fight, Murphy calls his parents, leading to a long impression of his drunken stepfather (another call-back to a popular bit from \"Delirious\"). This final segment runs for over 10 minutes and incorporates his stepfather's habit of misquoting Motown songs (including \"\"Ain't Too Proud to Beg\"\", which opened the film). The film contained the word \"fuck\" 223 times, setting the record for highest \"fuck count\" ever in a feature-length, theatrically released film at the time (surpassing \"Scarface\"). \"Raw\" held the record until 1990 before being surpassed by", "score": 0.66650390625, "summary": "The document is irrelevant to the question.", "extraction": "The passage is irrelevant to the question as it talks about the number of times the word \"fuck\" was used in the movie \"Raw\" and not in \"Scarface\"."}], "answer": "The use of profanity in films has always been controversial, but has increased significantly in recent years. Scarface was among the earliest films in which the f word is used persistently, 207 times in total, or 1.21 times per minute. Initial critical reception was negative due to its excessive violence, profanity, and graphic drug usage. In the years that followed, critics have reappraised it, and it is now considered one of the greatest gangster films ever made, as well as one of the greatest remakes ever made.", "output": "The 1983 film Scarface uses the word \"fuck\" 223 times, setting the record for highest \"fuck count\" ever in a feature-length, theatrically released film at the time [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface uses the word \"fuck\" 223 times, setting the record for highest \"fuck count\" ever in a feature-length, theatrically released film at the time.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 861, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The word \"fuck\" is used 857 times during the film.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The word \"fuck\" is used 857 times during the film.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6274360772177285, "ctx": {"title": "Fuck (film)", "text": "and Ice T; political commentators Alan Keyes and Pat Boone; and journalists Michael Medved and Judith Martin. The word \"fuck\" is used 857 times during the film. Scholarly analysis is provided by \"Maledicta\" publisher Reinhold Aman, journalism analyst David Shaw and \"Oxford English Dictionary\" editor Jesse Sheidlower. Language professor Geoffrey Nunberg says, \"You could think of that [word] as standing in for most of the changes that happened in the 20th century, at least many of the important ones\". The film next features author Hunter S. Thompson in his final documented interview. \"Fuck\" later includes archival footage of comedians Lenny"}, "final_score": 1.6274360772177285, "relevance_score": 0.9130583349918362, "ground_score": 0.7143777422258925, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.18346214494557075, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.08692876470745119, "[Relevant]": 0.9129228564834766}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6903017764824803, "[Partially supported]": 0.04661005681820849, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.26200899347100515}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0021281351216685474, "[Utility:2]": 0.00043067127473677086, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004620428314128504, "[Utility:4]": 0.0035087016420189392, "[Utility:5]": 0.9246958807588251}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface, directed by Brian De Palma, contains 1,694 uses of the word \"fuck\" or \"fucking\".[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface, directed by Brian De Palma, contains 1,694 uses of the word \"fuck\" or \"fucking\".[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.5533717553646539, "ctx": {"title": "Scarface (1983 film)", "text": "a haven for drugs and gangsters. Tony's opulent Miami mansion was portrayed by El Fureidis, a Roman-styled mansion in Santa Barbara, California. The picture was shot for 24 weeks from November 22, 1982 to May 6, 1983. The special effects were performed by Ken Pepiot and Stan Parks. \"Scarface\" was given an X rating in North America three times for extreme violence, frequent strong language, and hard drug usage. The restrictive rating was more associated with pornography at the time, and it both limited the number of cinemas willing to screen such a film and restricted promotional advertising, which would"}, "final_score": 0.5533717553646539, "relevance_score": 0.5234203489363239, "ground_score": 0.02995140642832999, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4261057941760153, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.4764389246332486, "[Relevant]": 0.523265791188937}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.011061782721362877, "[Partially supported]": 0.037715029273620165, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9501511491932882}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008510295863022014, "[Utility:2]": 0.00030463173514232536, "[Utility:3]": 0.000268836562692073, "[Utility:4]": 0.002768703376547904, "[Utility:5]": 0.9442684489531153}], "n_tokens": 43}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Irrelevant]The word \"fuck\" is said 11 times in the 1983 film Scarface, and 16 times in the 2013 film Scarface.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]The word \"fuck\" is said 11 times in the 1983 film Scarface, and 16 times in the 2013 film Scarface.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.0946776941279129, "ctx": {"title": "Deadwood (TV series)", "text": "to the audience the lawlessness of the camp in much the same way that the original inhabitants used it to show that they were living outside the bounds of \"civil society.\" The issue of the authenticity of \"Deadwood\"'s dialogue has even been alluded to in the show itself. Early in the second season, E.B. Farnum has fleeced Mr. Wolcott of $9,900, and Farnum tries to console the geologist: The word \"fuck\" is said 43 times in the first hour of the show. It has been reported that the series had a total count of 2,980 \"fucks\" and an average of"}, "final_score": 0.0946776941279129, "relevance_score": 0.0946776941279129, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4016605491082869, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.904631586442285, "[Relevant]": 0.09460545938623158}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0022446418369457656, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003885395627875961, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010768306733871764, "[Utility:4]": 0.002370814270898879, "[Utility:5]": 0.9829687345533574}], "n_tokens": 44}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface uses the word \"fuck\" 223 times, setting the record for highest \"fuck count\" ever in a feature-length, theatrically released film at the time.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface uses the word \"fuck\" 223 times, setting the record for highest \"fuck count\" ever in a feature-length, theatrically released film at the time.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.821362401761496, "ctx": {"title": "Eddie Murphy Raw", "text": "a large-scale brawl after which \"everybody sued me\" for millions of dollars. After the fight, Murphy calls his parents, leading to a long impression of his drunken stepfather (another call-back to a popular bit from \"Delirious\"). This final segment runs for over 10 minutes and incorporates his stepfather's habit of misquoting Motown songs (including \"\"Ain't Too Proud to Beg\"\", which opened the film). The film contained the word \"fuck\" 223 times, setting the record for highest \"fuck count\" ever in a feature-length, theatrically released film at the time (surpassing \"Scarface\"). \"Raw\" held the record until 1990 before being surpassed by"}, "final_score": 1.821362401761496, "relevance_score": 0.8848377598803628, "ground_score": 0.9365246418811332, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1582810847898368, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.11515039878129002, "[Relevant]": 0.8847467781203148}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9154483029379916, "[Partially supported]": 0.04053745268529079, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04315189376213695}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0014448509009026055, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002631268966170494, "[Utility:3]": 0.00032238981290900147, "[Utility:4]": 0.002094049777120025, "[Utility:5]": 0.7936162621546993}], "n_tokens": 53}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface uses the word \"fuck\" 14 times.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 1983 film Scarface uses the word \"fuck\" 14 times.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9354318846911586, "ctx": {"title": "Scarface (1983 film)", "text": "set in 1.33:1 pan and scan ratio \u2013 and quickly became a bestseller, preluding its cult status. A 2.35:1 Widescreen VHS would follow years later in 1998 to coincide with the special edition DVD release. The last VHS release was in 2003 to counterpart the 20th anniversary edition DVD. The television version of \"Scarface\" premiered on ABC on January 7, 1989. 32 minutes of violence, profanity and sex were edited out, and much of the dialogue, including the constant use of the word \"fuck\", which was muted after the beginning of \"f-\" or replaced with less offensive alternatives. The film"}, "final_score": 0.9354318846911586, "relevance_score": 0.8449215244884338, "ground_score": 0.09051036020272477, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.37500112530618096, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.15505399885944132, "[Relevant]": 0.8447881671662163}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.07369618106709254, "[Partially supported]": 0.03321747127012799, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8908165135785036}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001040242529471097, "[Utility:2]": 0.00021466617914343532, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016015072713190394, "[Utility:4]": 0.0019434289004334469, "[Utility:5]": 0.9130563040527412}], "n_tokens": 26}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 4], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The Hotel and Casino opened in 1996. When the casino first opened, it did not perform up to expectations. In 1999 a major addition to the casino and other amenities were added. The success of these changes has been demonstrated by continued expansions in later years.", "question": "When was addition to the orleans hotel in las vegas built?", "short_answers": ["1999"], "wikipage": "The Orleans"}, {"context": "The Hotel and Casino opened in 1996. When the casino first opened, it did not perform up to expectations. In 1999 a major addition to the casino and other amenities were added. The success of these changes has been demonstrated by continued expansions in later years.", "question": "When was the orleans hotel in las vegas first built?", "short_answers": ["1996", "December 28, 1996"], "wikipage": "The Orleans"}, {"context": "The Orleans Arena was added in 2003. A second hotel tower was added in 2004.", "question": "When was the orleans hotel second tower in las vegas built?", "short_answers": ["2004"], "wikipage": "The Orleans"}, {"context": "The Hotel and Casino opened in 1996. When the casino first opened, it did not perform up to expectations. In 1999 a major addition to the casino and other amenities were added. The success of these changes has been demonstrated by continued expansions in later years.", "question": "When was the orleans hotel in las vegas first built?", "short_answers": ["1996"], "wikipage": "The Orleans"}, {"context": "The Hotel and Casino opened in 1996. When the casino first opened, it did not perform up to expectations. In 1999 a major addition to the casino and other amenities were added. The success of these changes has been demonstrated by continued expansions in later years.", "question": "When was the first major addition to the Orleans hotel in Las Vegas built?", "short_answers": ["1999"], "wikipage": "The Orleans"}, {"context": "The Orleans Arena was added in 2003. A second hotel tower was added in 2004.", "question": "When was the orleans hotel second tower in las vegas built?", "short_answers": ["2004"], "wikipage": "The Orleans"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Orleans Arena", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orleans%20Arena"}, {"title": "The Orleans", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Orleans"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Orleans Hotel in Las Vegas was first built in 1996, while the first major addition to the Orleans Hotel was built in 1999, and the Orleans Hotel's second tower was built in 2004."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Orleans Arena was added in 2003. A second hotel tower was added in 2004.", "wikipage": "The Orleans"}], "long_answer": "The Orleans hotel and casino was first built in Las Vegas in 1996. The first major addition to the hotel occurred in 1999 and additional expansions have been made since then. For example, the Orleans Arena was added in 2003, and a second tower was built in 2004."}], "sample_id": "415388705857547580", "question": "When was the orleans hotel in las vegas built?", "docs": [{"id": "4735926", "title": "The Orleans", "text": "Casino opened in 1996. When the casino first opened, it did not perform up to expectations. In 1999 a major addition to the casino and other amenities were added. The success of these changes has been demonstrated by continued expansions in later years. The Orleans Arena was added in 2003. A second hotel tower was added in 2004. The location has the distinction of having hosted the final concert performance from stand-up comedian George Carlin, in June 2008. Besides its casino and arena, The Orleans includes: Musicians: Comedians: Dancers/illusionists/other celebrities: The Orleans The Orleans is a hotel and casino located", "score": 0.7470703125, "summary": "The Orleans Hotel in Las Vegas was built in 1996.", "extraction": "The Orleans hotel in Las Vegas was built in two phases. The first phase of the hotel was opened in 1996, and the second hotel tower was added in 2004."}], "answer": "The Orleans hotel and casino was first built in Las Vegas in 1996. The first major addition to the hotel occurred in 1999 and additional expansions have been made since then. For example, the Orleans Arena was added in 2003, and a second tower was built in 2004.", "output": "The Orleans Hotel and Casino opened in 1996 [1]. A second hotel tower was added in 2004 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino opened in 1996.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]A second hotel tower was added in 2004.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 863, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino opened in 1996.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]A second hotel tower was added in 2004.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino opened in 1996.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]A second hotel tower was added in 2004.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9341622658075028, "ctx": {"title": "The Orleans", "text": "Casino opened in 1996. When the casino first opened, it did not perform up to expectations. In 1999 a major addition to the casino and other amenities were added. The success of these changes has been demonstrated by continued expansions in later years. The Orleans Arena was added in 2003. A second hotel tower was added in 2004. The location has the distinction of having hosted the final concert performance from stand-up comedian George Carlin, in June 2008. Besides its casino and arena, The Orleans includes: Musicians: Comedians: Dancers/illusionists/other celebrities: The Orleans The Orleans is a hotel and casino located"}, "final_score": 1.9341622658075028, "relevance_score": 0.9967775150926548, "ground_score": 0.9373847507148482, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2062905730558242, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0032223982013705552, "[Relevant]": 0.9967506952413757}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8963590041238522, "[Partially supported]": 0.07591322714600668, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.024453624345295803}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00015949867223377667, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003104588059851907, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012741203725680862, "[Utility:4]": 0.0020724540648474213, "[Utility:5]": 0.78850587526523}], "n_tokens": 32}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel & Casino is a hotel and casino located on the Las Vegas Strip in Paradise, Nevada.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The Orleans Hotel was built in 1975.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel & Casino is a hotel and casino located on the Las Vegas Strip in Paradise, Nevada.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The Orleans Hotel was built in 1975.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.1605019985605087, "ctx": {"title": "Orleans Hotel", "text": "in 1852 along with many other Sacramento buildings in a large fire. It was rebuilt by the next year, this time in brick, only to meet the same fate in another fire in 1923. Several attempts were made to build on the site of the former Orleans Hotel since 1975, but none succeeded until a business owner financed a new mixed-use building in 2007, with construction finished in 2008. Today the new Orleans Hotel replica is a four-story building with an exterior that matches the Gold Rush-era original. There are 24 rental lofts on the upper floors and was built"}, "final_score": 1.1605019985605087, "relevance_score": 0.9905142869254598, "ground_score": 0.16998771163504883, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.31949249716307226, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.009484672601762435, "[Relevant]": 0.9904056389889908}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.018659564235713334, "[Partially supported]": 0.2918846944586267, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.6577722736113298}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00022171372502621025, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002791797313517514, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018488729030266435, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018419389003180102, "[Utility:5]": 0.7008018614537536}], "n_tokens": 44}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The St. Louis Hotel was built in 1838 at the corner of St. Louis and Chartres Street in New Orleans, Louisiana, United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Orleans Hotel and Casino was built in 1977.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The St. Louis Hotel was built in 1838 at the corner of St. Louis and Chartres Street in New Orleans, Louisiana, United States.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The Orleans Hotel and Casino was built in 1977.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9175345703462474, "ctx": {"title": "St. Louis Hotel", "text": "20th-century hotel encompassed the same European grand design as the old one, including the \u201cexact drawings of the remaining stone arches ... and [the] exact duplicates of the Spanish wrought iron railings\u201d which had once graced the hotel. In 1986 Omni Hotels of Dallas, Texas, took over the hotel's lease and named it the Omni Royal Orleans. As of 2008 Omni maintained a 25% stake in the property and continued to manage it. St. Louis Hotel The St. Louis Hotel was built in 1838 at the corner of St. Louis and Chartres Street in New Orleans, Louisiana, United States. Originally"}, "final_score": 1.9175345703462474, "relevance_score": 0.979195508873092, "ground_score": 0.9383390614731554, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.19748410134731254, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.02080175136083346, "[Relevant]": 0.9790665575510369}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8911212284943196, "[Partially supported]": 0.08353726943569052, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0195342214347411}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018193462544057934, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002976251835947235, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001562259991960528, "[Utility:4]": 0.004710522657659383, "[Utility:5]": 0.9082479471849552}], "n_tokens": 53}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino is a hotel and casino located in Las Vegas, Nevada, United States, on the Las Vegas Strip.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The Orleans Hotel was built in 1979.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino is a hotel and casino located in Las Vegas, Nevada, United States, on the Las Vegas Strip.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]The Orleans Hotel was built in 1979.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0795442350574855, "ctx": {"title": "St. Louis Hotel", "text": "St. Louis Hotel The St. Louis Hotel was built in 1838 at the corner of St. Louis and Chartres Street in New Orleans, Louisiana, United States. Originally it was referred to as the City Exchange Hotel. A hotel exists in the same place today but with a different name, the Omni Royal Orleans. Creoles of New Orleans built the hotel to rival an Anglo-American-made hotel at the time near Canal Street on the upriver side, called the St. Charles Hotel. In February 1838, a contract for the French Quarter hotel's construction was signed; it was given the name \u201cThe City"}, "final_score": 1.0795442350574855, "relevance_score": 0.9791955111498027, "ground_score": 0.10034872390768271, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3292361576391089, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.020800367699657065, "[Relevant]": 0.9790015428125511}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.010430349638659712, "[Partially supported]": 0.1750430690384569, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7906414768944549}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00018915122801981256, "[Utility:2]": 0.00037178941918365174, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001140557619098318, "[Utility:4]": 0.0019942336427026352, "[Utility:5]": 0.8497544006724487}], "n_tokens": 49}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas was built in 1979.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Orleans Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas was built in 1979.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5297680855722506, "ctx": {"title": "Omni Royal Orleans", "text": "Omni Royal Orleans The Omni Royal Orleans is a 345-room hotel on the corner of St. Louis and Royal Streets near Jackson Square in the French Quarter of New Orleans, Louisiana. It was constructed in 1960 as the Royal Orleans, on the site of the old St. Louis Hotel, which was ruined by the 1915 New Orleans hurricane. The exterior was designed by architect Samuel Wilson Jr., while the interior was designed by Arthur Davis. The hotel has been rated four diamonds by AAA. Its address is 621 St. Louis Street. A partnership led by Darryl Berger, Jr., and the"}, "final_score": 1.5297680855722506, "relevance_score": 0.9886682438184204, "ground_score": 0.5410998417538302, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.30852546450960683, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.011330095250051655, "[Relevant]": 0.9885233315708923}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.4427478036989348, "[Partially supported]": 0.18601230288355586, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3613602257469549}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00044462328876328884, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002728566616450595, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003177580863033139, "[Utility:4]": 0.0026814216024501234, "[Utility:5]": 0.7852756332989389}], "n_tokens": 23}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "\"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" is a song written by Harlan Howard and Max D. Barnes, and recorded by the American country music artist Pam Tillis. It was released in December 1990 as the first single from the album \"Put Yourself in My Place\". The song reached number 5 on the \"Billboard\" Hot Country Singles & Tracks chart.", "question": "Who sings \"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" as recorded in 1990?", "short_answers": ["Pam Tillis"], "wikipage": "Don't Tell Me What to Do"}, {"context": "Marty Stuart recorded this song in 1988 under the title \"I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To)\" for his \"Let There Be Country\" album, although the album was not released until 1992.", "question": "Who sings \"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" recorded as \"I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To)\"?", "short_answers": ["Marty Stuart"], "wikipage": "Don't Tell Me What to Do"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who sings \"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" as recorded in 1993?", "short_answers": ["Baby Animals"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Don't Tell Me (Madonna song)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don%27t%20Tell%20Me%20%28Madonna%20song%29"}, {"title": "Don't Tell Me What to Do", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don%27t%20Tell%20Me%20What%20to%20Do"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "\"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" is a song by Australian rock band Baby Animals. It was released in June 1993 as the first single from their second studio album Shaved and Dangerous (1993).", "wikipage": "Don't Tell Me What to Do (Baby Animals song)"}], "long_answer": "Don't Tell Me What to Do is a song written by Harlan Howard and Max D. Barnes, and recorded by the American country music artist Pam Tillis. It was released in December 1990 as the first single from the album Put Yourself in My Place. The song reached number 5 on the Billboard Hot Country Singles & Tracks chart. Marty Stuart recorded this song in 1988 under the title I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To) for his Let There Be Country album, although the album was not released until 1992. Don't Tell Me What to Do is a song by Australian rock band Baby Animals. It was released in June 1993 as the first single from their second studio album Shaved and Dangerous."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Marty Stuart recorded the song Don't Tell Me What to Do, recorded as I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To) in 1988. Pam Tillis sang Don't Tell Me What To Do in 1990, and in 1993, the Baby Animals recorded the song."}], "sample_id": "7640844637176662879", "question": "Who sings don't tell me what to do?", "docs": [{"id": "14975299", "title": "Don't Tell Me What to Do", "text": "Don't Tell Me What to Do \"for Baby Animals' song of the same name, see \"Don't Tell Me What to Do (Baby Animals song)\" \"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" is a song written by Harlan Howard and Max D. Barnes, and recorded by American country music artist Pam Tillis. It was released in December 1990 as the first single from the album \"Put Yourself in My Place\". The song reached number 5 on the \"Billboard\" Hot Country Singles & Tracks chart Marty Stuart recorded this song under the title \"I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To)\" in 1988", "score": 0.83154296875, "summary": "The song \"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" was recorded by Pam Tillis and written by Harlan Howard and Max D. Barnes. It was released in December 1990 and reached number 5 on the Billboard Hot Country Singles & Tracks chart. Marty Stuart also recorded the song in 1988 under the title \"I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To).\"", "extraction": "Pam Tillis sings \"Don't Tell Me What to Do\"."}], "answer": "Marty Stuart recorded the song Don't Tell Me What to Do, recorded as I'll Love You Forever (If I Want To) in 1988. Pam Tillis sang Don't Tell Me What To Do in 1990, and in 1993, the Baby Animals recorded the song.", "output": "\"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" is a song written by Harlan Howard and Max D. Barnes, and recorded by American country music artist Pam Tillis [1]. It was released in December 1990 as the first single from the album Put Yourself in My Place [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]\"Don't Tell Me What to Do\" is a song written by Harlan Howard and Max D. Barnes, and recorded by American country music artist Pam Tillis.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was released in December 1990 as the first single from the album Put Yourself in My Place.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 864, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0,