 covered the song for Arista Records, who released their recording as a single and on the compilation album \"Then & Now... The Best of The Monkees\" in June 1986.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song \"That Was Then, This Is Now\" was written by Vance Brescia for his band The Mosquitos and recorded as the title track of their 1985 EP.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Clive Davis of Arista records heard it and picked it to be the Monkees comeback single and theme of The Monkees 20th Anniversary Tour.[Continue to Use Evidence]The Monkees (at that point consisting of Micky Dolenz and Peter Tork) covered the song for Arista Records, who released their recording as a single and on the compilation album \"Then & Now... The Best of The Monkees\" in June 1986.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9966389097638935, "ctx": {"title": "That Was Then, This Is Now (song)", "text": "That Was Then, This Is Now (song) \"That Was Then, This Is Now\" is a song written by Vance Brescia for his band The Mosquitos and recorded as the title track of their 1985 EP. Clive Davis of Arista records heard it and picked it to be the Monkees comeback single and theme of The Monkees 20th Anniversary Tour. The Monkees (at that point consisting of Micky Dolenz and Peter Tork) covered the song for Arista Records, who released their recording as a single and on the compilation album \"Then & Now... The Best of The Monkees\" in June 1986."}, "final_score": 1.9966389097638935, "relevance_score": 0.9997305208244863, "ground_score": 0.9969083889394073, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07450219216616066, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0002694708309540511, "[Relevant]": 0.9996995636611218}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9955680965024221, "[Partially supported]": 0.0022469270807533924, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0019674750907106076}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00010849607318808184, "[Utility:2]": 8.189713270266191e-05, "[Utility:3]": 8.30245091782286e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00037209003535424947, "[Utility:5]": 0.744671399608167}], "n_tokens": 144}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]That Was Then, This Is Now is the fourth studio album by Josh Wilson.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Sparrow Records alongside distributor Capitol Christian Music Group released the album on July 31, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]That Was Then, This Is Now is the fourth studio album by Josh Wilson.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Sparrow Records alongside distributor Capitol Christian Music Group released the album on July 31, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9491292746167077, "ctx": {"title": "That Was Then, This Is Now (Josh Wilson album)", "text": "That Was Then, This Is Now (Josh Wilson album) That Was Then, This Is Now is the fourth studio album by Josh Wilson. Sparrow Records alongside distributor Capitol Christian Music Group released the album on July 31, 2015. Awarding the album four stars from \"CCM Magazine\", Rebekah Bell states, \"a great all-around album to listen to front to back\", where its \"a spiritually deep and thought-provoking collection.\" Amanda Furbeck, giving the album four stars at \"Worship Leader\", says, \"\"That Was Then, This Is Now\" exudes the excitement, enthusiasm, and charisma of a sinner who is thankful to be saved by"}, "final_score": 1.9491292746167077, "relevance_score": 0.997132841331388, "ground_score": 0.9519964332853197, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.10109277196617465, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002866905000203074, "[Relevant]": 0.9970446212045526}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9479096554923293, "[Partially supported]": 0.007294144441988426, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.04433432273439991}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 7.083707065056366e-05, "[Utility:2]": 6.576999362799453e-05, "[Utility:3]": 4.830668517156914e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00021776779519122567, "[Utility:5]": 0.7074109143382218}], "n_tokens": 48}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]That Was Then, This Is Now is the fourth studio album by Josh Wilson.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Sparrow Records alongside distributor Capitol Christian Music Group released the album on July 31, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]That Was Then, This Is Now is the fourth studio album by Josh Wilson.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]Sparrow Records alongside distributor Capitol Christian Music Group released the album on July 31, 2015.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9750174403982061, "ctx": {"title": "That Was Then, This Is Now (Josh Wilson album)", "text": "the album five stars for 365 Days of Inspiring Media, states, \"Josh has delivered a standout album\". Rating the album three stars at The Christian Manifesto, Calvin Moore writes, \"[on] \"That Was Then, This Is Now\", he shows no signs of slowing down.\" That Was Then, This Is Now (Josh Wilson album) That Was Then, This Is Now is the fourth studio album by Josh Wilson. Sparrow Records alongside distributor Capitol Christian Music Group released the album on July 31, 2015. Awarding the album four stars from \"CCM Magazine\", Rebekah Bell states, \"a great all-around album to listen to front"}, "final_score": 1.9750174403982061, "relevance_score": 0.9969956571295566, "ground_score": 0.9780217832686494, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08752317595436428, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.003004178341775433, "[Relevant]": 0.9969410580459995}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9747190978829856, "[Partially supported]": 0.0059333360327436975, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.019003997694444996}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 5.365288115287366e-05, "[Utility:2]": 3.247862060686013e-05, "[Utility:3]": 4.097671261710247e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00019021598132857537, "[Utility:5]": 0.7690007725921852}], "n_tokens": 48}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The song That Was Then, This Is Now was originally sung by the American rock band The Monkees.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was remade in 1986 by the American new wave band Tears for Fears.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The song That Was Then, This Is Now was originally sung by the American rock band The Monkees.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was remade in 1986 by the American new wave band Tears for Fears.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9573160553669333, "ctx": {"title": "That Was Then, This Is Now (song)", "text": "The compilation album with three newly recorded songs was released by Arista and went platinum during the tour. \u201cThat Was Then, This Is Now\u201d became the band's new single and a video for it was filmed at the Great Arena in Jackson, New Jersey on July 25. The video received heavy airplay on MTV, making the song a Billboard Top 20 hit the summer of 1986. The song was a surprise hit, capitalizing on Monkees nostalgia at the time, reaching number 20 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 (the band's first Top 40 hit since \"D.W. Washburn\" in 1968), and number"}, "final_score": 1.9573160553669333, "relevance_score": 0.9961456366200658, "ground_score": 0.9611704187468674, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.533161230129254, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0038540877923139943, "[Relevant]": 0.9960744120420201}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.94945761115902, "[Partially supported]": 0.020651044378894364, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.028448015192538537}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003923723137772439, "[Utility:2]": 0.00023798595180880207, "[Utility:3]": 0.00018462105923332093, "[Utility:4]": 0.0024319043658946617, "[Utility:5]": 0.8490716438494474}], "n_tokens": 51}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many goals did the Brazilian soccer player Ronaldo score in his international career?", "short_answers": ["62"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many goals has the Portuguese soccer player Cristiano Ronaldo scored in his international career?", "short_answers": ["99"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many goals has the Portuguese soccer player Cristiano Ronaldo scored in his club career?", "short_answers": ["626"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many goals did the Brazilian soccer player Ronaldo score in his club career?", "short_answers": ["352"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of international goals scored by Cristiano Ronaldo", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20international%20goals%20scored%20by%20Cristiano%20Ronaldo"}, {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cristiano%20Ronaldo"}, {"title": "List of career achievements by Cristiano Ronaldo", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20career%20achievements%20by%20Cristiano%20Ronaldo"}, {"title": "Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronaldo%20%28Brazilian%20footballer%29"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Ronaldo Lu\u00eds Naz\u00e1rio de Lima (Brazilian Portuguese: [\u0281o\u02c8nawdu \u02c8lwis n\u0250\u02c8za\u027eju d\u0292i \u02c8\u026b\u0129m\u0250]; born 18 September 1976),[2] commonly known as Ronaldo, is a Brazilian business owner, president of La Liga club Real Valladolid, and a retired professional footballer who played as a striker. Popularly dubbed O Fen\u00f4meno (\"The Phenomenon\"),[3] and also nicknamed R9,[3] he is widely considered one of the greatest players of all time. As a multi-functional striker who brought a new dimension to the position, Ronaldo has been the influence for a generation of strikers that have followed. His individual accolades include being named FIFA World Player of the Year three times, and winning two Ballon d'Or awards.", "wikipage": null}], "long_answer": "Ronaldo Lu\u00eds Naz\u00e1rio de Lima, commonly known as Ronaldo, is a Brazilian business owner, president of La Liga club Real Valladolid, and a retired professional footballer who played as a striker, scoring 62 goals in his international career, and 352 goals for his club career.  Another famous footballer with a similar name, Cristiano Ronaldo, a Portuguese soccer player has scored 99 goals in his international career, and 626 in his club career. "}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Ronaldo Lu\u00eds Naz\u00e1rio de Lima (Brazilian Portuguese: [\u0281o\u02c8nawdu \u02c8lwis n\u0250\u02c8za\u027eju d\u0292i \u02c8\u026b\u0129m\u0250]; born 18 September 1976),[2] commonly known as Ronaldo, is a Brazilian business owner, president of La Liga club Real Valladolid, and a retired professional footballer who played as a striker.", "wikipage": "Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)"}, {"content": "Cristiano Ronaldo dos Santos Aveiro GOIH ComM (Portuguese pronunciation: [k\u027ei\u0283\u02c8tj\u0250nu \u0281\u0254\u02c8na\u026bdu]; born 5 February 1985) is a Portuguese professional footballer who plays as a forward for Premier League club Manchester United and captains the Portugal national team.", "wikipage": "Cristiano Ronaldo"}], "long_answer": "Ronaldo Lu\u00eds Naz\u00e1rio de Lima, commonly known as Ronaldo, is a Brazilian business owner, president of La Liga club Real Valladolid, and a retired professional footballer who played as a striker. In his club career he scored 352 goals and in his international career he scored 62 goals. Cristiano Ronaldo is a Portuguese professional footballer who plays as a forward for Premier League club Manchester United and captains the Portugal national team. In his club career he scored 626 goals and in his international career he scored 99 goals."}], "sample_id": "-5915617794689671991", "question": "How many goals scored ronaldo in his career?", "docs": [{"id": "3048742", "title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "A prolific goalscorer, Ronaldo holds the records for most official goals scored in Europe's top-five leagues (405), the UEFA Champions League (121), the UEFA European Championship (9), as well as those for most assists in the UEFA Champions League (34) and the UEFA European Championship (6). He has scored over 680 senior career goals for club and country. Born and raised on the Portuguese island of Madeira, Ronaldo was diagnosed with a racing heart at age 15. He underwent an operation to treat his condition, and began his senior club career playing for Sporting CP, before signing with Manchester United", "score": 0.779296875, "summary": "Cristiano Ronaldo has scored over 680 senior career goals for club and country, including 405 in Europe's top-five leagues, 121 in the UEFA Champions League, and 9 in the UEFA European Championship.", "extraction": "Cristiano Ronaldo has scored over 680 senior career goals for club and country."}], "answer": "Ronaldo Lu\u00eds Naz\u00e1rio de Lima, commonly known as Ronaldo, is a Brazilian business owner, president of La Liga club Real Valladolid, and a retired professional footballer who played as a striker. In his club career he scored 352 goals and in his international career he scored 62 goals. Cristiano Ronaldo is a Portuguese professional footballer who plays as a forward for Premier League club Manchester United and captains the Portugal national team. In his club career he scored 626 goals and in his international career he scored 99 goals.", "output": "Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 681 senior career goals for club and country [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 681 senior career goals for club and country.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 914, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 311 career goals for Portugal and 77 career goals for Real Madrid.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 311 career goals for Portugal and 77 career goals for Real Madrid.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.58556165728558, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "scored the second goal in a 3\u20130 defeat of Levante at the Bernab\u00e9u to take his overall total for the club to 324 goals. Ronaldo also became the all-time top scorer in the Champions League with a hat-trick in the first group match against Shakhtar Donetsk, having finished the previous season level with Messi on 77 goals. A brace against Malm\u00f6 FF, scoring in a 2\u20130 away win on 30 September, saw him reach the milestone of 500 career goals for both club and country. He subsequently became the first player to score double figures in the competition's group stage,"}, "final_score": 1.58556165728558, "relevance_score": 0.98255751727491, "ground_score": 0.6030041400106698, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.35906731541714787, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.017439959141497235, "[Relevant]": 0.9824153605608344}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.4124780878206619, "[Partially supported]": 0.37556557280293634, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2074070077772998}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005307749208663721, "[Utility:2]": 0.000492807831304679, "[Utility:3]": 0.001051456687920942, "[Utility:4]": 0.008769416535286881, "[Utility:5]": 0.6699531432938385}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 681 senior career goals for club and country.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 681 senior career goals for club and country.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6920738926478367, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "A prolific goalscorer, Ronaldo holds the records for most official goals scored in Europe's top-five leagues (405), the UEFA Champions League (121), the UEFA European Championship (9), as well as those for most assists in the UEFA Champions League (34) and the UEFA European Championship (6). He has scored over 680 senior career goals for club and country. Born and raised on the Portuguese island of Madeira, Ronaldo was diagnosed with a racing heart at age 15. He underwent an operation to treat his condition, and began his senior club career playing for Sporting CP, before signing with Manchester United"}, "final_score": 1.6920738926478367, "relevance_score": 0.989986429515314, "ground_score": 0.7020874631325227, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3021999054950963, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.010012762892120703, "[Relevant]": 0.989906587297057}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6235563841390591, "[Partially supported]": 0.15280907285523837, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.22060565746435376}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0006203159811064316, "[Utility:2]": 0.000736643454161408, "[Utility:3]": 0.001376231155305959, "[Utility:4]": 0.010995363854432087, "[Utility:5]": 0.7241321223632708}], "n_tokens": 24}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]In his career, Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 767 goals.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In his career, Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 767 goals.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2133923150575852, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "half of the campaign, Ronaldo suggested that he was in the best form of his career. By late November, he had scored 32 goals from 22 matches played for both club and country, including hat-tricks against Galatasaray, Sevilla, Real Sociedad, Northern Ireland, and Sweden. He ended 2013 with a total of 69 goals in 59 appearances, his highest year-end goal tally. His efforts earned him the FIFA Ballon d'Or, an amalgamation of the Ballon d'Or and the FIFA World Player of the Year award, for the first time in his career. Concurrently with his individual achievements, Ronaldo enjoyed his greatest"}, "final_score": 1.2133923150575852, "relevance_score": 0.9643214459179988, "ground_score": 0.24907086913958623, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3992829489323499, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.035670254027522545, "[Relevant]": 0.9640971116998139}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.14440730240374094, "[Partially supported]": 0.2052441602096214, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.6421521287175432}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004883436423683178, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003317229446224836, "[Utility:3]": 0.0008242359559383967, "[Utility:4]": 0.010523139183077566, "[Utility:5]": 0.9253193031250913}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 400 career goals, 118 for Portugal, and 282 for Real Madrid.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 400 career goals, 118 for Portugal, and 282 for Real Madrid.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.365327918219411, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "Golden Shoe, receiving the latter award jointly with Liverpool striker Luis Su\u00e1rez. Among his haul was his 400th career goal, in 653 appearances for club and country, which came with a brace against Celta Vigo on 6 January; he dedicated his goals to compatriot Eus\u00e9bio, who had died two days before. A last-minute, backheeled volley scored against Valencia on 4 May\u2014his 50th goal in all competitions\u2014was recognised as the best goal of the season by the Liga de F\u00fatbol Profesional, which additionally named Ronaldo the Best Player in La Liga. During the next campaign, the 2014\u201315 season, Ronaldo set a"}, "final_score": 1.365327918219411, "relevance_score": 0.9600626895068168, "ground_score": 0.40526522871259396, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.36480164464540266, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.039927498301310906, "[Relevant]": 0.9598268118474924}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2971802040939144, "[Partially supported]": 0.2140508711864895, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.4861543677569847}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004299086274277166, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002852643920844899, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006580980561671511, "[Utility:4]": 0.017926609312155818, "[Utility:5]": 0.9486465228183203}], "n_tokens": 35}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 500 career goals.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Cristiano Ronaldo has scored 500 career goals.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5672509695143675, "ctx": {"title": "Cristiano Ronaldo", "text": "to keep him with Madrid until 2021. On 19 November, he scored a hat-trick in a 3\u20130 away win against Atl\u00e9tico Madrid, making him the all-time top scorer in the Madrid derby with 18 goals. On 15 December, Ronaldo scored his 500th club career goal in the 2\u20130 victory over Club Am\u00e9rica in the semi-finals of the FIFA Club World Cup. He then scored a hat-trick in the 4\u20132 win over Japanese club Kashima Antlers in the final. Ronaldo finished the tournament as top scorer with four goals and was also named the best player of the tournament. He won"}, "final_score": 1.5672509695143675, "relevance_score": 0.9525741305249488, "ground_score": 0.6146768389894187, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3900489278511438, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0474113018151428, "[Relevant]": 0.9522815312299954}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5248013257695991, "[Partially supported]": 0.17306109877548995, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2966957503853769}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0016359624295469296, "[Utility:2]": 0.0013043029668921638, "[Utility:3]": 0.0023161064536380096, "[Utility:4]": 0.012569758035989262, "[Utility:5]": 0.5939306978687632}], "n_tokens": 19}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many Major League Baseball Players are in the Hall of FAme?", "short_answers": ["235"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many total individuals are in the Major League Baseball Hall of Fame?", "short_answers": ["333"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many Major League Baseball Players are in the Hall of Fame?", "short_answers": ["235"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many total individuals are in the Major League Baseball Hall of Fame?", "short_answers": ["333"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National%20Baseball%20Hall%20of%20Fame%20and%20Museum"}, {"title": "List of members of the Baseball Hall of Fame", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20members%20of%20the%20Baseball%20Hall%20of%20Fame"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Through the elections for 2020, a total of 333 people have been inducted, including 235 former major league players, 31 executives, 35 Negro leagues players and executives, 22 managers, and 10 umpires.", "wikipage": "List of members of the Baseball Hall of Fame"}], "long_answer": "Through 2020, a total of 333 people have been inducted in the Major League Baseball Hall of Fame, including 235 former major league players, 31 executives, 35 Negro leagues players and executives, 22 managers, and 10 umpires."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum is a history museum and hall of fame in Cooperstown, New York, operated by private interests. It serves as the central point of the history of baseball in the United States and displays baseball-related artifacts and exhibits, honoring those who have excelled in playing, managing, and serving the sport. The Hall's motto is \"Preserving History, Honoring Excellence, Connecting Generations\". ", "wikipage": "National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum"}], "long_answer": "The National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum is a history museum and hall of fame in Cooperstown, New York, operated by private interests. It serves as the central point of the history of baseball in the United States and displays baseball-related artifacts and exhibits, honoring those who have excelled in playing, managing, and serving the sport. There are 235 Major League Baseball Players in the Hall of Fame, and 333 individuals in total. "}], "sample_id": "-3187212609087063102", "question": "How many mlb players are in the hall of fame?", "docs": [{"id": "48326", "title": "National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum", "text": "Ruth, Honus Wagner, Christy Mathewson and Walter Johnson, chosen in 1936; roughly 20 more were selected before the entire group was inducted at the Hall's 1939 opening. , 323 people had been elected to the Hall of Fame, including 226 former Major League Baseball players, 35 Negro league baseball players and executives, 22 managers, 10 umpires, and 30 pioneers, executives, and organizers. 114 members of the Hall of Fame have been inducted posthumously, including four who died after their selection was announced. Of the 35 Negro league members, 29 were inducted posthumously, including all 24 selected since the 1990s. The", "score": 0.7216796875, "summary": "226 former Major League Baseball players are in the National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum, among a total of 323 people.", "extraction": "There are 226 former Major League Baseball players in the Hall of Fame."}], "answer": "The National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum is a history museum and hall of fame in Cooperstown, New York, operated by private interests. It serves as the central point of the history of baseball in the United States and displays baseball-related artifacts and exhibits, honoring those who have excelled in playing, managing, and serving the sport. There are 235 Major League Baseball Players in the Hall of Fame, and 333 individuals in total. ", "output": "As of 2018, there are 226 former Major League Baseball players in the Hall of Fame [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 226 former Major League Baseball players in the Hall of Fame.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 915, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]There are 325 players and 104 non-players in the hall of fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]There are 325 players and 104 non-players in the hall of fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.7072264977668153, "ctx": {"title": "2007 Baseball Hall of Fame balloting", "text": "five of the 200 nominated players who would appear on the final ballot, so the final ballots would comprise 25 to 30 players and 15 contributors. Evidently the writers passed over two of the Hall of Fame members' five selections, for there were 27 on the final players ballot. The final ballots were announced on September 28, 2006. 23 of the 25 players on the 2005 ballot returned, with Lefty O'Doul, Cecil Travis, Mickey Vernon and one newly eligible player added as well, replacing Elston Howard and Smoky Joe Wood. Those selected played primarily from the 1950s onward, with only"}, "final_score": 0.7072264977668153, "relevance_score": 0.7025300449389326, "ground_score": 0.0046964528278828094, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5599793297856545, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.2974191465147555, "[Relevant]": 0.7024100512060647}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.0007914913962229364, "[Partially supported]": 0.007808604213847644, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9912595165780608}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.001821472139266879, "[Utility:2]": 0.0024225029725977237, "[Utility:3]": 0.0012666350178707378, "[Utility:4]": 0.03228636619393147, "[Utility:5]": 0.9289174934489814}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]There are 333 players in the hall of fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]There are 333 players in the hall of fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.6407099593680748, "ctx": {"title": "2005 Baseball Hall of Fame balloting", "text": "were starting pitchers (66), relief pitchers (9), catchers (10), first basemen (24), second basemen (8), third basemen (13), shortstops (19), left fielders (18), center fielders (17) and right fielders (16). The Historical Overview nominations were forwarded to a 60-member BBWAA screening committee comprising two writers from each major league city. In summer 2004 they elected 25 players who would appear on the final ballot. (Everyone voted for 25 nominees.) Meanwhile, a committee of six Hall of Fame members independently selected five of the 200 nominees who would appear on the final ballot, which would thereby comprise 25 to 30 players."}, "final_score": 0.6407099593680748, "relevance_score": 0.6352224414795978, "ground_score": 0.00548751788847704, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5767643992679522, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.36473233152651596, "[Relevant]": 0.6351436833410937}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.000998732797649863, "[Partially supported]": 0.008971424710585773, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.9894698925113292}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.002594330568375027, "[Utility:2]": 0.0032795411310536067, "[Utility:3]": 0.0014327254129179298, "[Utility:4]": 0.0211361482856293, "[Utility:5]": 0.8917378209573321}], "n_tokens": 17}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 226 former Major League Baseball players in the Hall of Fame.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]As of 2018, there are 226 former Major League Baseball players in the Hall of Fame.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7996697942078903, "ctx": {"title": "National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum", "text": "Ruth, Honus Wagner, Christy Mathewson and Walter Johnson, chosen in 1936; roughly 20 more were selected before the entire group was inducted at the Hall's 1939 opening. , 323 people had been elected to the Hall of Fame, including 226 former Major League Baseball players, 35 Negro league baseball players and executives, 22 managers, 10 umpires, and 30 pioneers, executives, and organizers. 114 members of the Hall of Fame have been inducted posthumously, including four who died after their selection was announced. Of the 35 Negro league members, 29 were inducted posthumously, including all 24 selected since the 1990s. The"}, "final_score": 1.7996697942078903, "relevance_score": 0.9943140936176291, "ground_score": 0.8053557005902613, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.26389407637781975, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0056852975873013896, "[Relevant]": 0.9942076315204664}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7461766234833436, "[Partially supported]": 0.11442988112391975, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.13695465185624936}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004932984976318327, "[Utility:2]": 0.0012646120985668593, "[Utility:3]": 0.0010121827967022952, "[Utility:4]": 0.007868680872514484, "[Utility:5]": 0.7932765086016984}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]There are 325 players in the National Baseball Hall of Fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]There are 325 players in the National Baseball Hall of Fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0410867869466405, "ctx": {"title": "National Baseball Hall of Fame and Museum", "text": "Hall of Fame includes one female member, Effa Manley. The newest members elected on January 24, 2018, are players Chipper Jones, Jim Thome, Vladimir Guerrero, and Trevor Hoffman. They were officially inducted on July 29, 2018, alongside players Jack Morris and Alan Trammell. Players are currently inducted into the Hall of Fame through election by either the Baseball Writers' Association of America (or BBWAA), or the Veterans Committee, which now consists of four subcommittees, each of which considers and votes for candidates from a separate era of baseball. Five years after retirement, any player with 10 years of major league"}, "final_score": 1.0410867869466405, "relevance_score": 0.9706877664927535, "ground_score": 0.07039902045388695, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.49642088300376935, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.029306034620639795, "[Relevant]": 0.9704824875810124}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.04718086053030862, "[Partially supported]": 0.04608791707328942, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.904256736447443}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010955917952349568, "[Utility:2]": 0.0017303538402738841, "[Utility:3]": 0.0010413452839897993, "[Utility:4]": 0.01431928413264875, "[Utility:5]": 0.8928509606204819}], "n_tokens": 19}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]There are 32 players in the MLB Hall of Fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]There are 32 players in the MLB Hall of Fame.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0959924977545896, "ctx": {"title": "Major League Baseball All-Star Game", "text": "teams; in 1945, the scheduled All-Star Game was cancelled due to travel restrictions during World War II and no players were officially named. From 1959 through 1962, All-Stars played in two All-Star Games each season. On January 29, 1936, Babe Ruth became the first of the original thirty-six All-Stars to be inducted in the National Baseball Hall of Fame; twenty original All-Stars are enshrined in the Hall of Fame. Hank Aaron holds the record for the most All-Star namings (21) and the most All-Star Game appearances (25). In 2017, each All-Star team had 32 players, with fans voting for the"}, "final_score": 1.0959924977545896, "relevance_score": 0.9372107962258134, "ground_score": 0.1587817015287763, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.44014698963696397, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.06277208922785606, "[Relevant]": 0.9369553392900756}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.1162848866658985, "[Partially supported]": 0.08310506077263971, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7946629914866261}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0015955162032629702, "[Utility:2]": 0.0018436154686475648, "[Utility:3]": 0.001223324707503656, "[Utility:4]": 0.017355614776582023, "[Utility:5]": 0.8329800465647192}], "n_tokens": 18}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who played zorro's son in the 2005 legend of zorro film?", "short_answers": ["Alonso", "Adri\u00e1n Alonso"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who voices zorro's adopted son/brother in the 1996 legend of zorro anime?", "short_answers": ["Rica Matsumoto", "Matsumoto"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "The Legend of Zorro (anime series)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Legend%20of%20Zorro%20%28anime%20series%29"}, {"title": "The Legend of Zorro", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Legend%20of%20Zorro"}, {"title": "The Mask of Zorro", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Mask%20of%20Zorro"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Rika Matsumoto (\u677e\u672c \u68a8\u9999, Matsumoto Rika, born November 30, 1968) is a Japanese actress, voice actress and singer who was born in Yokohama, Japan.", "wikipage": "Rika Matsumoto"}, {"content": "Adri\u00e1n Alonso Barona (born April 6, 1994), known as Adri\u00e1n Alonso Comelon, is a child actor best known for the movie The Legend of Zorro.", "wikipage": "Adri\u00e1n Alonso"}], "long_answer": "There is both a 2005 Legend of Zorro film, in which child actor Adri\u00e1n Alonso played Zorro's son, and a 1996 Legend of Zorro anime, in which Zorro's adopted son/brother was played by Rica Matsumoto\u2014a Japanese actress, voice actress, and singer."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Legend of Zorro is a 2005 American Western swashbuckler film directed by Martin Campbell, produced by Walter F. Parkes, Laurie MacDonald and Lloyd Phillips, with music by James Horner, and written by Roberto Orci and Alex Kurtzman.", "wikipage": "The Legend of Zorro"}, {"content": "Adri\u00e1n Alonso Barona (born April 6, 1994), known as Adri\u00e1n Alonso Comelon, is a child actor best known for the movie The Legend of Zorro.", "wikipage": "Adri\u00e1n Alonso"}, {"content": "The Legend of Zorro (Japanese: \u5feb\u5091\u30be\u30ed, Hepburn: Kaiketsu Zoro, lit. \"Extraordinary Zorro\") is an Italian-Japanese anime, based on the western character Zorro.", "wikipage": "The Legend of Zorro (anime series)"}, {"content": "Rika Matsumoto (\u677e\u672c \u68a8\u9999, Matsumoto Rika, born November 30, 1968) is a Japanese actress, voice actress and singer who was born in Yokohama, Japan.[1][2]", "wikipage": "Rika Matsumoto"}, {"content": "Her name is also sometimes romanized as Rica Matsumoto.", "wikipage": "Rika Matsumoto"}], "long_answer": "There are several Legend of Zorro series. The Legend of Zorro is a 2005 American Western swashbuckler film directed by Martin Campbell. A child actor best known for the movie The Legend of Zorro, Adri\u00e1n Alonso played Zorro's son. Also, The Legend of Zorro is an Italian-Japanese anime, based on the western character Zorro. Rika Matsumoto, sometimes romanized as Rica Matsumoto, voiced the adopted son and brother in the anime series."}], "sample_id": "4757523269455004982", "question": "Who played zorro son in the legend of zorro?", "docs": [{"id": "10406347", "title": "Jose\u0301 Mari\u0301a de Tavira", "text": "Jos\u00e9 Mar\u00eda de Tavira Jos\u00e9 Mar\u00eda de Tavira Bianchi (born September 27, 1983 in Mexico City) is a Mexican film and stage actor. The son of Mexican theatre director Luis de Tavira and Argentine actress Rosa Mar\u00eda Bianchi, he began acting on stage with his parents; at age seven had his first participation in an adaptation of \"Clotilde en su casa\" of Jorge Ibarg\u00fcengoitia, then in 1994 participated in the work \"Jubileo\" by Jose Ramon Henr\u00edquez. He made his film debut in \"The Mask of Zorro\" playing the main character Alejandro Murrieta (Zorro) as a child. In 2002, he has", "score": 0.74560546875, "summary": "Jos\u00e9 Mar\u00eda de Tavira played the role of Alejandro Murrieta (Zorro) as a child in the film \"The Mask of Zorro\".", "extraction": "The Mask of Zorro, playing the main character Alejandro Murrieta (Zorro) as a child."}], "answer": "There are several Legend of Zorro series. The Legend of Zorro is a 2005 American Western swashbuckler film directed by Martin Campbell. A child actor best known for the movie The Legend of Zorro, Adri\u00e1n Alonso played Zorro's son. Also, The Legend of Zorro is an Italian-Japanese anime, based on the western character Zorro. Rika Matsumoto, sometimes romanized as Rica Matsumoto, voiced the adopted son and brother in the anime series.", "output": "In the 2005 film The Legend of Zorro, the character of Zorro's son, Alejandro Murrieta, is played by Diego Luna [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]In the 2005 film The Legend of Zorro, the character of Zorro's son, Alejandro Murrieta, is played by Diego Luna.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 917, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 1947 film serial Son of Zorro, George Turner plays the title character.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 1947 film serial Son of Zorro, George Turner plays the title character.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7217024281914823, "ctx": {"title": "Son of Zorro", "text": "the Moon\" and \"Zorro's Fighting Legion\". The last original Republic serial release had been \"King of the Carnival\" in 1955. In the early 1950s, \"Son of Zorro\" was one of fourteen Republic serials edited into a television series. It was broadcast in six 26\u00bd-minute episodes. Son of Zorro Son of Zorro (1947) is a Republic film serial. It was the 43rd of the 66 serials produced by that studio. The serial was directed by Spencer Gordon Bennet and Fred C. Brannon. George Turner starred as a descendant of the original Zorro in 1860s United States. A man returning home after"}, "final_score": 1.7217024281914823, "relevance_score": 0.9839708546024272, "ground_score": 0.7377315735890552, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.375856396411109, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.016026005415780066, "[Relevant]": 0.9837781025566058}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5916673025460292, "[Partially supported]": 0.2883556136250754, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.11741996915734809}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0028304283643486934, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004061721541290348, "[Utility:3]": 0.00027162809190799185, "[Utility:4]": 0.0071995736982648295, "[Utility:5]": 0.552175331459651}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 1920 film The Mark of Zorro, the character Don Q, Son of Zorro, was played by Douglas Fairbanks Jr.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 1920 film The Mark of Zorro, the character Don Q, Son of Zorro, was played by Douglas Fairbanks Jr.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6059000953971152, "ctx": {"title": "Don Q, Son of Zorro", "text": "Don Q, Son of Zorro Don Q, Son of Zorro is the 1925 sequel to the 1920 silent film \"The Mark of Zorro\". It was loosely based upon the 1909 novel \"Don Q.'s Love Story\", written by the mother-and-son duo Kate and Hesketh Hesketh-Prichard. The story was reworked in 1925 (after Hesketh Hesketh-Prichard's death) into a vehicle for the Johnston McCulley character Zorro. The film adaptation was made by screenwriters Jack Cunningham and Lotta Woods for United Artists studios. Douglas Fairbanks both produced the film and starred as its lead character. It was directed by Donald Crisp, who also played"}, "final_score": 1.6059000953971152, "relevance_score": 0.9734434308342704, "ground_score": 0.6324566645628447, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.28426389765570254, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.02654819607592592, "[Relevant]": 0.9731365113216526}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.25443159188778164, "[Partially supported]": 0.4328027172016232, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05721661833347856}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0004188351063079979, "[Utility:2]": 0.00015228572886905728, "[Utility:3]": 0.00015468388014919826, "[Utility:4]": 0.0031558354803284975, "[Utility:5]": 0.875025791443243}], "n_tokens": 39}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 1947 serial Son of Zorro, George Turner plays the son of the original Zorro.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 1947 serial Son of Zorro, George Turner plays the son of the original Zorro.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7666660259251028, "ctx": {"title": "Son of Zorro", "text": "Son of Zorro Son of Zorro (1947) is a Republic film serial. It was the 43rd of the 66 serials produced by that studio. The serial was directed by Spencer Gordon Bennet and Fred C. Brannon. George Turner starred as a descendant of the original Zorro in 1860s United States. A man returning home after having fought in the Civil War discovers that corrupt politicians have taken over the county and are terrorizing and shaking down the citizens. He dons the costume of his ancestor, the famous Zorro, and sets out to bring them to justice. \"Son of Zorro\" was"}, "final_score": 1.7666660259251028, "relevance_score": 0.9835969663719178, "ground_score": 0.7830690595531851, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.31369492875019206, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.016398988520339337, "[Relevant]": 0.98335440418527}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6510959374450861, "[Partially supported]": 0.2589880781938095, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08675012947135975}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0014489525707190936, "[Utility:2]": 0.00022526277452316796, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002478864386255545, "[Utility:4]": 0.0052588029793189245, "[Utility:5]": 0.6007534878224055}], "n_tokens": 30}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 1998 film The Legend of Zorro, the son of Zorro is played by Alex Vega.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 1998 film The Legend of Zorro, the son of Zorro is played by Alex Vega.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.076227884245092, "ctx": {"title": "Fernando Lupiz", "text": "Fernando Lupiz Fernando L\u00fapiz (born 12 April 1953) is an Argentine fencer and actor. He competed at the 1972 and 1976 Summer Olympics. He later became known for playing Zorro on stage. L\u00fapiz was born in Buenos Aires, Argentina on 12 April 1953. He is the son of fencer Enrique L\u00fapiz and the great nephew of Argentine actor Fernando Ochoa. L\u00fapiz took interest in fencing from an early age. He became the Junior and Senior National champion in Argentina. In 1975, he placed fourth in the Pan American Games in Mexico. During his fencing career, he won two Silver Olimpia"}, "final_score": 1.076227884245092, "relevance_score": 0.9358173572859922, "ground_score": 0.14041052695909992, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.41440743209452857, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0640808103377584, "[Relevant]": 0.9343325866190609}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.056999762079741396, "[Partially supported]": 0.1662278957822418, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7746584195236244}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005427838926714663, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001460882332012238, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016425141564403964, "[Utility:4]": 0.005524918377657218, "[Utility:5]": 0.8526344289234695}], "n_tokens": 33}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]In the 2005 film The Legend of Zorro, the character of Zorro's son, Alejandro Murrieta, is played by Diego Luna.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In the 2005 film The Legend of Zorro, the character of Zorro's son, Alejandro Murrieta, is played by Diego Luna.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.812905107544319, "ctx": {"title": "Jose\u0301 Mari\u0301a de Tavira", "text": "Jos\u00e9 Mar\u00eda de Tavira Jos\u00e9 Mar\u00eda de Tavira Bianchi (born September 27, 1983 in Mexico City) is a Mexican film and stage actor. The son of Mexican theatre director Luis de Tavira and Argentine actress Rosa Mar\u00eda Bianchi, he began acting on stage with his parents; at age seven had his first participation in an adaptation of \"Clotilde en su casa\" of Jorge Ibarg\u00fcengoitia, then in 1994 participated in the work \"Jubileo\" by Jose Ramon Henr\u00edquez. He made his film debut in \"The Mask of Zorro\" playing the main character Alejandro Murrieta (Zorro) as a child. In 2002, he has"}, "final_score": 1.812905107544319, "relevance_score": 0.9970650488991536, "ground_score": 0.8158400586451653, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3370578461401072, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002934536250356924, "[Relevant]": 0.9969241154016547}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7032121553298447, "[Partially supported]": 0.22301100101644736, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.07240108035892591}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003727321624866963, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003669534859254735, "[Utility:3]": 0.00021614256728287476, "[Utility:4]": 0.0027866037758322716, "[Utility:5]": 0.7696356112390493}], "n_tokens": 44}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "\"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" is a song written and performed by the Charlie Daniels Band and released on their 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\".", "question": "What band performed the original recording of \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\"?", "short_answers": ["Charlie Daniels Band"], "wikipage": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia"}, {"context": "\"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" is a song written and performed by the Charlie Daniels Band and released on their 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\".", "question": "Who was the lead singer on the original recording of \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\"?", "short_answers": ["Charles Edward Daniels", "Charlie Daniels"], "wikipage": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Steve Ouimette", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve%20Ouimette"}, {"title": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Devil%20Went%20Down%20to%20Georgia"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Charlie Daniels Band sang the original song The Devil Went Down To Georgia."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Charles Edward Daniels (October 28, 1936 \u2013 July 6, 2020) was an American singer, songwriter and multi-instrumentalist[5] known for his contributions to Southern rock, country, and bluegrass music. He was best known for his number-one country hit \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\".", "wikipage": "Charlie Daniels"}, {"content": "The song was the band's biggest hit, reaching number three on the Billboard Hot 100, prevented from further chart movement by \"After The Love Has Gone\" by Earth, Wind and Fire and \"My Sharona\" by The Knack.", "wikipage": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia"}], "long_answer": "The Charlie Daniels Band with lead singer Charlie Daniels performed the original recording of the song \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia.\" The song was released on the band's 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\" and was their biggest hit. Charlie Daniels was an American singer, songwriter, and multi-instrumentalist best known for the song, which was a number-one country hit."}], "sample_id": "-636188049911121263", "question": "Who sang the original devil went down to georgia?", "docs": [{"id": "4793666", "title": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia", "text": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" is a song written and performed by the Charlie Daniels Band and released on their 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\". The song is written in the key of D minor. Vassar Clements originally wrote the basic melody an octave lower, in a tune called \"Lonesome Fiddle Blues\" released on Clements' self-titled 1975 album on which Charlie Daniels played guitar. The Charlie Daniels Band moved it up an octave and put words to it. The song's verses are closer to being spoken rather than sung (i.e., recitation), and tell", "score": 0.83251953125, "summary": "The original singer of \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" is the Charlie Daniels Band, released on their 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\".", "extraction": "\"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" is a song written and performed by the Charlie Daniels Band."}], "answer": "The Charlie Daniels Band with lead singer Charlie Daniels performed the original recording of the song \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia.\" The song was released on the band's 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\" and was their biggest hit. Charlie Daniels was an American singer, songwriter, and multi-instrumentalist best known for the song, which was a number-one country hit.", "output": "The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 918, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9919038811376666, "ctx": {"title": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia", "text": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\" is a song written and performed by the Charlie Daniels Band and released on their 1979 album \"Million Mile Reflections\". The song is written in the key of D minor. Vassar Clements originally wrote the basic melody an octave lower, in a tune called \"Lonesome Fiddle Blues\" released on Clements' self-titled 1975 album on which Charlie Daniels played guitar. The Charlie Daniels Band moved it up an octave and put words to it. The song's verses are closer to being spoken rather than sung (i.e., recitation), and tell"}, "final_score": 1.9919038811376666, "relevance_score": 0.9992445944125258, "ground_score": 0.9926592867251409, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.18848222044634894, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0007553929532761332, "[Relevant]": 0.999227881994235}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9914427482830986, "[Partially supported]": 0.0019289460060322838, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.00637437624846734}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000637550381349779, "[Utility:2]": 0.000173616974468227, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012456408552480083, "[Utility:4]": 0.0014200034120977945, "[Utility:5]": 0.6991587425009814}], "n_tokens": 20}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9887374867842866, "ctx": {"title": "Charlie Daniels", "text": "in 1979 for \"The Devil Went Down to Georgia\", which reached No. 3 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 in September 1979. The following year, \"Devil\" became a major crossover success on rock radio stations after its inclusion on the soundtrack for the hit movie \"Urban Cowboy\", in which he made an onscreen appearance. The song still receives regular airplay on U.S. classic rock and country stations. A hard rock/heavy metal cover version of the song was included in the video game \"\" as the final guitar battle against the last boss (Lou, the devil). Daniels has openly stated his opposition"}, "final_score": 1.9887374867842866, "relevance_score": 0.9994161675167436, "ground_score": 0.9893213192675432, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.22518210971747976, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0005838084453293147, "[Relevant]": 0.9993750189104341}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9851836207032294, "[Partially supported]": 0.007760741562014177, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0067955323692951}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0009352308692250772, "[Utility:2]": 0.00026379397948879797, "[Utility:3]": 0.00019375116241301807, "[Utility:4]": 0.0026642248140724832, "[Utility:5]": 0.7651480382670964}], "n_tokens": 20}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The Devil Went Down to Georgia is a song written by Hank Locklin and recorded by Locklin with the country music band The Charms in 1963.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was Locklin's only number one hit as a singer.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Devil Went Down to Georgia is a song written by Hank Locklin and recorded by Locklin with the country music band The Charms in 1963.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]It was Locklin's only number one hit as a singer.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8484038948775132, "ctx": {"title": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia", "text": "seven weeks on the chart and peaked at number 60. In 1993, a sequel to the song, titled \"The Devil Comes Back to Georgia\", was recorded and released by Daniels and fiddle player Mark O'Connor, featuring guest performances by Travis Tritt (as the devil), Marty Stuart (as Johnny) and Johnny Cash as the narrator. The song can be found on Mark O'Connor's album, \"Heroes\". In the sequel, the devil, his anger unabated ten years after his defeat at young Johnny's hands, decides to take up the boy's challenge to \"c'mon back if you ever wanna try again\". With Johnny now"}, "final_score": 1.8484038948775132, "relevance_score": 0.9976218762846453, "ground_score": 0.8507820185928678, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5024309450751454, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0023779810980681998, "[Relevant]": 0.9975620483942527}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8005779593271041, "[Partially supported]": 0.09561539773700771, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.10098998679496116}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003180794687071239, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005203430347631115, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014849963541987204, "[Utility:4]": 0.0013391639446444315, "[Utility:5]": 0.5175348195031202}], "n_tokens": 56}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The original Charlie Daniels Band sang the song Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The original Charlie Daniels Band sang the song Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9821930330592, "ctx": {"title": "Joel DiGregorio", "text": "Devil Went Down To Georgia,\" which achieved a number 3 placing on the US Pop Charts, was co-written by DiGregorio. In addition to his work with Daniels, he recorded the solo projects \"Midnight in Savannah\" (2008) and \"Shake Rag\" (2008). The original Charlie Daniels Band was Charlie, Jerry Corbitt from the Youngbloods, Billy Cox from the Band of Gypsys, Jeffrey Meyer, and Taz. They stayed together for six months, but then it didn't work out. After that, it was Charlie, Taz, Jeffrey, and Earl Grigsby. The original Charlie Daniels Band can still be heard on a bootleg album called \"Corbitt"}, "final_score": 1.9821930330592, "relevance_score": 0.9983896426928063, "ground_score": 0.9838033903663935, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2283277380229265, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0016103066040038506, "[Relevant]": 0.998358207719117}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9786340059086055, "[Partially supported]": 0.009594191790733413, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.011393384655360458}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005523435184285606, "[Utility:2]": 0.0002171475107302028, "[Utility:3]": 0.00012180871391705858, "[Utility:4]": 0.0023899170057919963, "[Utility:5]": 0.796201741912714}], "n_tokens": 21}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Charlie Daniels Band sang the original Devil Went Down to Georgia.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.980070610858957, "ctx": {"title": "The Devil Went Down to Georgia", "text": "Devil motif. Charlie Daniels has stated in interviews, \"I don't know where it came from, but it just did. Well, I think I might know where it came from, it may have come from an old poem called 'The Mountain Whippoorwill' that Stephen Vincent Ben\u00e9t wrote many, many years ago (1925), that I had in high school.\" The original version of the song spent fourteen weeks on the Hot Country Singles charts in 1979, peaking at number 1 and holding the position for one week. It spent two weeks at a peak of number 3 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100."}, "final_score": 1.980070610858957, "relevance_score": 0.9977829869600562, "ground_score": 0.9822876238989008, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1833614031314937, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002216865633944514, "[Relevant]": 0.9977166458083995}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.976700147670422, "[Partially supported]": 0.010434493452285584, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.01248845348489182}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000782670963418772, "[Utility:2]": 0.00020497092817785994, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014879293706558368, "[Utility:4]": 0.002477626023592198, "[Utility:5]": 0.7938003284822812}], "n_tokens": 20}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 2]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "The 6th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League that honored its best players from the 2016 NFL season. It was held on February 4, 2017 and aired on Fox in the United States at 8:00 PM EST. It was hosted by Keegan-Michael Key.", "question": "Who is the mc of the nfl honors in 2017?", "short_answers": ["Keegan-Michael Key"], "wikipage": "6th Annual NFL Honors"}, {"context": "The 5th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players from the 2015 NFL season. It was held on February 6, 2016 and aired on CBS in the United States at 9:00 PM EST. Comedian Conan O'Brien hosted the show.", "question": "Who is the mc of the nfl honors in 2016?", "short_answers": ["Conan O'Brien"], "wikipage": "5th Annual NFL Honors"}, {"context": "The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season. It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States. Seth Meyers hosted the show. For the second year, the newest Pro Football Hall of Fame class was announced and introduced during the show with the Class of 2015 appearing on stage at Symphony Hall in Phoenix. Unlike the previous three NFL Honors presentations, the GMC Never Say Never Moment of the Year Award was not presented; although Aaron Rodgers was announced as the winner of the 2014 season award.", "question": "Who is the mc of the nfl honors in 2015?", "short_answers": ["Seth Meyers"], "wikipage": "4th Annual NFL Honors"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "6th Annual NFL Honors", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/6th%20Annual%20NFL%20Honors"}, {"title": "7th Annual NFL Honors", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7th%20Annual%20NFL%20Honors"}, {"title": "9th Annual NFL Honors", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9th%20Annual%20NFL%20Honors"}, {"title": "5th Annual NFL Honors", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/5th%20Annual%20NFL%20Honors"}, {"title": "4th Annual NFL Honors", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4th%20Annual%20NFL%20Honors"}, {"title": "NFL Honors", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL%20Honors"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "The 10th Annual NFL Honors was an awards presentation by the National Football League that honored its players from the 2020 NFL season. It was held on February 6, 2021 at SoFi Stadium in Los Angeles, California.", "wikipage": "10th Annual NFL Honors"}, {"content": "For the third consecutive year, Steve Harvey hosted the show.", "wikipage": "10th Annual NFL Honors"}, {"content": "The NFL Honors is an annual awards presentation in the National Football League (NFL).", "wikipage": "NFL Honors"}], "long_answer": "There have been a number of MCs of the annual NFL Honors awards presentation. Steve Harvey hosted the NFL Honors in 2019, 2020, and 2021. The MCs of the NFL Honors were Keegan-Michael Key, Conan O'Brien, and Seth Meyers in 2017, 2016, and 2015, respectively."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The NFL Honors is an annual awards presentation in the National Football League (NFL).", "wikipage": "NFL Honors"}], "long_answer": "The NFL Honors is an annual awards presentation in the National Football League (NFL). The 4th Annual NFL Honors was held on January 31, 2015 and was hosted by Seth Meyers. The 5th Annual NFL Honors was held on February 6, 2016 and comedian Conan O'Brien hosted the show. The 6th Annual NFL Honors was held on February 4, 2017 and was hosted by Keegan-Michael Key."}], "sample_id": "3725813781881858440", "question": "Who is the mc of the nfl honors?", "docs": [{"id": "18434795", "title": "4th Annual NFL Honors", "text": "not presented; although Aaron Rodgers was announced as the winner of the 2014 season award. 4th Annual NFL Honors The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season. It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States. Seth Meyers hosted the show. For the second year, the newest Pro Football Hall of Fame class was announced and introduced during the show with the Class of 2015 appearing on stage at Symphony Hall in Phoenix. Unlike the previous", "score": 0.767578125, "summary": "Seth Meyers was the MC of the 4th Annual NFL Honors in 2015, where Aaron Rodgers was announced as the winner of the 2014 season award.", "extraction": "Seth Meyers hosted the show."}], "answer": "The NFL Honors is an annual awards presentation in the National Football League (NFL). The 4th Annual NFL Honors was held on January 31, 2015 and was hosted by Seth Meyers. The 5th Annual NFL Honors was held on February 6, 2016 and comedian Conan O'Brien hosted the show. The 6th Annual NFL Honors was held on February 4, 2017 and was hosted by Keegan-Michael Key.", "output": "The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season [1]. It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States [1]. Seth Meyers hosted the show [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States.[Continue to Use Evidence]Seth Meyers hosted the show.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 919, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States.[Continue to Use Evidence]Seth Meyers hosted the show.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States.[Continue to Use Evidence]Seth Meyers hosted the show.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.9919241677068732, "ctx": {"title": "4th Annual NFL Honors", "text": "4th Annual NFL Honors The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season. It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States. Seth Meyers hosted the show. For the second year, the newest Pro Football Hall of Fame class was announced and introduced during the show with the Class of 2015 appearing on stage at Symphony Hall in Phoenix. Unlike the previous three NFL Honors presentations, the GMC Never Say Never Moment of the Year Award was"}, "final_score": 0.9919241677068732, "relevance_score": 0.8998277847319702, "ground_score": 0.09209638297490298, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.05440415759162414, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.10015043275329755, "[Relevant]": 0.8996321165826232}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.08301135478530029, "[Partially supported]": 0.016733532906860957, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.8924560900990302}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0005006746535746451, "[Utility:2]": 0.00042491705963295593, "[Utility:3]": 3.742004397708313e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.00046668009245816983, "[Utility:5]": 0.6494786132116053}], "n_tokens": 76}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States.[Continue to Use Evidence]Seth Meyers hosted the show.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season.[No support / Contradictory][Continue to Use Evidence]It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States.[Continue to Use Evidence]Seth Meyers hosted the show.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.0352739925250884, "ctx": {"title": "4th Annual NFL Honors", "text": "not presented; although Aaron Rodgers was announced as the winner of the 2014 season award. 4th Annual NFL Honors The 4th Annual NFL Honors was the awards presentation by the National Football League honoring its best players and other individuals from the 2014 NFL season. It was held on January 31, 2015, and aired on NBC in the United States. Seth Meyers hosted the show. For the second year, the newest Pro Football Hall of Fame class was announced and introduced during the show with the Class of 2015 appearing on stage at Symphony Hall in Phoenix. Unlike the previous"}, "final_score": 1.0352739925250884, "relevance_score": 0.9615338074305333, "ground_score": 0.07374018509455504, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.059864615944984