in the 1950s tv show Highway Patrol?", "short_answers": ["Broderick Crawford"], "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (American TV series)"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who starred in the Australian TV show Highway Patrol?", "short_answers": ["members of the Victoria Police highway patrol", "Members of Victoria Police"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews. Crawford won an Academy Award for Best Actor in 1949 for \"All the King's Men\". William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams. Boyett went on to play Sgt. MacDonald in \"Adam-12\".", "question": "Who starred in the American tv show highway patrol?", "short_answers": ["William Broderick Crawford", "Broderick Crawford"], "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (American TV series)"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who starred in the Australian tv show highway patrol?", "short_answers": ["Members of Victoria Police"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Highway Patrol (American TV series)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highway%20Patrol%20%28American%20TV%20series%29"}, {"title": "Highway patrol", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highway%20patrol"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Highway Patrol stars Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. ", "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (American TV series)"}, {"content": "Highway Patrol is a 156-episode action crime drama series produced for syndication from 1955 to 1959.\n\n", "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (American TV series)"}, {"content": "Highway Patrol is an Australian factual television series screened on the Seven Network, which premiered on 21 September 2009.", "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (Australian TV series)"}, {"content": "Highway Patrol follows members of the Victoria Police highway patrol (formerly the Traffic Management Unit) as they intercept traffic and other criminal offenders on roads in Victoria, Australia.", "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (Australian TV series)"}], "long_answer": "In the American TV show \"Highway Patrol\", that aired from 1955 to1959,  Broderick Crawford starred as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. The Australian factual television series \"Highway Patrol\"  follows members of Victoria Police highway patrol as they intercept traffic and other criminal offenders on roads in Victoria, Australia."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Highway Patrol is a 156-episode action crime drama series produced for syndication from 1955 to 1959.", "wikipage": null}, {"content": "Highway Patrol stars Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. ", "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (American TV series)"}, {"content": "Highway Patrol is an Australian factual television series screened on the Seven Network, which premiered on 21 September 2009.[2] Highway Patrol follows members of the Victoria Police highway patrol (formerly the Traffic Management Unit) as they intercept traffic and other criminal offenders on roads in Victoria, Australia.", "wikipage": "Highway Patrol (Australian TV series)"}], "long_answer": "The American version of Highway Patrol is a 156-episode action crime drama series produced for syndication from 1955 to 1959. Broderick Crawford stars as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. The Australian Highway Patrol is a factual television series screened on the Seven Network, which premiered on 21 September 2009.  Members of Victoria Police are followed as they intercept traffic and other criminal offenders on roads in Victoria, Australia. "}], "sample_id": "3465016931089997251", "question": "Who starred in the tv show highway patrol?", "docs": [{"id": "2424835", "title": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series)", "text": "these: The style of these closings evolved slightly over time. In early episodes, Crawford promised next week's viewers a \"different\", \"unusual\", or \"exciting\" case; toward the end of the series this verbiage was dropped. The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews. Crawford won an Academy Award for Best Actor in 1949 for \"All the King's Men\". William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams. Boyett went on to play Sgt. MacDonald in \"Adam-12\". Another constant is the voice of Art Gilmore as the heard but unseen narrator. Gilmore", "score": 0.79931640625, "summary": "Broderick Crawford starred in the TV show Highway Patrol as Dan Mathews.", "extraction": "The star of the tv show \"Highway Patrol\" is Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews."}], "answer": "The American version of Highway Patrol is a 156-episode action crime drama series produced for syndication from 1955 to 1959. Broderick Crawford stars as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. The Australian Highway Patrol is a factual television series screened on the Seven Network, which premiered on 21 September 2009.  Members of Victoria Police are followed as they intercept traffic and other criminal offenders on roads in Victoria, Australia. ", "output": "The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews [1]. William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 923, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The show starred Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The show starred Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.994902764735512, "ctx": {"title": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series)", "text": "Amazon.com. On April 2, 2013, TGG Direct released seasons 2, 3, and 4 on DVD. Most episodes are also available, for free, via YouTube. Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series) Highway Patrol is a 156-episode action crime drama series produced for syndication from 1955 to 1959. \"Highway Patrol\" stars Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. A signature shot of the series is fedora-wearing Mathews barking rapid-fire dialogue into a radio microphone as he leans against the door of his black and white patrol car. Mathews growls \"21-50 to"}, "final_score": 1.994902764735512, "relevance_score": 0.9992083702746635, "ground_score": 0.9956943944608485, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.15433744181572556, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0007916159521501857, "[Relevant]": 0.999190985526904}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9932485778768317, "[Partially supported]": 0.0047456616007273715, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0019324591319146143}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007264619245335225, "[Utility:2]": 0.00048772346355266004, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003695938951520881, "[Utility:4]": 0.00971995309849829, "[Utility:5]": 0.5738141450544146}], "n_tokens": 38}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The show starred many actors who would later become successful stars in their own right, among them Stuart Whitman, Clint Eastwood, Robert Conrad, Larry Hagman, Barbara Eden, Paul Burke, and Leonard Nimoy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The show starred many actors who would later become successful stars in their own right, among them Stuart Whitman, Clint Eastwood, Robert Conrad, Larry Hagman, Barbara Eden, Paul Burke, and Leonard Nimoy.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9840742398045195, "ctx": {"title": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series)", "text": "running second to \"I Love Lucy\". Ziv Television Programs produced 156 episodes spanning four TV seasons, 1955\u20131959. In the four years of its run, \"Highway Patrol\" would feature many actors who would later become successful stars in their own right, among them Stuart Whitman, Clint Eastwood, Robert Conrad, Larry Hagman, Barbara Eden, Paul Burke and Leonard Nimoy. Episodes are generally fast-paced\u2014notable considering how a typical episode was filmed: two days on location and one day at the studio. The budget for an episode ranged from $20,000 to $25,000, somewhat higher when a Bell 47 helicopter was used. Producer Frederic W."}, "final_score": 1.9840742398045195, "relevance_score": 0.9997765836998718, "ground_score": 0.9842976561046478, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.17318500007551682, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00022341109648236267, "[Relevant]": 0.9997532976492673}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9703247654024311, "[Partially supported]": 0.02774188466989282, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0018297751943237815}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010378049405735579, "[Utility:2]": 0.000582155810503849, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006172856467964711, "[Utility:4]": 0.006533557567674459, "[Utility:5]": 0.7821980604211541}], "n_tokens": 55}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The show starred Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The show starred Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.996209772910754, "ctx": {"title": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series)", "text": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series) Highway Patrol is a 156-episode action crime drama series produced for syndication from 1955 to 1959. \"Highway Patrol\" stars Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews, the gruff and dedicated head of a police force in an unidentified Western state. A signature shot of the series is fedora-wearing Mathews barking rapid-fire dialogue into a radio microphone as he leans against the door of his black and white patrol car. Mathews growls \"21-50 to headquarters\" and the invariable response is \"Headquarters by\" (as in, standing by). Ziv Television Programs was founded by Frederick Ziv in 1948. Ten years"}, "final_score": 1.996209772910754, "relevance_score": 0.9997153774748189, "ground_score": 0.9964943954359352, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.15329062365020418, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0002846206347153347, "[Relevant]": 0.999708737354925}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.994165802676585, "[Partially supported]": 0.004532524233379517, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0012391231720973092}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0008137035156111129, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005212780414528091, "[Utility:3]": 0.00036533502825681464, "[Utility:4]": 0.009912938164735569, "[Utility:5]": 0.571650853163883}], "n_tokens": 38}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9966158385304875, "ctx": {"title": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series)", "text": "these: The style of these closings evolved slightly over time. In early episodes, Crawford promised next week's viewers a \"different\", \"unusual\", or \"exciting\" case; toward the end of the series this verbiage was dropped. The only constant regular on \"Highway Patrol\" is star Broderick Crawford as Dan Mathews. Crawford won an Academy Award for Best Actor in 1949 for \"All the King's Men\". William Boyett became a regular in the fourth season as Sgt. Ken Williams. Boyett went on to play Sgt. MacDonald in \"Adam-12\". Another constant is the voice of Art Gilmore as the heard but unseen narrator. Gilmore"}, "final_score": 1.9966158385304875, "relevance_score": 0.9998968978440705, "ground_score": 0.9967189406864171, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12283761879217285, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000103101246182009, "[Relevant]": 0.9998880750057809}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9951929777163495, "[Partially supported]": 0.0025651048102876338, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0019977056349122367}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.948765520919814e-05, "[Utility:2]": 0.00013335334280504532, "[Utility:3]": 0.00014195389449414453, "[Utility:4]": 0.004719263797843256, "[Utility:5]": 0.7573139087611916}], "n_tokens": 48}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The show starred Broderick Crawford as Chief Dan Mathews, a fictional police chief, and John Daly as Officer Jim McLain, a highway patrolman.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The show starred Broderick Crawford as Chief Dan Mathews, a fictional police chief, and John Daly as Officer Jim McLain, a highway patrolman.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.757336598272299, "ctx": {"title": "Highway Patrol (U.S. TV series)", "text": "later, Ziv TV was a major producer of 1950s and early 1960s first-run syndicated series, including \"Bat Masterson\", \"The Cisco Kid\", \"Science Fiction Theater\", \"Lock-Up\", \"Sea Hunt\", and \"Ripcord\". \"Highway Patrol\" was created by Ziv in response to the California Highway Patrol wanting to be featured in a TV series. However, because Ziv thought that the show needed to have a broader police scope than the real CHP, the generic show name was adopted. Crawford signed in April 1955. \"Highway Patrol\" premiered October 3, 1955, with \"Prison Break\", an episode filmed April 11\u201313, 1955. Initial ratings were strong the show"}, "final_score": 1.757336598272299, "relevance_score": 0.9938549071764886, "ground_score": 0.7634816910958104, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.5854959042651031, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0061448223690615715, "[Relevant]": 0.9938111661802341}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5585306069535273, "[Partially supported]": 0.4086297212922544, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.03200635315132839}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0010113534675350567, "[Utility:2]": 0.0005830445098212916, "[Utility:3]": 0.0006255154498572048, "[Utility:4]": 0.008304173756439208, "[Utility:5]": 0.7592897518186055}], "n_tokens": 44}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 4], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his \"Trait\u00e9 des poisons\", also called \"Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale\".", "question": "Who is known as the father of toxicology with Trait\u00e9 des poisons, also called Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale?", "short_answers": ["Mathieu Orfila"], "wikipage": "Toxicology"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is known as the father of toxicology with Alle Dinge sind Gift und nichts ist ohne Gift allein die Dosis macht, dass ein Ding kein Gift ist?", "short_answers": ["Paracelsus", "Theophrastus von Hohenheim"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Paracelsus", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paracelsus"}, {"title": "Toxicology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toxicology"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Paracelsus (/\u02ccp\u00e6r\u0259\u02c8s\u025bls\u0259s/; c. 1493[1] \u2013 24 September 1541), born Theophrastus von Hohenheim (full name Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim[11][12]), was a Swiss[13] physician, alchemist, lay theologian, and philosopher of the German Renaissance.[14][15]", "wikipage": "Paracelsus"}, {"content": "He was a pioneer in several aspects of the \"medical revolution\" of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\".[16]", "wikipage": "Paracelsus"}], "long_answer": "Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his Trait\u00e9 des poisons, also called Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale. Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, lay theologian, and philosopher of the German Renaissance. He was a pioneer in several aspects of the medical revolution of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. He is credited as the father of toxicology."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Paracelsus (/\u02ccp\u00e6r\u0259\u02c8s\u025bls\u0259s/; c. 1493[1] \u2013 24 September 1541), born Theophrastus von Hohenheim (full name Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim[10][11]), was a Swiss[12] physician, alchemist, lay theologian, and philosopher of the German Renaissance.[13][14]", "wikipage": "Paracelsus"}, {"content": "He was a pioneer in several aspects of the \"medical revolution\" of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\".", "wikipage": "Paracelsus"}], "long_answer": "Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his \"Trait\u00e9 des poisons\", also called \"Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale\". Also credited as the father of toxicology is Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, full name Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, who was a Swiss physician, alchemist, lay theologian, and philosopher of the German Renaissance. He was a pioneer in several aspects of the \"medical revolution\" of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. "}], "sample_id": "-6502702531638979425", "question": "Who is known as the father of toxicology?", "docs": [{"id": "1346984", "title": "Paracelsus", "text": "Paracelsus Paracelsus (; 1493/4 \u2013 24 September 1541), born Theophrastus von Hohenheim (full name Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim), was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance. He was a pioneer in several aspects of the \"medical revolution\" of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\". He also had a substantial impact as a prophet or diviner, his \"Prognostications\" being studied by Rosicrucians in the 1700s. Paracelsianism is the early modern medical movement inspired by the study of his works. Paracelsus was born", "score": 0.7021484375, "summary": "Paracelsus, also known as Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, is credited as the \"father of toxicology\". He was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance.", "extraction": "Paracelsus is credited as the \"father of toxicology\"."}], "answer": "Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his \"Trait\u00e9 des poisons\", also called \"Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale\". Also credited as the father of toxicology is Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, full name Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, who was a Swiss physician, alchemist, lay theologian, and philosopher of the German Renaissance. He was a pioneer in several aspects of the \"medical revolution\" of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. ", "output": "Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance [1]. He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\" [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\".[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 925, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]Theophrastus Phillipus Auroleus Bombastus von Hohenheim, also referred to as Paracelsus, is considered the father of toxicology.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is credited with the classic toxicology maxim, \"Alle Dinge sind Gift und nichts ist ohne Gift; allein die Dosis macht, dass ein Ding kein Gift ist.\" which translates as, \"All things are poisonous and nothing is without poison; only the dose makes a thing not a poison.\"[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Theophrastus Phillipus Auroleus Bombastus von Hohenheim, also referred to as Paracelsus, is considered the father of toxicology.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is credited with the classic toxicology maxim, \"Alle Dinge sind Gift und nichts ist ohne Gift; allein die Dosis macht, dass ein Ding kein Gift ist.\" which translates as, \"All things are poisonous and nothing is without poison; only the dose makes a thing not a poison.\"[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9916143744699688, "ctx": {"title": "Toxicology", "text": "to convict the Belgian Count Hippolyte Visart de Bocarm\u00e9 of killing his brother-in-law. Theophrastus Phillipus Auroleus Bombastus von Hohenheim (1493\u20131541) (also referred to as Paracelsus, from his belief that his studies were above or beyond the work of Celsus \u2013 a Roman physician from the first century) is also considered \"the father\" of toxicology. He is credited with the classic toxicology maxim, \"\"Alle Dinge sind Gift und nichts ist ohne Gift; allein die Dosis macht, dass ein Ding kein Gift ist.\"\" which translates as, \"All things are poisonous and nothing is without poison; only the dose makes a thing not"}, "final_score": 1.9916143744699688, "relevance_score": 0.9988926611099412, "ground_score": 0.9927217133600276, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.08551450263341222, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0011072727442121369, "[Relevant]": 0.9988329931967558}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9863271661300526, "[Partially supported]": 0.012034024787085144, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0012585062937204526}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0009861576983091602, "[Utility:2]": 0.0012153661327990466, "[Utility:3]": 0.0012937505139210125, "[Utility:4]": 0.008841180206537626, "[Utility:5]": 0.7505652769694451}], "n_tokens": 116}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his \"Trait\u00e9 des poisons\", also called \"Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale\".[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his \"Trait\u00e9 des poisons\", also called \"Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale\".[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9876391083998524, "ctx": {"title": "Toxicology", "text": "attempt to classify plants according to their toxic and therapeutic effect. Ibn Wahshiyya wrote the \"Book on Poisons\" in the 9th or 10th century. This was followed up in 1360 by Khagendra Mani Darpana. Mathieu Orfila is considered the modern father of toxicology, having given the subject its first formal treatment in 1813 in his \"Trait\u00e9 des poisons\", also called \"Toxicologie g\u00e9n\u00e9rale\". In 1850, Jean Stas became the first person to successfully isolate plant poisons from human tissue. This allowed him to identify the use of nicotine as a poison in the famous Bocarm\u00e9 murder case, providing the evidence needed"}, "final_score": 1.9876391083998524, "relevance_score": 0.9994927188198278, "ground_score": 0.9881463895800244, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.07501451553762309, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0005072160044779293, "[Relevant]": 0.9993643035850084}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9814702996505354, "[Partially supported]": 0.012647876282695642, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005525447934796401}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0019035363219950298, "[Utility:2]": 0.0012099613032926413, "[Utility:3]": 0.0009313404638169317, "[Utility:4]": 0.023009355137681632, "[Utility:5]": 0.6937771427225443}], "n_tokens": 50}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\".[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Paracelsus, born Theophrastus von Hohenheim, was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\".[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9963602514561485, "ctx": {"title": "Paracelsus", "text": "Paracelsus Paracelsus (; 1493/4 \u2013 24 September 1541), born Theophrastus von Hohenheim (full name Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim), was a Swiss physician, alchemist, and astrologer of the German Renaissance. He was a pioneer in several aspects of the \"medical revolution\" of the Renaissance, emphasizing the value of observation in combination with received wisdom. He is credited as the \"father of toxicology\". He also had a substantial impact as a prophet or diviner, his \"Prognostications\" being studied by Rosicrucians in the 1700s. Paracelsianism is the early modern medical movement inspired by the study of his works. Paracelsus was born"}, "final_score": 1.9963602514561485, "relevance_score": 0.9998813320218175, "ground_score": 0.996478919434331, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.13003334215999598, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.00011866710938560225, "[Relevant]": 0.9998740116489376}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9924553776953211, "[Partially supported]": 0.0052079283713755056, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0009121002709225437}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00047537990454010135, "[Utility:2]": 0.0011315026231866825, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001854355823097574, "[Utility:4]": 0.004726731762671896, "[Utility:5]": 0.791814886402889}], "n_tokens": 54}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]Izaak Kolthoff is considered the father of analytical chemistry.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Izaak Kolthoff is considered the father of analytical chemistry.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8585157375748467, "ctx": {"title": "Izaak Kolthoff", "text": "Izaak Kolthoff Izaak Maurits (Piet) Kolthoff (February 11, 1894 \u2013 March 4, 1993) was a highly influential analytical chemist and chemical educator. He is widely considered the father of analytical chemistry for his large volume of published research in diverse fields of analysis, his work to modernize and promote the field, and for advising a large number of students who went on to influential careers of their own. Kolthoff's best-known research contribution was the development of the \"cold process\" for producing synthetic rubber, which he undertook under the U.S. synthetic rubber program during World War II. He was also active"}, "final_score": 1.8585157375748467, "relevance_score": 0.9938070101917938, "ground_score": 0.8647087273830527, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3162133858742891, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.006191791409345741, "[Relevant]": 0.9936146996559599}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7795863525297828, "[Partially supported]": 0.16341017188782983, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.05305152181906984}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.015333258721840838, "[Utility:2]": 0.003151860747232647, "[Utility:3]": 0.0012009936332088979, "[Utility:4]": 0.02440695738198527, "[Utility:5]": 0.6859506187832092}], "n_tokens": 20}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]Mathieu Joseph Bonaventure Orfila i Rotger, known as Mateu Orfila in Catalan, was a Spanish toxicologist and chemist, the founder of the science of toxicology.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is considered the father of toxicology.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]Mathieu Joseph Bonaventure Orfila i Rotger, known as Mateu Orfila in Catalan, was a Spanish toxicologist and chemist, the founder of the science of toxicology.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]He is considered the father of toxicology.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9820221521071528, "ctx": {"title": "Mathieu Orfila", "text": "Mathieu Orfila Mathieu Joseph Bonaventure Orfila (Catalan: \"Mateu Josep Bonaventura Orfila i Rotger\") (24 April 1787 \u2013 12 March 1853) was a Spanish toxicologist and chemist, the founder of the science of toxicology. If there is reason to believe that a murder or attempted murder may have been committed using poison, a forensic toxicologist is often brought in to examine pieces of evidence such as corpses and food items for poison content. In Orfila's time the primary type of poison in use was arsenic, but there were no reliable ways of testing for its presence. Orfila created new techniques and"}, "final_score": 1.9820221521071528, "relevance_score": 0.9996485617669432, "ground_score": 0.9823735903402095, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2579540144227697, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0003514160009710017, "[Relevant]": 0.9995853237052771}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9727729139087331, "[Partially supported]": 0.01867201164222716, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.008285654940040908}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0016773619787807867, "[Utility:2]": 0.0020793722776072327, "[Utility:3]": 0.00039223164153029443, "[Utility:4]": 0.009067733186262654, "[Utility:5]": 0.822653218308824}], "n_tokens": 60}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "Kristian Matsson (born 30 April 1983) is a singer-songwriter from Dalarna, Sweden, who performs under the stage name of The Tallest Man on Earth. Matsson grew up in Leksand, and began his solo career in 2006, having previously been the lead singer of the indie band Montezumas. His music has often drawn comparisons to the music of Bob Dylan.", "question": "Who has the stage name the tallest person on the earth?", "short_answers": ["Kristian Matsson"], "wikipage": "The Tallest Man on Earth"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the tallest man in recorded history on the earth?", "short_answers": ["Robert Wadlow"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the unconfirmed tallest woman in history on the earth?", "short_answers": ["Trijntje Keever"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is the tallest confirmed woman in recorded history on the earth?", "short_answers": ["Zeng Jinlian"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "List of tallest people", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20tallest%20people"}, {"title": "The Tallest Man on Earth", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The%20Tallest%20Man%20on%20Earth"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "Robert Pershing Wadlow (February 22, 1918 \u2013 July 15, 1940), also known as the Alton Giant and the Giant of Illinois, was an American man who was the tallest person in recorded history for whom there is irrefutable evidence.", "wikipage": "Robert Wadlow"}, {"content": "Wadlow's height was 8 ft 11 in (2.72 m)[2][3][4] while his weight reached 439 lb (199 kg) at his death at age 22.", "wikipage": "Robert Wadlow"}, {"content": "Trijntje Cornelisdochter Keever (April 10 or 16, 1616 \u2013 July 2, 1633), nicknamed De Groote Meid (in English, The Big Girl), is alleged to be the tallest female person in recorded history, standing 9 Amsterdam feet[1] or 2.49 metres (8 ft 2 in)[2] tall at the time of her death at age seventeen.", "wikipage": "Trijntje Keever"}, {"content": "Zeng Jinlian (simplified Chinese: \u66fe\u91d1\u83b2; traditional Chinese: \u66fe\u91d1\u84ee; pinyin: Z\u0113ng J\u012bnli\u00e1n, June 26, 1964 \u2013February 13, 1982) was the tallest woman verified in modern times,[1] surpassing Jane Bunford's record. In the year between Don Koehler's death and her own, she surpassed fellow \"eight-footers\" Gabriel Est\u00eav\u00e3o Monjane and Suleiman Ali Nashnush.", "wikipage": "Zeng Jinlian"}], "long_answer": "Robert Wadlow, also known as the Alton Giant and the Giant of Illinois, was an American man who was the tallest person in recorded history for whom there is irrefutable evidence. Wadlow's height was 8 ft 11 in (2.72 m) at his death at age 22. Trijntje Keever, nicknamed De Groote Meid (in English, The Big Girl), is the tallest unconfirmed female person in recorded history, standing 9 Amsterdam feet or 2.49 metres (8 ft 2 in) tall at the time of her death at age seventeen. Zeng Jinlian was the tallest woman verified in modern times, surpassing Jane Bunford's record. In the year between Don Koehler's death and her own, she surpassed fellow \"eight-footers\" Gabriel Est\u00eav\u00e3o Monjane and Suleiman Ali Nashnush. Kristian Matsson is a singer-songwriter from Dalarna, Sweden, who performs under the stage name of The Tallest Man on Earth."}, {"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The tallest person on the Earth can mean different things. For instance, Kristian Matsson is a singer-songwriter from Dalarna, Sweden, who performs under the stage name of The Tallest Man on Earth. The actual tallest man in recorded history on the Earth was Robert Wadlow. The unconfirmed tallest woman on Earth in history was Trijntje Keever. The tallest woman in recorded history Zeng Jinlian."}], "sample_id": "2187765416989332036", "question": "Who is the tallest person on the earth?", "docs": [{"id": "8568819", "title": "Alam Channa", "text": "Alam Channa Haji Mohammad Alam Channa (1953 in Sehwan, Sindh province, Pakistan \u2013 2 July 1998 in New York City) was one of the tallest living people with the height of . According to the Guinness Book of World Records, the tallest living man in the world (between 1982 and 1998), was Pakistan\u2019s Mohammad Alam Channa. Before his death in 1998, Channa was reported to be tall. Born in 1953 in the city of Sehwan (in Pakistan\u2019s Sindh province), Alam Channa is said to have stood 6 ft 4 inches by the time he was 18. He continued to grow", "score": 0.71826171875, "summary": "The tallest living man in the world between 1982 and 1998 was Mohammad Alam Channa from Pakistan, who stood tall at the time of his death in 1998.", "extraction": "Pakistan\u2019s Mohammad Alam Channa was the tallest living man in the world between 1982 and 1998, with a reported height of tall."}], "answer": "The tallest person on the Earth can mean different things. For instance, Kristian Matsson is a singer-songwriter from Dalarna, Sweden, who performs under the stage name of The Tallest Man on Earth. The actual tallest man in recorded history on the Earth was Robert Wadlow. The unconfirmed tallest woman on Earth in history was Trijntje Keever. The tallest woman in recorded history Zeng Jinlian.", "output": "The tallest living man is Mohammad Alam Channa from Pakistan, who was 7 feet and 4 inches tall [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The tallest living man is Mohammad Alam Channa from Pakistan, who was 7 feet and 4 inches tall.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 926, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The Tallest Man on Earth is a Swedish indie folk band formed in 2006 by Kristian Matsson.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Tallest Man on Earth is a Swedish indie folk band formed in 2006 by Kristian Matsson.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2886950842533524, "ctx": {"title": "The Tallest Man on Earth", "text": "The Tallest Man on Earth Kristian Matsson (born 30 April 1983) is a singer-songwriter from Dalarna, Sweden, who performs under the stage name of The Tallest Man on Earth. Matsson grew up in Leksand, and began his solo career in 2006, having previously been the lead singer of the indie band Montezumas. His music has often drawn comparisons to the music of Bob Dylan. Since 2006, Matsson has released four full-length albums and two EPs. He records and produces these in his home, and usually records his voice and guitar together on one track. He is known both by critics"}, "final_score": 1.2886950842533524, "relevance_score": 0.9914225150694009, "ground_score": 0.2972725691839515, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.37937296954770583, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.008572754032099643, "[Relevant]": 0.990875697520118}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.178729017473918, "[Partially supported]": 0.2331065837416306, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5814693315447068}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0022035327210222786, "[Utility:2]": 0.0020944282506804098, "[Utility:3]": 0.0007266479673209938, "[Utility:4]": 0.013185587206418069, "[Utility:5]": 0.5349907271746291}], "n_tokens": 31}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The Tallest Man on Earth is a Swedish indie folk band formed in 2006 by singer-songwriter Kristian Matsson.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The Tallest Man on Earth is a Swedish indie folk band formed in 2006 by singer-songwriter Kristian Matsson.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2967946447154244, "ctx": {"title": "The Tallest Man on Earth", "text": "discovered open tunings while listening to Nick Drake in his early twenties. He was drawn to this style of playing because it allowed him to focus on singing while still performing intricate music. The Tallest Man on Earth Kristian Matsson (born 30 April 1983) is a singer-songwriter from Dalarna, Sweden, who performs under the stage name of The Tallest Man on Earth. Matsson grew up in Leksand, and began his solo career in 2006, having previously been the lead singer of the indie band Montezumas. His music has often drawn comparisons to the music of Bob Dylan. Since 2006, Matsson"}, "final_score": 1.2967946447154244, "relevance_score": 0.9834704422614344, "ground_score": 0.31332420245398984, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.31180150242876803, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.016524486809001107, "[Relevant]": 0.9831687336845757}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.19638136851665555, "[Partially supported]": 0.23139380126447023, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.5682483314506945}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0034723329840518342, "[Utility:2]": 0.002452653372586826, "[Utility:3]": 0.0008848290625812934, "[Utility:4]": 0.021860261556376256, "[Utility:5]": 0.680052884829135}], "n_tokens": 35}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The tallest living man is Robert Wadlow, who was 7 feet and 5 inches tall.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The tallest living man is Robert Wadlow, who was 7 feet and 5 inches tall.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.096667784389541, "ctx": {"title": "Alam Channa", "text": "he also sent some photos of Channa he had taken at the circus. Months later some officials from Guinness landed in Sindh\u2019s capital city, Karachi, and from there they reached Sehwan where they met and measured Channa. They measured him at being 7 ft 7 inches. On their return to the UK, the men entered Channa\u2019s name in the Guinness book as the tallest living human on earth. In 1998 his kidneys began to fail. The government decided to send him to the US and finance his treatment. But in a US hospital, he slipped into a coma and died,"}, "final_score": 1.096667784389541, "relevance_score": 0.8984105568012132, "ground_score": 0.19825722758832776, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.43462111839975337, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.1015670183666197, "[Relevant]": 0.8982122418452585}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.11076783846963487, "[Partially supported]": 0.17290617128774471, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.7110989249425227}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007411614464674431, "[Utility:2]": 0.00033146774382675594, "[Utility:3]": 0.000471110878923564, "[Utility:4]": 0.004540164291967777, "[Utility:5]": 0.5696342276992948}], "n_tokens": 26}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]V\u00e4in\u00f6 Myllyrinne was an acromegalic giant who was at one time the world's tallest living person, and may have become the tallest after the death of Robert Wadlow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]V\u00e4in\u00f6 Myllyrinne was an acromegalic giant who was at one time the world's tallest living person, and may have become the tallest after the death of Robert Wadlow.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6387790755660205, "ctx": {"title": "Va\u0308ino\u0308 Myllyrinne", "text": "V\u00e4in\u00f6 Myllyrinne V\u00e4in\u00f6 Myllyrinne (27 February 1909 \u2013 13 April 1963)) was an acromegalic giant who was at one time (1940?\u20131963) the world's tallest living person, and may have become the tallest after the death of Robert Wadlow. He stood and weighed at the age of 21, but experienced a second phase of growth in his late thirties, attaining a height of and weighing 376 pounds. Myllyrinne was born in Helsinki, Grand Duchy of Finland, and is considered the tallest soldier ever, having served in the Finnish Defence Forces. He underwent his conscript training in 1929 in the Viipuri Heavy"}, "final_score": 1.6387790755660205, "relevance_score": 0.9878528306489542, "ground_score": 0.6509262449170664, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.11813151503235317, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.012145707349797233, "[Relevant]": 0.9877339352890546}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.52466739934651, "[Partially supported]": 0.24783534553704153, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.2239005760451753}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007153263931478778, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004916361608467523, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005420700206277295, "[Utility:4]": 0.009385842185977919, "[Utility:5]": 0.6377539511624091}], "n_tokens": 51}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The tallest living man is Mohammad Alam Channa from Pakistan, who was 7 feet and 4 inches tall.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The tallest living man is Mohammad Alam Channa from Pakistan, who was 7 feet and 4 inches tall.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.6717242583884295, "ctx": {"title": "Alam Channa", "text": "Alam Channa Haji Mohammad Alam Channa (1953 in Sehwan, Sindh province, Pakistan \u2013 2 July 1998 in New York City) was one of the tallest living people with the height of . According to the Guinness Book of World Records, the tallest living man in the world (between 1982 and 1998), was Pakistan\u2019s Mohammad Alam Channa. Before his death in 1998, Channa was reported to be tall. Born in 1953 in the city of Sehwan (in Pakistan\u2019s Sindh province), Alam Channa is said to have stood 6 ft 4 inches by the time he was 18. He continued to grow"}, "final_score": 1.6717242583884295, "relevance_score": 0.9868787280895626, "ground_score": 0.684845530298867, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.4288748361451003, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.013117370184243086, "[Relevant]": 0.9865852709757736}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.6153316335399841, "[Partially supported]": 0.1351704376419737, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.24668170552013396}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0003654747676388826, "[Utility:2]": 0.00028352236008767124, "[Utility:3]": 0.00048417540186796533, "[Utility:4]": 0.0034405435328411426, "[Utility:5]": 0.5372216856959903}], "n_tokens": 31}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 5], "1": [null, 0, 4]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the most played song on Spotify (total plays)?", "short_answers": ["Shape of You"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "In the UK, \"7 Rings\" became Grande's fourth number one in the country. The single sold 126,000 units, becoming Grande's biggest opening week. It also set the record for the most streams of a song in a week with 16.9 million streams. On the issue dated 21 February 2019, \"7 Rings\" was replaced by \"Break Up with Your Girlfriend, I'm Bored\", making Grande the second female artist to occupy the top two positions on the UK Singles Chart and the first female artist to self-replace on the top of the chart. The song reclaimed the summit position the following week, pushing \"Break Up with Your Girlfriend, I'm Bored\" down to number two, and thus making Grande the first artist in the chart's history to self-replace for two consecutive weeks.", "question": "What is the most played song ever on spotify in a single week?", "short_answers": ["7 Rings"], "wikipage": "7 Rings"}, {"context": "In 2010 \"The Daily Telegraph\" named \"All I Want for Christmas Is You\" the most popular and most played Christmas song of the decade in the United Kingdom; by 2015 \"Fairytale of New York\" had become the UK\u2019s most-played Christmas song of the 21st century. \"Rolling Stone\" ranked it fourth on its Greatest Rock and Roll Christmas Songs list, calling it a \"holiday standard.\" In a UK-wide poll in December 2012, it was voted fifth on the ITV television special \"The Nation's Favourite Christmas Song\". Due to the song's lasting impact, Carey was dubbed the \"Queen of Christmas\", a title she is hesitant to accept. \"I don't accept that name because I feel like it's [too much],\" she said. \"I humbly thank them and I do have an extraordinary love for the holiday season, and it is the best time of the year.\"", "question": "What is the most played song ever on spotify in a single day?", "short_answers": ["All I Want for Christmas is You"], "wikipage": "All I Want for Christmas Is You"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "7 Rings", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7%20Rings"}, {"title": "List of most-streamed artists on Spotify", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20most-streamed%20artists%20on%20Spotify"}, {"title": "List of most-streamed songs on Spotify", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List%20of%20most-streamed%20songs%20on%20Spotify"}, {"title": "All I Want for Christmas Is You", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/All%20I%20Want%20for%20Christmas%20Is%20You"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "As of September 2021, all of the top 100 songs have exceeded 1.2 billion streams, of which eight have reached 2 billion streams, with Ed Sheeran's \"Shape of You\" ranked in the top position.[1][2][3]", "wikipage": "List of most-streamed songs on Spotify"}, {"content": "On November 24, 2019, the song won three records in Guinness World Records for one of the best-selling and most recognizable Christmas songs, most streamed song on Spotify in 24 hours (female) (10,819,009 streams in December 2018) and most weeks in the UK singles Top 10 chart for a Christmas song (20) titles.[92]", "wikipage": "All I Want for Christmas Is You Legacy and cultural impact"}], "long_answer": "As of September 2021, all of the top 100 songs have exceeded 1.2 billion streams, of which eight have reached 2 billion streams, with Ed Sheeran's Shape of You ranked in the top position. The most played song on Spotify in a single week is 7 Rings by Ariana Grande with 16.9 million streams. The most played song on Spotify in a single day is All I Want for Christmas is You by Mariah Carey, which received 10.8 million streams in December 2018."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "As of August 2021, all of the top 100 songs have exceeded 1 billion streams, of which eight have reached 2 billion streams, with Ed sheeran's \"Shape of you\" ranked in the top position.", "wikipage": "List of most-streamed songs on Spotify"}, {"content": "\"All I Want for Christmas Is You\" is a Christmas song by American singer-songwriter Mariah Carey. ", "wikipage": "List of most-streamed songs on Spotify"}], "long_answer": "As of August 2021, Ed Sheeran's \"Shape of You\" is the most streamed song on Spotify with over 2 billion streams. 7 Rings by by American singer Ariana Grande, from her fifth studio album Thank U, Next, is the most played song for a single week, and a Christmas song by Mariah Carey \"All I Want for Christmas Is You\" is the most played song on a single day. "}], "sample_id": "-5144857012337911560", "question": "What is the most played song ever on spotify?", "docs": [{"id": "19432911", "title": "One Dance", "text": "year. On October 15, 2016, \"One Dance\" became the most played song ever on streaming media service Spotify, with over one billion individual streams, overtaking the previous record held by Major Lazer and DJ Snake's \"Lean On\". Ed Sheeran's \"Shape of You\" overtook \"One Dance\" as the most streamed song on Spotify on September 21, 2017. \"One Dance\" was the best-performing single worldwide of 2016 and is one of the best-selling singles of all-time. In early 2016, UK funky artist Kyla was first contacted by Drake's production team regarding the use of her 2008 song \"Do You Mind (Crazy Cousinz", "score": 0.72412109375, "summary": "\"One Dance\" is the most played song ever on Spotify with over one billion individual streams as of October 15, 2016, but it was later overtaken by \"Shape of You\" on September 21, 2017.", "extraction": "\"One Dance\" became the most played song ever on streaming media service Spotify, with over one billion individual streams."}], "answer": "As of August 2021, Ed Sheeran's \"Shape of You\" is the most streamed song on Spotify with over 2 billion streams. 7 Rings by by American singer Ariana Grande, from her fifth studio album Thank U, Next, is the most played song for a single week, and a Christmas song by Mariah Carey \"All I Want for Christmas Is You\" is the most played song on a single day. ", "output": "The most played song ever on Spotify is \"One Dance\" by Drake featuring Kyla and Majid Jordan [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"One Dance\" by Drake featuring Kyla and Majid Jordan.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 927, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Irrelevant]Drake's song \"One Dance\" is the most played song ever on Spotify.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Irrelevant]Drake's song \"One Dance\" is the most played song ever on Spotify.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 0.41869690935568665, "ctx": {"title": "Spotify", "text": "December 2016, Drake's just-under 26 million monthly listeners were overtaken by the Weeknd's 36.068 million. Later that month, Drake's song \"One Dance\" became the first song to hit one billion streams on Spotify. Upon its release in August 2017, the single \"Look What You Made Me Do\" by Taylor Swift earned over eight million streams within 24 hours, breaking the record for the most single-day streams for a track. On June 19, 2018, XXXTentacion's hit single \"Sad!\" broke Swift's single-day streaming record, amassing 10.4 million streams the day after he was fatally shot in Florida. In March 2011, Spotify announced"}, "final_score": 0.41869690935568665, "relevance_score": 0.41869690935568665, "ground_score": 0.0, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2156141081726199, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.5812287489397743, "[Relevant]": 0.41864336303462335}, "support_probs": [], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00167920899917683, "[Utility:2]": 0.0003248944887222477, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003248944887222477, "[Utility:4]": 0.002520791271124682, "[Utility:5]": 0.9151648681595055}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]In 2016, \"Lean On\" by Major Lazer and DJ Snake featuring M\u00d8 was the most played song ever on Spotify.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]In 2016, \"Lean On\" by Major Lazer and DJ Snake featuring M\u00d8 was the most played song ever on Spotify.[Partially supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.2848066468288861, "ctx": {"title": "Spotify", "text": "including the creation of a new Arabic hub and several playlists, while supporting Right-to-left text in their apps. In October 2015, \"Thinking Out Loud\" by Ed Sheeran became the first song to pass 500 million streams. A month later, Spotify announced that \"Lean On\" by Major Lazer and DJ Snake featuring M\u00d8 was its most streamed song of all time with over 525 million streams worldwide. In April 2016, Rihanna overtook Justin Bieber to become the biggest artist on Spotify, with 31.3 million monthly active listeners. In May 2016, Rihanna was overtaken by Drake with 3.185 billion total streams. In"}, "final_score": 1.2848066468288861, "relevance_score": 0.8104789155369047, "ground_score": 0.4743277312919816, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.27571920684223233, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.18948259438258397, "[Relevant]": 0.8103143143327688}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.28609646925764876, "[Partially supported]": 0.370236340209254, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3371040239199491}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00010211809239977975, "[Utility:2]": 5.225878638637545e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002319315794978076, "[Utility:4]": 0.0015664992979263041, "[Utility:5]": 0.8912216811925359}], "n_tokens": 38}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"One Dance\" by Drake featuring Kyla and Majid Jordan.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"One Dance\" by Drake featuring Kyla and Majid Jordan.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.5382345121331573, "ctx": {"title": "One Dance", "text": "year. On October 15, 2016, \"One Dance\" became the most played song ever on streaming media service Spotify, with over one billion individual streams, overtaking the previous record held by Major Lazer and DJ Snake's \"Lean On\". Ed Sheeran's \"Shape of You\" overtook \"One Dance\" as the most streamed song on Spotify on September 21, 2017. \"One Dance\" was the best-performing single worldwide of 2016 and is one of the best-selling singles of all-time. In early 2016, UK funky artist Kyla was first contacted by Drake's production team regarding the use of her 2008 song \"Do You Mind (Crazy Cousinz"}, "final_score": 1.5382345121331573, "relevance_score": 0.985718067176223, "ground_score": 0.5525164449569343, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3425449578665801, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.014280928281860844, "[Relevant]": 0.9856487351657636}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.47316226987876925, "[Partially supported]": 0.15481810240839386, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.36849914630142894}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0001342943132438258, "[Utility:2]": 5.901361228284085e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0002771733531127063, "[Utility:4]": 0.0018867515712635377, "[Utility:5]": 0.773156776278165}], "n_tokens": 29}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"Despacito\" by Luis Fonsi ft.[No support / Contradictory]Daddy Yankee.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"Despacito\" by Luis Fonsi ft.[No support / Contradictory]Daddy Yankee.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.237426429540091, "ctx": {"title": "Despacito", "text": "Phonographic Industry, \"Despacito\" was the second best-selling digital single of 2017 worldwide, with 24.3 million sales plus track-equivalent streams. \"Despacito\" became the world's most-streamed song of all-time in July 2017, with 4.6 billion streams between the original and remix version, surpassing Justin Bieber's \"Sorry\". It was streamed 7.5 billion times as of April 2018. In February 2018, the remix version became the first Latin and eight overall song to surpass one billion streams on Spotify. In the United Kingdom, it became the longest-reigning foreign language number-one, surpassing The Manhattan Transfer's three weeks achieved in 1977 with their single \"Chanson D'Amour\"."}, "final_score": 1.237426429540091, "relevance_score": 0.9838471675755406, "ground_score": 0.25357926196455044, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.21962456192159152, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.016151090508859524, "[Relevant]": 0.9837410698532306}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.08902449288237156, "[Partially supported]": 0.0955093389537181, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.3548602861984991}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00016779250989275063, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001309324159015416, "[Utility:3]": 0.00017515948572968166, "[Utility:4]": 0.0012642816900441884, "[Utility:5]": 0.9603681115269773}], "n_tokens": 32}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"Shape of You\" by Ed Sheeran.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The most played song ever on Spotify is \"Shape of You\" by Ed Sheeran.[No support / Contradictory][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.3314152644528727, "ctx": {"title": "Shape of You", "text": "was also Australia's highest selling song of 2017 with 630,000 copies sold by the end of the year. The song is the first ever number one single of the Philippine Hot 100 upon the chart's inception on 12 June 2017. It stayed at the peak position for 4 consecutive weeks before it was dethroned by \"Despacito\" by Luis Fonsi and Daddy Yankee featuring Justin Bieber. Worldwide, the song reached one billion streams on Spotify in June 2017. It became the most streamed song on Spotify in September 2017, reaching 1,318,420,396 streams overtaking Drake's \"One Dance\". In August 2018, \"Billboard\" published"}, "final_score": 1.3314152644528727, "relevance_score": 0.905322295654202, "ground_score": 0.42609296879867087, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.2097044993241434, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.09466538629536024, "[Relevant]": 0.9052045086231651}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.2937632963565854, "[Partially supported]": 0.2612784844247909, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.44099093894536295}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 9.920077655028915e-05, "[Utility:2]": 5.2275135762188536e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0001641945643096907, "[Utility:4]": 0.001083303927442567, "[Utility:5]": 0.950852816617136}], "n_tokens": 25}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 3], "1": [null, 0, 5]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "\"Only Fools and Horses\" has separate theme songs for the opening and closing credits, \"Only Fools and Horses\" and \"Hooky Street\", respectively. The original theme tune was produced by Ronnie Hazlehurst and recorded on 6 August 1981 at Lime Grove Studios. Alf Bigden, Paul Westwood, Don Hunt, John Dean, Judd Proctor, Eddie Mordue, and Rex Morris were hired to play the music. The tune was changed after the first series, and the new one was written by John Sullivan (he disliked the tune for the first series, and his new one explained the show's title), and Hazlehurst conducted it. It was recorded at Lime Grove on 11 May 1982, with musicians John Horler, Dave Richmond, Bigden, and Proctor. Sullivan had intended Chas & Dave to sing it because they had enjoyed success with the \"Rockney\" style, a mixture of rock n' roll and traditional Cockney music. Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt. Despite the creation of a new theme tune, the original one remained in occasional use. Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\". Both songs are performed by Sullivan himself, and not \u2013 as is sometimes thought \u2013 by Nicholas Lyndhurst.", "question": "Who sings the original opening theme after the first series to Only Fools and Horses?", "short_answers": ["John Sullivan"], "wikipage": "Only Fools and Horses"}, {"context": "\"Only Fools and Horses\" has separate theme songs for the opening and closing credits, \"Only Fools and Horses\" and \"Hooky Street\", respectively. The original theme tune was produced by Ronnie Hazlehurst and recorded on 6 August 1981 at Lime Grove Studios. Alf Bigden, Paul Westwood, Don Hunt, John Dean, Judd Proctor, Eddie Mordue, and Rex Morris were hired to play the music. The tune was changed after the first series, and the new one was written by John Sullivan (he disliked the tune for the first series, and his new one explained the show's title), and Hazlehurst conducted it. It was recorded at Lime Grove on 11 May 1982, with musicians John Horler, Dave Richmond, Bigden, and Proctor. Sullivan had intended Chas & Dave to sing it because they had enjoyed success with the \"Rockney\" style, a mixture of rock n' roll and traditional Cockney music. Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt. Despite the creation of a new theme tune, the original one remained in occasional use. Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\". Both songs are performed by Sullivan himself, and not \u2013 as is sometimes thought \u2013 by Nicholas Lyndhurst.", "question": "Who sings the revise theme to only fools and horses?", "short_answers": ["John Sullivan"], "wikipage": "Only Fools and Horses"}, {"context": "\"Only Fools and Horses\" has separate theme songs for the opening and closing credits, \"Only Fools and Horses\" and \"Hooky Street\", respectively. The original theme tune was produced by Ronnie Hazlehurst and recorded on 6 August 1981 at Lime Grove Studios. Alf Bigden, Paul Westwood, Don Hunt, John Dean, Judd Proctor, Eddie Mordue, and Rex Morris were hired to play the music. The tune was changed after the first series, and the new one was written by John Sullivan (he disliked the tune for the first series, and his new one explained the show's title), and Hazlehurst conducted it. It was recorded at Lime Grove on 11 May 1982, with musicians John Horler, Dave Richmond, Bigden, and Proctor. Sullivan had intended Chas & Dave to sing it because they had enjoyed success with the \"Rockney\" style, a mixture of rock n' roll and traditional Cockney music. Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt. Despite the creation of a new theme tune, the original one remained in occasional use. Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\". Both songs are performed by Sullivan himself, and not \u2013 as is sometimes thought \u2013 by Nicholas Lyndhurst.", "question": "Who sings the closing theme in the episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\" of the series Only Fools and Horses?", "short_answers": ["Chas & Dave"], "wikipage": "Only Fools and Horses"}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Only Fools and Horses", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Only%20Fools%20and%20Horses"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "John Sullivan sings the opening theme song to Only Fools and Horses, and Chas & Dave sing the closing theme song."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Only Fools and Horses.... is a British television sitcom created and written by John Sullivan.", "wikipage": "Only Fools and Horses"}], "long_answer": "Only Fools and Horses.... is a British television sitcom created and written by John Sullivan. Only Fools and Horses has separate theme songs for the opening and closing credits, \"Only Fools and Horses\" and \"Hooky Street\", respectively. The tune was changed after the first series, and the new one was written by John Sullivan. Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt. Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\"."}], "sample_id": "-8076774342159568962", "question": "Who sings the theme to only fools and horses?", "docs": [{"id": "4484168", "title": "Chas & Dave", "text": "and Dave's Knees-up\", broadcast in 1983. In 1985, Chas & Dave recorded the theme song to the BBC sitcom \"In Sickness and in Health\". Chas & Dave had previously been offered the chance to record the theme song for another BBC sitcom, \"Only Fools and Horses\", but turned it down as they were in Australia at the time due to the success of \"Ain't No Pleasing You\". However, their song \"Margate\" was used in a feature-length episode of the series entitled \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\" in 1989. They also created the theme tune and incidental music for the children television", "score": 0.8125, "summary": "Chas & Dave were offered the chance to record the theme song for \"Only Fools and Horses\" but turned it down. Therefore, the document is relevant but does not provide the answer to the question of who sings the theme to \"Only Fools and Horses.\"", "extraction": "Chas & Dave turned down the chance to record the theme song for \"Only Fools and Horses\"."}], "answer": "Only Fools and Horses.... is a British television sitcom created and written by John Sullivan. Only Fools and Horses has separate theme songs for the opening and closing credits, \"Only Fools and Horses\" and \"Hooky Street\", respectively. The tune was changed after the first series, and the new one was written by John Sullivan. Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt. Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\".", "output": "The theme to Only Fools and Horses was sung by Chas & Dave [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses was sung by Chas & Dave.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 929, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses is sung by John Sullivan, the creator of the British sitcom.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses is sung by John Sullivan, the creator of the British sitcom.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.978752631892778, "ctx": {"title": "The Green Green Grass", "text": "Grass\" when he wrote the first series. He wrote the lyrics to explain the title and why Boycie and his family were running away to the countryside. Music for sitcoms such as \"Last of the Summer Wine\" have remained instrumental however John Sullivan wanted a theme tune that explained the obscure title (as with Only Fools and Horses) and reasons for the characters' sudden dash. The song is performed by John Sullivan. There were rumours at one point, that Status Quo would be involved in the theme tune, but this was dismissed after the first episode aired. Status Quo performed"}, "final_score": 1.978752631892778, "relevance_score": 0.9940889300547145, "ground_score": 0.9846637018380634, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.27816836616696605, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0059107480863266155, "[Relevant]": 0.9940348017106064}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9745739223291718, "[Partially supported]": 0.019604327847215716, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.005529654578558928}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007546178418496931, "[Utility:2]": 9.879113258176338e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00016192746281057748, "[Utility:4]": 0.002200684993061681, "[Utility:5]": 0.5845251818939731}], "n_tokens": 32}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses was sung by Chas & Dave.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses was sung by Chas & Dave.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9942350033092682, "ctx": {"title": "Chas & Dave", "text": "and Dave's Knees-up\", broadcast in 1983. In 1985, Chas & Dave recorded the theme song to the BBC sitcom \"In Sickness and in Health\". Chas & Dave had previously been offered the chance to record the theme song for another BBC sitcom, \"Only Fools and Horses\", but turned it down as they were in Australia at the time due to the success of \"Ain't No Pleasing You\". However, their song \"Margate\" was used in a feature-length episode of the series entitled \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\" in 1989. They also created the theme tune and incidental music for the children television"}, "final_score": 1.9942350033092682, "relevance_score": 0.9992327074553101, "ground_score": 0.9950022958539582, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.306425486136188, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0007672824415783094, "[Relevant]": 0.9992195503359808}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9919759599307262, "[Partially supported]": 0.004281854192630581, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.002852329812384803}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000348660311297606, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011497596748750472, "[Utility:3]": 8.850016161361015e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0017500145447254443, "[Utility:5]": 0.615789748308974}], "n_tokens": 23}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses was sung by John Sullivan, the show's creator, and Hazlehurst.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The theme to Only Fools and Horses was sung by John Sullivan, the show's creator, and Hazlehurst.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.963031204349482, "ctx": {"title": "Only Fools and Horses", "text": "Hunt, John Dean, Judd Proctor, Eddie Mordue, and Rex Morris were hired to play the music. The tune was changed after the first series, and the new one was written by John Sullivan (he disliked the tune for the first series, and his new one explained the show's title), and Hazlehurst conducted it. It was recorded at Lime Grove on 11 May 1982, with musicians John Horler, Dave Richmond, Bigden, and Proctor. Sullivan had intended Chas & Dave to sing it because they had enjoyed success with the \"Rockney\" style, a mixture of rock n' roll and traditional Cockney music."}, "final_score": 1.963031204349482, "relevance_score": 0.9969005845888039, "ground_score": 0.966130619760678, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.336074897379553, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0030993180326737623, "[Relevant]": 0.9968692636159788}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9478758056430968, "[Partially supported]": 0.03507008170207642, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.016309106491248983}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0007478080296269407, "[Utility:2]": 0.00013096875720674283, "[Utility:3]": 0.000264564161952619, "[Utility:4]": 0.002923180603252696, "[Utility:5]": 0.6070491750086686}], "n_tokens": 36}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]John Sessions played the role of John Sullivan in the 2005 TV series Only Fools and Horses.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]John Sullivan wrote the theme song for the British sitcom Only Fools and Horses.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]John Sessions played the role of John Sullivan in the 2005 TV series Only Fools and Horses.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]John Sullivan wrote the theme song for the British sitcom Only Fools and Horses.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.7015485745293335, "ctx": {"title": "Only Fools and Horses", "text": "in a 2008 interview, Sullivan was quoted as saying: \"There will not be another series of \"Only Fools And Horses\". I can say that. We had our day, it was wonderful but it is best to leave it now\". Though Sullivan died in 2011, it returned for a special Sport Relief episode in 2014. \"Only Fools and Horses\" has separate theme songs for the opening and closing credits, \"Only Fools and Horses\" and \"Hooky Street\", respectively. The original theme tune was produced by Ronnie Hazlehurst and recorded on 6 August 1981 at Lime Grove Studios. Alf Bigden, Paul Westwood, Don"}, "final_score": 1.7015485745293335, "relevance_score": 0.9975656175329399, "ground_score": 0.7039829569963936, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.3779210942634204, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.002434325702382038, "[Relevant]": 0.9975423564012532}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.5900073668657945, "[Partially supported]": 0.22046963230517844, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.184209272789402}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00028734221819250877, "[Utility:2]": 0.0004263261554553141, "[Utility:3]": 0.00010632849146852017, "[Utility:4]": 0.002683959548056515, "[Utility:5]": 0.6490919960278306}], "n_tokens": 54}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]John Sullivan was the creator of the British sitcom Only Fools and Horses, which was first broadcast on BBC One in 1978.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]John Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt.[Continue to Use Evidence]Despite the creation of a new theme tune, the original one remained in occasional use.[Continue to Use Evidence]Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\".[Continue to Use Evidence]Both songs are performed by Sullivan himself, and not \u2013 as is sometimes thought \u2013 by Nicholas Lyndhurst.[Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]John Sullivan was the creator of the British sitcom Only Fools and Horses, which was first broadcast on BBC One in 1978.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]John Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt.[Continue to Use Evidence]Despite the creation of a new theme tune, the original one remained in occasional use.[Continue to Use Evidence]Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\".[Continue to Use Evidence]Both songs are performed by Sullivan himself, and not \u2013 as is sometimes thought \u2013 by Nicholas Lyndhurst.[Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.876652849990119, "ctx": {"title": "Only Fools and Horses", "text": "Sullivan was persuaded to do it himself by Ray Butt. Despite the creation of a new theme tune, the original one remained in occasional use. Chas & Dave did later contribute to the show, performing the closing credits song for the 1989 episode \"The Jolly Boys' Outing\". Both songs are performed by Sullivan himself, and not \u2013 as is sometimes thought \u2013 by Nicholas Lyndhurst. The opening credits see images of the three principal actors peel on and off the screen sequentially. These appear over a background of still photographs of everyday life in South London. The sequence was conceived"}, "final_score": 1.876652849990119, "relevance_score": 0.998389642917034, "ground_score": 0.8782632070730849, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.14555555707278853, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0016102866399229589, "[Relevant]": 0.9983459696191518}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8438487825802491, "[Partially supported]": 0.0650706339268171, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.08894101130740446}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 5.593235572657702e-05, "[Utility:2]": 8.16996139909254e-05, "[Utility:3]": 1.4477280045356683e-05, "[Utility:4]": 0.0009684588600897763, "[Utility:5]": 0.937250445817525}], "n_tokens": 140}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 2], "1": [null, 0, 1]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the major circle of latitude at 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 N?", "short_answers": ["Arctic Circle"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the major circle of latitude at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 N?", "short_answers": ["Tropic of Cancer"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "The latitude of the circle is approximately the angle between the Equator and the circle, with the angle's vertex at Earth's centre. The equator is at 0\u00b0, and the North Pole and South Pole are at 90\u00b0 north and 90\u00b0 south, respectively. The Equator is the longest circle of latitude and is the only circle of latitude which also is a great circle.", "question": "What is the major circle of latitude at 0\u00b0 latitude?", "short_answers": ["Equator"], "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the major circle of latitude at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 S?", "short_answers": ["Tropic of Capricorn"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the major circle of latitude at 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 S?", "short_answers": ["Antarctic Circle"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "Circle of latitude", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circle%20of%20latitude"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "There are five major circles of latitude.", "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}, {"content": "The equator is the circle that is equidistant from the North Pole and South Pole.", "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}, {"content": "The Arctic Circle is the southernmost latitude in the Northern Hemisphere at which the sun can remain continuously above or below the horizon for 24 hours.", "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}, {"content": "The Antarctic Circle marks the northernmost latitude in the Southern Hemisphere at which the sun can remain continuously above or below the horizon for 24 hours.", "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}, {"content": "The Tropic of Cancer and Tropic of Capricorn mark the northernmost and southernmost latitudes at which the sun may be seen directly overhead.", "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}], "long_answer": "There are five major circles of latitude: The Equator at 0\u00b0 latitude, The Tropic of Cancer at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 N, the Tropic of Capricorn at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 S, the Arctic Circle at 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 N, and the Antarctic Circle at 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 S."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "A circle of latitude or line of latitude on Earth is an abstract east\u2013west small circle connecting all locations around Earth (ignoring elevation) at a given latitude coordinate line.", "wikipage": "Circle of latitude"}], "long_answer": "A circle of latitude or line of latitude on Earth is an abstract east\u2013west small circle connecting all locations around Earth (ignoring elevation) at a given latitude coordinate line. There are 5 major circles of latitude. At 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 N is the Arctic Circle, and at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 N is the Tropic of Cancer. The equator is at 0\u00b0 latitude. The Tropic of Capricorn is at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 S and the Antarctic Circle is at 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 S."}], "sample_id": "5059862462835087163", "question": "What are the 5 major circles of latitude?", "docs": [{"id": "1277132", "title": "Circle of latitude", "text": "is at 37\u00b0N. Roughly half the length of border between the United States and Canada follows 49\u00b0N. There are five major circles of latitude, listed below from north to south. The position of the Equator is fixed (90 degrees from Earth's axis of rotation) but the latitudes of the other circles depend on the tilt of this axis relative to the plane of Earth's orbit, and so are not perfectly fixed. The values below are for : These circles of latitude, excluding the Equator, mark the divisions between the five principal geographical zones. The equator is the circle that is", "score": 0.80908203125, "summary": "The document states that there are five major circles of latitude, but only lists the position of one at 37\u00b0N and another at 49\u00b0N. It does not provide the names or positions of the other three circles, making the information irrelevant to the question.", "extraction": "The five major circles of latitude, excluding the Equator, mark the divisions between the five principal geographical zones."}], "answer": "A circle of latitude or line of latitude on Earth is an abstract east\u2013west small circle connecting all locations around Earth (ignoring elevation) at a given latitude coordinate line. There are 5 major circles of latitude. At 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 N is the Arctic Circle, and at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 N is the Tropic of Cancer. The equator is at 0\u00b0 latitude. The Tropic of Capricorn is at 23\u00b026\u203212.0\u2033 S and the Antarctic Circle is at 66\u00b033\u203248.0\u2033 S.", "output": "The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 930, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 0, "pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9822004642483135, "ctx": {"title": "Circle of latitude", "text": "is at 37\u00b0N. Roughly half the length of border between the United States and Canada follows 49\u00b0N. There are five major circles of latitude, listed below from north to south. The position of the Equator is fixed (90 degrees from Earth's axis of rotation) but the latitudes of the other circles depend on the tilt of this axis relative to the plane of Earth's orbit, and so are not perfectly fixed. The values below are for : These circles of latitude, excluding the Equator, mark the divisions between the five principal geographical zones. The equator is the circle that is"}, "final_score": 1.9822004642483135, "relevance_score": 0.9996898440314526, "ground_score": 0.982510620216861, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1282980138804594, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.000310146010710599, "[Relevant]": 0.9996577480879734}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9674433011637456, "[Partially supported]": 0.027444253992917646, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.0037433074223493026}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.000275050995584153, "[Utility:2]": 0.0001234918632105627, "[Utility:3]": 0.00044645183357372946, "[Utility:4]": 0.0011854693119008935, "[Utility:5]": 0.6985767490316428}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 2, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 1, "pred": "[Relevant]The 5 major circles of latitude are the Equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The 5 major circles of latitude are the Equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9412899974039362, "ctx": {"title": "Circle of latitude", "text": "can be calculated by a common sine or cosine function. The 60th parallel north or south is half as long as the Equator (disregarding Earth's minor flattening by 0.3%). A circle of latitude is perpendicular to all meridians. The latitude of the circle is approximately the angle between the Equator and the circle, with the angle's vertex at Earth's centre. The equator is at 0\u00b0, and the North Pole and South Pole are at 90\u00b0 north and 90\u00b0 south, respectively. The Equator is the longest circle of latitude and is the only circle of latitude which also is a great"}, "final_score": 1.9412899974039362, "relevance_score": 0.9981178139554213, "ground_score": 0.943172183448515, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.13080315749906982, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0018821266307802685, "[Relevant]": 0.9980863069900238}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.8999901096265441, "[Partially supported]": 0.08113657158158336, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.016102045341438012}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00019392992274263493, "[Utility:2]": 7.927839307480348e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.00033377450185931697, "[Utility:4]": 0.0012113958850275218, "[Utility:5]": 0.8120693389232437}], "n_tokens": 38}, {"node_id": 3, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 2, "pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the Equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the Equator, the Arctic Circle, the North Pole, the South Pole, and the Antarctic Circle.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9759107145352295, "ctx": {"title": "Circle of latitude", "text": "Circle of latitude A circle of latitude on Earth is an abstract east\u2013west circle connecting all locations around Earth (ignoring elevation) at a given latitude. Circles of latitude are often called parallels because they are parallel to each other; that is, any two circles are always the same distance apart. A location's position along a circle of latitude is given by its longitude. Circles of latitude are unlike circles of longitude, which are all great circles with the centre of Earth in the middle, as the circles of latitude get smaller as the distance from the Equator increases. Their length"}, "final_score": 1.9759107145352295, "relevance_score": 0.999312148703387, "ground_score": 0.9765985658318426, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1315079285094037, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0006878183646640516, "[Relevant]": 0.9992643050824983}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9567723737662568, "[Partially supported]": 0.03738905772590157, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.004679788183736717}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00024923784212098305, "[Utility:2]": 9.72224235483863e-05, "[Utility:3]": 0.0004093216919621401, "[Utility:4]": 0.0012756614785983653, "[Utility:5]": 0.7089444488177598}], "n_tokens": 37}, {"node_id": 4, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 3, "pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the Antarctic Circle, the Tropic of Cancer, and the Tropic of Capricorn.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the Antarctic Circle, the Tropic of Cancer, and the Tropic of Capricorn.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.9640901679474725, "ctx": {"title": "Earth's circumference", "text": "system of latitude and longitude. Longditude lines, also known as meridian lines, follow the polar circumference, and are all exactly the same length. Latitude lines, known as circles of latitude are parallel to each other; the equatorial circumference is the largest circle of latitude, and the Earth\u2019s longest circumference distance. Other circles of latitude have smaller circumference; for example, the 60th parallel north or south is approximately half as long as the Equator. According to Cleomedes' \"On the Circular Motions of the Celestial Bodies\", around 240 BC, Eratosthenes, the librarian of the Library of Alexandria, calculated the circumference of the"}, "final_score": 1.9640901679474725, "relevance_score": 0.9993687564536016, "ground_score": 0.9647214114938709, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.1353116824769962, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0006312275737320866, "[Relevant]": 0.9993434689337729}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.9333277525812563, "[Partially supported]": 0.060605246507127467, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.004936067518771291}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.00022532787660749118, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011967073193474552, "[Utility:3]": 0.0005300788651679252, "[Utility:4]": 0.0013695596553178673, "[Utility:5]": 0.7178121886199328}], "n_tokens": 42}, {"node_id": 5, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_idx": 4, "pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the Antarctic Circle, the Tropic of Cancer, and the Tropic of Capricorn.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "processed_pred": "[Relevant]The five major circles of latitude are the equator, the Arctic Circle, the Antarctic Circle, the Tropic of Cancer, and the Tropic of Capricorn.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>", "beam_score": 1.8648137611522797, "ctx": {"title": "Circle of latitude", "text": "the Earth onto a plane. On an equirectangular projection, centered on the equator, the circles of latitude are horizontal, parallel, and equally spaced. On other cylindrical and pseudocylindrical projections, the circles of latitude are horizontal and parallel, but may be spaced unevenly to give the map useful characteristics. For instance, on a Mercator projection the circles of latitude are more widely spaced near the poles to preserve local scales and shapes, while on a Gall\u2013Peters projection the circles of latitude are spaced more closely near the poles so that comparisons of area will be accurate. On most non-cylindrical and non-pseudocylindrical"}, "final_score": 1.8648137611522797, "relevance_score": 0.99734774067545, "ground_score": 0.8674660204768297, "utility_score": 0.0, "seq_score": -0.12074073665715446, "relevance_probs": {"[Irrelevant]": 0.0026521883538672014, "[Relevant]": 0.9973210530700654}, "support_probs": [{"[Fully supported]": 0.7610762963001168, "[Partially supported]": 0.2064474872404954, "[No support / Contradictory]": 0.028826528192953074}], "utility_probs": [{"[Utility:1]": 0.0002808508840334005, "[Utility:2]": 0.00011106209859477562, "[Utility:3]": 0.0003720667932276624, "[Utility:4]": 0.0016288603701259468, "[Utility:5]": 0.7803602371477114}], "n_tokens": 42}], "best_paths": {"0": [null, 0, 1], "1": [null, 0, 3]}, "output_path": 0}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who wrote and sang the song i'm not going to miss you?", "short_answers": ["Glen Campbell"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who wrote and produced the song i'm not going to miss you?", "short_answers": ["Julian Raymond"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "I'm Not Gonna Miss You", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%27m%20Not%20Gonna%20Miss%20You"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [{"content": "\"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is a song recorded by American country music artist Glen Campbell and The Wrecking Crew. Co-written by Campbell and producer Julian Raymond, the song was released on September 30, 2014 for the soundtrack to the documentary Glen Campbell: I'll Be Me, which focuses on the singer's diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and his final tour.[2]", "wikipage": "I'm Not Gonna Miss You"}, {"content": "\"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is the last song to be recorded by Campbell.[3] The song was nominated for Best Original Song at the 87th Academy Awards. During the ceremony, the song was performed by Tim McGraw.[4] It also won the Grammy Award for Best Country Song.[5]", "wikipage": "I'm Not Gonna Miss You"}], "long_answer": "I'm Not Gonna Miss You is a song recorded by American country music artist Glen Campbell and The Wrecking Crew. Co-written by Campbell and producer Julian Raymond, the song was released on September 30, 2014 for the soundtrack to the documentary Glen Campbell: I'll Be Me, which focuses on the singer's diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and his final tour. I'm Not Gonna Miss You is the last song to be recorded by Campbell. The song was nominated for Best Original Song at the 87th Academy Awards. During the ceremony, the song was performed by Tim McGraw. It also won the Grammy Award for Best Country Song."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "\"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is a song recorded by American country music artist Glen Campbell and The Wrecking Crew.", "wikipage": "I'm Not Gonna Miss You"}, {"content": "Julian Raymond is an American songwriter and music producer.", "wikipage": "Julian Raymond"}], "long_answer": "Glen Campbell and Julian Raymond wrote the song I'm Not Gonna Miss You. Campbell, an American country music artist, also sang the song and recorded it with The Wrecking Crew. Raymond, an American songwriter and music producer, also produced the song."}], "sample_id": "-6211863698115989244", "question": "Who wrote the song i'm not going to miss you?", "docs": [{"id": "18606535", "title": "I'm Not Gonna Miss You", "text": "part of the soundtrack for \"\" which premiered on October 24, 2014. I'm Not Gonna Miss You \"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is a song recorded by American country music artist Glen Campbell and The Wrecking Crew. Co-written by Campbell and producer Julian Raymond, the song was released on September 30, 2014 for the soundtrack to the documentary \"\", which focuses on the singer's diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and his final tour. \"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is the last song to be recorded by Campbell. The song was nominated for Best Original Song at the 87th Academy Awards. During", "score": 0.80810546875, "summary": "The song \"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" was written by Glen Campbell and producer Julian Raymond, and recorded by Campbell and The Wrecking Crew. It was released on September 30, 2014 for the soundtrack to the documentary about Campbell's battle with Alzheimer's disease. The song was also nominated for Best Original Song at the 87th Academy Awards.", "extraction": "Glen Campbell and producer Julian Raymond co-wrote the song \"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\"."}], "answer": "Glen Campbell and Julian Raymond wrote the song I'm Not Gonna Miss You. Campbell, an American country music artist, also sang the song and recorded it with The Wrecking Crew. Raymond, an American songwriter and music producer, also produced the song.", "output": "\"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is a song recorded by American country music artist Glen Campbell and The Wrecking Crew [1]. The song was released on September 30, 2014 for the soundtrack to the documentary \"Glen Campbell: I'll Be Me\", which focuses on the singer's diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and his final tour [1]. The song was nominated for Best Original Song at the 87th Academy Awards [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "[Relevant]\"I'm Not Gonna Miss You\" is a song recorded by American country music artist Glen Campbell and The Wrecking Crew.[Fully supported][Continue to Use Evidence]The song was released on September 30, 2014 for the soundtrack to the documentary \"Glen Campbell: I'll Be Me\", which focuses on the singer's diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and his final tour.[Continue to Use Evidence]The song was nominated for Best Original Song at the 87th Academy Awards.[Utility:5]</s>"], "q_idx": 931, "candidates": [{"node_id": 1, "parent": 0, "depth": 1, "p_id